 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/06/01/world/europe/russia-ex-officer-sentenced-to-12-years-after-being-convicted-of-spying-for-us.html', 'snippet': 'The retired officer, Col. Vladimir Lazar, was charged with espionage for selling what officials said were classified topographical maps to a man they said was a Pentagon agent.', 'lead_paragraph': ' A Moscow court has sentenced a retired Russian military officer to 12 years in prison for spying for the United States, prosecutors said Thursday. The retired officer, Col. Vladimir Lazar, was charged with espionage for selling what officials said were classified topographical maps to a man they said was a Pentagon agent. Prosecutors said Colonel Lazar bought computer disks containing 7,000 maps, then smuggled them into Belarus and passed them to Alexander Lesment, a Russian émigré they identified as an agent for the United States. Marina Gridneva, a spokeswoman for the prosecutor, said the disks contained state secrets that “could inflict real damage on the domestic security of Russia, allowing the planning of possible military actions.” Colonel Lazar will be stripped of his rank and confined to a high-security prison, according to the Federal Security Service. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Russia: Ex-Officer Sentenced to 12 Years After Being Convicted of Spying for U.S.', 'kicker': 'World Briefing | Europe', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Russia: Ex-Officer Sentenced to 12 Years After Being Convicted of Spying for U.S.', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Espionage and Intelligence Services', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Russia', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lazar, Vladimir', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sentences (Criminal)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-01T03:49:42+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Europe', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ellen Barry', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ellen', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Barry', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/01a20242-03c5-5c2f-8586-0cac3aa7d74c', 'word_count': 139, 'uri': 'nyt://article/01a20242-03c5-5c2f-8586-0cac3aa7d74c'}, {'abstract': 'City lawyers say there was probable cause for the arrests, but the plaintiffs disagree.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/06/01/nyregion/city-battles-false-arrest-suits-at-2004-republican-convention.html', 'snippet': 'City lawyers say there was probable cause for the arrests, but the plaintiffs disagree.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Lawyers for New York City asked a federal judge on Thursday to dismiss dozens of lawsuits filed against the city by hundreds of people who contend they were falsely arrested during the 2004 Republican National Convention in Manhattan.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'City Seeks to Dismiss False-Arrest Suits Stemming From 2004 Republican Convention', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'City Seeks to Dismiss False-Arrest Suits Stemming From 2004 Republican Convention', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Republican National Convention', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Demonstrations, Protests, and Riots', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'False Arrests, Convictions and Imprisonments', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Police Department (NYC)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Suits and Litigation', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'War Resisters League', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'gl