avel, Messiaen and Henri Dutilleux.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/06/03/arts/music/renee-flemings-poemes-with-alan-gilbert-and-seiji-ozawa.html', 'snippet': 'Renée Fleming’s latest album offers French works for soprano and orchestra by Ravel, Messiaen and Henri Dutilleux.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Renée Fleming, soprano; Orchestre Philharmonique de Radio France, conducted by Alan Gilbert; Orchestre National de France, conducted by Seiji Ozawa. Decca 478 3500; CD and download.', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Renée Fleming’s ‘Poèmes’ With Alan Gilbert and Seiji Ozawa', 'kicker': 'Classical Recordings', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Renée Fleming: ‘Poèmes’', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Fleming, Renee', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Classical Music', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-01T14:08:51+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Anthony Tommasini', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Anthony', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Tommasini', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/c6111e82-cfee-5857-8eed-c319453b6757', 'word_count': 302, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c6111e82-cfee-5857-8eed-c319453b6757'}, {'abstract': 'Michael Hearst, best known as a member of One Ring Zero, has been branching out on his own.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/06/03/arts/music/michael-hearsts-songs-for-unusual-creatures.html', 'snippet': 'Michael Hearst, best known as a member of One Ring Zero, has been branching out on his own.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Kronos Quartet; Microscopic Septet; Margaret Leng Tan, toy pianist; Michael Hearst, multi-instrumentalist; others. Urban Geek Records UGR043; CD. ', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Michael Hearst’s ‘Songs for Unusual Creatures’', 'kicker': 'Classical Recordings', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Michael Hearst: ‘Songs for Unusual Creatures’', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Classical Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hearst, Michael', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Tan, Margaret Leng', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Kronos Quartet', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-01T14:10:14+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Allan Kozinn', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Allan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kozinn', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/34a2487a-642b-5d94-9416-2efea8a8e24f', 'word_count': 324, 'uri': 'nyt://article/34a2487a-642b-5d94-9416-2efea8a8e24f'}, {'abstract': 'The Rev. Al Sharpton on having been “like a son” to the legendary singer James Brown.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/06/03/books/review/up-front.html', 'snippet': 'The Rev. Al Sharpton on having been “like a son” to the legendary singer James Brown.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In his review of “The One,” RJ Smith’s biography of James Brown, the Rev. Al Sharpton describes himself as having been “like a son” to the Godfather of Soul. “I grew up part of my life in Queens,” Sharpton said in a telephone interview, “and all the kids would look over the back fence when he had a mansion in St. Albans.” Brown and Sharpton met years later at various events, but it was in 1973, backstage at Newark Symphony Hall, when they truly first connected. Brown’s son Teddy had recently died, and Sharpton’s father had walked out on the family when he was still a boy. “I became a replacement for Teddy,” Sharpton said, while Brown “became the father I lost. The only memories I have of doing things with my father are going to the Apollo to see James Brown.” In 1982, Brown and Sharpton flew to Washington to lobby President Ronald Reagan on behalf of a national holiday in honor of Martin Luther King Jr. On the way there, Brown said: “Do me a favor. I want you to style your hair like mine.” By the time they reached Washington, Sharpton recalled, “I had my hair styled like his,” and he promised to keep it like that for as long as Brown was alive.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/03/books/review/0603-bks-Upfront/0603-bks-Upfront-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 668, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/03/books/review/0603-bks-Upfront/0603-bks-Upfront-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1336, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Up Front', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Up Front', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-01T14:10:15+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Editors', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Editors'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a45562d1-9251-53d6-a8ac-762b927546f5', 'word_count': 324, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a45562d1-9251-53d6-a8ac-762b927546f5'}, {'abstract': 'The Orchestra of the Swan have recorded Hans Gál’s Fourth Symphony and Robert Schumann’s Second Symphony in a pairing that situates Gál in music history.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/06/03/arts/music/orchestra-of-the-swan-record-gal-and-schumann.html', 'snippet': 'The Orchestra of the Swan have recorded Hans Gál’s Fourth Symphony and Robert Schumann’s Second Symphony in a pairing that situates Gál in music history.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Orchestra of the Swan, conducted by Kenneth Woods. Avie AV2231; CD.', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Orchestra of the Swan Record Gál and Schumann', 'kicker': 'Classical Recordings', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Gál: Symphony No. 4; Schumann: Symphony No. 2', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Classical Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Orchestra of the Swan', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gal, Hans', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Woods, Kenneth', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-01T14:10:42+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By James R. Oestreich', 'person': [{'firstname': 'James', 'middlename': 'R.', 'lastname': 'Oestreich', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/bd3ca864-8fb2-52ee-9116-210222ab0fea', 'word_count': 321, 'uri': 'nyt://article/bd3ca864-8fb2-52ee-9116-210222ab0fea'}, {'abstract': 'RJ Smith’s biography of James Brown links the legendary singer’s work to broader cultural changes in America.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/06/03/books/review/the-one-rj-smiths-biography-of-james-brown.html', 'snippet': 'RJ Smith’s biography of James Brown links the legendary singer’s work to broader cultural changes in America.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When James Brown’s children and I brought his body back to Harlem from Georgia after his untimely death in 2006, tens of thousands greeted us in the streets upon our arrival. There were no stars, no concerts; just everyday folks paying homage to the Godfather of Soul (and so much more) in person. As we laid him in state at the Apollo Thea\xadter, we took a moment to absorb this extraordinary show of support. I remember walking the streets of Harlem and shaking hands with many gathered outside the Apollo that day, and I’ll never forget a writer who came up to me and said he was shocked by the sheer numbers of people, and taken aback by how much of a revered figure James Brown was. People were often surprised at his relevance, but James never doubted his own significance, or the fact that he was a historic figure and an undeniably game-changing artist. His showmanship and art altered the music world. But James didn’t bring blacks to the mainstream; instead, he brought the mainstream to blacks and made them appreciate and internalize black music and culture themselves. “The One: The Life and Music of James Brown,” by RJ Smith, is the first book to capture this remarkable reality. ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '11', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/03/books/review/03SHARPTON/03SHARPTON-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 995, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/03/books/review/03SHARPTON/03SHARPTON-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1990, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'su