a was once the smaller, bolder company in Manhattan. It remains smaller, but not always bolder and not always in Manhattan.', 'lead_paragraph': 'EVERY institution, whether a corporation, a college or a newspaper, should be able to define what it is and what it does. Performing arts organizations are no exception. For decades New York City Opera was a model of an organization with a clear mission. Now there may be no opera company, orchestra or ensemble more in need of a mission reboot.', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/03/arts/03OPERA1_SPAN/03OPERA1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 360, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/06/03/arts/03OPERA1_SPAN/03OPERA1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 360}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/03/arts/03OPERA1_SPAN/03OPERA1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/03/arts/03OPERA1_SPAN/03OPERA1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/03/arts/03OPERA1_SPAN/03OPERA1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/06/03/arts/03OPERA1_SPAN/03OPERA1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/03/arts/03OPERA1_SPAN/03OPERA1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'City Opera, Haunted by Its Past', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'City Opera, Haunted By Its Past', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Opera', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Steel, George', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York City Opera', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Classical Music', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-01T14:12:33+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Anthony Tommasini', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Anthony', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Tommasini', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f28eb84d-7e29-57d2-9fba-7c0dba1d1eea', 'word_count': 1839, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f28eb84d-7e29-57d2-9fba-7c0dba1d1eea'}, {'abstract': '“Pinstripe Empire” is an insider’s history of the New York Yankees, while the writers in “Damn Yankees” offer essays about loving and hating baseball’s most iconic team.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/06/03/books/review/pinstripe-empire-and-damn-yankees.html', 'snippet': '“Pinstripe Empire” is an insider’s history of the New York Yankees, while the writers in “Damn Yankees” offer essays about loving and hating baseball’s most iconic team.', 'lead_paragraph': 'People always ask me if I still root for the Yankees. The truth is, the only time I rooted for them was when I was a Yankee. And when I was in the minor leagues, I rooted for the Yankees to lose so they’d need help.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/03/books/review/03BOUTON/03BOUTON-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 776, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/03/books/review/03BOUTON/03BOUTON-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1553, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/03/books/review/03BOUTON/03BOUTON-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/06/03/books/review/03BOUTON/03BOUTON-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/03/books/review/03BOUTON/03BOUTON-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Bronx Tales', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Bronx Tales', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Fleder, Rob', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Baseball', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Appel, Marty', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Yankees', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-01T14:15:59+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jim Bouton', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jim', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bouton', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/0557b7e6-2915-59d9-9523-0e23153ef96b', 'word_count': 1057, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0557b7e6-2915-59d9-9523-0e23153ef96b'}, {'abstract': 'This book marking the centennial of Fenway Park portrays the home of the Red Sox as the St. Peter’s Basilica of American sports.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/06/03/books/review/fenway-park-100-years.html', 'snippet': 'This book marking the centennial of Fenway Park portrays the home of the Red Sox as the St. Peter’s Basilica of American sports.', 'lead_paragraph': 'April 20, 1912, should have been a grand day in Boston. Following two rainouts, the hometown Red Sox opened their new ballpark in glorious sunshine by defeating the New York Highlanders (soon to be the Yankees) in extra innings before a crowd so large that it poured from the stands and encircled the outfield. But the city, truth be told, saw no reason to celebrate. An unimaginable tragedy had just occurred at sea, turning the birth of Fenway Park into a sour afterthought. Most Red Sox fans attribute the team’s endless woes to selling Babe Ruth to the hated Yankees in 1919, the so-called Curse of the Bambino. I blame the Titanic.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '23', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/03/books/review/03OSHINSKY/03OSHINSKY-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 231, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/06/03/books/review/03OSHINSKY/03OSHINSKY-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 231}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/03/books/review/03OSHINSKY/03OSHINSKY-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 395, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/20