w.nytimes.com/2012/06/02/business/daily-stock-market-activity.html', 'snippet': 'Stocks on Wall Street closed sharply lower and benchmark bond prices marched higher as the latest jobs data in the United States provided evidence that the economy was still struggling.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In their worst daily performance this year, stocks dropped more than 2 percent on Wall Street Friday, and United States government bond yields fell to record lows as investors sought refuge amid new signs of trouble in the global economy.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/02/business/MARKETS/MARKETS-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 799, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/02/business/MARKETS/MARKETS-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1597, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Wall Street Tumbles 2% on Jobs Data', 'kicker': 'Stocks and Bonds', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Wall Street Tumbles 2% on Jobs Data', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Economy', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Labor and Jobs', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Stocks and Bonds', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-01T14:16:32+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Christine Hauser', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Christine', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hauser', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/062423b6-6767-5b12-8356-294b8cf5e855', 'word_count': 882, 'uri': 'nyt://article/062423b6-6767-5b12-8356-294b8cf5e855'}, {'abstract': 'Beginning in the early 1960s, Frank Deford’s career has paralleled the modern age of sports reporting.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/06/03/books/review/over-time-a-memoir-by-frank-deford.html', 'snippet': 'Beginning in the early 1960s, Frank Deford’s career has paralleled the modern age of sports reporting.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Unlike this one, perhaps, a first sentence can tell you a lot about what you’re about to read. It contains clues to the writer’s voice, the writer’s attitude toward a subject, the writer’s rhetorical imagination and skill. It can be a turn-on or a turnoff.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '30', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/03/books/review/03WEBER/03WEBER-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 396, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/06/03/books/review/03WEBER/03WEBER-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 396}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/03/books/review/03WEBER/03WEBER-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 806, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/03/books/review/03WEBER/03WEBER-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1612, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/03/books/review/03WEBER/03WEBER-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/06/03/books/review/03WEBER/03WEBER-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/03/books/review/03WEBER/03WEBER-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Sportswriter', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Sportswriter', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Deford, Frank', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-01T14:17:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Bruce Weber', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Bruce', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Weber', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/4e403025-eef8-55e9-9785-15507b653a5d', 'word_count': 1262, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4e403025-eef8-55e9-9785-15507b653a5d'}, {'abstract': 'This collection of images examines the recreational and symbolic value of swimming pools in 20th-century Southern California photography.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/06/03/books/review/backyard-oasis-edited-by-daniell-cornell.html', 'snippet': 'This collection of images examines the recreational and symbolic value of swimming pools in 20th-century Southern California photography.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When my husband and I lived in Los Angeles, we lucked into an original black-and-white print by the photographer Julius Shulman for a ridiculously low price. It showed a glimmering infinity swimming pool that, we later learned, was part of Silvertop, a famous house designed by John Lautner. The photograph was framed and mounted in a place of honor on the wall of our dining room, where inevitably it would inspire guests to ask, “Is that your pool?”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/03/books/review/03JACOBS/03JACOBS-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 875, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/03/books/review/03JACOBS/03JACOBS-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1750, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/03/books/review/03JACOBS/03JACOBS-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/06/03/books/review/03JACOBS/03JACOBS-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/03/books/review/03JACOBS/03JACOBS-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Pool Party', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Pool Party', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cornell, Daniell', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Swimming Pools', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Photography', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'California', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-01T14:17:46+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Alexandra Jacobs', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Alexandra', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Jacobs', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/194dd658-96b6-554e-b229-de01289f7193', 'word_count': 799, 'uri': 'nyt://article/194dd658-96b6-554e-b229-de01289f7193'}, {'abstract': 'The living must deal with the dead in this novel set on the brink of the French Revolution.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/06/03/books/review/pure-a-novel-by-andrew-miller.html', 'snippet': 'The living must deal with the dead in this novel set on the brink of the French Revolution.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Some stories are too wonderful — too filled with wonders — to set in the present. They can’t really be called historical fiction because they don’t serve history so much as plunder it to invent what might have been. Such is the case with “Pure,” by Andrew Miller, a novel set during the Age of Enlightenment that pays homage not to the dawn of reason but to its witching hour, teeming with all that reason mocks — hobgoblins, specters and whatever else might be lurking in the dark. A novel of ideas disguised as a ghost story, voluptuously atmospheric, “Pure” exerts a sensual hold over the reader.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/03/books/review/03HARRISON/03HARRISON-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 347, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/06/03/books/review/03HARRISON/03HARRISON-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 347}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/03/books/review/03HARRISON/03HARRISON-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 594, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/03/books/review/03HARRISON/03HARRISON-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1188, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/03/books/review/03HARRISON/03HARRISON-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/06/03/books/review/03HARRISON/03HARRISON-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/03/books/review/03HARRISON/03HARRISON-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Les Innocents', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Les Innocents', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Miller, Andrew', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-01T14:18:29+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kathryn Harrison', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kathryn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Harrison', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/2b272978-2883-538b-8d89-d3aa4c0ed7fb', 'word_count': 1196, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2b272978-2883-538b-8d89-d3aa4c0ed7fb'}, {'abstract': 'In Anuradha Roy’s novel, a woman mourning her husband finds refuge — and then disturbances — in a small town in India.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/06/03/books/review/the-folded-earth-by-anuradha-roy.html', 'snippet': 'In Anuradha Roy’s novel, a woman mourning her husband finds refuge — and then disturbances — in a small town in India.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Late in this quietly mesmerizing novel, set in a Himalayan hill town in the north of India, Anuradha Roy describes the crystalline beauty of the peaks in winter, viewed long after the haze of the summer months and the fog of the monsoon, held in secret for those who choose to brave the cold: “After the last of the daylight is gone, at dusk, the peaks still glimmer in the slow-growing darkness as if jagged pieces of the moon had dropped from sky to earth.” In the mountains, one of Roy’s characters observes, “love must be tested by adversity.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '19', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/03/books/review/03THOMPSON/03THOMPSON-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 878, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/03/books/review/03THOMPSON/03THOMPSON-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1755, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/03/books/review/03THOMPSON/03THOMPSON-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/06/03/books/review/03THOMPSON/03THOMPSON-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/03/books/review/03THOMPSON/03THOMPSON-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Peaks', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Peaks', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Roy, Anuradha', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-01T14:18:49+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andrea Thompson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andrea', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Thompson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/d554960b-b494-58a9-aa20-da854fd55fba', 'word_count': 596, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d554960b-b494-58a9-aa20-da854fd55fba'}, {'abstract': 'This first novel is narrated from the perspective of two women: one a reluctant missionary in 1923; the other a young resident of present-day London.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/06/03/books/review/a-lady-cyclists-guide-to-kashgar-by-suzanne-joinson.html', 'snippet': 'This first novel is narrated from the perspective of two women: one a reluctant missionary in 1923; the other a young resident of present-day London.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A 10-year-old Turkic girl is giving birth in the dust, screaming “loud enough to kill trees.” Happening past, a bony-fingered missionary dismounts from her horse, tips back her head “so that her eyeglasses retreated along her nose” and catches the blue-red baby “like a fish.” The dramatic opening of Suzanne Joinson’s thrilling and densely plotted first novel offers only a suggestion of the tumult to come.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '20', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/