and now the veteran defender hopes to stick with the United States national team.', 'web_url': 'https://goal.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/06/01/u-s-defender-parkhurst-and-his-championship-season/', 'snippet': 'Michael Parkhurst won a title in Denmark this season and now the veteran defender hopes to stick with the United States national team.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The 2011-12 European season was a good one for many American players, and many of the players on the United States national team’s current roster reflect that success.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'U.S. Defender Parkhurst, and His Championship Season', 'kicker': 'Goal', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Soccer', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Klinsmann, Jurgen', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': "United States Men's National Soccer Team", 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'United States', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-01T14:19:48+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Soccer', 'byline': {'original': 'By Brian Sciaretta', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Brian', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Sciaretta', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2ae0d2e5-5927-587a-ba88-f436e2b8e54a', 'word_count': 947, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2ae0d2e5-5927-587a-ba88-f436e2b8e54a'}, {'abstract': 'Inspector Salvo Montalbano, the life force of Andrea Camilleri’s droll police procedurals, is back in “The Age of Doubt.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/06/03/books/review/andrea-camilleris-the-age-of-doubt-and-more.html', 'snippet': 'Inspector Salvo Montalbano, the life force of Andrea Camilleri’s droll police procedurals, is back in “The Age of Doubt.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'It’s almost summer, so let’s blow this town and go to the beach — always a popular setting for murder.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '21', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/03/books/review/0603-bks-Crime/0603-bks-Crime-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 851, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/03/books/review/0603-bks-Crime/0603-bks-Crime-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1701, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/03/books/review/0603-bks-Crime/0603-bks-Crime-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/06/03/books/review/0603-bks-Crime/0603-bks-Crime-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/03/books/review/0603-bks-Crime/0603-bks-Crime-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Buried in the Sand', 'kicker': 'Crime', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Buried in the Sand', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Grabenstein, Chris', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Viets, Elaine', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Siger, Jeffrey', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Camilleri, Andrea', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cotterill, Colin', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-01T14:19:56+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Marilyn Stasio', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Marilyn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Stasio', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/2e2911d3-7206-5e99-b134-0f7c0d9c7069', 'word_count': 928, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2e2911d3-7206-5e99-b134-0f7c0d9c7069'}, {'abstract': 'An expatriate American and sometime spy gets entangled in a final job after World War II in this novel.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/06/03/books/review/istanbul-passage-a-novel-by-joseph-kanon.html', 'snippet': 'An expatriate American and sometime spy gets entangled in a final job after World War II in this novel.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Joseph Kanon is a specialist in fin de guerre thrillers, whose previous novels set in 1945-46 include “Alibi,” “Los Alamos,” “The Good German” and “Stardust.” The period is well chosen: dark secrets are finally coming to the surface, and people are being called to account for what the war has made them. For readers, it’s immediately recognizable territory: the aftermath of the conflict, shabby, filmic. Now, in “Istanbul Passage,” Kanon compounds the fraught postwar mood with a location to match.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '34', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/03/books/review/03GOODWIN/03GOODWIN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/06/03/books/review/03GOODWIN/03GOODWIN-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'cap