 party, this ain’t no disco, this ain’t no fooling around.” Jonathan Lethem’s monograph on Talking Heads’ “Fear of Music” isn’t your everyday, step-by-step, track-by-track snapshot of a great record. “This ain’t the Mudd Club, or CBGB, I ain’t got time for that now.” And, most definitely, Lethem’s contribution to Continuum’s “33 1/3” series isn’t a quick spin down memory lane. It’s obsessive, passionate and personal. In short, the name of this new book by Jonathan Lethem is “Fear of Music.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/03/books/review/03IRWIN/03IRWIN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 352, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/06/03/books/review/03IRWIN/03IRWIN-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 352}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/03/books/review/03IRWIN/03IRWIN-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 602, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/03/books/review/03IRWIN/03IRWIN-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1203, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/03/books/review/03IRWIN/03IRWIN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/06/03/books/review/03IRWIN/03IRWIN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/03/books/review/03IRWIN/03IRWIN-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Memories Can’t Wait', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Memories Can’t Wait', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Rock Music', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Talking Heads', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lethem, Jonathan', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-01T14:23:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Pat Irwin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Pat', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Irwin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/af88c30e-dd4d-5109-8665-2acdd6ea5ae6', 'word_count': 803, 'uri': 'nyt://article/af88c30e-dd4d-5109-8665-2acdd6ea5ae6'}, {'abstract': 'One biographer tries to get inside the head of Bob Dylan, while another assesses the social and personal factors that shaped Bruce Springsteen.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/06/03/books/review/new-books-about-bob-dylan-and-bruce-springsteen.html', 'snippet': 'One biographer tries to get inside the head of Bob Dylan, while another assesses the social and personal factors that shaped Bruce Springsteen.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Sometimes the “you” in my songs is me talking to me. Other times I can be talking to somebody else. . . . When I say “I” right now I don’t know who I’m talking about.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '28', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/03/books/review/03FINN/03FINN-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 915, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/03/books/review/03FINN/03FINN-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1830, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/03/books/review/03FINN/03FINN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/06/03/books/review/03FINN/03FINN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/03/books/review/03FINN/03FINN-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Rock Center', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Rock Center', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Springsteen, Bruce', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Dylan, Bob', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Rock Music', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Dalton, David', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Dolan, Marc', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-01T14:23:40+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Robin Finn', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Robin', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Finn', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/48cd569a-e391-5a4f-b83e-e85b45ede1d1', 'word_count': 1326, 'uri': 'nyt://article/48cd569a-e391-5a4f-b83e-e85b45ede1d1'}, {'abstract': 'An analysis suggests that the power sector will incur far lower costs if federal pollution rules are phased in more gradually.', 'web_url': 'https://green.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/06/01/when-cleaning-up-power-plants-time-is-money/', 'snippet': 'An analysis suggests that the power sector will incur far lower costs if federal pollution rules are phased in more gradually.', 'lead_paragraph': 'New rules on pollution from coal plants will cost the American economy $175 billion to $275 billion between now and 2035, according to a new analysis from the Electric Power Research Institute, a nonprofit utility consortium. But the price can be be closer to the lower figure if the government shows flexibility in how the rules are phased in, the researchers said. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'When Cleaning Up Power Plants, Time is Money', 'kicker': 'Green', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Air Pollution', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Chemicals', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Coal', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computers and the Internet', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Electric Light and Power', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Greenhouse Gas Emissions', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hazardous and Toxic Substances', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mercury (Metal)', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Natural Gas', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Research', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Wind Power', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Electric Power Research Institute', 'rank': 12, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-01T14:24:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Science', 'subsection_name': 'Environment', 'byline': {'original': 'By Matthew L. Wald', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Matthew', 'middlename': 'L.', 'lastname': 'Wald', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/52d06fc0-3a43-5d43-9100-f0e55880ef1c', 'word_count': 645, 'uri': 'nyt://article/52d06fc0-3a43-5d43-9100-f0e55880ef1c'}, {'abstract': 'Rick Moody’s essays wrestle with performers and songs as if they were his better angels and personal hellhounds.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/06/03/books/review/on-celestial-music-by-rick-moody.html', 'snippet': 'Rick Moody’s essays wrestle with performers and songs as if they were his better angels and personal hellhounds.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Some of my favorite music writing of the last decade has turned up in novels: the precise absurdist riffs and def(t) beats of Paul Beatty’s “Slumberland”; the beatific polluted surf tones of Thomas Pynchon’s “Inherent Vice”; the deceptively quiet way music saturates Mary Gaitskill’s “Veronica” as a fateful promise that turns into a blinding curse; the measured obsessive-compulsiveness traced by Dana Spiotta’s “Eat the Document” and “Stone Arabia,” testaments to how damaged people can use music to map the world and the scars it leaves behind. These books gauge how music penetrates (or reinforces) our defenses. In very different ways, they confirm what a woman I love once told me: the intimacy and raw power of music can be more dangerous than sex. ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '29', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/03/books/review/03HAMPTON/03HAMPTON-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/06/03/books/review/03HAMPTON/03HAMPTON-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/03/books/review/03HAMPTON/03HAMPTON-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 854, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/03/books/review/03HAMPTON/03HAMPTON-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1708, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/03/books/review/03HAMPTON/03HAMPTON-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/06/03/books/review/03HAMPTON/03HAMPTON-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/03/books/review/03HAMPTON/03HAMPTON-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 118, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Riffs', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Riffs', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Moody, Rick', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-01T14:24:11+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Howard Hampton', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Howard', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hampton', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/d482099b-e9bc-5406-99dc-48e5e2e36fc6', 'word_count': 1269, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d482099b-e9bc-5406-99dc-48e5e2e36fc6'}, {'abstract': 'This illustrated book offers a potted history and some 300 photographs of the ultimate symbol of French grandeur.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/06/03/books/review/versailles-by-valerie-bajou.html', 'snippet': 'This illustrated book offers a potted history and some 300 photographs of the ultimate symbol of French grandeur.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Palace of Versailles served as France’s seat of government for just a century: Louis XIV moved his court there in 1682, there was a short break during Louis XV’s childhood and, in 1789, Louis XVI and Marie Antoinette were driven out by the swelling revolution. Yet ever since, in its various guises of museum, ceremonial court and tourist attraction, the palace — the French call it a chateau, although it is not a fortified castle — has remained a symbol of French grandeur. Today’s elected governments see no contradiction in spending heavily to conserve this monument to absolute power.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '35', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/03/books/review/03RIDING/03RIDING-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 327, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/06/03/books/review/03RIDING/03RIDING-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 327}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/03/books/review/03RIDING/03RIDING-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 560, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/03/books/review/03RIDING/03RIDING-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1119, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/03/books/review/03RIDING/03RIDING-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/06/03/books/review/03RIDING/03RIDING-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/03/books/review/03RIDING/03RIDING-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Here Comes the Sun King', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Here Comes the Sun King', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Versailles (France)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bajou, Valerie', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Shugaar, Antony', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-01T14:24:55+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Alan Riding', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Alan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Riding', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/792a6f29-5331-5cc9-b8d4-e660b1a3e223', 'word_count': 775, 'uri': 'nyt://article/792a6f29-5331-5cc9-b8d4-e660b1a3e223'}, {'abstract': 'Anna Quindlen’s collection of essays deals with crossing the Rubicon from late middle age to early old age.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/06/03/books/review/lots-of-candles-plenty-of-cake-by-anna-quindlen.html', 'snippet': 'Anna Quindlen’s collection of essays deals with crossing the Rubicon from late middle age to early old age.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Here’s the thing about being a brand name: What people want most from you is a kind of comforting predictability. Variations on a theme, sure, but when we pop open a can of Coke, it had bloody well better taste like Coke. (Remember New Coke? No? There’s a reason.) And so it is with Anna Quindlen, who’s as close to a brand name as a writer can be. Her twinkling aphorisms, her gentle homespun humor, her mulling over what might be termed White People Problems: this is what her fans expect from her. And this is what she serves to them in generous portions in “Lots of Candles, Plenty of Cake.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '40', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/03/books/review/Newman/Newman-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 851, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/03/books/review/Newman/Newman-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1701, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/03/books/review/Newman/Newman-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/06/03/books/review/Newman/Newman-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/03/books/review/Newman/Newman-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 181, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Life in the 50s', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Life in the 50s', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Quindlen, Anna', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-01T14:25:37+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Judith Newman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Judith', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Newman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/624d6fa2-9c56-5cdb-87d4-60a74ac1c917', 'word_count': 813, 'uri': 'nyt://article/624d6fa2-9c56-5cdb-87d4-60a74ac1c917'}, {'abstract': 'Maria Sharapova defeated Ayiumi Morita in straight sets at the French Open on Friday.', 'web_url': 'https://straightsets.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/06/01/sharapova-sails-into-third-round/', 'snippet': 'Maria Sharapova defeated Ayiumi Morita in straight sets at the French Open on Friday.', 'lead_paragraph': 'PARIS — With customary shrieks and powerful strokes, Maria Sharapova of Russia moved into the third round of the French Open Friday with a decisive 6-1, 6-1 victory over Ayiumi Morita of Japan.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Sharapova Sails Into Third Round', 'kicker': 'Straight Sets', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'French Open (Tennis)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Tennis', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Sharapova, Maria', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-01T14:26:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Tennis', 'byline': {'original': 'By John Martin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'John', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Martin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/89a09904-1e2e-5066-82c0-5bb3588163b2', 'word_count': 173, 'uri': 'nyt://article/89a09904-1e2e-5066-82c0-5bb3588163b2'}, {'abstract': 'This book is the study of a 67-year-old Nantucket doctor and his anachronistic approach to medicine.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/06/03/books/review/island-practice-by-pam-belluck.html', 'snippet': 'This book is the study of a 67-year-old Nantucket doctor and his anachronistic approach to medicine.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The American health care system works — when it works — by shuttling patients around a network of specialists. The highest obstacle to access is usually a lack of money. But the 10,000 permanent residents of Nantucket must also cope with the elements: routine fog or storms can put a stop to all transportation between the island and the doctors on the mainland, 30 miles away, which Nantucketers refer to with some vagueness as “America.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '42', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/03/books/review/03PHELEAN/03PHELEAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 209, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/06/03/books/review/03PHELEAN/03PHELEAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 209}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/03/books/review/03PHELEAN/03PHELEAN-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 356, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/03/books/review/03PHELEAN/03PHELEAN-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 712, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/03/books/review/03PHELEAN/03PHELEAN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/06/03/books/review/03PHELEAN/03PHELEAN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/03/books/review/03PHELEAN/03PHELEAN-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Med Man', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Med Man', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Belluck, Pam', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Nantucket (Mass)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-01T14:27:00+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Benjamin Phelan', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Benjamin', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Phelan', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/c2edb899-8138-539d-96d0-be6dc4bebeb2', 'word_count': 659, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c2edb899-8138-539d-96d0-be6dc4bebeb2'}, {'abstract': 'New books by Guy Delisle, Jean-Pierre Filiu, Catel, Roger Langridge and Brandon Graham.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/06/03/books/review/jerusalem-by-guy-delisle-and-more.html', 'snippet': 'New books by Guy Delisle, Jean-Pierre Filiu, Catel, Roger Langridge and Brandon Graham.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Canadian artist Guy Delisle has made a career of moving to foreign places and drawing casual vignettes on cross-cultural confusion. Over the past few years, he’s documented his tenures in Pyongyang, Shenzhen and Burma (now Myanmar). Like the books that resulted from those trips, JERUSALEM: Chronicles From the Holy City (Drawn & Quarterly, $24.95) isn’t exactly reportage. It’s a series of brief strips, many only a page or two long, depicting his experiences caring for his kids, seeking opportunities to draw local landmarks without being interrupted by soldiers, and trying to comprehend how the residents of a heavily contested territory live day to day. Delisle, a former animator, has a knack for visual shorthand (his self-\xadportrait is a few jauntily canted lines with dots for eyes) and for drawing environments: religious shrines and settlements, but also grocery stores, playgrounds and checkpoints — lots of checkpoints. The cultural and physical barriers among the Jewish, Muslim and Christian communities in and around Jerusalem, and the compromises and work-arounds the city’s residents have been forced to devise, become the source of dark but gentle comedy: absurdity teetering on the edge of tragedy.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '36', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/05/31/books/review/tbr-Wolk-img/tbr-Wolk-img-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 725, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/05/31/books/review/tbr-Wolk-img/tbr-Wolk-img-superJumbo.jpg', 'he