'By Paul Brownfield', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Brownfield', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e101d704-1e42-5ff1-aeb7-83e54f5c6fcf', 'word_count': 1353, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e101d704-1e42-5ff1-aeb7-83e54f5c6fcf'}, {'abstract': 'In Marianne Dubuc’s “Animal Masquerade,” a cavalcade of birds and beasts prepare for a costume party.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/06/03/books/review/animal-masquerade-by-marianne-dubuc-and-more.html', 'snippet': 'In Marianne Dubuc’s “Animal Masquerade,” a cavalcade of birds and beasts prepare for a costume party.', 'lead_paragraph': ' It was a day for a parent to remember when the not-quite-2-year-old daughter of the Russian children’s poet Kornei Chukovsky toddled up to her father and, looking “mischievous and embarrassed at the same time, as if she were up to some intrigue,” blurted out, “Daddy, ’oggie — meow!” Chukovsky’s initial response was to correct the little girl, but he caught himself and joined in the game, having grasped that in telling her first joke, his daughter had arrived at a major milestone: the discovery that words were as much her playthings as the dolls and building blocks called toys. ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '24', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/05/23/books/review/0603-bks-MARCUS1/0603-bks-MARCUS1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 986, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/05/23/books/review/0603-bks-MARCUS1/0603-bks-MARCUS1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1972, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption':