and typically photogenic. In our short-\xadattention-span era, we’re both inspired and intrigued by the athletes’ intense discipline and singular focus. The dark side of the gymnasium holds its own fascination, of course. See: concerns about injuries to still-\xadgrowing girls; overbearing coaches; delayed puberty; the dangers of eating disorders; and the rest.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '27', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Gymnasts', 'kicker': "Children's Books", 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Gymnasts', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Moceanu, Dominique', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Gymnastics', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Thompson, Alicia', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Freitas, Donna', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-01T15:06:16+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Whitney Joiner', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Whitney', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Joiner', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/01fa3bbd-0f32-5f11-94a0-ccdf97f3c31d', 'word_count': 790, 'uri': 'nyt://article/01fa3bbd-0f32-5f11-94a0-ccdf97f3c31d'}, {'abstract': 'Jake and Lily, the twins at the center of Jerry Spinelli’s novel, take their unusual closeness for granted until the night of their sixth birthday.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/06/03/books/review/jake-and-lily-by-jerry-spinelli.html', 'snippet': 'Jake and Lily, the twins at the center of Jerry Spinelli’s novel, take their unusual closeness for granted until the night of their sixth birthday.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Being twins is a mixed blessing, or so it is for Jake and Lily, the title characters of Jerry Spinelli’s most recent novel for middle graders. But it’s still a blessing.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '26', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Twin Powers', 'kicker': "Children's Books", 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Twin Powers', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Spinelli, Jerry', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-01T15:06:21+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Richard Peck', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Richard', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Peck', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/b5eed3a9-f5e5-5f46-8ab8-6ec7a46b92cd', 'word_count': 888, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b5eed3a9-f5e5-5f46-8ab8-6ec7a46b92cd'}, {'abstract': 'Two new books tell the story of the historic upset at golf’s 1955 U.S. Open, and a third serves as a primer for course newcomers and veterans alike.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/06/03/books/review/the-longest-shot-the-upset-and-on-par.html', 'snippet': 'Two new books tell the story of the historic upset at golf’s 1955 U.S. Open, and a third serves as a primer for course newcomers and veterans alike.', 'lead_paragraph': 'On Saturday, June 18, 1955, at 9 p.m., NBC, in its second year of televising the United States Open golf championship, signed off from its scheduled one hour of live coverage at the Olympic Club in San Francisco by declaring Ben Hogan the winner of his record fifth Open. The legendary golfer Gene Sarazen, serving as color man for NBC and pressed for time, quickly congratulated Hogan as he came off the 18th green and persuaded him to hold up five fingers, which he did, somewhat reluctantly.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '31', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/03/books/review/03SHEFT/03SHEFT-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 983, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/03/books/review/03SHEFT/03SHEFT-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1965, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/03/books/review/03SHEFT/03SHEFT-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/06/03/books/review/03SHEFT/03SHEFT-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/03/books/review/03SHEFT/03SHEFT-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Upsets on, and Ground Rules for, the Links', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Golf', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Golf', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Pennington, Bill', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Sagebiel, Neil', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Barkow, Al', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-01T15:07:21+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Bill Scheft', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Bill', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Scheft', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/465f5658-6771-5e26-9fa7-83b67b591589', 'word_count': 1163, 'uri': 'nyt://article/465f5658-6771-5e26-9fa7-83b67b591589'}, {'abstract': 'The season’s travel books include a memoir about Alaska, an homage to Naples and a tour of the world’s most polluted places.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/06/03/books/review/alaskan-travels-naples-declared-and-more.html', 'snippet': 'The season’s travel books include a memoir about Alaska, an homage to Naples and a tour of the world’s most polluted places.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The fragility of the environment is a running concern in many of this season’s travel books. Writers wander from Alaska to China to the Amazon documenting scenes of natural grandeur — and mankind’s irresistible urge to destroy them. The dumping of industrial effluent into the Ganges River and the surrender of China’s rural beauty to urban sprawl are just two examples of these clashes between man and nature. Fortunately, evocative volumes about London and Naples remind us that human creations can also be sublime.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '16', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/03/books/review/03travel-img/03travel-img-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 974, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/03/books/review/03travel-img/03travel-img-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1948, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/03/books/review/03travel-img/03travel-img-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/06/03/books/review/03travel-img/03travel-img-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/03/books/review/03travel-img/03travel-img-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Travel', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Travel', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Travel and Vacations', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-01T15:08:42+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Joshua Hammer', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Joshua', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hammer', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/1655ccc1-eb76-52e4-a98f-a9438bd69318', 'word_count': 2554, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1655ccc1-eb76-52e4-a98f-a9438bd69318'}, {'abstract': 'Michelle Obama’s book about the White House garden, a look at Thomas Jefferson’s garden at Monticello and other summer reads for green thumbs.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/06/03/books/review/michelle-obamas-american-grown-and-more.html', 'snippet': 'Michelle Obama’s book about the White House garden, a look at Thomas Jefferson’s garden at Monticello and other summer reads for green thumbs.', 'lead_paragraph': 'No gardener worth her salt has time for lengthy books these days. It’s already “War and Peace” out there in the vegetable patch, what with the unhealthy trend of swift but pummeling rainfall and the climate’s hot flashes, not to mention the cutworm munching through the cabbage. Bounty and brevity will be our guides, I promise, but it may be hard: there’s so much coming up. I don’t think I’ve ever seen so many books devoted to the importance of tomatoes and onions.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/03/books/review/03garden-img/03garden-img-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 970, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/03/books/review/03garden-img/03garden-img-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1943, 'width': 1840, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/03/books/review/03garden-img/03garden-img-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/06/03/books/review/03garden-img/03garden-img-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/03/books/review/03garden-img/03garden-img-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Gardening', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Gardening', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Gardens and Gardening', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-01T15:09:24+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dominique Browning', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dominique', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Browning', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/1fd8e60c-36f0-5ff2-9a9c-7774664ab17f', 'word_count': 2191, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1fd8e60c-36f0-5ff2-9a9c-7774664ab17f'}, {'abstract': 'Recipes from the restaurant Mugaritz, the chef April Bloomfield, the American Academy in Rome and more.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/06/03/books/review/mugaritz-a-girl-and-her-pig-and-more.html', 'snippet': 'Recipes from the restaurant Mugaritz, the chef April Bloomfield, the American Academy in Rome and more.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The funny thing about cooking in summer is that you don’t really want to — nor do you really have to, unless you consider slicing tomatoes and flipping burgers a chore. And yet, as long as our friends keep migrating into our backyards, the summer cookbooks keep coming.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/03/books/review/03cooking-img/03cooking-img-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 961, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/03/books/review/03cooking-img/03cooking-img-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1922, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/03/books/review/03cooking-img/03cooking-img-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/06/03/books/review/03cooking-img/03cooking-img-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/03/books/review/03cooking-img/03cooking-img-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Cooking', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Cooking', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cooking and Cookbooks', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-01T15:09:55+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Christine Muhlke', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Christine', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Muhlke', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/014709d2-4eb6-5766-ae4e-40171deb6366', 'word_count': 2438, 'uri': 'nyt://article/014709d2-4eb6-5766-ae4e-40171deb6366'}, {'abstract': 'Some experts offered possible explanations for how the suspect could have strangled a boy and lived with the secret all these years; others wondered if he confessed to a crime he did not commit.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/06/02/nyregion/psychiatrists-puzzled-by-hernandezs-confession-in-etan-patz-case.html', 'snippet': 'Some experts offered possible explanations for how the suspect could have strangled a boy and lived with the secret all these years; others wondered if he confessed to a crime he did not commit.', 'lead_paragraph': 'His confession came more than three decades after the fact. He had no apparent motive or criminal record. And his mental struggles — he had diagnoses of schizophrenia and bipolar disorder — were reportedly being treated. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '15', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/02/nyregion/JP-PSYCH/JP-PSYCH-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/02/nyregion/JP-PSYCH/JP-PSYCH-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Psychiatrists Offer Theories About Suspect in Patz Case', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Psychiatrists Offer Theories About Suspect In Patz Case', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mental Health and Disorders', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Confessions', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Patz, Etan', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hernandez, Pedro (1961- )', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Psychiatry and Psychiatrists', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Murders and Attempted Murders', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-01T15:10:47+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Benedict Carey', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Benedict', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Carey', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/cd15f61b-3368-5dde-beb6-84a865e49852', 'word_count': 1249, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cd15f61b-3368-5dde-beb6-84a865e49852'}, {'abstract': 'Justin Halpern, whose new memoir, “I Suck at Girls,” is No. 6 on the e-book nonfiction list, feels as lucky as a plane crash survivor or Oprah’s long-lost sister.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/06/10/books/review/inside-the-list.html', 'snippet': 'Justin Halpern, whose new memoir, “I Suck at Girls,” is No. 6 on the e-book nonfiction list, feels as lucky as a plane crash survivor or Oprah’s long-lost sister.', 'lead_paragraph': 'NOT LUCKY: Last year, Justin Halpern spent 40 weeks on the hardcover nonfiction list with his first book, “_ My Dad Says,” a memoir that grew out of Halpern’s Twitter feed after his girlfriend dumped him and he moved back in with his mother and his feisty 73-year-old father. (The book also inspired a short-lived TV series starring William Shatner.) Looking back on that success in a new memoir, “I Suck at Girls” — it’s at No. 6 on the e-book nonfiction list — Halpern is mostly baffled and grateful. “What began as an attempt to take my mind off my heartache, and make a couple friends laugh, exploded,” he writes. “Within two months I had more than half a million followers, a book deal with a major publisher and a TV deal, which is all the more ridiculous when you take into account that it was solely because I was just writing down things my dad said. They weren’t even my words. To say I was ‘lucky’ would be inaccurate. Finding your wallet after you’ve left it in a crowded bar is lucky. Getting a book deal and a TV show based on less than 500 total words is a level of luck reserved for people who survive plane crashes or find out they’re Oprah’s long-lost sister.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '30', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/10/books/review/10list-img/10list-img-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 289, 'width': 282, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'capti