, 'news_desk': 'RealEstate', 'section_name': 'Real Estate', 'byline': {'original': 'By Christopher Gray', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Christopher', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gray', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/26358468-d234-501c-ab04-ac0c4309d48f', 'word_count': 805, 'uri': 'nyt://article/26358468-d234-501c-ab04-ac0c4309d48f'}, {'abstract': 'A teacher who, at times, lives in two very different realities — Southern California and Afghanistan — reflects on the surprising similarities between the two.', 'web_url': 'https://atwar.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/06/01/seeing-a-change-in-kabul-from-a-beach-in-california/', 'snippet': 'A teacher who, at times, lives in two very different realities — Southern California and Afghanistan — reflects on the surprising similarities between the two.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Living on the beach in Southern California is a bit surreal. I wake to the crashing of waves, the chirping of birds. Each day I open my eyes, I cannot quite believe that I get to live in a place where the beauty seems unchangeable. But just the other day as I was running by a usually calm point, I noticed that the waves were breaking in a new place; with the turning of the seasons, a new sandbar had formed, providing fresh fun for the crop of surfers playing in the water. The hollering, happy people made the water prettier than I remembered it. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Seeing a Change in Kabul From a Beach in California', 'kicker': 'At War', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-06-01T17:08:15+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'World', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jaala A. Thibault', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jaala', 'middlename': 'A.', 'lastname': 'Thibault', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/56f2351f-f818-5067-9b83-c2dfec145680', 'word_count': 973, 'uri': 'nyt://article/56f2351f-f818-5067-9b83-c2dfec145680'}, {'abstract': 'For the last seven months, the White House has blasted out a statement with the verb “recover” or “heal.” But the response to the May jobs report? No more healing, no more recovering.', 'web_url': 'https://thecaucus.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/06/01/the-white-house-strikes-recover-from-response-to-jobs-report/', 'snippet': 'For the last seven months, the White House has blasted out a statement with the verb “recover” or “heal.” But the response to the May jobs report? No more healing, no more recovering.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The anemic jobs report released on Friday was one thing. But here’s another worrisome economic indicator: Even the White House written response this month dropped the words “recover” or “heal” in describing the economy.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'White House Strikes ‘Recover’ From Response to Jobs Report', 'kicker': 'The Caucus', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Labor and Jobs', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Recession and Depression', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Economy', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Krueger, Alan B', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'White House Council of Economic Advisers', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-01T17:09:33+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Peter Baker', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Peter', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Baker', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6df2659d-262d-54d6-93db-5b2b74d22330', 'word_count': 402, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6df2659d-262d-54d6-93db-5b2b74d22330'}, {'abstract': 'Even after losing twice to Florida this season, Northwestern won the women’s lacrosse championship for the seventh time in eight years.', 'web_url': 'https://thequad.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/06/01/theres-no-doubt-its-a-dynasty/', 'snippet': 'Even after losing twice to Florida this season, Northwestern won the women’s lacrosse championship for the seventh time in eight years.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Often the right answer is the most obvious one. After a lacrosse season in which everyone tried to frantically figure out which team would finally dethrone Northwestern, the Wildcats did what they have done in seven of the past eight years – win a national championship.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'There’s No Doubt It’s a Dynasty', 'kicker': 'The Quad', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'College Athletics', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Lacrosse', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'NCAA Lacrosse Championships', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Amonte Hiller, Kelly', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Northwestern University', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Syracuse University', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-01T17:13:41+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Pro Basketball', 'byline': {'original': 'By Rohan Nadkarni', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Rohan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Nadkarni', 'qualif