ely admit the emotional affair was a breach on a number of levels, am I responsible for reimbursing her? NAME WITHHELD', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/03/magazine/03ethicist_male/mag-03Ethicist-t_CA0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 414, 'width': 414, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/03/magazine/03ethicist_male/mag-03Ethicist-t_CA0-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 414, 'width': 414, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/03/magazine/03ethicist_male/03ethicist_male-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/06/03/magazine/03ethicist_male/03ethicist_male-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/03/magazine/03ethicist_male/03ethicist_male-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Costly Conversations', 'kicker': 'The Ethicist', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Costly Conversations', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Crossword Puzzles', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Gambling', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Ethics (Personal)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'AIRLINES AND AIRPLANES', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Casinos', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Ethicist, The (Times Column)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ADULTERY', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-01T18:25:58+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Philip Levine', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Philip', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Levine', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Question', '_id': 'nyt://article/deecd52e-d007-5209-a1b9-5930f964b287', 'word_count': 906, 'uri': 'nyt://article/deecd52e-d007-5209-a1b9-5930f964b287'}, {'abstract': 'The four best personal innovations from our readers, as selected by our panel of experts.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/06/03/magazine/the-innovation-whiteboard-winners.html', 'snippet': 'The four best personal innovations from our readers, as selected by our panel of experts.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A month ago, we invited our readers to share an innovation — whether a theoretical invention, an ingenious reimagining of an everyday item or simply a nifty idea — by submitting it online. The suggestions we received are a little window into the vexations of daily life: from ‘‘ChapStick tandems’’ (a double-ended lip balm that might appeal to germophobes) to ‘‘Café au Frosted Flakes’’ (created by pouring the leftover milk from your cereal into your morning coffee). We invited a panel of experts to each select a favorite; here are the four they chose. (You can also judge for yourself; check out the rest of the submissions and click “recommend” for your favorites.)', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '74', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Innovation Whiteboard Winners', 'kicker': 'The Innovation Issue', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Innovation Whiteboard', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kaufman, Ben', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'ANTONELLI, PAOLA', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Dyson, James', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Stewart, Martha', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-01T18:26:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/1fa3f9db-5a6b-5dff-b048-89e5a99c3666', 'word_count': 482, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1fa3f9db-5a6b-5dff-b048-89e5a99c3666'}, {'abstract': 'A new ‘Dallas’ TV reboot misses all the ’80s-era Texan swagger of the original. But then, the last few decades haven’t been so kind to the ’80s, Texas or swagger.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/06/03/magazine/when-dallas-was-the-capital-of-america.html', 'snippet': 'A new ‘Dallas’ TV reboot misses all the ’80s-era Texan swagger of the original. But then, the last few decades haven’t been so kind to the ’80s, Texas or swagger.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In the 1980s, there were two competing visions of wealth on TV: “Dallas” and “Dynasty.” People tend to lump the two nighttime soaps together, but aside from the characters’ habit of resolving major conflicts in the swimming pool, the two shows couldn’t be more different. “Dynasty” constructed a fantasy of domestic royalty and jet-setting escapism, offering a portrait of the rich as diamond-strewn, evening-gown-clad ghosts drifting from their private jets to their enormous mansions as servants rushed about carrying silver platters. “Dallas,” by contrast, stayed firmly rooted in middle-class American values, presenting a picture that was more Big Boy breakfast buffet than “Breakfast at Tiffany’s.” With its cowboy hats and flocked wallpaper and Hatfields-versus-McCoys-style feuds, “Dallas” was as provincial and preglobal-economy as it gets. Even Southfork’s décor, with its dark paneling, garish paintings and low ceilings, mirrored the claustrophobia of the country club: cramped, wall-to-wall carpeted spaces packed with small-minded people lugging their gigantic egos around behind them like overstuffed golf bags.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '68', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/03/magazine/03riff-dallas2/03riff-dallas2-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 646, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/03/magazine/03riff-dallas2/03riff-dallas2-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1292, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/03/magazine/03riff-dallas2/03riff-dallas2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/06/03/magazine/03riff-dallas2/03riff-dallas2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/03/magazine/03riff-dallas2/03riff-dallas2-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'When ‘Dallas’ Was the Capital of America', 'kicker': 'Riff', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'When ‘Dallas’ Was the Capital of America', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Dallas (Tex)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Nineteen Hundred Eighties', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Oil (Petroleum) and Gasoline', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-01T18:26:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Heather Havrilesky', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Heather', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Havrilesky', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2abc8aea-fc0b-5f4c-b537-f46badd974bd', 'word_count': 1712, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2abc8aea-fc0b-5f4c-b537-f46badd974bd'}, {'abstract': 'Readers respond.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/06/03/magazine/the-5-20-12-issue.html', 'snippet': 'Readers respond.', 'lead_paragraph': 'PACIFISTS IN THE E.R.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The 5.20.12 Issue', 'kicker': 'Reply All | Letters', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The 5.20.12 Issue', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-06-01T18:26:03+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Letter', '_id': 'nyt://article/5b54c92f-3368-582a-9a70-0abdf4023bc6', 'word_count': 778, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5b54c92f-3368-582a-9a70-0abdf4023bc6'}, {'abstract': 'The mayor of London likes Keith Richards and exhilarating bus rides.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/06/03/magazine/boris-johnson-tory-with-an-attitude.html', 'snippet': 'The mayor of London likes Keith Richards and exhilarating bus rides.', 'lead_paragraph': 'While I was reading your book “Johnson’s Life of London,” in which you take readers on a tour of the city while discussing some of history’s most famous Londoners, I thought to myself, Being mayor of London can’t be that taxing if you could find time to write such a decent book.  The job of mayor of London is unbelievably taxing, particularly in the run-up to the Olympics. It just happens I write fast and always have done. Some people play the piano, some do Sudoku, some watch television, some people go out to dinner parties. I write books.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '16', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/03/magazine/03talk/mag-03Talk-t_CA0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 475, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/03/magazine/03talk/mag-03Talk-t_CA0-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1272, 'width': 590, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/03/magazine/03talk/03talk-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/06/03/magazine/03talk/03talk-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/03/magazine/03talk/03talk-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 151, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Boris Johnson, Tory With an Attitude', 'kicker': 'Talk', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Tory With An Attitude', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Olympic Games (2012)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mayors', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'London (England)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Johnson, Boris', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], '