, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-01T21:17:13+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Edward Wong', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Edward', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Wong', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c1662fab-2750-56f2-b807-6079db522a27', 'word_count': 1045, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c1662fab-2750-56f2-b807-6079db522a27'}, {'abstract': 'Carroll Shelby, the builder of the Cobra sports car who died in May, was remembered by friends and fans at an event that drew 1,200 people to the museum.', 'web_url': 'https://wheels.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/06/01/at-petersen-museum-a-rowdy-farewell-to-dr-go-fast/', 'snippet': 'Carroll Shelby, the builder of the Cobra sports car who died in May, was remembered by friends and fans at an event that drew 1,200 people to the museum.', 'lead_paragraph': 'LOS ANGELES — The retired racecar driver Dan Gurney perhaps best summarized the life of Carroll Shelby at the Petersen Automotive Museum on Wednesday: “He was a man of contradictions, kind on one hand and very cunning on another. Charming and mean, romantic and rough. A friend to many, and a foe to some. He loved cars, women and business.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'At Petersen Museum, a Rowdy Farewell to Dr. Go-Fast', 'kicker': 'Wheels', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Automobiles', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Collectors and Collections', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Shelby, Carroll', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Ford Motor Company', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'California', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-01T21:25:20+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Automobiles', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dave Kinney', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dave', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kinney', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5f0ed744-07b5-5097-9cd3-4c112f15d8ef', 'word_count': 540, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5f0ed744-07b5-5097-9cd3-4c112f15d8ef'}, {'abstract': 'The German rock band Tangerine Dream is synonymous with a period of moviemaking. Some classics of the period are being shown at BAMcinématek.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/06/03/movies/tangerine-dreams-music-in-bamcinematek-series.html', 'snippet': 'The German rock band Tangerine Dream is synonymous with a period of moviemaking. Some classics of the period are being shown at BAMcinématek.', 'lead_paragraph': 'SYNTHESIZERS were the sonic wallpaper of the 1980s, for film soundtracks as much as in popular music. If you’re hearing the tinny bounce of keyboards and drum machine, the Hollywood movie you’re watching is probably from the Reagan-Bush years. The prevalence of the electronic sound often made it seem banal, but it had brawnier, moodier ancestors. One pioneer was the German rock band Tangerine Dream, which formed in 1967 and broke through in the 1970s with a sound marked by expansive synthesized washes and percussive chords.', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '20', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/03/arts/03TANGERINE2_SPAN/03TANGERINE2_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 360, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/06/03/arts/03TANGERINE2_SPAN/03TANGERINE2_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 360}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jum