y Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-06-01T22:25:13+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Patrick McGeehan', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Patrick', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'McGeehan', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ae995a21-dd53-522a-b659-097895412438', 'word_count': 283, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ae995a21-dd53-522a-b659-097895412438'}, {'abstract': 'In a performance Wednesday night, New York City Ballet presented two works from the repertory as well as a piece that had its premiere earlier this year.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/06/02/arts/dance/new-york-city-ballet-revisits-three-works.html', 'snippet': 'In a performance Wednesday night, New York City Ballet presented two works from the repertory as well as a piece that had its premiere earlier this year.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Is any art more elusive than choreography? What are the secret laws that make a structurally disparate work cohere as a stage world and a seemingly unified one fall apart? We ask a ballet to succeed in impossibly many ways: as body language and sociology; in its organization of time and space; in terms of movement-by-movement, narrativelike sense; and as an artist’s view of human energy. No wonder so few choreographers have won universal admiration.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/02/arts/MOVES/MOVES-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 707, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/02/arts/MOVES/MOVES-superJu