orests violated the Endangered Species Act. The 7-to-4 decision, by the United States Court of Appeals for the Ninth Circuit, came in a lawsuit filed in 2004 by the Karuk Tribe of Northern California. The tribe had argued that some types of mining on the Klamath River, including suction dredging and motorized sluicing, posed risks to coho salmon, a federally protected species. Coho are important to the tribe for cultural, religious and subsistence reasons. The Forest Service had argued that the Mining Act of 1872 prevailed over the Endangered Species Act. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'California: Tribe Wins Ruling Against Gold Mining', 'kicker': 'National Briefing | West', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'California: Tribe Wins Ruling Against Gold Mining', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Gold', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Salmon', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Endangered and Extinct Species', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mines and Mining', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Forest Service', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Decisions and Verdicts', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'California', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Klamath River', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-01T23:49:16+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By William Yardley', 'person': [{'firstname': 'William', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Yardley', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/860d3cb2-68bf-55bd-8b48-cb6439810ea8', 'word_count': 112, 'uri': 'nyt://article/860d3cb2-68bf-55bd-8b48-cb6439810ea8'}, {'abstract': 'A coalition of advocacy groups announced Friday that it had raised enough money to make up for financing that the Roman Catholic Church is cutting off to Compañeros, which provides immigrant services.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/06/02/us/colorado-immigrant-groups-financing-is-replaced.html', 'snippet': 'A coalition of advocacy groups announced Friday that it had raised enough money to make up for financing that the Roman Catholic Church is cutting off to Compañeros, which provides immigrant services.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A coalition of advocacy groups announced Friday that it had raised enough money to make up for financing that the Roman Catholic Church is cutting off to Compañeros, which provides services for immigrants in southwestern Colorado. The Catholic Campaign for Human Development ended about $30,000 in annual financing to Compañeros because it had joined a coalition that supported same-sex marriage. The Catholic Campaign, an arm of the United States Conference of Catholic Bishops that is devoted to reducing poverty, has stopped financing groups that run astray of Catholic positions. Nicole Mosher, executive director of Compañeros, said a fund-raising campaign had netted more than $60,000. The Gill Foundation, which supports gay rights legislation, matched donations raised by several immigrant, religious and gay advocacy groups. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Colorado: Immigrant Group’s Financing Is Replaced', 'kicker': 'National Briefing | Rockies', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Colorado: Immigrant Group’s Financing Is Replaced', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Roman Catholic Church', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Catholic Campaign for Human Development', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizatio