f_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3d8ea9f0-86db-5326-bc2c-90856302d349', 'word_count': 507, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3d8ea9f0-86db-5326-bc2c-90856302d349'}, {'abstract': 'On Sundays, Ramon A. Dominguez, a jockey who won the 2011 Breeders’ Cup, often rides several races. But though he buys doughnuts for the grooms and workers in the barns when he wins, he won’t indulge.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/06/03/nyregion/on-sundays-ramon-a-dominguez-races-to-win-but-avoids-doughnuts.html', 'snippet': 'On Sundays, Ramon A. Dominguez, a jockey who won the 2011 Breeders’ Cup, often rides several races. But though he buys doughnuts for the grooms and workers in the barns when he wins, he won’t indulge.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Ramon A. Dominguez is in an elite class of horse whisperers: his gift for guiding thoroughbreds across the finish line brought him the Eclipse Award for Outstanding Jockey in 2010 and again in 2011, when his mounts earned almost $20 million, tops in the nation. He won the 2011 Breeders’ Cup Juvenile aboard Hansen, the Pegasus-like stallion that was also his 2012 Kentucky Derby mount; but his horse for the June 9 Belmont Stakes was withdrawn. Tall for his trade at 5-foot-6, Mr. Dominguez, 35, who was born in Venezuela, lives in Floral Park, N.Y., with his wife, Sharon; their sons, Alex, 7, and Matthew, 5; and their dog, Lucy. He also owns a 14-acre horse farm near Elkton, Md. Sunday is a race day, but afterward, he indulges in a normal Sunday dinner. Monday and Tuesday are off days, with no weigh-ins.', 'print_section': 'MB', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/03/nyregion/03ROUTINE-3_SPAN/03ROUTINE-3_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 360, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/06/03/nyregion/03ROUTINE-3_SPAN/03ROUTINE-3_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlar