obal economy. Meanwhile, Paul Krugman laments, Britain is obsessed with count...', 'lead_paragraph': 'LONDON — We said it last week. And now things have gotten worse. The latest data from the United States and Europe to India and China show dangerous signs of a slowing global economy. Meanwhile, Paul Krugman laments, Britain is obsessed with counterproductive austerity.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Things Fall Apart II', 'kicker': 'IHT Rendezvous', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-06-02T03:45:18+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'World', 'byline': {'original': 'By The International Herald Tribune', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The International Herald Tribune'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/fe12e7b6-c4f6-5c67-97d0-fa8657696877', 'word_count': 44, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fe12e7b6-c4f6-5c67-97d0-fa8657696877'}, {'abstract': 'The Celtics put together their best performance of the series in dominating the middle two quarters, building a 24-point lead and then hanging on for dear life through Miami’s late rally.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/06/02/sports/basketball/celtics-jump-out-to-big-early-lead-and-beat-heat.html', 'snippet': 'The Celtics put together their best performance of the series in dominating the middle two quarters, building a 24-point lead and then hanging on for dear life through Miami’s late rally.', 'lead_paragraph': 'BOSTON — The foul was hard and sudden, and it left Kevin Garnett flat on his back, his lanky frame spread across a broad expanse of green-toned hardwood. He flipped over, put his knuckles to the floor and pressed hard for eight push-ups, the din at the TD Garden growing louder with each one.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': Non