37+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Al Baker', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Al', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Baker', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/eecdd375-6432-5458-a48c-b00c4e06c863', 'word_count': 624, 'uri': 'nyt://article/eecdd375-6432-5458-a48c-b00c4e06c863'}, {'abstract': 'Goats, ponies, monkeys, dogs, sheep, rats, roosters, rabbits and cats have all been known to be companions of thoroughbreds throughout the nation. But at Churchill Downs, it is the cats who serve as ambassadors and companions for the humans who sometimes need a friend.', 'web_url': 'https://therail.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/06/02/after-the-crowds-leave-the-track-who-keeps-you-company/', 'snippet': 'Goats, ponies, monkeys, dogs, sheep, rats, roosters, rabbits and cats have all been known to be companions of thoroughbreds throughout the nation. But at Churchill Downs, it is the cats who serve as ambassadors and companions for the humans who so...', 'lead_paragraph': 'When I wrote my first short story, I used my cat Moses as a muse. I created a world where he was a wise old tabby barn cat wearing a leather collar with a brass nameplate who interacted with everyone at the track and bettered their lives. Imagine my surprise when I walked past Security Gate 5 at Churchill Downs the week before this year’s Kentucky Derby and was greeted by Bill, an old tabby cat wearing the same exact collar. I stopped to return his affection and the security guards proudly told me that he “ran Churchill Downs, and in his day, probably fathered most of the kittens around.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'After the Crowds Leave the Track, Who Keeps You Company?', 'kicker': 'The Rail', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None},