neties', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-02T21:15:51+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Baseball', 'byline': {'original': 'By Stuart Miller', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Stuart', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Miller', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3f1bb53c-1398-5cdc-8747-77d8c70a1a15', 'word_count': 1136, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3f1bb53c-1398-5cdc-8747-77d8c70a1a15'}, {'abstract': 'All the state’s indie filmmakers aren’t in Austin. A thriving film scene has emerged in Dallas, and this month the inaugural Oak Cliff Film Festival will make it official.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/06/03/us/dallas-has-its-own-indie-film-scene-and-now-a-festival.html', 'snippet': 'All the state’s indie filmmakers aren’t in Austin. A thriving film scene has emerged in Dallas, and this month the inaugural Oak Cliff Film Festival will make it official.', 'lead_paragraph': 'For a generation of young Texans eager to write and direct their own movies, the message has long been the same: head to Austin, that promised land of “Slacker” and South by Southwest and the ever-expanding Alamo Drafthouse franchise. With its high-profile residents — among them Richard Linklater, Robert Rodriguez and Terrence Malick — its myriad film festivals and the University of Texas film program, it is a city known for its worshipful devotion to a certain brand of independent-spirited cinema. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '25B', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/03/us/03ttculture2/03ttculture2-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type':