resident Bashar al-Assad denied Sunday that the Syrian government played a role in the massacre in the village of Houla, using his first speech in five months to reiterate his line that foreigners were fomenting the violence in Syria. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/04/world/middleeast/04syria1/04syria1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 790, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/04/world/middleeast/04syria1/04syria1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1579, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/04/world/middleeast/04syria1/04syria1-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/06/04/world/middleeast/04syria1/04syria1-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/04/world/middleeast/04syria1/04syria1-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Assad Condemns Houla Massacre, Blaming Terrorists', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Assad Denies Government Role in Massacre, Blaming Terrorism', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Civilian Casualties', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Houla (Syria)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Syria', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Assad, Bashar al-', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Middle East and North Africa Unrest (2010- )', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-03T11:28:16+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Middle East', 'byline': {'original': 'By Neil MacFarquhar', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Neil', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'MacFarquhar', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/df511932-dbe1-55ff-85f1-635d47beb9c5', 'word_count': 931, 'uri': 'nyt://article/df511932-dbe1-55ff-85f1-635d47beb9c5'}, {'abstract': 'The chamber music series is not the main draw at the Spoleto Festival USA, but it continues to thrive under the caring if quirky leadership of Geoff Nuttall.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/06/03/at-the-spoleto-festival-usa-chamber-music-continues-to-fly-under-the-radar/', 'snippet': 'The chamber music series is not the main draw at the Spoleto Festival USA, but it continues to thrive under the caring if quirky leadership of Geoff Nuttall.', 'lead_paragraph': 'CHARLESTON, S.C. — The big classical music draws at the Spoleto Festival USA are usually the operas: this year, Philip Glass’s “Kepler” and Guo Wenjing’s “Feng Yi Ting” (which will make its way to the Lincoln Center Festival next month). But if comparatively few festivalgoers travel to Charleston specifically for the chamber music concerts, they have been a popular and consistent part of this 35-year-old festival’s personality from the start.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'At the Spoleto Festival USA, Chamber Music Continues to Fly Under the Radar', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Classical Music', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'n