ther engaging programs with a thematic hook. But as she notes on the chorus’s Web site, a “theme is only good if every piece is a winner, not included just because it serves a theme.” ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Lifting Off to Copland and Stucky', 'kicker': 'Music Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Lifting Off To Copland And Stucky', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Classical Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New Amsterdam Singers', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-03T22:15:09+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Anthony Tommasini', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Anthony', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Tommasini', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/f22b7c01-3aed-52eb-97e5-3b23295cf9f4', 'word_count': 409, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f22b7c01-3aed-52eb-97e5-3b23295cf9f4'}, {'abstract': 'The composer Pauline Oliveros was celebrated at the Darmstadt Institute new-music festival.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/06/04/arts/music/pauline-oliveros-tribute-at-darmstadt-essential-repertoire.html', 'snippet': 'The composer Pauline Oliveros was celebrated at the Darmstadt Institute new-music festival.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Darmstadt Institute, an annual new-music festival founded in 2008 by the New York composers Nick Hallett and Zach Layton, has become one of the city’s most reliable sources for concerts as entertaining as they are edifying. Equally important, the series is a crowbar meant to pry open a rigid canon and admit outliers whose work, however obscure, has proved significant and durable.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/04/arts/subaltpauline1/subaltpauline1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 362, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/06/04/arts/subaltpauline1/subaltpauline1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 362}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/04/arts/subaltpauline1/subaltpauline1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 773, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/04/arts/subaltpauline1/subaltpauline1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1547, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/04/arts/subaltpauline1/subaltpauline1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/06/04/arts/subaltpauline1/subaltpauline1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/04/arts/subaltpauline1/subaltpauline1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Tribute to Composer Revered for Her Iconoclastic Ways', 'kicker': 'Music Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Tribute to Composer Revered for Her Iconoclastic Ways', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Oliveros, Pauline', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Classical Music', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-03T22:18:51+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Steve Smith', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Steve', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Smith', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/afde3509-d14f-5dc0-b3b2-b7ed48efde57', 'word_count': 496, 'uri': 'nyt://article/afde3509-d14f-5dc0-b3b2-b7ed48efde57'}, {'abstract': '“Music From the Big House” is a documentary about a concert by inmates at the Louisiana State Penitentiary.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/06/04/movies/music-from-the-big-house-by-bruce-mcdonald.html', 'snippet': '“Music From the Big House” is a documentary about a concert by inmates at the Louisiana State Penitentiary.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Filmed at the Louisiana State Penitentiary, “Music From the Big House,” Bruce McDonald’s loosely built documentary, yields the cognitive dissonance of being entertained by performers convicted of rape, murder and other offenses. The Canadian blues singer Rita Chiarelli acts as our ambassador to a realm of life sentences and strained redemption as she and the inmates put on a concert within this notorious prison known as Angola, formerly the site of a slave plantation. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/04/arts/music/music-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 853, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/04/arts/music/music-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1527, 'width': 1833, 'subType