w city of seven million plus.” Seven decades later, it still is. The same adjectives apply, but the city is even bigger: The population has grown to a record 8.3 million. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '20', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/04/nyregion/04census_span/04census_span-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 252, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/06/04/nyregion/04census_span/04census_span-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 252}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/04/nyregion/04census_span/04census_span-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 252, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/04/nyregion/04census_span/04census_span-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 252, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/04/nyregion/04census_span/04census_span-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/06/04/nyregion/04census_span/04census_span-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/04/nyregion/04census_span/04census_span-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 63, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'From Morrisania to Williamsburg, How the City Has Changed in 70 Years', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'From Morrisania to Williamsburg, How City Has Changed in 70 Years', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Center for Urban Research', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Census', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Population', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Times', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-04T04:17:49+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sam Roberts', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sam', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Roberts', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0ef5ca37-fb85-5255-aa1c-599bbd1bb501', 'word_count': 268, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0ef5ca37-fb85-5255-aa1c-599bbd1bb501'}, {'abstract': 'If love of mangoes is nearly universal in India, so is disagreement over which variety is best.', 'web_url': 'https://india.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/06/04/as-mango-season-peaks-mango-jingoism-runs-rampant/', 'snippet': 'If love of mangoes is nearly universal in India, so is disagreement over which variety is best.', 'lead_paragraph': '“Mangoes are objects of envy, love and rivalry as well as a new status symbol for India’s new rich,” Jim Yardley writes in The New York Times. “Mangoes have even been tools of diplomacy. The allure is foremost about the taste but also about anticipation and uncertainty: Mango season in the region lasts only about 100 days, traditionally from late March through June; is vulnerable to weather; and usually brings some sort of mango crisis, real or imagined.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'As Mango Season Peaks, Mango Jingoism Runs Rampant', 'kicker': 'India Ink', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mangoes', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'India', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Mumbai (India)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New Delhi (India)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-04T04:28:54+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/31e8f679-257c-5a01-87c8-62db928ad65e', 'word_count': 317, 'uri': 'nyt://article/31e8f679-257c-5a01-87c8-62db928ad65e'}, {'abstract': 'The visit of Secretary of Defense Leon Panetta to the strategic port of Cam Ranh Bay has struck security analysts as a bold step for the Vietnamese, a move that is not likely to please Beijing. As one analyst put it: “Hanoi knows that China is watching them like a hawk.”', 'web_url': 'https://rendezvous.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/06/04/lips-teeth-and-the-u-s-navy/', 'snippet': 'The visit of Secretary of Defense Leon Panetta to the strategic port of Cam Ranh Bay has struck security analysts as a bold step for the Vietnamese, a move that is not likely to please Beijing. As one analyst put it: “Hanoi knows that China is wat...', 'lead_paragraph': 'HONG KONG — When Chinese and Vietnamese officials raise their glasses at official banquets, they still sometimes toast each other with the old Communist-brotherhood slogan that their countries are “as close as lips and teeth.” ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Lips, Teeth and the U.S. Navy', 'kicker': 'IHT Rendezvous', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Defense and Military Forces', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'U