6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Bihar (India)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Ganges River', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'India', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-04T10:45:57+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dan Morrison', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Morrison', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/15af204b-90bc-58af-8acd-e3010ed46b5b', 'word_count': 1245, 'uri': 'nyt://article/15af204b-90bc-58af-8acd-e3010ed46b5b'}, {'abstract': 'The mixed verdict against Egypt’s former president, Hosni Mubarak, left many Egyptians fearing that the deep state is alive and well.', 'web_url': 'https://latitude.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/06/04/the-loneliness-of-the-long-distance-revolutionary/', 'snippet': 'The mixed verdict against Egypt’s former president, Hosni Mubarak, left many Egyptians fearing that the deep state is alive and well.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Saturday was a day of mixed emotions for many Egyptians. Jubilation met the news that Egypt’s former president, Hosni Mubarak, along with his interior minister, was sentenced to life in prison for being an accomplice to the killings of unarmed protesters last year. But tens of thousands of protesters took to the streets over the acquittal of senior police officers and the dismissal of corruption charges against Mubarak and his sons.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Loneliness of the Long-Distance Revolutionary', 'kicker': 'Latitude', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Middle East and North Africa Unrest (2010- )', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sentences (Criminal)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Mubarak, Hosni', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Egypt', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-04T10:54:14+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Daniel Politi', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Daniel', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Politi', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/551657c3-36cd-5679-b2ba-9dd92cd6aabb', 'word_count': 357, 'uri': 'nyt://article/551657c3-36cd-5679-b2ba-9dd92cd6aabb'}, {'abstract': 'If you are a boss, the day will arrive when you will have to fire someone. One subject that is not often addressed is the actual mechanics of the firing.', 'web_url': 'https://boss.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/06/04/how-i-fire-people/', 'snippet': 'If you are a boss, the day will arrive when you will have to fire someone. One subject that is not often addressed is the actual mechanics of the firing.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Jay Goltz, like any normal person, hates firing people. The commenters on his post about firing brought up a wide range of subjects, but one issue that wasn’t addressed is the actual mechanics of firing. I don’t have wide experience with this, but over the last 26 years, I have had to fire five workers for breaking the rules, and I also laid off 11 people one day in 2008. If you are a boss, the day will arrive when you have to fire someone.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'How I Fire People', 'kicker': 'You&#039;re the Boss', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Absenteeism and Truancy', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hiring and Promotion', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Labor and Jobs', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Layoffs and Job Reductions', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Productivity', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Small Business', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-04T11:00:16+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Entrepreneurship', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Downs', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Downs', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/39f1f0da-7caf-5153-9f4e-b2444dd41e00', 'word_count': 1999, 'uri': 'nyt://article/39f1f0da-7caf-5153-9f4e-b2444dd41e00'}, {'abstract': 'Abu Yahya al-Libi, the Al Qaeda commander who escaped American custody in 2005, was either killed or seriously wounded after a drone strike in Pakistan’s tribal belt, according to tribal sources.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/06/05/world/asia/drone-strike-kills-14-in-pakistan.html', 'snippet': 'Abu Yahya al-Libi, the Al Qaeda commander who escaped American custody in 2005, was either killed or seriously wounded after a drone strike in Pakistan’s tribal belt, according to tribal sources.', 'lead_paragraph': 'ISLAMABAD, Pakistan — The fate of one of the United States’ most dedicated enemies was the subject of mounting speculation on Monday after a drone strike in Pakistan’s tribal belt was said to have targeted Abu Yahya al-Libi, a Qaeda commander who escaped American custody in 2005 and became the group’s deputy leader after Osama bin Laden’s death last year.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/05/world/PAKISTAN/PAKISTAN-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'widt