article/bd21a931-3889-58ab-b101-f7cbc79a4050'}, {'abstract': 'Cone-shaped cells on most petals act like a surface of Velcro that pollinating bees can cling to, especially when the wind blows strongly, a study finds.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/06/05/science/flowers-petals-are-a-sticky-carpet-for-bees.html', 'snippet': 'Cone-shaped cells on most petals act like a surface of Velcro that pollinating bees can cling to, especially when the wind blows strongly, a study finds.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Scientists have long known that the petals of most flowering plants have cone-shaped cells that are not found anywhere else on the plants. ', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/05/science/05OBOX3/05OBOX3-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 814, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/05/science/05OBOX3/05OBOX3-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1628, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/05/science/05OBOX3/05OBOX3-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/06/05/science/05OBOX3/05OBOX3-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/05/science/05OBOX3/05OBOX3-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Flowers’ Cells Lay Out a Sticky Carpet for Bees', 'kicker': 'Observatory', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Flowers’ Cells Lay Out A Sticky Carpet for Bees', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Flowers and Plants', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Bees', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-04T20:10:11+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Science', 'section_name': 'Science', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sindya N. Bhanoo', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sindya', 'middlename': 'N.', 'lastname': 'Bhanoo', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/876de4dd-2f55-53bc-8e3b-60b7eb02e141', 'word_count': 229, 'uri': 'nyt://article/876de4dd-2f55-53bc-8e3b-60b7eb02e141'}, {'abstract': 'Some patients with epilepsy benefit from treatment with anti-inflammatory drugs, suggesting that brain inflammation plays a larger role in the disease than has been suspected.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/06/05/health/research/new-epilepsy-tactic-fight-inflammation.html', 'snippet': 'Some patients with epilepsy benefit from treatment with anti-inflammatory drugs, suggesting that brain inflammation plays a larger role in the disease than has been suspected.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In November 2008, when he was just 6, William Moller had his first epileptic seizure, during a reading class at school. For about 20 seconds, he simply froze in place, as if someone had pressed a pause button. He could not respond to his teacher.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/05/science/05EPIL/05EPIL-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 390, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/06/05/science/05EPIL/05EPIL-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 390}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None