, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'French Open (Tennis)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Tennis', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'del Potro, Juan Martin', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Federer, Roger', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-05T17:39:42+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Tennis', 'byline': {'original': 'By John Martin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'John', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Martin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/01df7d6e-f7d8-55a4-a369-937224abd252', 'word_count': 156, 'uri': 'nyt://article/01df7d6e-f7d8-55a4-a369-937224abd252'}, {'abstract': 'Undaunted by the withering bookstore industry, a woman with a passion for literature has opened a Latino bookstore in East Harlem.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/06/05/in-el-barrio-a-new-bookstore-is-counting-on-its-community/', 'snippet': 'Undaunted by the withering bookstore industry, a woman with a passion for literature has opened a Latino bookstore in East Harlem.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It seems to make little business sense. Not when Amazon’s dominance continues to threaten brick-and-mortar storefronts. And not when the economy has already claimed many small businesses.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '23', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'In El Barrio, a New Bookstore Is Counting on Its Community', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'In El Barrio, a New Bookstore Is Counting on Its Community', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hispanic-Americans', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Shopping and Retail', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'East Harlem (NYC)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-05T17:45:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Frederick Bernas', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Frederick', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bernas', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/42c9e521-abce-5858-8d86-28353777119d', 'word_count': 952, 'uri': 'nyt://article/42c9e521-abce-5858-8d86-28353777119d'}, {'abstract': 'In Mississippi, two women have asked the governor to spare their brother from execution, even though he killed four of their children.', 'web_url': 'https://takingnote.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/06/05/death-penalty-death-watch-mississippi/', 'snippet': 'In Mississippi, two women have asked the governor to spare their brother from execution, even though he killed four of their children.', 'lead_paragraph': 'There is really only one rationale for the death penalty: Vengeance. Supporters of capital punishment talk about deterrence, but there’s scant evidence that the threat of dying at the hands of the state actually reduces violent crime. On the contrary, as Princeton’s Peter Singer wrote in October, “murder rates in Europe and other Western industrialized nations are lower, often much lower, than those in the US. In the US itself, the 16 states that have abolished the death penalty generally have lower murder rates than those that retain it.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Death Penalty Death Watch: Mississippi', 'kicker': 'Taking Note', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Capital Punishment', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-05T17:45:14+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andrew Rosenthal', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andrew', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rosenthal', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/17a4d3ae-cd20-57a7-9655-1b318cd0926c', 'word_count': 254, 'uri': 'nyt://article/17a4d3ae-cd20-57a7-9655-1b318cd0926c'}, {'abstract': 'Same-sex couples should take extra precautions when setting up their financial and legal affairs. Here’s how to find professionals with expertise in this area.', 'web_url': 'https://bucks.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/06/05/how-same-sex-couples-can-find-financial-advisers/', 'snippet': 'Same-sex couples should take extra precautions when setting up their financial and legal affairs. Here’s how to find professionals with expertise in this area.', 'lead_paragraph': 'And though a recent federal appeals court ruling has provided some hope to gays and lesbians that their unions could potentially be recognized by the federal government, nothing has changed just yet.\xa0 That means that same-sex couples, including those who live in states that recognize their unions, should take extra care when setting up their financial life with a partner or spouse.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'How Same-Sex Couples Can Find Financial Advisers', 'kicker': 'Bucks', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Gay Couples: Money Advice', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Financial Planners', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Personal Finances', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'College for Financial Planning', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Lambda Legal Defense and Education Fund', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'org