inion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Seyom Brown and Vanda Felbab-Brown', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Seyom', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Brown', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Vanda', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Felbab-Brown', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/4498afb1-ea3a-5b82-9b3e-6d693cb08d5e', 'word_count': 820, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4498afb1-ea3a-5b82-9b3e-6d693cb08d5e'}, {'abstract': 'Europe, the United States, China and the Arab world have all squandered huge dividends, and digging out of their respective holes won’t be easy.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/06/06/opinion/friedman-what-the-locusts-ate.html', 'snippet': 'Europe, the United States, China and the Arab world have all squandered huge dividends, and digging out of their respective holes won’t be easy.', 'lead_paragraph': 'One of the most troubling features of today’s global economic crisis is the lack of political leadership anywhere. No one has the courage to tell their people the truth. And the truth, alas, is that four of the pillars of today’s global economy — Europe, America, China and the Arab world — have, each in their own way, squandered huge dividends they enjoyed in recent decades, and now they have to dig out of their respective holes with fewer resources, less time and, almost certainly, more pain. There is no easy way out. But, as confronting these hard truths becomes unavoidable, I think we’re likely to see some wild, angry and destabilizing politics that could make the economic recovery even more difficult. Deep holes and weak leaders are a bad combination.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '27', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/02/opinion/thomas-l-f