erage job.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I have gained tremendous insight reading the comments on this blog. One in particular really struck me recently. It came in response to a post that I had written quite a while ago, “The Dirty Little Secret of Successful Companies,” and it illustrates the huge difference in perspective that can exist between the owners of companies and their employees. Here is the comment:', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Is It O.K. to Be a Mediocre Employee?', 'kicker': 'You&#039;re the Boss', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Mediocrity In the Workplace', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hiring and Promotion', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Labor and Jobs', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Layoffs and Job Reductions', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Small Business', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-06T11:00:31+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Entrepreneurship', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jay Goltz', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jay', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Goltz', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c6573844-af79-5366-8645-73b65df0a588', 'word_count': 788, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c6573844-af79-5366-8645-73b65df0a588'}, {'abstract': 'Many years later, graduates of the Horace Mann School, an elite private school in New York, are finally able to tell their stories of sexual abuse.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/06/10/magazine/the-horace-mann-schools-secret-history-of-sexual-abuse.html', 'snippet': 'Many years later, graduates of the Horace Mann School, an elite private school in New York, are finally able to tell their stories of sexual abuse.', 'lead_paragraph': 'From the elevated platform of the No. 1 train’s last stop at 242nd Street, you can just about see the lush 18-acre campus of the Horace Mann School. The walk from the station is short, but it traverses worlds. Leaving the cluttered din of Broadway, you enter the leafy splendor of Fieldston, an enclave of mansions and flowering trees that feels more like a wealthy Westchester suburb than the Bronx. Head up the steep hill, turn left, then walk a bit farther, past the headmaster’s house. From the stone wall that runs along Tibbett Avenue, you can see practically the whole school: Pforzheimer Hall, Mullady Hall, the auditorium, the gymnasium and, right in the center, the manicured green expanse of the baseball field, home of the Lions, pride of the school.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '26', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/10/magazine/10horacemann_ss-slide-3V5E/10horacemann_ss-slide-3V5E-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/06/10/magazine/10horacemann_ss-slide-3V5E/10horacemann_ss-slide-3V5E-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/10/magazine/10horacemann_ss-slide-3V5E/10horacemann_ss-slide-3V5E-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/10/magazine/10horacemann_ss-slide-3V5E/10horacemann_ss-slide-3V5E-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/10/magazine/10horacemann_ss-slide-3V5E/10horacemann_ss-slide-3V5E-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/06/10/magazine/10horacemann_ss-slide-3V5E/10horacemann_ss-slide-3V5E-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/10/magazine/10horacemann_ss-slide-3V5E/10horacemann_ss-slide-3V5E-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Prep-School Predators', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '‘Great Is The Truth, And It Prevails’', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Teachers and School Employees', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Private and Sectarian Schools', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sex Crimes', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Bronx (NYC)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Clark, R Inslee Jr', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Child Abuse and Neglect', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Horace Mann School', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-06T11:00:32+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Amos Kamil', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Amos', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kamil', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/89caa948-e4df-56b2-afc3-ba753068ed3e', 'word_count': 9157, 'uri': 'nyt://article/89caa948-e4df-56b2-afc3-ba753068ed3e'}, {'abstract': 'Insurance is often out of reach for those in need. But in India, Africa and elsewhere, small, inexpensive policies are protecting millions.', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/06/06/the-microinsurance-revolution/', 'snippet': 'Insurance is often out of reach for those in need. But in India, Africa and elsewhere, small, inexpensive policies are protecting millions.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Six years ago David Patient felt his immune system slipping. He had been H.I.V.-positive for a long time, but now he made two decisions: He started on antiretroviral medicines to protect himself, and he began trying to buy life insurance to provide for his partner.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Microinsurance Revolution', 'kicker': 'Fixes', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Health Insurance and Managed Care', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Insurance', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Life Insurance', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-06T11:00:49+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Tina Rosenberg', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Tina', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rosenberg', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ff920919-1785-502d-86d2-2ff6bcffbc53', 'word_count': 1554, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ff920919-1785-502d-86d2-2ff6bcffbc53'}, {'abstract': 'Hewlett-Packard’s chief executive, Meg Whitman, has outlined a multiyear strategy to turn around the struggling computing giant. Innovative consumer products are coming, she said, but possibly not in time for Christmas. Most of the corporate businesses still need fixing. H.P. can perhaps be a leader in sales of cloud computing gear to cutting-edge companies, but “we have work to do on that,” she said.', 'web_url': 'https://bits.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/06/06/whitman-vision-isnt-stunning-h-p-has-work-to-do/', 'snippet': 'Hewlett-Packard’s chief executive, Meg Whitman, has outlined a multiyear strategy to turn around the struggling computing giant. Innovative consumer products are coming, she said, but possibly not in time for Christmas. Most of the corporate busin...', 'lead_paragraph': 'LAS VEGAS — Meg Whitman has a clear vision of Hewlett-Packard making radically new consumer products. She sees it selling cloud computing technologies that she hopes will attract fast-growing start-ups to the venerable brand. She wants to transform its printing and services businesses. Much of the promise is on show at a big show for corporate clients in Las Vegas this week.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/05/25/technology/25whitman/25whitman-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/05/25/technology/25whitman/25whitman-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Whitman’s Simple Vision: Hewlett-Packard Has Work to Do', 'kicker': 'Bits', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cloud Computing', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computers and the Internet', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Enterprise Computing', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Whitman, Margaret C', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Hewlett-Packard Company', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-06T11:01:47+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'se