m together here so you can quickly scan the news that could hit you in your wallet.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Wednesday Reading: The Quest for a  Frugal Scandinavian Summer', 'kicker': 'Bucks', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-06-06T12:34:54+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Your Money', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ann Carrns', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ann', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Carrns', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/337c3ecd-9063-5267-b67f-ba850ee87c26', 'word_count': 142, 'uri': 'nyt://article/337c3ecd-9063-5267-b67f-ba850ee87c26'}, {'abstract': 'Meg Whitman, the chief executive of Hewlett-Packard, spoke to customers and employees at a company event in Las Vegas. She also spoke to several reporters about her plans for the company. Nothing new in consumer products is in the offing, she said, but she’s making “a big bet” on corporate sales of laptops and personal computers when Windows 8 comes out.', 'web_url': 'https://bits.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/06/06/daily-report-whither-hewlett-packard/', 'snippet': 'Meg Whitman, the chief executive of Hewlett-Packard, spoke to customers and employees at a company event in Las Vegas. She also spoke to several reporters about her plans for the company. Nothing new in consumer products is in the offing, she said...', 'lead_paragraph': 'Meg Whitman, the chief executive of Hewlett-Packard, has spent much of this week trying to persuade customers as well as employees that she can straighten out the company and make it grow again. Quentin Hardy of The New York Times interviewed her Tuesday to learn what she is thinking. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit':