ue': 'Democratic Party', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Republican Party', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Wisconsin', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-06T12:49:55+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Monica Davey', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Monica', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Davey', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/242a147a-1e74-5d7c-945e-b92233de81ad', 'word_count': 294, 'uri': 'nyt://article/242a147a-1e74-5d7c-945e-b92233de81ad'}, {'abstract': 'The city is paying contractors more than $1 billion this year for a special education that serves 25,000 3- and 4-year-olds  with physical, learning, developmental and other disabilities, nearly double the amount it paid six years ago. Audits into some of the contractors by the state comptroller, Thomas P. DiNapoli, have already led to a felony guilty plea by one Brooklyn contractor, and officials said more arrests were expected.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/schoolbook/2012/06/06/spending-for-a-special-education-program-is-investigated/', 'snippet': 'The city is paying contractors more than $1 billion this year for a special education that serves 25,000 3- and 4-year-olds  with physical, learning, developmental and other disabilities, nearly double the amount it paid six years ago. Audits into...', 'lead_paragraph': '', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Spending for a Special Education Program Is Investigated', 'kicker': 'SchoolBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-06-06T13:00:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Education', 'byline': {'original': 'By MARIA NEWMAN', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Maria', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'NEWMAN', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/1752467d-66f4-50a6-b1fc-2181ae8b37cd', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1752467d-66f4-50a6-b1fc-2181ae8b37cd'}, {'abstract': 'Aaron Sorkin, the Academy- and Emmy Award-winning screenwriter of “The Social Network” and creator of “The West Wing,” talks about his new HBO series, “The Newsroom,” which is set at a fictional cable-TV news program.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/06/06/back-to-you-aaron-sorkin-returns-to-television-with-the-newsroom/', 'snippet': 'Aaron Sorkin, the Academy- and Emmy Award-winning screenwriter of “The Social Network” and creator of “The West Wing,” talks about his new HBO series, “The Newsroom,” which is set at a fictional cable-TV news program.', 'lead_paragraph': 'LOS ANGELES — A new one comes every few years, like a presidential election or the Olympic Games: an Aaron Sorkin television series set in a high-pressure work environment. “The Newsroom,” which will have its HBO debut on June 24, is the latest show from Mr. Sorkin, the Academy and Emmy Award-winning screenwriter, and his first in nearly six years. It chronicles the behind-the-scenes drama (and comedy) at a fictional cable-news program, hosted by an anchor (played by Jeff Daniels) who has recently returned from a very public “mad as hell” moment of unraveling.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Back to You: Aaron Sorkin Returns to Television With ‘The Newsroom’', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'News and News Media', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Writing and Writers', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Daniels, Jeff', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Sorkin, Aaron', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Home Box Office', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-06T13:00:09+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dave Itzkoff', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dave', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Itzkoff', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e1679cb8-bec1-5c53-bc00-ca3f561d07c7', 'word_count': 1654, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e1679cb8-bec1-5c53-bc00-ca3f561d07c7'}, {'abstract': 'The city is considering changes to the student discipline code to reduce students to suspensions. But at a demonstration, others said the changes did not go far enough.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/schoolbook/2012/06/06/students-and-others-rally-before-discipline-code-hearing/', 'snippet': 'The city is considering changes to the student discipline code to reduce students to suspensions. But at a demonstration, others said the changes did not go far enough.', 'lead_paragraph': '', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Students and Others Rally Before Discipline Code Hearing', 'kicker': 'SchoolBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-06-06T13:10:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Education', 'byline': {'original': 'By THEODORIC MEYER', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Theodoric', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'MEYER', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/fb13bd2a-c197-5010-9281-8618ff06e080', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fb13bd2a-c197-5010-9281-8618ff06e080'}, {'abstract': 'It’s never over.', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/06/06/wisconsin-2/', 'snippet': 'It’s never over.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The best lack all conviction, while the worst are filled with a passionate intensity. Obviously I’m not happy with the result; not just out of political sympathies, but because all the recent political trends have been rewarding the side that caused the very crisis from which it is now benefiting, not to mention politicians who have been wrong about everything since the crisis hit.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Wisconsin', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-06-06T13:18:35+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/da00fb20-816f-5f6c-9242-eec7a6804c8a', 'word_count': 189, 'uri': 'nyt://article/da00fb20-816f-5f6c-9242-eec7a6804c8a'}, {'abstract': 'Sharp increases in wealth, coupled with masses of visible poor.', 'web_url': 'https://india.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/06/06/todays-india-recalls-the-reign-of-victoria/', 'snippet': 'Sharp increases in wealth, coupled with masses of visible poor.', 'lead_paragraph': '“As Britain celebrates six decades of Elizabeth II as monarch, it is the reign of another queen that contemporary India resembles,” Manu Joseph writes in The International Herald Tribune. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Today’s India Recalls the Reign of Victoria', 'kicker': 'India Ink', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'su