John Martin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'John', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Martin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/25237a46-5e9f-5e02-8919-89fbf70900be', 'word_count': 626, 'uri': 'nyt://article/25237a46-5e9f-5e02-8919-89fbf70900be'}, {'abstract': 'The author of “The Mansion of Happiness” on how we define when life starts, when it ends and how best to live it.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/06/06/jill-lepore-talks-about-life-death-and-the-stages-in-between/', 'snippet': 'The author of “The Mansion of Happiness” on how we define when life starts, when it ends and how best to live it.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In “The Mansion of Happiness: A History of Life and Death” (Knopf), Jill Lepore examines the ways in which we’ve historically defined where life begins, where it ends and how it should be conducted while it lasts. In a recent interview via e-mail, Ms. Lepore discussed why death is a less contentious subject than conception, why the word “parenthood” had to be invented and more. Below are excerpts from the conversation.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Jill Lepore Talks About Life, Death and the Stages in Between', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lepore, Jill', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-06T21:17:13+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By John Williams', 'person': [{'firstname': 'John', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Williams', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ac1c7b05-9f06-5caf-a042-c89b9432a45f', 'word_count': 1378, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ac1c7b05-9f06-5caf-a042-c89b9432a45f'}, {'abstract': 'Tom Coughlin, 65, the Giants’ coach since 2004, became the oldest coach to win a Super Bowl last season.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/06/07/sports/football/giants-extend-tom-coughlins-contract-through-2014.html', 'snippet': 'Tom Coughlin, 65, the Giants’ coach since 2004, became the oldest coach to win a Super Bowl last season.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Once the Giants won the Super Bowl in February for the second time in Tom Coughlin’s coaching tenure, the question of his latest contract extension became a matter of when instead of if. The Giants answered Wednesday, announcing that Coughlin’s contract, which was set to expire after the coming season, had been extended through 2014.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '16', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/07/sports/07giants/07giants-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 630, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/07/sports/07giants/07giants-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1260, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/07/sports/07giants/07giants-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/06/07/sports/07giants/07giants-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/07/sports/07giants/07giants-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Giants Extend Coughlin’s Contract to Reward a Two-Time Champion', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Giants Extend Coughlin’s Contract To Reward a Two-Time Champion', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Coughlin, Tom', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Coaches and Managers', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Football', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Giants', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-06T21:19:25+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Pro Football', 'byline': {'original': 'By Lynn Zinser', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Lynn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Zinser', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c8a6afa9-dad3-56a0-99b0-abbd66a65b06', 'word_count': 451, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c8a6afa9-dad3-56a0-99b0-abbd66a65b06'}, {'abstract': '“I just bought a 1930s-style house, and it actually looks a lot like this collection,” said Scott Sternberg of his resort collection.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/06/06/just-one-look-boy-by-band-of-outsiders-2/', 'snippet': '“I just bought a 1930s-style house, and it actually looks a lot like this collection,” said Scott Sternberg of his resort collection.', 'lead_paragraph': '“I just bought a 1930s-style house, and it actually looks a lot like this collection,” said Scott Sternberg, the designer behind Band of Outsiders. The L.A.-based collection featured many California design influences (thanks to LACMA’s recent interiors show): Bauhaus-inspired architecture and primary colors translated into classic captain blazers and striped kerchief dresses and anoraks (above), for example. In addition, Sternberg points out: “We experimented with evening wear last season, and it went over really well.” He has even taken a liking to jumpsuits. “So we’re going to keep at it.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Just One Look | Boy by Band of Outsiders', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-06-06T21:20:06+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Chelsea Zalopany', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Chelsea', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Zalopany', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0f49d517-e6b4-5621-bb0e-3628fe70f79b', 'word_count': 90, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0f49d517-e6b4-5621-bb0e-3628fe70f79b'}, {'abstract': 'Recent high school graduates who do not have bachelor’s degrees overwhelmingly believe they will need more education to have successful careers — but most do not expect to enroll anytime soon.', 'web_url': 'https://economix.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/06/06/dont-think-college-is-worth-it-ask-people-who-havent-gone/', 'snippet': 'Recent high school graduates who do not have bachelor’s degrees overwhelmingly believe they will need more education to have successful careers — but most do not expect to enroll anytime soon.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Last month the Heldrich Center for Workforce Development released data showing that college graduates generally do not regret going to college, despite lots of criticism of the value of higher education. Today the center released a new report focusing on the depressing state of America’s recent high school graduates, who seem to agree about the importance of further education.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Don’t Think College Is Worth It? Ask People Who Haven’t Gone', 'kicker': 'Economix', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Colleges and Universities', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Youth', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Center for Workforce Development', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-06T21:20:31+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Economy', 'byline': {'original': 'By Catherine Rampell', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Catherine', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rampell', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2766e623-4816-5d52-a7b6-f2f06badaaea', 'word_count': 319, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2766e623-4816-5d52-a7b6-f2f06badaaea'}, {'abstract': 'LinkedIn said it would stop taking users’ meeting notes from their calendars on Wednesday, after security researchers discovered that the company was transmitting sensitive data back to its Web servers without users’ permission. The revelation came just one day before LinkedIn’s Web servers were breached.', 'web_url': 'https://bits.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/06/06/linkedin-tweaks-calendar-feature-to-stop-taking-meeting-notes/', 'snippet': 'LinkedIn said it would stop taking users’ meeting notes from their calendars on Wednesday, after security researchers discovered that the company was transmitting sensitive data back to its Web servers without users’ permission. The revelation cam...', 'lead_paragraph': 'On Tuesday, Bits reported that LinkedIn’s mobile application for the iPhone and iPad contained an opt-in feature that transported sensitive information from users’ calendars — including meeting notes, dial-in information, passwords and participants’ contact information — back to its servers, without users’ knowledge. Security researchers noted that the practice could prove problematic in cases where people inserted sensitive information, like confidential financial data or strategic plans, for instance, into those meeting notes.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'LinkedIn Tweaks Calendar Feature to Stop Taking Meeting Notes', 'kicker': 'Bits', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mobile Applications', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'LinkedIn Corporation', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-06T21:23:57+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nicole Perlroth', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nicole', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Perlroth', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3983fb2c-4ccd-51eb-afbb-684b7cd7d19a', 'word_count': 412, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3983fb2c-4ccd-51eb-afbb-684b7cd7d19a'}, {'abstract': 'The winner of the 2000 Pulitzer Prize brings his new work, “Vespers Cantata: Hesperus Is Phosphorus,” to New York, with its sections culling from the work of varied authors.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/06/07/arts/music/hesperus-is-phosphorus-lewis-spratlans-new-work.html', 'snippet': 'The winner of the 2000 Pulitzer Prize brings his new work, “Vespers Cantata: Hesperus Is Phosphorus,” to New York, with its sections culling from the work of varied authors.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In the section of David Eagleman’s work of fiction “Sum: Forty Tales From the Afterlives” called “Egalitaire,” a mature female God is imagined mulling the difficulties of arbitrating morality.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url':