d last year. Adele’s “21” (XL/Columbia) remained at No. 2, and Regina Spektor’s “What We Saw From the Cheap Seats” (Sire/Warner Brothers) entered the chart at No. 3 with 42,000 copies sold. One Direction’s “Up All Night” (Syco/Columbia) was just behind at No. 4 with 41,000 in sales, and Edward Sharp and the Magnetic Zeros made their debut at No. 5 with “Here,” (Community Music) which sold 35,000 copies.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Mayer Keeps Top Spot on Album Chart', 'kicker': 'Arts, Briefly', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Mayer Keeps Top Spot On Album Chart', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Mayer, John', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-06T22:35:00+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By James C. McKinley Jr', 'person': [{'firstname': 'James', 'middlename': 'C.', 'lastname': 'McKinley', 'qualifier': 'Jr', 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/02b6d4c6-2e69-5e83-8dad-3a40ff6887c5', 'word_count': 154, 'uri': 'nyt://article/02b6d4c6-2e69-5e83-8dad-3a40ff6887c5'}, {'abstract': 'As smartphones have reached only 20 percent of the developing world, Facebook needs to get into the phone business because it could reach an audience that can’t afford today’s smartphones or the monthly data and voice costs that come with them. It’s the same market it wants to reach with its basic Web-based service.', 'web_url': 'https://bits.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/06/06/why-facebook-should-build-a-phone/', 'snippet': 'As smartphones have reached only 20 percent of the developing world, Facebook needs to get into the phone business because it could reach an audience that can’t afford today’s smartphones or the monthly data and voice costs that come with them. It...', 'lead_paragraph': 'Last week\xa0I reported\xa0about Facebook assembling a team of experienced engineers with the hopes of building a Facebook smartphone.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Why Facebook Should Build a Phone', 'kicker': 'Bits', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-06-06T22:37:15+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nick Bilton', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nick', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bilton', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/475fdd99-ea23-54e2-8375-1cddbd91fae2', 'word_count': 533, 'uri': 'nyt://article/475fdd99-ea23-54e2-8375-1cddbd91fae2'}, {'abstract': 'In an age of e-communications, publishers and writers hope the personal touch possible at BookExpo America will help sell books.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/06/07/books/bookexpo-america-at-jacob-k-javits-convention-center.html', 'snippet': 'In an age of e-communications, publishers and writers hope the personal touch possible at BookExpo America will help sell books.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Last year at BookExpo America, the publishing industry’s annual trade convention, the unknown author Amor Towles arrived to pitch his first book, “Rules of Civility,” to the throngs. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Expo’s Hope: Personal Touch to Sell Books', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Expo’s Hope: Personal Touch To Sell Books', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Book Trade and Publishing', 'rank': 2, 