t and anticipation permeated the atmosphere around Staples Center on Wednesday before Game 4 of the Stanley Cup finals. The Los Angeles Kings needed one more victory to win the first Stanley Cup in their 45-year history.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '15', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/07/sports/subCUP/subCUP-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 751, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/07/sports/subCUP/subCUP-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1501, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Kings Let Their Chance Slip Away', 'kicker': 'Stanley Cup Finals Game 4: Devils 3, Kings 1', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Kings Let Their Chance Slip Away', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hockey, Ice', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Los Angeles Kings', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gagne, Simon', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New Jersey Devils', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Penner, Dustin', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Stanley Cup', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Quick, Jonathan', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-07T04:45:57+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Hockey', 'byline': {'original': "By Joseph D'Hippolito", 'person': [{'firstname': 'Joseph', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': "D'Hippolito", 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9162cd8f-eb6e-5bd1-9c66-9edf9ace3378', 'word_count': 429, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9162cd8f-eb6e-5bd1-9c66-9edf9ace3378'}, {'abstract': 'Intravenous drips and oxygen masks during cram sessions. Nannies with college degrees. Temples jammed with anxious, prayerful parents. Rerouted funerals, plane flights and highway traffic. No honking. Free taxi rides. It’s all part of the high-pressure phenomenon of gao kao, the annual college-entrance exam in China.', 'web_url': 'https://rendezvous.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/06/07/putting-chinese-students-to-the-test/', 'snippet': 'Intravenous drips and oxygen masks during cram sessions. Nannies with college degrees. Temples jammed with anxious, prayerful parents. Rerouted funerals, plane flights and highway traffic. No honking. Free taxi rides. It’s all part of the high-pre...', 'lead_paragraph': 'HONG KONG — They’ve been at it for years now, cramming for China’s annual college-entrance exam, some of them hooked up to oxygen canisters and intravenous drips of amino acids during late-night and weekend study marathons. Their parents hire special tutors, force the kids into educational boot camps in the summer and promise lavish gifts of cash and cars for good results. Housekeepers and nannies with college degrees come at a huge premium. Some girls take contraceptives or get hormone injections to make sure they won’t be distracted by getting their period during the exam. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/07/blogs/test-rdv/test-rdv-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 759, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/07/blogs/test-rdv/test-rdv-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1518, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Putting Chinese Students to the Test', 'kicker': 'IHT Rendezvous', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ADMISSIONS STANDARDS', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cheating', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Chinese Language', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Colleges and Universities', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Education (K-12)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Foreign Students (in US)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Language and Languages', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Parenting', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Tests and Examinations', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'China Daily', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Chronicle of Higher Education', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Beijing (China)', 'rank': 12, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'China', 'rank': 13, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Shanghai (China)', 'rank': 14, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-07T04:51:22+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'World', 'byline': {'original': 'By Mark McDonald', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mark', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'McDonald', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/19897121-f720-5621-a8cc-e8ef43bc57a3', 'word_count': 909, 'uri': 'nyt://article/19897121-f720-5621-a8cc-e8ef43bc57a3'}, {'abstract': 'Despite economic and development woes of its own, India may be a practical aid partner for Afghanistan.', 'web_url': 'https://india.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/06/07/can-india-fix-afghanistan/', 'snippet': 'Despite economic and development woes of its own, India may be a practical aid partner for Afghanistan.', 'lead_paragraph': 'As the United States winds down its military engagement in Afghanistan, optimism is growing about the role India can play to stabilize and develop the country.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Can India ‘Fix’ Afghanistan?', 'kicker': 'India Ink', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Afghanistan War (2001- )', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Economic Conditions and Trends', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Infrastructure (Public Works)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mines and Mining', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Philanthropy', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Karzai, Hamid', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Panetta, Leon V', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Singh, Manmohan', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Taliban', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'AFGHANISTAN', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'India', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Kabul (Afghanistan)', 'rank': 12, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Pakistan', 'rank': 13, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'United States', 'rank': 14, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-07T05:01:28+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Heather Timmons', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Heather', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Timmons', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/985583c6-331f-57f7-91d7