ni', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Monkovic', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6413ac3e-46ac-54fc-9d82-6003a94df620', 'word_count': 291, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6413ac3e-46ac-54fc-9d82-6003a94df620'}, {'abstract': 'In this week’s links: robot ethics, provocative philosophers, courtroom aesthetics, and more.', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/06/07/stone-links-robot-right-and-wrong/', 'snippet': 'In this week’s links: robot ethics, provocative philosophers, courtroom aesthetics, and more.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Driverless cars may be just around the corner, but who do we blame if they cause an accident? An article in the Economist argues that the proliferation of robots, now in heavy use by the military and increasingly by civilians, demands that we reconsider long-held concepts of agency and autonomy in order to develop a “machine ethics.” Sorting out this new landscape may well involve asking the kind of questions experimental philosophy is currently asking about everyday moral intuitions. And at the very least, ethicists and engineers will have to get used to the idea of working closely together. The article also suggests it will be necessary “to determine whether the designer, the programmer, the manufacturer or the operator is at fault” if something goes wrong. But if responsibility can ultimately be assigned to a human being, its unclear why we would need to scrap the good old fashioned moral system we already have in place. If we can’t blame the robots, why bother developing a system in which they’re accountable?', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Stone Links: Robot Right and Wrong', 'kicker': 'The Stone', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Philosophy', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-07T11:49:15+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By A.c. Lee', 'person': [{'firstname': '', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/09c0d0be-09f7-5969-918a-3baa0d42b71b', 'word_count': 520, 'uri': 'nyt://article/09c0d0be-09f7-5969-918a-3baa0d42b71b'}, {'abstract': 'Highlights from the DealBook newsletter.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2012/06/07/morning-take-out-500/', 'snippet': 'Highlights from the DealBook newsletter.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Goldman Sachs Expected to Name Fewer Partners \xa0|\xa0 As Goldman Sachs shrinks, its elite inner circle will also be getting smaller. The Wall Street firm is expected to name fewer than 100 new partners this fall, one of the smallest classes in recent years, according to people briefed on the matter but not authorized to speak on the record.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Morning Take-Out', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-06-07T11:50:33+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By William Alden', 'person': [{'firstname': 'William', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Alden', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9d1ea97d-fb34-5a21-a095-9c83c7b8bffa', 'word_count': 2389, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9d1ea97d-fb34-5a21-a095-9c83c7b8bffa'}, {'abstract': 'The latest survey by The New York Times and CBS News shows that more than two-thirds of Americans want the Supreme Court to overturn some or all of the legislation.', 'web_url': 'https://thecaucus.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/06/07/new-poll-the-supreme-court-and-the-health-care-law/', 'snippet': 'The latest survey by The New York Times and CBS News shows that more than two-thirds of Americans want the Supreme Court to overturn some or all of the legislation.', 'lead_paragraph': 'More than two-thirds of Americans hope the Supreme Court will overturn some or all of the 2010 health care law, according to a new poll conducted by The New York Times and CBS News. Just 24 percent said they hoped the court “would keep the entire health care law in place.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'New Poll: The Supreme Court and the Health Care Law', 'kicker': 'The Caucus', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Patient Protection and Affordable Care Act (2010)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Polls and Public Opinion', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'CBS News', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Times', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Supreme Court', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-07T12:00:41+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Adam Liptak and Allison Kopicki', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Adam', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Liptak', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Allison', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kopicki', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/375619f6-4b0d-5104-be1d-c3124649e0ef', 'word_count': 398, 'uri': 'nyt://article/375619f6-4b0d-5104-be1d-c3124649e0ef'}, {'abstract': 'A summary of what’s on today’s editorial page.', 'web_ur