anization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d126ddc6-1a4b-5bc5-ac24-0425d8e0203d', 'word_count': 189, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d126ddc6-1a4b-5bc5-ac24-0425d8e0203d'}, {'abstract': 'College admissions books for summer reading, Nasdaq sets aside $40 million to settle Facebook I.P.O. claims, a study finds that CT scans increase children’s cancer risk and other consumer-focused news in today’s Times.', 'web_url': 'https://bucks.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/06/07/thursday-reading-college-admissions-books-for-summer-reading/', 'snippet': 'College admissions books for summer reading, Nasdaq sets aside $40 million to settle Facebook I.P.O. claims, a study finds that CT scans increase children’s cancer risk and other consumer-focused news in today’s Times.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A variety of consumer-focused articles appears daily in The New York Times and on our blogs. Each weekday morning, we gather them together here so you can quickly scan the news that could hit you in your wallet.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Thursday Reading: College Admissions Books for Summer Reading', 'kicker': 'Bucks', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-06-07T13:23:31+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Your Money', 'byline': {'original': 'By Tara Siegel Bernard', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Tara', 'middlename': 'Siegel', 'lastname': 'Bernard', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/22016e56-be16-5eea-862b-1a6ff0b7aaf0', 'word_count': 248, 'uri': 'nyt://article/22016e56-be16-5eea-862b-1a6ff0b7aaf0'}, {'abstract': 'Also, a European “shareholder spring” erupts over payouts to advertising executives, and “The Clock” is coming to Lincoln Center, free of charge.', 'web_url': 'https://mediadecoder.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/06/07/the-breakfast-meeting-ray-bradburys-long-shadow-and-a-first-ladys-journalism/', 'snippet': 'Also, a European “shareholder spring” erupts over payouts to advertising executives, and “The Clock” is coming to Lincoln Center, free of charge.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Ray Bradbury, who conjured up life on Mars, virtual reality and, perhaps most shockingly, a world without books, died on Tuesday at age 91. His was a prolific professional writing career – that produced novels, short stories, a screenplay and plays — that spanned 70 years, Gerald Jonas writes. The impulse began in childhood, he wrote in an autobiographical essay for The New Yorker. “It was one frenzy after one elation after one enthusiasm after one hysteria after another,” he wrote, noting, “You rarely have such fevers later in life that fill your entire day with emotion.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Breakfast Meeting: Ray Bradbury’s Long Shadow, and a First Lady’s Journalism', 'kicker': 'Media Decoder', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-06-07T13:25:50+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Media', 'byline': {'original': 'By Noam Cohen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Noam', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cohen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/65a444b6-6d76-545b-8b33-379f66b7f2a2', 'word_count': 496, 'uri': 'nyt://article/65a444b6-6d76-545b-8b33-379f66b7f2a2'}, {'abstract': 'A weaker Europe has meant a stronger dollar, making American products more expensive.', 'web_url': 'https://boss.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/06/07/do-you-expect-europes-problems-to-affect-your-business/', 'snippet': 'A weaker Europe has meant a stronger dollar, making American products more expensive.', 'lead_paragraph': 'This week The Times reported that an increasing number of American companies are warning that sales in Europe are slowing — and that the situation could get much worse. “Prospects for the European economy darkened in recent days,” Nathaniel Popper wrote, “as surveys showed that manufacturing across the euro zone contracted in May and the unemployment rate climbed to a record 11 percent.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Do You Expect Europe’s Problems to Affect Your Business?', 'kicker': 'You&#039;re the Boss', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'International Trade and World Market', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Small Business', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Europe', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-07T13:42:00+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Entrepreneurship', 'byline': {'original': "By You're the Boss Editors", 'person': [{'firstname': '', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/65a86b4b-a7fa-5a0d-8cb7-21170eeb4d4e', 'word_count': 163, 'uri': 'nyt://article/65a86b4b-a7fa-5a0d-8cb7-21170eeb4d4e'}, {'abstract': 'A group of bondholders pursuing a litigation strategy that involves seeking to lay claim to Argentine assets wherever they can be found may have a tough time making its case, DealBook’s bankruptcy columnist writes.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2012/06/07/at-a-house-hearing-a-focus-on-argentine-bonds/', 'snippet': 'A group of bondholders pursuing a litigation strategy that involves seeking to lay claim to Argentine assets wherever they can be found may have a tough time making its case, DealBook’s bankruptcy columnist writes.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The House Financial Services Subcommittee on Capital Markets is scheduled to hold a hearing later on Thursday on what it has titled “The Need to Protect Investors From the Government.” ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'At a House Hearing, a Focus on Argentine Bonds', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Decisions and Verdicts', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Stocks and Bonds', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Suits and Litigation', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'ARGENTINA', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-07T13:50:14+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Stephen J. Lubben', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Stephen', 'middlename': 'J.', 'lastname': 'Lubben', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f00cd6d7-3169-599f-b5eb-4e02af07044e', 'word_count': 539, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f00cd6d7-3169-599f-b5eb-4e02af07044e'}, {'abstract': 'Cellphone carriers like AT&T and Verizon say they are worried about running out of the radio spectrum that carries wireless calls and data. But as Brian X. Chen reports in Thursday’s New York Times, a number of companies are developing technology that could change the whole spectrum game by using radio frequencies more efficiently.', 'web_url': 'https://bits.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/06/07/daily-report-squeezing-more-data-into-spectrum/', 'snippet': 'Cellphone carriers like AT&T and Verizon say they are worried about running out of the radio spectrum that carries wireless calls and data. But as Brian X. Chen reports in Thursday’s New York Times, a number of companies are developing technology ...', 'lead_paragraph': 'Cellphone carriers like AT&T and Verizon say they are worried about running out of the radio spectrum that carries wireless calls and data, and they want the government to give them more chunks of it. But as Brian X. Chen reports in Thursday’s New York Times, a number of companies are developing technology that could change the whole spectrum game by using radio frequencies more efficiently.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/11/30/technology/bits-daily-report/bits-daily-report-articleLarge-v4.png', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/11/30/technology/bits-daily-report/bits-daily-report-articleLarge-v4.png', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/11/30/technology/bits-daily-report/bits-daily-report-jumbo-v6.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/11/30/technology/bits-daily-report/bits-daily-report-superJumbo-v3.png', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/11/30/technology/bits-daily-report/bits-daily-report-thumbStandard-v5.png', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/11/30/technology/bits-daily-report/bits-daily-report-thumbStandard-v5.png', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/11/30/technology/bits-daily-report/bits-daily-report-thumbLarge-v5.png', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Daily Report: Squeezing More Data Into Spectrum', 'kicker': 'Bits', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-06-07T13:50:45+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f6a5b1ed-4789-566a-bfac-a92054aa8019', 'word_count': 251, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f6a5b1ed-4789-566a-bfac-a92054aa8019'}, {'abstract': 'John Mayer needed to sell only 65,000 copies to hold onto the top slot, the smallest number for a No. 1 album since Feb. 2011.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/06/07/another-week-for-mayer-at-top-of-charts/', 'snippet': 'John Mayer needed to sell only 65,000 copies to hold onto the top slot, the smallest number for a No. 1 album since Feb. 2011.', 'lead_paragraph': 'John Mayer’s “Born and Raised” (Columbia) topped the Billboard album chart for a second week, but that was not a huge accomplishment, since sales were anemic all around. Mr. Mayer needed to sell only 65,000 copies to hold onto the top slot, the smallest number for a No. 1 album since Feb. 2011, when Nicki Minaj’s “Pink Friday” was on top after selling only 45,000. Overall, album sales are down 3 percent since January compared to the same period last year.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Another Week for Mayer at Top of Charts', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Mayer Keeps Top Spot On Album Chart', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-06-07T13:55:54+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By James C. McKinley Jr', 'person': [{'firstname': 'James', 'middlename': 'C.', 'lastname': 'McKinley', 'qualifier': 'Jr', 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/bac068cb-b207-523f-9456-a3348fc49604', 'word_count': 350, 'uri': 'nyt://article/bac068cb-b207-523f-9456-a3348fc49604'}, {'abstract': 'With the Tonys upon us, it’s worth noting that some of the finest new playwriting is not necessarily Broadway-bound.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/06/10/theater/amy-herzog-and-others-bring-new-voices-to-stage.html', 'snippet': 'With the Tonys upon us, it’s worth noting that some of the finest new playwriting is not necessarily Broadway-bound.', 'lead_paragraph': 'ONE of the happier stories of the Broadway season, to be capped on Sunday night with the annual presentation of the Tony Awards, was the wealth of fine new plays. There’s a story behind that story: the continuing importance of Off Broadway as the cultivating ground for America’s best dramatists. All four of this year’s nominees for best play — “Clybourne Park,” “Other Desert Cities,” “Peter and the Starcatcher” and “Venus in Fur” — were first produced Off Broadway. ', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Dramatic Rush From the Beat of Delicate Hearts', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Dramatic Rush From the Beat Of Delicate Hearts', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Writing and Writers', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Karam, Stephen', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Baker, Annie', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Herzog, Amy', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Eno, Will', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Doran, Bathsheba', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-07T14:00:02+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Theater', 'byline': {'original': 'By Charles Isherwood', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Charles', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Isherwood', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/942a6b74-5d0e-5aa5-aee3-f5d6c17b84b4', 'word_count': 1678, 'uri': 'nyt://article/942a6b74-5d0e-5aa5-aee3-f5d6c17b84b4'}, {'abstract': 'Los Angeles’s go-to purveyor of rare and out-of-print books moves house.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/06/07/seeing-things-arcanas-next-chapter/', 'snippet': 'Los Angeles’s go-to purveyor of rare and out-of-print books moves house.', 'lead_paragraph': 'var data;function jsonSlideshowCallback(response){data = response;}NYTD.options = {source: data,cropType: \'blog480\',appendTo: \'fullScreenSlideShow\',showHeadLine: \'false\', renderstyle: "embeddedSlideShowTmag"}', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Seeing Things | Arcana’s Next Chapter', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-06-07T14:00:32+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Brooke Hodge', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Brooke', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hodge', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c5f6930d-a166-5e03-82f0-457a271ef7b9', 'word_count': 425, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c5f6930d-a166-5e03-82f0-457a271ef7b9'}, {'abstract': 'Lackluster jobs data and economic storm clouds have not convinced Federal Reserve officials that growth is slowing or that they should respond, the Fed’s chairman, Ben S. Bernanke, said on Thursday.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/06/08/business/economy/seeing-us-growth-bernanke-offers-no-signs-of-new-action.html', 'snippet': 'Lackluster jobs data and economic storm clouds have not convinced Federal Reserve officials that growth is slowing or that they should respond, the Fed’s chairman, Ben S. Bernanke, said on Thursday.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WASHINGTON — Lackluster jobs data and storm clouds on the economic horizon have not yet convinced Federal Reserve officials that growth is slowing, nor that they should respond, the Fed’s chairman, Ben S. Bernanke, said on Thursday.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/08/business/Fedjp/Fedjp-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 330, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/06/08/business/Fedjp/Fedjp-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 330}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/08/business/Fedjp/Fedjp-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/08/business/Fedjp/Fedjp-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/08/business/Fedjp/Fedjp-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/06/08/business/Fedjp/Fedjp-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailhei