tions', 'value': 'Mumbai (India)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-07T16:00:06+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0fa54055-886a-50f0-913d-994dcd05a99d', 'word_count': 1, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0fa54055-886a-50f0-913d-994dcd05a99d'}, {'abstract': 'Cooking was my husband’s attempt to contain his illness, a way of adjusting his highs and lows through the stove’s dial.', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/06/07/still-too-raw-to-eat-meat/', 'snippet': 'Cooking was my husband’s attempt to contain his illness, a way of adjusting his highs and lows through the stove’s dial.', 'lead_paragraph': 'One evening, as I sprawled on our sofa reading a book, I heard my husband rummaging through the kitchen cabinets. There was a clang as he removed a skillet from the pile of pans, nested together like children’s stacking toys. This was unusual. Since he’d gotten sick a few months earlier, I was the one who cooked. Puttering around our small kitchen and preparing meals comforted me; it was the one small routine that remained consistent when everything else was falling apart. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Still Too Raw to Eat Meat', 'kicker': 'Townies', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Meat', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mental Health and Disorders', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Brooklyn (NYC)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-07T16:00:06+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sue Sanders', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sue', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Sanders', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b1c7d6e9-a34d-5da3-a6c9-7d2a99355581', 'word_count': 1096, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b1c7d6e9-a34d-5da3-a6c9-7d2a99355581'}, {'abstract': 'The fantasy writer Ray Bradbury, who died this week at the age of 91, touched on environmental themes in 1948 with a mixture of anger and elegy that anticipated the writings of Rachel Carson and David Brower.', 'web_url': 'https://green.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/06/07/the-martian-eco-chronicles/', 'snippet': 'The fantasy writer Ray Bradbury, who died this week at the age of 91, touched on environmental themes in 1948 with a mixture of anger and elegy that anticipated the writings of Rachel Carson and David Brower.', 'lead_paragraph': 'So, we’ll go no more a roving So late into the night,Though the heart be still as loving, And the moon be still as bright.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Martian Eco-Chronicles', 'kicker': 'Green', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Earth', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mars (Planet)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sustainable Living', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Writing and Writers', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bradbury, Ray', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Byron, Lord', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gorney, Cynthia', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'The Martian Chronicles (Movie)', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-07T16:00:17+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Science', 'subsection_name': 'Environment', 'byline': {'original': 'By Felicity Barringer', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Felicity', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Barringer', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ccb2ffac-eb81-509c-abee-0bf64e3b62bc', 'word_count': 934, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ccb2ffac-eb81-509c-abee-0bf64e3b62bc'}, {'abstract': 'Thakoon Panichugal talked about two things at his resort presentation: cake frosting and a recent New York City gallery show.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/06/07/just-one-look-thakoon-2/', 'snippet': 'Thakoon Panichugal talked about two things at his resort presentation: cake frosting and a recent New York City gallery show.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The designer Thakoon Panichugal talked about two things at his resort presentation: cake frosting and the recent Fred Wilson show at the Pace Gallery in New York. What he showed were pretty jacquard dresses with acidic pastel stripes, a number of leather and silk mash-ups, a series of longer, more fluid floral pieces (jumpsuit included) and this look: a Caribbean blue dress with an elastic waist, deep V and sporty, perforated sleeves. (In black it was quite edgy and architectural.)', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Just One Look | Thakoon', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-06-07T16:04:46+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jane Herman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jane', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Herman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/954