e': {'original': 'By John Martin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'John', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Martin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a190d283-b8e5-5e74-b9f2-8cc2bf997316', 'word_count': 189, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a190d283-b8e5-5e74-b9f2-8cc2bf997316'}, {'abstract': 'Eight weeks after the annual South by Southwest Interactive conference, Austin again became a hub for creative technologists. The CHI 2012 conference is an annual gathering for researchers who focus on human-computer interaction (HCI). I spoke to CHI General Conference Chair Joseph Konstan to get his take on where the field of HCI is headed and how it applies to media and journalism.', 'web_url': 'https://open.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/06/07/chi-2012-conference-qa-with-joseph-konstan/', 'snippet': 'Eight weeks after the annual South by Southwest Interactive conference, Austin again became a hub for creative technologists. The CHI 2012 conference is an annual gathering for researchers who focus on human-computer interaction (HCI). I spoke to ...', 'lead_paragraph': 'Eight weeks after the annual South by Southwest Interactive conference, Austin again became a hub for creative technologists. The CHI 2012 conference is an annual gathering for researchers who focus on human-computer interaction (HCI). CHI 2012 is produced by ACM, the Association for Computing Machinery, a professional society for computing researchers. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'CHI 2012 Conference Q&A With Joseph Konstan', 'kicker': 'Open', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-06-07T17:54:10+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Blogs', 'byline': {'original': 'By BRAD STENGER', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Brad', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'STENGER', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4286ec58-defb-589a-9a3d-29d81caa440d', 'word_count': 1552, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4286ec58-defb-589a-9a3d-29d81caa440d'}, {'abstract': '13P is a group of playwrights who were frustrated by the long developmental process for new plays at New York City theaters.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/06/07/playwrights-collective-to-disband-after-production-of-ruhl-work/', 'snippet': '13P is a group of playwrights who were frustrated by the long developmental process for new plays at New York City theaters.', 'lead_paragraph': '13P, a New York City collective of 13 writers who teamed to produce one play by each member, will mount its final show in July before disbanding after nearly nine years, the organization announced on Thursday. The last production will be a chamber music adaptation by the playwright Sarah Ruhl and the composer Todd Almond of Ms. Ruhl’s 2001 farce, “Melancholy Play,” which will be performed July 18-27 at the Invisible Dog Art Center in Brooklyn. The director will be Davis McCallum (“February House”), and the actors will be David Greenspan, Erik Lochtefeld, April Matthis, Sarah Tolan-Mee, and Amy Warren.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Playwrights’ Collective to Disband After Production of Ruhl Work', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Playwrights’ Collective Prepares to Disband', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Writing and Writers', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Almond, Todd', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Callaghan, Sheila', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'George, Madeleine', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Greenspan, David', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lee, Young Jean', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'McCallum, Davis', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ruhl, Sarah', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Schmoll, Ken Rus', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Washburn, Anne', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': '13P Co', 'rank': 12, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Brooklyn (NYC)', 'rank': 13, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 14, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-07T17:58:21+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Patrick Healy', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Patrick', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Healy', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b955f16d-236d-5e54-bb3c-c995a931a70a', 'word_count': 371, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b955f16d-236d-5e54-bb3c-c995a931a70a'}, {'abstract': 'The designer’s new resort collection has an urban spin, with strong emphasis on long and lean silhouettes.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/06/07/just-one-look-donna-karan-3/', 'snippet': 'The designer’s new resort collection has an urban spin, with strong emphasis on long and lean silhouettes.', 'lead_paragraph': '“Inspired by the elements, designed for the city,” Donna Karan is dressing women for parties with resort. At the presentation earlier this week, formal, celebration-ready cocktail dresses dominated the collection’s 43 looks, many of which came decorated with large pieces of quartz, crystal or jet (as pins on jackets and swinging as earrings). Here, a slimmer silhouette (long and lean is in this season) offers a new kind of tuxedo dressing.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Just One Look | Donna Karan', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-06-07T18:00:53+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jane Herman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jane', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Herman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/390584be-2ace-5470-9b3f-82156fe8e2fe', 'word_count': 74, 'uri': 'nyt://article/390584be-2ace-5470-9b3f-82156fe8e2fe'}, {'abstract': 'If DNA sequencing a fetus becomes as routine as a blood test, what will that mean?', 'web_url': 'https://parenting.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/06/07/would-you-want-to-read-your-fetuss-dna/', 'snippet': 'If DNA sequencing a fetus becomes as routine as a blood test, what will that mean?', 'lead_paragraph': 'It’s now possible, although still financially impractical, to sequence the DNA of a baby from nothing but a blood sample from the pregnant woman and a saliva sample from the father. The New York Times reporter Andrew Pollack reported that genome scientists from the University of Washington say they “took advantage of new high-speed DNA sequencing and some statistical and computational acrobatics to deduce the DNA sequence of the fetus with about 98 percent accuracy.” For now, it’s expensive ($20,000 to $50,000) and still inaccurate, but: ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Would You Want To Read Your Fetus’s DNA?', 'kicker': 'Motherlode', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'AMNIOCENTESIS', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Children and Childhood', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}