ainst Luka Rocco Magnotta, who is accused of killing Jun Lin, a Chinese student, before dismembering his body and mailing parts to ...', 'lead_paragraph': 'OTTAWA — The Internet — and particularly, Facebook — is playing a critical role as the police in Montreal build their case against Luka Rocco Magnotta, who is accused of killing Jun Lin, a Chinese student, and then dismembering his body and mailing parts to the offices of political parties here.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Multiple Facebook Accounts for Suspect in Canadian Killing Case', 'kicker': 'Bits', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computers and the Internet', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Police', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lin, Jun', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Magnotta, Luka Rocco', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Facebook Inc', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-07T19:24:09+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ian Austen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ian', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Austen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/99662b8b-1e5b-5d52-ae39-d81e6ccf9270', 'word_count': 443, 'uri': 'nyt://article/99662b8b-1e5b-5d52-ae39-d81e6ccf9270'}, {'abstract': 'Supporters gathered Wednesday night to raise funds to bring table tennis to more middle and high schools in the city.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/schoolbook/2012/06/07/bringing-table-tennis-to-the-schools/', 'snippet': 'Supporters gathered Wednesday night to raise funds to bring table tennis to more middle and high schools in the city.', 'lead_paragraph': '', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Bringing Table Tennis to the Schools', 'kicker': 'SchoolBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-06-07T19:40:03+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Education', 'byline': {'original': 'By THEODORIC MEYER', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Theodoric', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'MEYER', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/54d7c9b2-4469-546f-973f-1e46199cf28b', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://article/54d7c9b2-4469-546f-973f-1e46199cf28b'}, {'abstract': 'Yes, it’s true. The engaging, witty author is our new Ethicist.', 'web_url': 'https://6thfloor.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/06/07/meet-the-new-ethicist-chuck-klosterman/', 'snippet': 'Yes, it’s true. The engaging, witty author is our new Ethicist.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Following a murderer’s row of guest Ethicists — Betsey Stevenson, Andrew Light, Cheryl Strayed and the poet laureate Philip Levine — a new full-time Ethicist starts this Sunday. He is Chuck Klosterman, who may be familiar to you for any number of reasons. He wrote for our magazine in years past, including this memorable 2002 profile of Billy Joel, and more recently, he was instrumental in the start of ESPN’s Grantland, Bill Simmons’s excellent sports journalism Web site (the magazine profiled Simmons last year). Chuck’s eighth book, “I Wear the Black Hat,” will be published next year. Now living in Brooklyn, he is from Wyndmere, N.D. Like other longtime Klosterman fans, I first heard of him when his first book, “Fargo Rock City,” was published to immediate acclaim in 2001.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Meet the New Ethicist: Chuck Klosterman', 'kicker': 'The 6th Floor', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Ethics (Personal)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Klosterman, Chuck', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-07T19:41:27+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Hugo Lindgren', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Hugo', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Lindgren', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/038a2953-94bc-56bd-aa3b-b45fa92675bf', 'word_count': 340, 'uri': 'nyt://article/038a2953-94bc-56bd-aa3b-b45fa92675bf'}, {'abstract': 'A distant relative, cops around the corner and a kitty in the E.R.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/06/10/magazine/halfhearted-half-brother.html', 'snippet': 'A distant relative, cops around the corner and a kitty in the E.R.', 'lead_paragraph': 'My father recently died. His daughter from his first marriage (which ended several years before his marriage to my mother) has made efforts to stay in touch with me since his death. I have never had a direct relationship with my half sister. She has had a difficult life, missing out on many of the advantages my sister and I enjoyed. I want to be sympathetic to her struggles, but though I’ve tried, I’ve never felt an attachment to her. She has been an intermittent presence and not always a pleasant one. Is it ethical to decide not to carry on this relationship? Or does someone else’s desire for connection, which perhaps comes out of a strong wish to be part of a family, outweigh my personal preference? J.G., NEW JERSEY', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/10/magazine/10ethicist/10ethicist-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 432, 'width': 432, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop