ter can do his best only with what lies within the range and character of his deepest sympathies.” By that measure, and any other, Richard Ford is doing his very best in his extraordinary new novel, “Canada,” his first book since “The Lay of the Land” six years ago. Here, Ford is clearly writing within the range and character of his deepest sympathies — in this case, from the point of view of an abandoned 15-year-old boy — and he’s doing it with a level of linguistic mastery that is rivaled by few, if any, in American letters today.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/10/books/review/10DUBUS/10DUBUS-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 780, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/10/books/review/10DUBUS/10DUBUS-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1560, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/10/books/review/10DUBUS/10DUBUS-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/06/10/books/review/10DUBUS/10DUBUS-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/10/books/review/10DUBUS/10DUBUS-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Points North', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Points North', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ford, Richard', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-07T22:57:51+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andre Dubus Iii', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andre', 'middlename': 'Dubus', 'lastname': 'Iii', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/3eefab4e-2fcf-523a-9ea7-2ad276642768', 'word_count': 1172, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3eefab4e-2fcf-523a-9ea7-2ad276642768'}, {'abstract': 'Applicants at a job fair in Brooklyn had different backgrounds, but they repeatedly spoke of the difficulty in finding work.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/06/07/at-another-job-fair-hope-this-will-be-the-one/', 'snippet': 'Applicants at a job fair in Brooklyn had different backgrounds, but they repeatedly spoke of the difficulty in finding work.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Linda Baker walked from employer to employer at a job fair in Brooklyn on Thursday, inquiring about opportunities. Ever since being laid off from her construction job three years ago, Ms. Baker, who is in her 40s, has been constantly searching for some kind of work. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'At Another Job Fair, Hope This Will Be the One', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Labor and Jobs', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Unemployment', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Adams, Eric', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Crown Heights (NYC)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-07T23:00:13+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Rhea Chedid', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Rhea', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Chedid', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5c787c85-ad50-529f-ba1b-3830ca4be6d4', 'word_count': 575, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5c787c85-ad50-529f-ba1b-3830ca4be6d4'}, {'abstract': 'The celebrity trainer Tracy Anderson has studios in TriBeCa and Water Mill, on Long Island.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/06/08/arts/dance/a-tracy-anderson-workout-in-new-york.html', 'snippet': 'The celebrity trainer Tracy Anderson has studios in TriBeCa and Water Mill, on Long Island.', 'lead_paragraph': 'PEOPLE have been talking about the supposed body-transforming powers of Tracy Anderson for several years. She’s the trainer who has traveled with Madonna, established her roots in Los Angeles and runs a studio there that has become a mainstay for stars like Jennifer Lopez, Gwyneth Paltrow and Courteney Cox. Word spread so quickly about her workouts that Ms. Anderson herself became a celebrity.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '31', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/08/arts/08URBAN_SPAN/08URBAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 330, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/06/08/arts/08URBAN_SPAN/08URBAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 330}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/08/arts/08URBAN_SPAN/08URBAN-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/08/arts/08URBAN_SPAN/08URBAN-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name'