e-8-2012/', 'snippet': 'Can you choose the word pair that best completes a passage?', 'lead_paragraph': 'This question was written by Shannon Doyne. It comes from the Op-Ed “A Maestro Sets the Tone.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Test Yourself | English, June 8, 2012', 'kicker': 'The Learning Network', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-06-08T08:23:40+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Education', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Learning Network', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Learning Network'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ac8954a4-2df4-542d-aa6d-a7bf3f591719', 'word_count': 44, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ac8954a4-2df4-542d-aa6d-a7bf3f591719'}, {'abstract': 'As the Cave Singers discovered before their set, when a drummer starts steadily tapping his kit to make sure it’s coming through, a good part of the audience might start clapping along.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/06/08/bonnaroo-getting-in-on-the-act/', 'snippet': 'As the Cave Singers discovered before their set, when a drummer starts steadily tapping his kit to make sure it’s coming through, a good part of the audience might start clapping along.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Bonnaroo’s participatory spirit runneth over. During the brief and efficient equipment changes between acts, on stages without curtains, band members often arrive to plug in, play and tweak their instruments, greeted, often, by roars of applause. And as the Cave Singers discovered before their set of bluesy, growly roots-rock, when a drummer starts steadily tapping his kit to make sure it’s coming through, a good part of the audience might start clapping along.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Bonnaroo: Getting in on the Act', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Bonnaroo Festival (Manchester, Tenn)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-08T08:29:39+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jon Pareles', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jon', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Pareles', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/67d60092-e1ee-5e08-9999-e0447ae61b68', 'word_count': 167, 'uri': 'nyt://article/67d60092-e1ee-5e08-9999-e0447ae61b68'}, {'abstract': 'Confronting blood, body parts and shelled buildings, a United Nations team quietly collected evidence on Friday of a mass atrocity in a desolate hamlet.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/06/09/world/middleeast/syrians-bar-un-monitors-from-a-massacre-inquiry.html', 'snippet': 'Confronting blood, body parts and shelled buildings, a United Nations team quietly collected evidence on Friday of a mass atrocity in a desolate hamlet.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Confronting a scene of congealed blood, scattered body parts, shelled buildings, bullet holes and the smell of burned flesh, United Nations monitors in Syria quietly collected evidence on Friday of a mass atrocity in a desolate hamlet, more than 24 hours after Syrian forces and government supporters blocked their first attempt to visit the site.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/09/world/09syria_span/09syria_span-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 330, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/06/09/world/09syria_span/09syria_span-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 330}}, {'