me': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/df573ed7-bb33-559e-8a6f-c424d060a52b', 'word_count': 134, 'uri': 'nyt://article/df573ed7-bb33-559e-8a6f-c424d060a52b'}, {'abstract': 'Andre Dubus III on writing as a “sustained act of empathy.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/06/10/books/review/up-front.html', 'snippet': 'Andre Dubus III on writing as a “sustained act of empathy.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'Near the end of “Townie,” the flinty-eyed memoir he published last year, Andre Dubus III acknowledged that it wasn’t always easy sharing a name with his father, the renowned story writer Andre Dubus. “I was treated with a vaguely disguised pity,” he writes: “How hard it must be to follow the footsteps of a real master, a writer’s writer, to share his name and probably not his gifts.” Yet the younger Dubus, who reviews Richard Ford’s novel “Canada” on this week’s cover, has been writing for more than a quarter of a century now, long enough to establish his own formidable gifts. In five books — including “Townie” and the novel “House of Sand and Fog,” both of them best sellers — Dubus has more than made a name for himself.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/10/books/review/0610-bks-UPFRONT-sub/0610-bks-UPFRONT-sub-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 596, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/10/books/review/0610-bks-UPFRONT-sub/0610-bks-UPFRONT-sub-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1192, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Up Front', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_