[{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Gifts', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Grilling (Cooking)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Weddings and Engagements', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cooking and Cookbooks', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-08T15:10:43+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Society', 'section_name': 'Fashion & Style', 'subsection_name': 'Weddings', 'byline': {'original': 'By Marianne Rohrlich', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Marianne', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rohrlich', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/1f3d8afa-1476-5b25-b1df-6e6251bbbc74', 'word_count': 387, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1f3d8afa-1476-5b25-b1df-6e6251bbbc74'}, {'abstract': 'Li Wangyang still had a fighting spirit and was too feeble to hang himself, friends and family members say.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/06/09/world/asia/chinese-activist-li-wangyang-death-called-suicide-but-supporters-are-suspicious.html', 'snippet': 'Li Wangyang still had a fighting spirit and was too feeble to hang himself, friends and family members say.', 'lead_paragraph': 'BEIJING — Li Wangyang, one of China’s longest-serving political prisoners, was not the kind of man to go down without a fight. After serving 11 years in prison for organizing workers during the pro-democracy protests of 1989, he gained his freedom and promptly went on a hunger strike. His goal was to shame the government into providing restitution and the medical care he required after successive beatings left him nearly blind and deaf.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/09/world/09SUICIDE1/09SUICIDE1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 676, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/09/world/09SUICIDE1/09SUICIDE1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1351, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Chinese Activist’s Death Called Suicide, but Supporters Are Suspicious', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Ruling in Activist’s Death Draws Suspicion in China', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Political Prisoners', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Suicides and Suicide Attempts', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Human Rights and Human Rights Violations', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Li Wangyang', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Prisons and Prisoners', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'China', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-08T15:17:36+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andrew Jacobs', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andrew', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Jacobs', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/51a40a8a-9f34-5782-911d-a552934cc954', 'word_count': 978, 'uri': 'nyt://article/51a40a8a-9f34-5782-911d-a552934cc954'}, {'abstract': 'Paperback books of particular interest.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/06/10/books/review/paperback-row.html', 'snippet': 'Paperback books of particular interest.', 'lead_paragraph': 'BLUE NIGHTS,  by Joan Didion. (Vintage International, $15.) After her husband, John Gregory Dunne, died suddenly of a heart attack in 2003, Didion made out of her bereavement a remarkable memoir, “The Year of Magical Thinking.” “Blue Nights,” a luminous companion volume, traces the author’s efforts to come to terms with a second calamity: the death of her daughter, Quintana Roo, in 2005 at age 39. Moving between recent painful memories and bright family snapshots, Didion’s elegy also becomes a meditation on the passage of time.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '32', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Paperback Row', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-08T15:20:40+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ihsan Taylor', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ihsan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Taylor', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/70cc16fe-8d70-5b2f-b78e-880bc6092fb4', 'word_count': 489, 'uri': 'nyt://article/70cc16fe-8d70-5b2f-b78e-880bc6092fb4'}, {'abstract': 'It’s that time of year again: Martha Rose Shulman fires up the blender to create a new crop of delicious fruit smoothies.', 'web_url': 'https://well.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/06/08/its-smoothie-time/', 'snippet': 'It’s that time of year again: Martha Rose Shulman fires up the blender to create a new crop of delicious fruit smoothies.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Every year we can count on Martha Rose Shulman to blend up a new batch of delicious, healthful smoothies for the summer. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'It’s Smoothie Time!', 'kicker': 'Well', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Diet and Nutrition', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Recipes', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-08T15:24:43+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Science', 'section_name': 'Health', 'byline': {'original': 'By Tara Parker-Pope', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Tara', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Parker-Pope', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d1d34f53-00de-5519-b7da-f1ed48893ca2', 'word_count': 360, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d1d34f53-00de-5519-b7da-f1ed48893ca2'}, {'abstract': 'The Recording Academy trustees restore the award after complaints from Latin jazz musicians.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/06/08/grammys-reinstate-latin-jazz-award/', 'snippet': 'The Recording Academy trustees restore the award after complaints from Latin jazz musicians.', 'lead_paragraph': '6:41 p.m. | Updated A year after the trustees who oversee the Grammy Awards cut 31 categories and drew fierce protests from Latin jazz musicians and others, they have voted to reinstate the award for best Latin jazz album.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Grammys Reinstate Latin Jazz Award', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Grammys Restore Latin Jazz Category', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Grammy Awards', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Portnow, Neil', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Sanabria, Bobby', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Mexico', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-08T15:25:34+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By James C. McKinley Jr', 'person': [{'firstname': 'James', 'middlename': 'C.', 'lastname': 'McKinley', 'qualifier': 'Jr', 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9649aee7-fc48-5bbf-b47a-33e57c197dc7', 'word_count': 572, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9649aee7-fc48-5bbf-b47a-33e57c197dc7'}, {'abstract': 'Recently reviewed books of particular interest.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/06/10/books/review/editors-choice.html', 'snippet': 'Recently reviewed books of particular interest.', 'lead_paragraph': 'FEAR OF MUSIC,  by Jonathan Lethem. (Continuum, paper, $12.95.) The novelist’s obsessive, passionate and personal monograph on Talking Heads’ album of the same name evokes the New York music scene in 1979 — as well as the teenage Lethem.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '30', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Editors’ Choice', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': "Editor's Choice | Recent Books Of Particular Interest", 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-08T15:27:16+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'List', '_id': 'nyt://article/966c3cd7-c6c2-566a-9f5e-2d5e7b6dbb9f', 'word_count': 293, 'uri': 'nyt://article/966c3cd7-c6c2-566a-9f5e-2d5e7b6dbb9f'}, {'abstract': 'The vegetal flavor of protein-rich quinoa makes it a natural for hearty salads with great staying power.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/06/11/health/nutrition/quinoa-and-asparagus-salad.html', 'snippet': 'The vegetal flavor of protein-rich quinoa makes it a natural for hearty salads with great staying power.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I’m always tweaking the way I cook quinoa, and this week I changed my method for basic quinoa. I’d been cooking it sort of like pasta, in 3 parts water, then draining it and letting it dry out in a towel-covered pan. Ordinarily, this is a good way to obtain very fluffy grains, and you get a lot of volume too. But lately I’ve been finding that my quinoa is soggy – more waterlogged than fluffy – when I cook it this way, even after it rests under the towel. Are the grains different? I don’t know. Whatever the reason, I decided to change the grain-to-water ratio and followed the directions on my Alter Eco quinoa packages (Alter Eco is a company dedicated to fair-trade foods, and they import red, rainbow and pearl quinoas). I cooked the pearl and the red quinoas in 1 1/2 parts water and the rainbow in 2 parts water. The black quinoa in the rainbow mix takes a little longer to soften and requires a little more liquid to be properly hydrated. The grains were tighter and less moist than quinoa cooked in abundant water, and the yield was not as great because the grains don’t swell as much. But I liked the results, especially for salads, which were the destination for my quinoa experiments.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/11/health/11recipehealth/11recipehealth-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 447, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/06/11/health/11recipehealth/11recipehealth-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 447}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/11/health/11recipehealth/11recipehealth-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 763, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/11/health/11recipehealth/11recipehealth-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 763, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/11/health/11recipehealth/11recipehealth-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/06/11/health/11recipehealth/11recipehealth-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/11/health/11recipehealth/11recipehealth-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Quinoa Salads With Spring Vegetables', 'kicker': 'Recipes for Health', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Medicine and Health', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Recipes', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Diet and Nutrition', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Quinoa', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-08T15:29:49+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Science', 'section_name': 'Health', 'subsection_name': 'Fitness & Nutrition', 'byline': {'original': 'By Martha Rose Shulman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Martha', 'middlename': 'Rose', 'lastname': 'Shulman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'recipe', '_id': 'nyt://article/795db6e4-658c-5fdd-9049-932b155676d7', 'word_count': 693, 'uri': 'nyt://article/795db6e4-658c-5fdd-9049-932b155676d7'}, {'abstract': 'My thesis is that the big banks like to use the $20 million measurement so they can stick bigger, safer loans into their numbers and still look like they’re helping small business.', 'web_url': 'https://boss.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/06/08/how-big-can-a-small-business-be-and-why-it-matters/', 'snippet': 'My thesis is that the big banks like to use the $20 million measurement so they can stick bigger, safer loans into their numbers and still look like they’re helping small business.', 'lead_paragraph': 'There is an age-old debate about what constitutes a small business. Most likely, we think about the question differently depending upon our vantage point in life. I’ve met corporate folks before who think a business with 500 employees or less is small. And I’ve met self-employed massage therapists who think they’re too “small” to be considered a small business.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'How Big Can a Small Business Be? (And Why It Matters)', 'kicker': 'You&#039;re the Boss', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-06-08T15:37:41+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Entrepreneurship', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ami Kassar', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ami', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kassar', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ce279db4-a635-54ae-bb8e-be00f2112d92', 'word_count': 933, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ce279db4-a635-54ae-bb8e-be00f2112d92'}, {'abstract': 'President Hu Jintao of China and President Hamid Karzai of Afghanistan announced a new strategic partnership, a sign of China’s interest in Afghanistan after NATO forces leave in 2014.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/06/09/world/asia/china-signals-interest-in-afghanistan-after-nato-leaves.html', 'snippet': 'President Hu Jintao of China and President Hamid Karzai of Afghanistan announced a new strategic partnership, a sign of China’s interest in Afghanistan after NATO forces leave in 2014.', 'lead_paragraph': 'BEIJING — In a sign of China’s growing interest in neighboring Afghanistan after the departure of the United States and NATO-led forces, President Hu Jintao met the Afghan president, Hamid Karzai, in the Great Hall of the People on Friday and announced a new strategic partnership between the two countries.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/09/world/09CHINA/09CHINA-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 664, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/09/world/09CHINA/09CHINA-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1328, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/09/world/09CHINA/09CHINA-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/06/09/world/09CHINA/09CHINA-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/09/world/09CHINA/09CHINA-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'China Shows Interest in Afghan Security, Fearing Taliban Would Help Separatists', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'China Shows Interest in Afghan Security, Fearing Taliban Would Help Separatists', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Espionage and Intelligence Services', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'International Relations', 'rank': 2, 'major': '