tps://thecaucus.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/06/08/in-remarks-on-the-economy-obama-calls-private-sector-hiring-fine/', 'snippet': 'The president urged Congress to “take another look” at his job-growth proposals and warned that Europe’s problems were producing “headwinds” that make action at home critical.', 'lead_paragraph': 'President Obama on Friday urged Congress to “take another look” at his proposals to spur job growth and warned that economic problems in Europe were producing “headwinds” that made Congressional action even more critical for the United States.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Obama Prods Congress, Citing Euro Crisis', 'kicker': 'The Caucus', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-06-08T15:41:00+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael D. Shear', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/61064154-c9cd-5b8c-aaff-3114f67002e5', 'word_count': 677, 'uri': 'nyt://article/61064154-c9cd-5b8c-aaff-3114f67002e5'}, {'abstract': 'Jeff Shaara, whose Civil War novel “Blaze of Glory” hits the hardcover fiction list at No. 7, follows a friend’s advice: “If you expect me to read your books, you’ve gotta hook me in Chapter 1.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/06/17/books/review/inside-the-list.html', 'snippet': 'Jeff Shaara, whose Civil War novel “Blaze of Glory” hits the hardcover fiction list at No. 7, follows a friend’s advice: “If you expect me to read your books, you’ve gotta hook me in Chapter 1.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'MAN’S WORLD: The cutoff for this week’s list was June 2 — too early, you’d think, to reflect any kind of Father’s Day sales bump. Yet with subjects including the Civil War, international espionage, Navy SEALs and the most trusted man in America, the list’s new titles throw off a decidedly masculine funk. The Civil War book is Jeff Shaara’s “Blaze of Glory,” a novel about the Battle of Shiloh that hits the hardcover fiction list at No. 7 and contains one character as notable to literary historians as military ones: the Union general Lew Wallace. Blamed for the battle’s heavy casualties after he was late with reinforcements, Wallace went on to serve as governor of the New Mexico Territory before becoming, like Shaara, a wildly popular historical novelist. (His “Ben-Hur,” published in 1880, was the best-selling American novel of the 19th century, and has never gone out of print.) Shaara, a former coin dealer who started writing after the death of his novelist father, told a Florida newspaper recently that he tries to grab his audiences right away: “A very good friend of mine who is not especially a reader said to me, ‘If you expect me to read your books, you’ve gotta hook me in Chapter 1.’ ”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '24', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/17/books/review/17list-img/17list-img-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 221, 'width': 229, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/17/books/review/17list-img/17list-img-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 221, 'width': 229, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/17/books/review/17list-img/17list-img-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/06/17/books/review/17list-img/17list-img-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/17/books/review/17list-img/17list-img-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Inside the List', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'TBR: Inside the List', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-08T15:41:24+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Gregory Cowles', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Gregory', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cowles', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2bebdce9-bd4d-57ab-880b-7d6f5d261c4d', 'word_count': 623, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2bebdce9-bd4d-57ab-880b-7d6f5d261c4d'}, {'abstract': 'Steve Coll examines the worldwide operations and political influence of Exxon Mobil.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/06/10/books/review/private-empire-steve-colls-book-about-exxon-mobil.html', 'snippet': 'Steve Coll examines the worldwide operations and political influence of Exxon Mobil.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In the great reach for colonies that Europe began hundreds of years ago, at center stage were several curious entities that wielded more power than many governments. The United Company of Merchants of England Trading to the East Indies (commonly known as the East India Company) and its counterpart, the Dutch East India Company, fielded their own warships and armies, coined money and ruled territory eventually taken over by Britain and the Netherlands. Later, the seizure of land by Cecil Rhodes’s British South Africa Company also preceded the spread of British pink across the map.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '20', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/10/books/review/10HOTHSCHILD/10HOTHSCHILD-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 322, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/06/10/books/review/10HOTHSCHILD/10HOTHSCHILD-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 322}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/10/books/review/10HOTHSCHILD/10HOTHSCHILD-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 550, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/10/books/review/10HOTHSCHILD/10HOTHSCHILD-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1099, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/10/books/review/10HOTHSCHILD/10HOTHSCHILD-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/06/10/books/review/10HOTHSCHILD/10HOTHSCHILD-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/10/books/review/10HOTHSCHILD/10HOTHSCHILD-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Well-Oiled Machine', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Well-Oiled Machine', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Oil (Petroleum) and Gasoline', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Exxon Mobil Corporation', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Coll, Steve', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-08T15:41:55+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Adam Hochschild', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Adam', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hochschild', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/d99cf995-3e31-5179-8d5b-37e11415511a', 'word_count': 1194, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d99cf995-3e31-5179-8d5b-37e11415511a'}, {'abstract': 'In a world ever pressed for time, good novels still obey their own laws of “narrative physics.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/06/10/books/review/the-narrative-physics-of-novels.html', 'snippet': 'In a world ever pressed for time, good novels still obey their own laws of “narrative physics.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'I share my name with an aerobatic bird that can whiz across a whole summer sky in seconds. A swift is so equipped for speed that it can scarcely cope with being stationary. I once came across an unlucky young specimen that had somehow grounded itself on a lawn and, with its minuscule legs and long encumbering wings, couldn’t regain the air. I lifted it on the perch of my finger and it was gone in a flash.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '35', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/10/books/review/10-swift-img/10-swift-img-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 372, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/06/10/books/review/10-swift-img/10-swift-img-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheig