count': 915, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cd34d35c-0691-5e5d-a608-30186f9c197b'}, {'abstract': 'Food-related events around New York', 'web_url': 'https://dinersjournal.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/06/08/weekend-fare-96/', 'snippet': 'Food-related events around New York', 'lead_paragraph': 'Free cupcakes, one per person in any flavor, will be handed out between 10 a.m. to 9 p.m. Friday at Georgetown Cupcake SoHo, 111 Mercer Street (between Prince and Spring Streets), for an upcoming episode of “DC Cupcakes,” a reality show that follows the two sisters behind this popular bakery.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Weekend Fare', 'kicker': 'Diner&#039;s Journal', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-06-08T15:44:15+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Bao Ong', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Bao', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ong', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b9c5f7e0-c56e-54bb-9263-731436bb6282', 'word_count': 868, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b9c5f7e0-c56e-54bb-9263-731436bb6282'}, {'abstract': 'A glowing celestial pinwheel spinning in the night sky over the Middle East on Thursday night may have been from a Russian ballistic missile test.', 'web_url': 'https://thelede.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/06/08/mysterious-light-seen-over-middle-east-coincides-with-russian-missile-launch/', 'snippet': 'A glowing celestial pinwheel spinning in the night sky over the Middle East on Thursday night may have been from a Russian ballistic missile test.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A glowing celestial pinwheel spun in the night sky over the Middle East on Thursday night, grabbing attention across the region and fueling speculation about the possible origin of the unexplained phenomenon. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Mysterious Light Seen Over Middle East Coincides With Russian Missile Launch', 'kicker': 'The Lede', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-06-08T15:47:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Blogs', 'byline': {'original': 'By J. David Goodman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'J.', 'middlename': 'David', 'lastname': 'Goodman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a499df58-d765-5d7e-b632-faba4d17a00e', 'word_count': 296, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a499df58-d765-5d7e-b632-faba4d17a00e'}, {'abstract': 'An account of the murder of a young British woman in Japan.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/06/10/books/review/people-who-eat-darkness-by-richard-lloyd-parry.html', 'snippet': 'An account of the murder of a young British woman in Japan.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I opened this book as a skeptic. I am not a lover of true crime, and as a parent I recoiled from the lurid tale of a 21-year-old English bar hostess in Tokyo who was drugged and dismembered by a serial sex offender. But Richard Lloyd Parry’s remarkable examination of that crime, what it revealed about Japanese society and how it unsettled conventional notions of bereavement, elevates his book far above the genre. “People Who Eat Darkness” is a searing exploration of evil and trauma, and how both ultimately elude understanding or resolution.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/10/books/review/10CHIRA2/10CHIRA2-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1015, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/10/books/review/10CHIRA2/10CHIRA2-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1580, 'width': 1594, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/10/books/review/10CHIRA2/10CHIRA2-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/06/10/books/review/10CHIRA2/10CHIRA2-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/10/books/review/10CHIRA2/10CHIRA2-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Trail of Shadows', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Trail of Shadows', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Parry, Richard Lloyd', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-08T15:47:36+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Susan Chira', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Susan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Chira', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/4202f85a-9991-5e23-a5cf-0b2ecca46d5d', 'word_count': 849, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4202f85a-9991-5e23-a5cf-0b2ecca46d5d'}, {'abstract': 'A historian’s study of the dramatist with a genius for the concise phrase and the provocative gesture.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/06/10/books/review/a-difficult-woman-by-alice-kessler-harris.html', 'snippet': 'A historian’s study of the dramatist with a genius for the concise phrase and the provocative gesture.', 'lead_paragraph': 'During the great performance that was her life, Lillian Hellman always addressed the 20th century from center stage. While she defended many causes — justice, loyalty, American civil liberties, anti-fascism and Soviet-style Communism among them — what she represented most staunchly was herself, and what she believed in most fiercely was her own unassailability.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '16', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/10/books/review/10RIFKIND/10RIFKIND-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 982, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/10/books/review/10RIFKIND/10RIFKIND-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1964, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/10/books/review/10RIFKIND/10RIFKIND-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/06/10/books/review/10RIFKIND/10RIFKIND-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/10/books/review/10RIFKIND/10RIFKIND-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Becoming a Legend', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Becoming a Legend', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kessler-Harris, Alice', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hellman, Lillian', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-08T15:47:44+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Donna Rifkind', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Donna', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rifkind', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/8082c5ba-2f52-5289-88d4-c469edec1375', 'word_count': 1269, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8082c5ba-2f52-5289-88d4-c469edec1375'}, {'abstract': 'A professor deplores the current state of colleges.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/06/10/books/review/college-by-andrew-delbanco.html', 'snippet': 'A professor deplores the current state of colleges.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Andrew Delbanco must be a great teacher. A longtime faculty member at Columbia, he is devoted to the development of his students as individuals, and recognizes that their time in college should be formative: “They may still be deterred from sheer self-interest toward a life of enlarged sympathy and civic responsibility.” Like most professors devoted to teaching, he has no interest in telling undergraduates what to think, but he does want to draw them toward a sense of skepticism about the status quo and to a feeling of wonder about the natural world. College, he tells us, is a time to learn to “make connections among seemingly disparate phenomena,” to see things from another’s point of view and to develop a sense of ethical responsibility. At a time when many are trying to reduce the college years to a training period for economic competition, Delbanco reminds readers of the ideal of democratic education.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '17', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Light, Truth and Whatever', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Light, Truth and Whatever', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Delbanco, Andrew', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Colleges and Universities', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-08T15:47:51+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael S. Roth', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': 'S.', 'lastname': 'Roth', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/3886f587-eae3-5a89-84d5-770e301049c7', 'word_count': 947, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3886f587-eae3-5a89-84d5-770e301049c7'}, {'abstract': 'Thomas McNamee traces the career of Craig Claiborne, the food critic who expanded the culinary horizons of American home cooks.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/06/10/books/review/the-man-who-changed-the-way-we-eat-by-thomas-mcnamee.html', 'snippet': 'Thomas McNamee traces the career of Craig Claiborne, the food critic who expanded the culinary horizons of American home cooks.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The richest, most influential restaurant owners lived in fear of a single critic’s word, a man whose enthusiastic home visit to an amateur’s kitchen might well inspire a lucrative career as a cookbook writer. Drawing on his limitless budget, he could roam the earth and report back on what he found.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/10/books/review/10KUMMER/10KUMMER-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/10/books/review/10KUMMER/10KUMMER-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/10/books/review/10KUMMER/10KUMMER-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/06/10/books/review/10KUMMER/10KUMMER-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/10/books/review/10KUMMER/10KUMMER-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'First Foodie', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'First Foodie', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'McNamee, Thomas', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Claiborne, Craig', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cooking and Cookbooks', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-08T15:47:58+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Corby Kummer', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Corby', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kummer', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/3ae432a2-b41f-535d-9a8c-6986abb65ef5', 'word_count': 1235, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3ae432a2-b41f-535d-9a8c-6986abb65ef5'}, {'abstract': 'Herta Müller’s novel of a Soviet labor camp.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/06/10/books/review/the-hunger-angel-by-herta-muller.html', 'snippet': 'Herta Müller’s novel of a Soviet labor camp.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Although the 58 years between the rise of Hitler and the fall of the Soviet Union have been as well documented as any comparable historical period, there are fewer literary accounts of their most evil components than might be expected. Of course most of those who suffered directly are dead or otherwise incapacitated, and few indirect sufferers have been sufficiently talented. We do have marvelous books by Primo Levi; “Fatelessness,” by Imre Kertesz (who dismissed the film “Schindler’s List” as kitsch); Solzhenitsyn’s “Gulag Archipelago”; and perhaps Wiesel’s “Night,” but it’s a small list. Now we can add “The Hunger Angel,” by the 2009 Nobel laureate Herta Müller.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '23', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/10/books/review/0610-bks-STERN/0610-bks-STERN-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 970, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/10/books/review/0610-bks-STERN/0610-bks-STERN-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1940, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/10/books/review/0610-bks-STERN/0610-bks-STERN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/06/10/books/review/0610-bks-STERN/0610-bks-STERN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/10/books/review/0610-bks-STERN/0610-bks-STERN-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'To the Bone', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'To the Bone', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Muller, Herta', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-08T15:48:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Richard Stern', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Richard', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Stern', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/4fd785ef-63c9-529c-afbb-c393d56e489c', 'word_count': 583, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4fd785ef-63c9-529c-afbb-c393d56e489c'}, {'abstract': 'In Laura Moriarty’s novel, a Midwestern matron accompanies young Louise Brooks to New York in the summer of 1922.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/06/10/books/review/the-chaperone-by-laura-moriarty.html', 'snippet': 'In Laura Moriarty’s novel, a Midwestern matron accompanies young Louise Brooks to New York in the summer of 1922.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Early on in “The Chaperone,” Laura Moriarty’s fourth novel, it becomes clear that Cora Carlisle is fighting a losing battle. Her charge is Louise Brooks, soon-to-be silent-film star and something of a force of nature. As the critic Kenneth Tynan wrote, Brooks was “a creature of impulse, a creator of impulses, a temptress with no pretensions, capable of dissolving into a giggling fit at a peak of erotic ecstasy, . . . with that sleek jet cloche of hair that rings such a peal of bells in my subconscious.” Moriarty’s Louise is only 15, a Jazz Age lost girl playing uncertainty as brashness, but impulse and temptation are already in bloom.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '24', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/10/books/review/0610-HENDRIX/0610-HENDRIX-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 727, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/10/books/review/0610-HENDRIX/0610-HENDRIX-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1455, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/10/books/review/0610-HENDRIX/0610-HENDRIX-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/06/10/books/review/0610-HENDRIX/0610-HENDRIX-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/10/books/review/0610-HENDRIX/0610-HENDRIX-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'City of Dreams', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'City of Dreams', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Moriarty, Laura', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-08T15:48:15+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jenny Hendrix', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jenny', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hendrix', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/04bd8092-baf5-50e9-81cd-179f0fc72330', 'word_count': 983, 'uri': 'nyt://article/04bd8092-baf5-50e9-81cd-179f0fc72330'}, {'abstract': 'Norman Manea explores the implications of exile in this novel about Romanian intellectuals living in the United States.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/06/10/books/review/the-lair-a-novel-by-norman-manea.html', 'snippet': 'Norman Manea explores the implications of exile in this novel about Romanian intellectuals living in the United States.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In “Against Simplification,” an essay posted on the Project Syndicate Web site last August, Norman Manea rightly pointed out that in North America literary translation is too rare a practice, arguing that “imported books are thought to be too ‘complicated,’ which is another way of saying that literature should deal with simple issues in a simple way, obeying the rules of the mass market, with its tricks of packaging, accessibility, advertisement and comfort.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/10/books/review/10HEIGHTON/10HEIGHTON-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 396, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/06/10/books/review/10HEIGHTON/10HEIGHTON-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 396}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/10/books/review/10HEIGHTON/10HEIGHTON-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 676, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/10/books/review/10HEIGHTON/10HEIGHTON-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1352, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/10/books/review/10HEIGHTON/10HEIGHTON-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/06/10/books/review/10HEIGHTON/10HEIGHTON-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/10/books/review/10HEIGHTON/10HEIGHTON-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Displaced', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Displaced', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Manea, Norman', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-08T15:48:22+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Steven Heighton', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Steven', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Heighton', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/f8d0a11b-6677-5cf4-9727-a9a661abae2c', 'word_count': 977, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f8d0a11b-6677-5cf4-9727-a9a661abae2c'}, {'abstract': 'This novel follows four generations of a British family, from World War I to the present.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/06/10/books/review/the-undertow-by-jo-baker.html', 'snippet': 'This novel follows four generations of a British family, from World War I to the present.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When it was published in Britain last year, Jo Baker’s fourth novel was called “The Picture Book.” The title made some sense — it referred to both a cherished postcard album and, presumably, the snapshot structure of the narrative — but Baker’s American publishers were wise to change it. “The Picture Book” suggests something sweet and nostalgic. In its best moments, this novel, Baker’s first to be published in the United States, is gripping and ruthless.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '25', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/10/books/review/10thomas-img/10thomas-img-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 802, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/10/books/review/10thomas-img/10thomas-img-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1104, 'width': 1410, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/10/books/review/10thomas-img/10thomas-img-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/06/10/books/review/10thomas-img/10thomas-img-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/10/books/review/10thomas-img/10thomas-img-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Winds of Fate', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Winds of Fate', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Baker, Jo (1973- )', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-08T15:48:29+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Louisa Thomas', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Louisa', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Thomas', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/7a0c2afa-6206-5cf1-b130-8e3d95bc67fe', 'word_count': 916, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7a0c2afa-6206-5cf1-b130-8e3d95bc67fe'}, {'abstract': 'The British narrator of Patrick McGuinness’s first novel is an uneasy witness to the collapse of the Ceausescu regime.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/06/10/books/review/the-last-hundred-days-by-patrick-mcguinness.html', 'snippet': 'The British narrator of Patrick McGuinness’s first novel is an uneasy witness to the collapse of the Ceausescu regime.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In his eloquent essay “The Great Christmas Killing,” the Hungarian novelist Peter Nadas describes his intense, ultimately chagrined response to a documentary about the execution of Nicolae and Elena Ceausescu. Neither his opposition to capital punishment nor his belief in the importance of “just, legal procedures” keeps Nadas from feeling a surge of vengeful delight as he witnesses the final hours of the Romanian dictator. “Ceausescu just kept looking at his wife, rolling his small, shifty eyes and grinning nervously; you could tell he couldn’t grasp what was really happening or figure out how he might gain the upper hand.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '13', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/10/books/review/10PROSE/10PROSE-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 982, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/10/books/review/10PROSE/10PROSE-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1964, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/10/books/review/10PROSE/10PROSE-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/06/10/books/review/10PROSE/10PROSE-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/10/books/review/10PROSE/10PROSE-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Uncertain Times', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Uncertain Times', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-08T15:48:38+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Francine Prose', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Francine', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Prose', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/baf1cfa8-a594-59ac-b6a8-92add7531b22', 'word_count': 1207, 'uri': 'nyt://article/baf1cfa8-a594-59ac-b6a8-92add7531b22'}, {'abstract': 'How presidential actions are scrutinized.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/06/10/books/review/power-and-constraint-by-jack-goldsmith.html', 'snippet': 'How presidential actions are scrutinized.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When the Qaeda leader Abu Zubaydah was captured, six months after the terrorist attacks of 9/11, C.I.A. lawyers were asked to approve a battery of tough interrogation techniques hastily thrown together. They did not much fear that posterity would think them ruthless. On the contrary, the C.I.A.’s No. 2 attorney worried that “with all the restrictions and precautions and scrutiny by lawyers and doctors and psychologists,” they’d look like a bunch of wimps. ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/10/books/review/10CALDWELL/10CALDWELL-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 789, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/10/books/review/10CALDWELL/10CALDWELL-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1578, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/10/books/review/10CALDWELL/10CALDWELL-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/06/10/books/review/10CALDWELL/10CALDWELL-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/10/books/review/10CALDWELL/10CALDWELL-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Vetted, Altered, Blessed', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Vetted, Altered, Blessed', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Goldsmith, Jack L', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Defense and Military Forces', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Constitution (US)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-08T15:48:47+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Christopher Caldwell', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Christopher', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Caldwell', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/32e37ff3-6714-5fbb-8ece-1d00f6d2461a', 'word_count': 1216, 'uri': 'nyt://article/32e37ff3-6714-5fbb-8ece-1d00f6d2461a'}, {'abstract': 'Alexander Masters writes about meeting Simon Norton, an eccentric British mathematician.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/06/10/books/review/simon-the-genius-in-my-basement-by-alexander-masters.html', 'snippet': 'Alexander Masters writes about meeting Simon Norton, an eccentric British mathematician.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When Simon Norton was 3 1/2, his I.Q. was measured at 178. For three years running in high school, he was among the top scorers in the world at the International Mathematical Olympiad. At the age of 27, he and a colleague, John Conway, formulated an audacious conjecture in group theory called “monstrous moonshine,” which inspired a frenzy of mathematical work around the globe that culminated in a Fields Medal-winning proof by Richard Borcherds almost two decades later.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '26', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'From Here to Infinity', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'From Here to Infinity', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Masters, Alexander', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-08T15:49:00+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jordan Ellenberg', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jordan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ellenberg', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/c4679823-8d29-5604-9de7-6d039d9dd5ce', 'word_count': 643, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c4679823-8d29-5604-9de7-6d039d9dd5ce'}, {'abstract': 'How much are parents shelling out to give their children a leg up in sports? A heck of a lot, Mark Hyman discovers.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/06/10/books/review/the-most-expensive-game-in-town-by-mark-hyman.html', 'snippet': 'How much are parents shelling out to give their children a leg up in sports? A heck of a lot, Mark Hyman discovers.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The mantra in America is that everyone needs a dream. So why not dream that your kid will be the one to land an athletic scholarship or sign a pro contract? Because — as Mark Hyman has documented in two important but troubling books — the lengths to which many of us go in pursuit of that vision can turn it into a nightmare.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '26', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Money Ball', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Money Ball', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Parenting', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hyman, Mark', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-08T15:49:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Gordon Marino', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Gordon', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Marino', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/ba1984fe-96e5-503e-86f8-5b37a9448e6f', 'word_count': 600, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ba1984fe-96e5-503e-86f8-5b37a9448e6f'}, {'abstract': 'For Gail Collins, Texas is the home of guns, deregulation and right-wing dogma.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/06/10/books/review/as-texas-goes-by-gail-collins.html', 'snippet': 'For Gail Collins, Texas is the home of guns, deregulation and right-wing dogma.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Texas — that vast and various landmass of nearly 26 million souls — boasts some of the most acclaimed public and private universities, a vibrant art and architecture scene, and world-class symphony orchestras and opera companies. It is one of the nation’s leaders in medical research; rightly celebrates its reputation for manned and unmanned space exploration; rejoices in its richly diverse and tasty cultural stew; and glories in its professional football teams.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/10/books/review/Grove/Grove-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 1013, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/10/books/review/Grove/Grove-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 2026, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/10/books/review/Grove/Grove-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/06/10/books/review/Grove/Grove-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/10/books/review/Grove/Grove-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Forget the Alamo', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Forget the Alamo', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Collins, Gail', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Texas', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-08T15:49:24+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Lloyd Grove', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Lloyd', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Grove', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/f30f2cd2-3851-51b2-a138-db967cffbe80', 'word_count': 1204, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f30f2cd2-3851-51b2-a138-db967cffbe80'}, {'abstract': 'I’ll Have Another has been scratched from the Belmont Stakes, ending his bid to become horse racing’s first Triple Crown winner since Affirmed in 1978.', 'web_url': 'https://therail.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/06/08/ill-have-another-scratched-from-belmont-ending-triple-crown-bid/', 'snippet': 'I’ll Have Another has been scratched from the Belmont Stakes, ending his bid to become horse racing’s first Triple Crown winner since Affirmed in 1978.', 'lead_paragraph': 'There was I’ll Have Another at one end of the leather tether, looking every bit the wonder horse, with his coat combed to a chestnut glow and his mane braided and glimmering. At the other was his trainer, Doug O’Neill, leading his colt out of Barn 2 at Belmont Park for a shockingly final bow before a battery of cameras on Friday.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Triple Crown Chase Ends Day Early, in Farewell', 'kicker': 'The Rail', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Triple Crown Chase Ends Day Early, in Farewell', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Belmont Stakes', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Horse Racing', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': "I'll Have Another (Race Horse)", 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Triple Crown (Horse Racing)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-08T15:49:31+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'byline': {'original': 'By Joe Drape', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Joe', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Drape', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6c8d21bd-f164-5e47-af1d-fc9abf72e7a7', 'word_count': 1162, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6c8d21bd-f164-5e47-af1d-fc9abf72e7a7'}, {'abstract': 'Tirunesh Dibaba, the defending women’s Olympic champion in the 5,000 and 10,000 meters, missed almost all of 2011 because of shin splints. Now, she is competing again, and will race at the Adidas Grand Prix in New York on Saturday.', 'web_url': 'https://london2012.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/06/08/the-return-of-tirunesh-dibaba/', 'snippet': 'Tirunesh Dibaba, the defending women’s Olympic champion in the 5,000 and 10,000 meters, missed almost all of 2011 because of shin splints. Now, she is competing again, and will race at the Adidas Grand Prix in New York on Saturday.', 'lead_paragraph': 'One of the most anticipated entrants at this year’s Adidas Grand Prix track meet in New York on Saturday is Tirunesh Dibaba of Ethiopia, the defending women’s Olympic champion at 5,000 and 10,000 meters and quite possibly the fastest finisher in the history of women’s distance running.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Return of Tirunesh Dibaba', 'kicker': '2012 London Olympics', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Olympic Games', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Track and Field', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Dibaba, Tirunesh', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-08T15:51:34+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Olympics', 'byline': {'original': 'By David Gendelman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gendelman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/639201b3-e7a6-5e0b-b11e-27257b9b22a8', 'word_count': 883, 'uri': 'nyt://article/639201b3-e7a6-5e0b-b11e-27257b9b22a8'}, {'abstract': 'Texas is considering a speed limit of 85 miles per hour for a new highway nearing completion.', 'web_url': 'https://wheels.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/06/08/wheelies-the-lone-star-speed-edition/', 'snippet': 'Texas is considering a speed limit of 85 miles per hour for a new highway nearing completion.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In which we bring you motoring news from around the Web:', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Wheelies: The Lone-Star Speed Edition', 'kicker': 'Wheels', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Automobile Racing', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Automobiles', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'McLaren Automotive', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Nascar Hall of Fame', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Assn of Stock Car Auto Racing', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Toyota Motor Corporation', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Texas', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-08T15:52:55+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Automobiles', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/1078c6d3-40fa-5f52-9e3a-f1412636309c', 'word_count': 353, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1078c6d3-40fa-5f52-9e3a-f1412636309c'}, {'abstract': 'They’re all the austerians have these days.', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/06/08/why-the-baltics-matter/', 'snippet': 'They’re all the austerians have these days.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Some readers have urged me not to spend time on Latvia, Estonia, etc., on the grounds that they are too small to matter. Sorry, but that’s not true. For one thing, every economy — even a small one — is potentially a “natural experiment” that teaches us more about how economies in general work. Beyond that, the Baltics now loom large in the imagination of austerity’s defenders, particularly since Ireland keeps refusing to play along and be a success story.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Why the Baltics Matter', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-06-08T15:53:22+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d1299da9-abd8-54bc-b6e3-9d0d2e0791be', 'word_count': 143, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d1299da9-abd8-54bc-b6e3-9d0d2e0791be'}, {'abstract': 'After a successful reintroduction of its heroes in 52 new series last fall, DC Comics now plans to unveil an issue No. 0 for each.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/06/08/dc-comics-going-even-further-back-in-time/', 'snippet': 'After a successful reintroduction of its heroes in 52 new series last fall, DC Comics now plans to unveil an issue No. 0 for each.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Last fall, DC Comics reintroduced its heroes in 52 new series, all starting with issue No. 1. This September the publisher will be going even further back in time, with plans to unveil an issue No. 0 for each of those series.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'DC Comics Going Even Further Back in Time', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'DC Comics Going Further Back in Time', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-06-08T15:55:21+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By George Gene Gustines', 'person': [{'firstname': 'George', 'middlename': 'Gene', 'lastname': 'Gustines', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c76e8580-e50c-51f5-b1f9-92078e328e40', 'word_count': 267, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c76e8580-e50c-51f5-b1f9-92078e328e40'}, {'abstract': 'The brothers Tom Magliozzi and Ray Magliozzi, whose show has been a staple of NPR for decades, will stop producing new shows this fall.', 'web_url': 'https://mediadecoder.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/06/08/hosts-of-car-talk-to-retire-after-35-years-of-automotive-banter/', 'snippet': 'The brothers Tom Magliozzi and Ray Magliozzi, whose show has been a staple of NPR for decades, will stop producing new shows this fall.', 'lead_paragraph': '6:23 p.m. | Updated For years now, after their broadcasts, Tom and Ray Magliozzi, the hosts of the public-radio hit “Car Talk,” have been ranking the listener calls they receive on a scale from five to one, with five being the most entertaining.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Hosts of ‘Car Talk’ to Retire After 35 Years of Automotive Banter', 'kicker': 'Media Decoder', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '‘Car Talk’ Is Heading To Garage', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Automobiles', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Radio', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Magliozzi, Ray', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Magliozzi, Tom', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Public Radio', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-08T15:58:20+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Media', 'byline': {'original': 'By Brian Stelter', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Brian', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Stelter', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/85b5c711-4c16-5888-90aa-eb5127eb3367', 'word_count': 821, 'uri': 'nyt://article/85b5c711-4c16-5888-90aa-eb5127eb3367'}, {'abstract': 'Killer Whales at SeaWorld, where to sleep outside in New York City and more in our daily capsule of travel news.', 'web_url': 'https://intransit.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/06/08/morning-walkabout-68-seaworld-killer-whales-ruling-and-where-to-sleep-outside-in-new-york-city/', 'snippet': 'Killer Whales at SeaWorld, where to sleep outside in New York City and more in our daily capsule of travel news.', 'lead_paragraph': 'SeaWorld and Killer Whales More than two years after a whale killed a trainer during a show at SeaWorld, a judge has ruled that the park cannot resume similar shows that allow trainers in the water to ride and kiss the animals. SeaWorld has introduced a new show that keeps trainers our of the water, which doesn’t seem to have dimmed ticket sales. (The New York Times)', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Morning Walkabout — 6/8: SeaWorld Killer Whales Ruling, and Where to Sleep Outside in New York City', 'kicker': 'In Transit', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'AMUSEMENT AND THEME PARKS', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Travel and Vacations', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Disney, Walt, World', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'SeaWorld Adventure Parks', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Dublin (Ireland)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Orlando (Fla)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-08T15:59:33+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Travel', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d7b194fd-1928-531a-ae86-cae25063ed4b', 'word_count': 230, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d7b194fd-1928-531a-ae86-cae25063ed4b'}, {'abstract': 'Waka Flocka Flame seemed an outlier when he first came on the hip-hop scene three years ago; now it looks as though he may have carved out a niche that others, like Chief Keef, can join.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/06/10/arts/music/raw-rap-waka-flocka-flame-and-chief-keef.html', 'snippet': 'Waka Flocka Flame seemed an outlier when he first came on the hip-hop scene three years ago; now it looks as though he may have carved out a niche that others, like Chief Keef, 