ubject', 'value': 'Real Estate and Housing (Residential)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-08T18:21:42+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'RealEstate', 'section_name': 'Real Estate', 'byline': {'original': 'By Alison Gregor', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Alison', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gregor', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/af836a07-183f-59f5-b5c3-43bc9a04a0c6', 'word_count': 1525, 'uri': 'nyt://article/af836a07-183f-59f5-b5c3-43bc9a04a0c6'}, {'abstract': 'Do you have a painting or sculpture, a drawing or installation, to which you have returned over and over?', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/06/08/name-the-art-that-calls-your-name/', 'snippet': 'Do you have a painting or sculpture, a drawing or installation, to which you have returned over and over?', 'lead_paragraph': '“There is only one valuable thing in art: the thing you cannot explain,” Georges Braque once said. It may be possible, however, to explain why you love a particular work of art, and in a column in Friday’s Times, I made a stab at doing so, writing about my repeated visits over a decade to a wonderful, deeply unsettling Caravaggio painting at the Metropolitan Museum of Art, “The Denial of St. Peter.” It has become like a fascinating, stubborn, moody old friend — one I admire, argue with and don’t pretend to know very well, even after so many years.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Name the Art That Calls Your Name', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Museums', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-08T18:30:14+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Randy Kennedy', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Randy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kennedy', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6996b675-4672-5626-bc4e-90925959b21b', 'word_count': 322, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6996b675-4672-5626-bc4e-90925959b21b'}, {'abstract': 'A South Bronx neighborhood where most residential property is rent-stabilized, apart from a few new two-families.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/slideshow/2012/06/10/realestate/20120610-LIVINGIN.html', 'snippet': 'A South Bronx neighborhood where most residential property is rent-stabilized, apart from a few new two-families.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A South Bronx neighborhood where most residential property is rent-stabilized, apart from a few new two-families.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/10/realestate/20120610-LIVINGIN-slide-KAC1/20120606-LIVINGIN-slide-KAC1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/10/realestate/20120610-LIVINGIN-slide-KAC1/20120606-LIVINGIN-slide-KAC1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1363, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/10/realestate/20120610-LIVINGIN-slide-KAC1/20120606-LIVINGIN-slide-KAC1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/06/10/realestate/20120610-LIVINGIN-slide-KAC1/20120606-LIVINGIN-slide-KAC1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'ca