lures', 'kicker': 'Wheels', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-06-08T18:53:22+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Automobiles', 'byline': {'original': 'By Christopher Jensen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Christopher', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Jensen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/25f2d1d8-e69b-58c7-a975-81c999d29e43', 'word_count': 357, 'uri': 'nyt://article/25f2d1d8-e69b-58c7-a975-81c999d29e43'}, {'abstract': 'As Ginger Grant on “Gilligan’s Island,” Tina Louise had a remarkable wardrobe, of course, it helped that the designer of her wardrobe was the great costume designer Nolan Miller, who died on Wednesday at the age of 79.', 'web_url': 'https://runway.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/06/08/dressing-for-gilligans-island/', 'snippet': 'As Ginger Grant on “Gilligan’s Island,” Tina Louise had a remarkable wardrobe, of course, it helped that the designer of her wardrobe was the great costume designer Nolan Miller, who died on Wednesday at the age of 79.', 'lead_paragraph': 'As Ginger Grant on “Gilligan’s Island,” Tina Louise had a rather remarkable wardrobe for someone who was shipwrecked while on a three-hour tour. Those marvelous beaded dresses that just matched her skin tone surely were not just lying around on the S.S. Minnow.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Dressing for ‘Gilligan’s Island’', 'kicker': 'On the Runway', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-06-08T19:00:06+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Eric Wilson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Eric', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Wilson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b4467bee-8bf6-5a5f-a7d8-7e052a51a904', 'word_count': 410, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b4467bee-8bf6-5a5f-a7d8-7e052a51a904'}, {'abstract': '“Absolute Batman: Dark Victory” hits the hardcover list at No. 8.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/06/08/graphic-books-best-sellers-batmans-early-days/', 'snippet': '“Absolute Batman: Dark Victory” hits the hardcover list at No. 8.', 'lead_paragraph': '“Absolute Batman: Dark Victory” lands at No. 8 on the hardcover graphic books best-seller list this week. The “Absolute” hardcovers from DC tend to cost $99.99, but they present the comics in an oversized format, with better paper and rich colors, so they are often worth the price of admission. “Dark Victory,” written by Jeph Loeb and illustrated by Tim Sale, is a sequel to “The Long Halloween,” in which Batman was learning the ropes, confronting the mob, discovering a new breed of freak (the Joker, the Penguin and more) and trying to discover the identity of the Holiday killer. “Dark Victory” is still set in the earliest days of the hero’s career. It picks up some dangling threads, brings Robin into the fight for justice and introduces the Hangman, another serial killer. These early looks into the careers of heroes have become something of a trademark for Mr. Loeb and Mr. Sale. Other recommend books: “Superman For All Seasons,” “Daredevil: Yellow,” “Spider-Man: Blue” and “Hulk: Gray.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Graphic Books Best Sellers: Batman’s Early Days', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Graphic Novels', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-08T19:00:55+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By George Gene Gustines', 'person': [{'firstname': 'George', 'middlename': 'Gene', 'lastname': 'Gustines', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/32c06167-f15f-50ce-bfcb-06c887155b22', 'word_count': 184, 'uri': 'nyt://article/32c06167-f15f-50ce-bfcb-06c887155b22'}, {'abstract': 'Johan Santana’s no-hitter was one of those moments baseball fans live for.', 'web_url': 'https://bats.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/06/08/for-the-patient-baseball-fan-a-beautiful-reward/', 'snippet': 'Johan Santana’s no-hitter was one of those moments baseball fans live for.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Baseball fans are frequently asked by people who aren’t fans, How can you watch such a slow game?', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'For the Patient Baseball Fan, a Beautiful Reward', 'kicker': 'Bats', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Baseball', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Santana, Johan', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Mets', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-08T19:06:44+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Baseball', 'byline': {'original': 'By Eric Trager', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Eric', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Trager', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0261091d-243f-5c6d-9104-117f7d002dcf', 'word_count': 324, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0261091d-243f-5c6d-9104-117f7d002dcf'}, {'abstract': 'A selection of cultural events this week.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/06/10/arts/june-10-16.html', 'snippet': 'A selection of cultural events this week.', 'lead_paragraph': 'THE desire of the world’s have-nots for a more prosperous and secure life in Western Europe informs two films — Emanuele Cialese’s “Terraferma” and the twin brothers Massimiliano and Gianluca De Serio’s first feature, “Seven Acts of Mercy” — in the Film Society of Lincoln Center’s series OPEN ROADS: NEW ITALIAN CINEMA. The series, which began on Friday and continues through Thursday at the Walter Reade Theater, showcases the latest in contemporary Italian movies.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/10/arts/10GUIDE2_SPAN/10GUIDE2-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 360, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/06/10/arts/10GUIDE2_SPAN/10GUIDE2-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 360}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/10/arts/10GUIDE2_SPAN/10GUIDE2-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 681, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/10/arts/10GUIDE2_SPAN/10GUIDE2-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1363, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/10/arts/10GUIDE2_SPAN/10GUIDE2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/06/10/arts/10GUIDE2_SPAN/10GUIDE2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/10/arts/10GUIDE2_SPAN/10GUIDE2-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'June 10 — 16', 'kicker': 'The Week Ahead', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-06-08T19:14:44+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/57d33e1d-a2ee-5f02-a2db-e7ff5a6a73e3', 'word_count': 1580, 'uri': 'nyt://article/57d33e1d-a2ee-5f02-a2db-e7ff5a6a73e3'}, {'abstract': 'Sara Errani is enjoying a brilliant 2012 campaign. Maria Sharapova has a surprising new assuredness on clay. They will meet on Saturday in the women’s final of the French Open.', 'web_url': 'https://straightsets.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/06/08/sharapova-vs-errani-serves-will-be-crucial/', 'snippet': 'Sara Errani is enjoying a brilliant 2012 campaign. Maria Sharapova has a surprising new assuredness on clay. They will meet on Saturday in the women’s final of the French Open.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Maria Sharapova looks to complete a career Grand Slam when she faces Sara Errani in the women’s singles final at Roland Garros on Saturday. Sharapova will be the overwhelming favorite, with her extensive experience and formidable arsenal of offensive weapons, but Errani has made an improbable run to the finals with upsets over former French Open champions Ana Ivanovic and Svetlana Kuznetsova, and Samantha Stosur, a finalist in 2010. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Sharapova vs. Errani: Serves Will Be Crucial', 'kicker': 'Straight Sets', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'French Open (Tennis)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Errani, Sara', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Sharapova, Maria', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-08T19:16:30+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Tennis', 'byline': {'original': 'By Geoff Macdonald', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Geoff', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Macdonald', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f05637f8-bc38-5c50-a780-2b2033a0c8be', 'word_count': 561, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f05637f8-bc38-5c50-a780-2b2033a0c8be'}, {'abstract': 'The Australian Ballet, which last brought its full company to New York in 1999, will perform programs at Lincoln Center.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/06/10/arts/dance/australian-ballet-visits-lincoln-center.html', 'snippet': 'The Australian Ballet, which last brought its full company to New York in 1999, will perform programs at Lincoln Center.', 'lead_paragraph': 'IT’S not every season the Lincoln Center studios ring with “G’day!” But that’s about to change, at least for a week, when the Australian Ballet visits New York for the first time since 1999. ', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '11', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/10/arts/10AUSTRALIAN2_SPAN/10AUSTRALIAN2_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 360, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/06/10/arts/10AUSTRALIAN2_SPAN/10AUSTRALIAN2_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 360}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/10/arts/10AUSTRALIAN2_SPAN/10AUSTRALIAN2_SPAN-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/10/arts/10AUSTRALIAN2_SPAN/10AUSTRALIAN2_SPAN-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1366, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/10/arts/10AUSTRALIAN2_SPAN/10AUSTRALIAN2_SPAN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/06/10/arts/10AUSTRALIAN2_SPAN/10AUSTRALIAN2_SPAN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/10/arts/10AUSTRALIAN2_SPAN/10AUSTRALIAN2_SPAN-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 100, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Touring Is a Tonic for Australians', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Touring is a Tonic For Australians', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Australian Ballet', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Dancing', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-08T19:16:54+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Dance', 'byline': {'original': 'By Diane Nottle', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Diane', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Nottle', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5ac769da-1066-5703-82c5-9b2cd5199b13', 'word_count': 1073, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5ac769da-1066-5703-82c5-9b2cd5199b13'}, {'abstract': 'With technology’s rapid change, even recent new-music works struggle to duplicate electronic effects of scant decades ago.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/06/10/arts/music/new-music-works-with-surprising-problem-dated-instruments.html', 'snippet': 'With technology’s rapid change, even recent new-music works struggle to duplicate electronic effects of scant decades ago.', 'lead_paragraph': 'IN an onstage interview during an evening of music by Kaija Saariaho at Zankel Hall this spring Jeremy Geffen, Carnegie Hall’s director of artistic planning, asked Ms. Saariaho a provocative question, suggested by the nature of the music she was presenting. Though she writes prolifically for conventional ensembles, Ms. Saariaho also composes electronic music, and the works performed that evening not only combined live voices with recorded or electronically altered live sounds but also had video components, by Jean-Baptiste Barrière, that combined live and recorded elements. ', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/10/arts/10OBSOLETE2_SPAN/10OBSOLETE2-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 380, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/06/10/arts/10OBSOLETE2_SPAN/10OBSOLETE2-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 380}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/10/arts/10OBSOLETE2_SPAN/10OBSOLETE2-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 852, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/10/arts/10OBSOLETE2_SPAN/10OBSOLETE2-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1704, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/10/arts/10OBSOLETE2_SPAN/10OBSOLETE2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/06/10/arts/10OBSOLETE2_SPAN/10OBSOLETE2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight'