': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/243887ac-3733-526a-b8e4-36a01898f5e1', 'word_count': 1217, 'uri': 'nyt://article/243887ac-3733-526a-b8e4-36a01898f5e1'}, {'abstract': 'PHotoEspaña is one of the world’s most wide-ranging photography festivals, encompassing the visual arts as well as photography. This year, works by more than 250 artists from 44 countries will be on view in galleries, installations, major museums and art centers through July 22.', 'web_url': 'https://intransit.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/06/08/avedon-to-warhol-madrid-hosts-visual-arts-festival/', 'snippet': 'PHotoEspaña is one of the world’s most wide-ranging photography festivals, encompassing the visual arts as well as photography. This year, works by more than 250 artists from 44 countries will be on view in galleries, installations, major museums ...', 'lead_paragraph': 'PHotoEspaña is one of the world’s most wide-ranging photography festivals, encompassing the visual arts as well as photography. This year, works by more than 250 artists from 44 countries will be on view in galleries, installations, major museums and art centers through July 22. More than 700,000 people visit the festival annually, making it Spain’s most popular cultural event. Andy Warhol, Richard Avedon, Carlos Garaicoa, Santiago Sierra, Chen Chieh-jen, Alberto García-Alix and Helena Almeida are just a few of the top artists whose works will be exhibited.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Avedon to Warhol: Madrid Hosts Visual Arts Festival', 'kicker': 'In Transit', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Photography', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Travel and Vacations', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Avedon, Richard', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Chang, Chien-Chi', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Garaicoa, Carlos', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Warhol, Andy', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Spain', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-08T19:48:43+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Travel', 'byline': {'original': 'By Valerie Gladstone', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Valerie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gladstone', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/bafed3a1-3448-5268-9975-ec3b394e9eaa', 'word_count': 209, 'uri': 'nyt://article/bafed3a1-3448-5268-9975-ec3b394e9eaa'}, {'abstract': 'Why do physicists have to be so churlish toward philosophy?', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/06/10/opinion/sunday/what-physics-learns-from-philosophy.html', 'snippet': 'Why do physicists have to be so churlish toward philosophy?', 'lead_paragraph': 'A KERFUFFLE has broken out between philosophy and physics. It began earlier this spring when a philosopher (David Albert) gave a sharply negative review in this paper to a book by a physicist (Lawrence Krauss) that purported to solve, by purely scientific means, the mystery of the universe’s existence. The physicist responded to the review by calling the philosopher who wrote it “moronic” and arguing that philosophy, unlike physics, makes no progress and is rather boring, if not totally useless. And then the kerfuffle was joined on both sides.', 'print_section': 'SR', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Physicists, Stop the Churlishness', 'kicker': 'Gray Matter', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Physicists, Stop the Churlishness', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Penrose, Roger', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Physics', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Philosophy', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Science and Technology', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Weinberg, Steven', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hawking, Stephen W', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-08T19:49:47+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'subsection_name': 'Sunday Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jim Holt', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jim', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Holt', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/3d6cca05-fe9e-53b4-abec-787d512e2691', 'word_count': 771, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3d6cca05-fe9e-53b4-abec-787d512e2691'}, {'abstract': 'When crews are seen from high overhead, they aren’t bodies pulling on oars; they’re geometries. What that perspective fails to reveal — and actually obscures — is how much that elegance and abstraction hurts.', 'web_url': 'https://6thfloor.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/06/08/not-a-pretty-picture-rowing-pain/', 'snippet': 'When crews are seen from high overhead, they aren’t bodies pulling on oars; they’re geometries. What that perspective fails to reveal — and actually obscures — is how much that elegance and abstraction hurts.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When crews are seen from high overhead, as they are in our latest Look pages, they aren’t bodies pulling on oars; they’re geometries — parallels and circles and angles acute and obtuse. Seen from the shore, they typically strike viewers as all swinging gliding gracefulness.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Not a Pretty Picture (Rowing = Pain)', 'kicker': 'The 6th Floor', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 