ocument_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Phillip Lutz', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Phillip', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Lutz', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5d5f5dc1-81e6-5751-88b4-ae38da00f92f', 'word_count': 1001, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5d5f5dc1-81e6-5751-88b4-ae38da00f92f'}, {'abstract': 'Louise Pitre steps into the title role of “Mame” — made famous by Angela Lansbury in 1966 — at the Goodspeed Opera House in East Haddam, Connecticut.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/06/10/nyregion/a-review-of-mame-at-the-goodspeed-opera-house.html', 'snippet': 'Louise Pitre steps into the title role of “Mame” — made famous by Angela Lansbury in 1966 — at the Goodspeed Opera House in East Haddam, Connecticut.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Louise Pitre knows how to punch home a song. Starring as the irrepressible title character in “Mame” at the Goodspeed Opera House, she lends a fierce energy to the snazzy choreography by Vince Pesce and the efficient book scenes of Jerome Lawrence and Robert E. Lee. But it’s not until everyone else departs and she’s left alone on James Youmans’s stylish set to sing the show’s rueful, climactic Jerry Herman ballad, “If He Walked Into My Life,” that this production comes fully into its own.', 'print_section': 'CT', 'print_page': '11', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/10/nyregion/10ARTSCT1_SPAN/10ARTSCT1_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 360, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/06/10/nyregion/10ARTSCT1_SPAN/10ARTSCT1_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 360}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'imag