.', 'lead_paragraph': 'For years, New York taxpayers have been able to find out the pension benefits for retired public employees, like state troopers, corrections officers and public schoolteachers. But New York City police pension officials have refused to disclose the names of retirees receiving pensions and the amount of the benefits. Those officials — unlike other state and local officials — decided to hide behind an ambiguity in the state’s Freedom of Information law. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '20', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Public Pension Should Be Public', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Public Pension Should Be Public', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Pensions and Retirement Plans', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York State', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Law and Legislation', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Police Department (NYC)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Editorials', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Government Employees', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-09T01:53:28+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/04de8a2a-9d4f-5596-8e9a-2f58254cbe6c', 'word_count': 321, 'uri': 'nyt://article/04de8a2a-9d4f-5596-8e9a-2f58254cbe6c'}, {'abstract': 'With warm weather comes a phenomenon that is centuries old: the burglar who breaks in upper-floor apartments through open windows.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/06/09/nyregion/the-season-of-open-windows-and-the-second-story-man.html', 'snippet': 'With warm weather comes a phenomenon that is centuries old: the burglar who breaks in upper-floor apartments through open windows.', 'lead_paragraph': 'He left the apartment that Wednesday morning as he did every other day, planning on getting some work done on a book he was writing. But something strange happened.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '15', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/09/nyregion/CRIMESCENE/CRIMESCENE-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 759, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/09/nyregion/CRIMESCENE/CRIMESCENE-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1518, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/09/nyregion/CRIMESCENE/CRIMESCENE-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/06/09/nyregion/CRIMESCENE/CRIMESCENE-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/09/nyregion/CRIMESCENE/CRIMESCENE-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Season of Open Windows, and the Second-Story Man', 'kicker': 'Crime Scene', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Season of Open Windows, and the Second-Story Man', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Police Department (NYC)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Robberies and Thefts', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Williamsburg (NYC)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Summer (Season)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-09T01:54:15+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael Wilson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Wilson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/71e855bc-a42d-52c9-9f75-a289cecf6b69', 'word_count': 756, 'uri': 'nyt://article/71e855bc-a42d-52c9-9f75-a289cecf6b69'}, {'abstract': 'Religious conservatives are losing a primary argument for banning the pills. The latest research indicates that they work by delaying ovulation, not by aborting an embryo.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/06/09/opinion/how-morning-after-pills-really-work.html', 'snippet': 'Religious conservatives are losing a primary argument for banning the pills. The latest research indicates that they work by delaying ovulation, not by aborting an embryo.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Religious conservatives are losing one of their primary arguments for trying to ban the morning-after birth control pill that can prevent pregnancy if taken within days after sexual intercourse. They and their political allies have long contended that the pills work by aborting an embryo at the earliest stages of development. But, as Pam Belluck reported in The Times on Wednesday, the latest scientific findings and expert opinion indicate that the pills work by delaying ovulation so that sperm can’t fertilize an egg to create an embryo. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '20', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'How Morning-After Pills Really Work', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'How Morning-After Pills Really Work', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Plan 