byline': {'original': 'By Deb Amlen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Deb', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Amlen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/527243b3-2314-5c41-bff2-a47ad901e751', 'word_count': 281, 'uri': 'nyt://article/527243b3-2314-5c41-bff2-a47ad901e751'}, {'abstract': 'Three teachers from Public School 189 in Washington Heights and their guests dined at Saggio, a homey, full-flavored trattoria on West 181st Street.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/06/10/nyregion/at-saggio-a-day-of-school-revelry-capped-by-wine.html', 'snippet': 'Three teachers from Public School 189 in Washington Heights and their guests dined at Saggio, a homey, full-flavored trattoria on West 181st Street.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The neighborhood restaurant plays a limited, but critical, role in New York’s culinary life: It is the place one goes not to be surprised, but satisfied. It’s always a little surprising, then, to find one that’s more than satisfactory. Saggio, a homey, full-flavored trattoria at 829 West 181st Street, is one of the newer restaurants in the micro-neighborhood that sits on the downslope between Fort Washington Avenue and the Henry Hudson Parkway in Washington Heights. On a late spring evening, you will find its front window invitingly thrown open to homebound commuters, congregating dog owners and perhaps a group of teachers from a local elementary school relaxing after a hard day with some pasta and a glass of wine.', 'print_section': 'MB', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/10/nyregion/10TABLE_SPAN/10TABLE_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 369, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/06/10/nyregion/10TABLE_SPAN/10TABLE_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg'