{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Myanmar', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-09T06:15:50+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ec25518e-7399-5c51-a315-9d4184839acd', 'word_count': 496, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ec25518e-7399-5c51-a315-9d4184839acd'}, {'abstract': 'The search by Jeff Vanderbeek, the controlling owner of the Devils, for outside investment to stabilize the team’s finances has apparently ended successfully with an estimated cash deal of $200 million.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/06/09/sports/hockey/devils-receive-possible-cash-infusion.html', 'snippet': 'The search by Jeff Vanderbeek, the controlling owner of the Devils, for outside investment to stabilize the team’s finances has apparently ended successfully with an estimated cash deal of $200 million.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The search by Jeff Vanderbeek, the controlling owner of the Devils, for outside investment to stabilize the team’s finances has apparently ended successfully. A person briefed on the pending transaction said that Vanderbeek had found at least one investor to inject cash into the team and deal with its overdue debt. The person who described the estimated $200 million deal said it would lead to a reduction of the 47 percent stake owned by Ray Chambers and Michael Gilfillan. At the same time, part of the $77 million debt payment owed by the team and Prudential Center will be repaid as part of the complex transaction. Banks have extended the deadline for repaying the debt; if they had not, the team would have been in default. The deal was first reported by The Sports Business Journal. ', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Devils Receive Possible Cash Infusion', 'kicker': 'Sports Briefing | Hockey', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Possible Cash Infusion for Devils', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mergers, Acquisitions and Divestitures', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hockey, Ice', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Vanderbeek, Jeffrey', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New Jersey Devils', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-09T06:22:15+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Hockey', 'byline': {'original': 'By Richard Sandomir', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Richard', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Sandomir', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/47895640-893b-5e44-9942-c97ffb6a7368', 'word_count': 137, 'uri': 'nyt://article/47895640-893b-5e44-9942-c97ffb6a7368'}, {'abstract': 'The reigning world champion Jordyn Wieber closed out the first day of the United States women’s gymnastics championships in St. Louis tied for first place with Gabrielle Douglas.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/06/09/sports/reigning-world-champion-is-tied-at-top-in-us-gymnastics-meet.html', 'snippet': 'The reigning world champion Jordyn Wieber closed out the first day of the United States women’s gymnastics championships in St. Louis tied for first place with Gabrielle Douglas.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The reigning world champion Jordyn Wieber closed out the first day of the United States women’s gymnastics championships in St. Louis tied for first place with Gabrielle Douglas. Wieber, who won last year’s national title by more than six points, said the tie didn’t faze her because this is just a warm-up meet for Olympic trials later this month. ', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times'