out to find out.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '‘Mary Poppins’ to Flit to New Zealand', 'kicker': 'Media Decoder', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '‘Mary Poppins’ To Flit to New Zealand', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Schumacher, Thomas', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Walt Disney Company', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Auckland (New Zealand)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-09T21:32:14+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Media', 'byline': {'original': 'By Brooks Barnes', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Brooks', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Barnes', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/43571a36-7986-5c2f-bff5-c9faebc36403', 'word_count': 209, 'uri': 'nyt://article/43571a36-7986-5c2f-bff5-c9faebc36403'}, {'abstract': 'House Republicans propose gutting the agencies designed to prevent economic meltdowns like the Great Recession in 2007.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/06/10/opinion/sunday/lost-the-vote-deny-the-money.html', 'snippet': 'House Republicans propose gutting the agencies designed to prevent economic meltdowns like the Great Recession in 2007.', 'lead_paragraph': 'If you wanted to reproduce the conditions that led to the Great Recession in 2007, the easiest way would be the plan unveiled last week by House Republicans: gut the regulators who are supposed to keep the worst business practices in check.', 'print_section': 'SR', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Lost the Vote? Deny the Money', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Lost the Vote? Deny the Money', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Regulation and Deregulation of Industry', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Banking and Financial Institutions', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Commodity Futures Trading Commission', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Securities and Exchange Commission', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Internal Revenue Service', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'House of Representatives', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Dodd-Frank Wall Street Reform and Consumer Protection Act (2010)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Budgets and Budgeting', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Editorials', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-09T21:36:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'subsection_name': 'Sunday Review', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/8e6b31d8-0598-5cff-831e-9dd77d16f82f', 'word_count': 710, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8e6b31d8-0598-5cff-831e-9dd77d16f82f'}, {'abstract': 'Since the 1970s, the percentage of Americans who are obese has been rising. Among children, the percentage has more than tripled.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/interactive/2012/06/09/sunday-review/getting-bigger.html', 'snippet': 'Since the 1970s, the percentage of Americans who are obese has been rising. Among children, the percentage has more than tripled.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Since the 1970s, the percentage of Americans who are obese has been rising. Among children, the percentage has more than tripled.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Getting Bigger', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-06-09T21:36:37+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Sunday Review', 'section_name': 'Sunday Review', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Interactive Feature', '_id': 'nyt://interactive/d3d83c43-eec0-52b9-ba50-aea9ac7a9bac', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://interactive/d3d83c43-eec0-52b9-ba50-aea9ac7a9bac'}, {'abstract': 'While obesity has often been seen as a personal failure, experts increasingly point to societal factors and the need for nationwide remedies.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/06/10/opinion/sunday/how-do-you-put-a-nation-on-a-diet.html', 'snippet': 'While obesity has often been seen as a personal failure, experts increasingly point to societal factors and the need for nationwide remedies.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Walt Disney Company last week announced plans to restrict junk food commercials on its television programs aimed at children. A few days before, Mayor Michael Bloomberg took steps to restrict the sale of supersized sugary drinks in New York. But isolated changes may not make a substantial difference at a time when Americans are gaining weight as a nation. Reducing obesity will require a broader shift in the culture. ', 'print_section': 'SR', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'How Do You Put a Nation on a Diet?', 'kicker': 'Deconstruction', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'How Do You Put A Nation on a Diet?', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Obesity', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Diet and Nutrition', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Weight', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Food', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Medicine and Health', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bloomberg, Michael R', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Editorials', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Institute of Medicine', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-09T21:36:38+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'subsection_name': 'Sunday Review', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/eb29dfa1-1b0b-5d02-9151-30a149b87948', 'word_count': 631, 'uri': 'nyt://article/eb29dfa1-1b0b-5d02-9151-30a149b87948'}, {'abstract': 'By opposing proposed guidelines to lower their high fees, bankruptcy lawyers show they are out of touch with economic realities and undermine public confidence in the bankruptcy process.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/06/10/opinion/sunday/the-trouble-with-bankruptcy-lawyers.html', 'snippet': 'By opposing proposed guidelines to lower their high fees, bankruptcy lawyers show they are out of touch with economic realities and undermine public confidence in the bankruptcy process.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Bankruptcy lawyers moved to the top tier of corporate practice in 1978 when a federal statute allowed them to be paid top hourly fees, which courts had traditionally limited in bankruptcy proceedings. In recent years, legal fees in Chapter 11 reorganization cases have soared as deals have grown bigger and more complex. ', 'print_section': 'SR', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Trouble With Bankruptcy Lawyers', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Trouble With Bankruptcy Lawyers', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Legal Profession', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Bankruptcies', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Justice Department', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Prices (Fares, Fees and Rates)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Editorials', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-09T21:36:42+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'subsection_name': 'Sunday Review', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/91d39933-81ff-5ca4-8e3f-cf2b568def4b', 'word_count': 421, 'uri': 'nyt://article/91d39933-81ff-5ca4-8e3f-cf2b568def4b'}, {'abstract': 'Americans need to know that hydrofracturing is safe. Stronger federal rules are plainly needed.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/06/10/opinion/sunday/natural-gas-by-the-book.html', 'snippet': 'Americans need to know that hydrofracturing is safe. Stronger federal rules are plainly needed.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Reports from international agencies usually make for dull reading. “Golden Rules for a Golden Age of Gas,” from the Paris-based International Energy Agency, does not. It should be required reading for regulators and the industry — and for anyone who cares about energy, the environment and climate change.', 'print_section': 'SR', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Natural Gas, by the Book', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Natural Gas, by the Book', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'International Energy Agency', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hydraulic Fracturing', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Natural Gas', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Obama, Barack', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Environmental Protection Agency', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Interior Department', 'rank