'lead_paragraph': 'Many of you were amused or baffled to read, in my post about the things I actually bought, that among the items I carry in my laptop bag is a checkbook.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Value of a Checkbook', 'kicker': 'Pogue&#039;s Posts', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Checks and Checking Accounts', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-09T22:44:55+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'byline': {'original': 'By David Pogue', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Pogue', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9d0fa9aa-d596-5446-b8ee-fb2ea6f641c6', 'word_count': 166, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9d0fa9aa-d596-5446-b8ee-fb2ea6f641c6'}, {'abstract': 'Studies and programs can help players improve, or they can be approached like a crossword puzzle, and there is only one solution for each.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/06/10/crosswords/chess/chess-studies-and-problems-help-players-improve.html', 'snippet': 'Studies and programs can help players improve, or they can be approached like a crossword puzzle, and there is only one solution for each.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Players have their own jargon and shorthand to describe what happens on the board. So when they use the word “beautiful” to talk about a move — or even a whole game — they are describing chess play that is hard to predict but afterward seems to have had an elegant logic. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '26', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Exercises for Fun and Better Gamesmanship', 'kicker': 'Chess', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Exercises for Fun and Better Gamesmanship', 'name': Non