e': 'glocations', 'value': 'Bronx (NYC)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-10T00:04:40+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Colin Moynihan', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Colin', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Moynihan', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/1cd5565f-dcc3-5071-84c5-8eb04ebbfb40', 'word_count': 501, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1cd5565f-dcc3-5071-84c5-8eb04ebbfb40'}, {'abstract': 'Planners are looking to convert water from saline aquifers beneath the state’s surface, as well as seawater from the Gulf of Mexico.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/06/10/science/earth/turning-saltwater-from-earth-and-sea-into-drinking-water.html', 'snippet': 'Planners are looking to convert water from saline aquifers beneath the state’s surface, as well as seawater from the Gulf of Mexico.', 'lead_paragraph': 'SAN ANTONIO — Drilling rigs in the midst of cow pastures are hardly a novelty for Texans. But on a warm May day at a site about 30 miles south of San Antonio, a rig was not trying to reach oil or fresh water, but rather something unconventional: a salty aquifer. After a plant is built and begins operating in 2016, the site will become one of the state’s largest water desalination  facilities.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '29A', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/07/us/ttdesalination1/ttdesalination1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 360, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/06/07/us/ttdesalination1/ttdesalination1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 360}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06