ans and ends, but the FiveThirtyEight model is operating in a crowded field.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Where Electoral Forecasts Agree and Disagree', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-06-10T10:00:26+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Micah Cohen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Micah', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cohen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4c09ab82-c0aa-5feb-91ab-b5bf7c9bb042', 'word_count': 715, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4c09ab82-c0aa-5feb-91ab-b5bf7c9bb042'}, {'abstract': 'D’Angelo took the stage last night at Bonnaroo, making his first appearance in the United States since the end of his 2001 tour.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/06/10/dangelo-back-in-the-u-s-a/', 'snippet': 'D’Angelo took the stage last night at Bonnaroo, making his first appearance in the United States since the end of his 2001 tour.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Bonnaroo’s late-night surprise was a doozy: the return of D’Angelo, the all-around soul man who has not played a concert in the United States since the end of his 2001 tour for his album “Voodoo,” a masterpiece released the year before.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Bonnaroo: D’Angelo, Back in the U.S.A.', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'D’Angelo a Surprise at Bonnaroo', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Bonnaroo Festival (Manchester, Tenn)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'United States', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-10T11:07:20+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jon Pareles', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jon', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Pareles', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/878d2ed2-1ea0-5dbe-89ee-421d2279dd7c', 'word_count': 373, 'uri': 'nyt://article/878d2ed2-1ea0-5dbe-89ee-421d2279dd7c'}, {'abstract': 'Buying some time, maybe.', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/06/10/the-eurotarp-cometh/', 'snippet': 'Buying some time, maybe.', 'lead_paragraph': 'OK, so Spain gets a bank bailout. It’s basically like the TARP: a Spanish government agency will give banks cash, presumably in return for an ownership stake, with the goal of reassuring depositors and interbank lenders that their funds will remain safe even if the banks turn out to have big losses; the point is that these losses will initially come out of the new cash hoard, so that default on debts won’t happen.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The EuroTARP Cometh', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-06-10T11:17:30+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/67226ed7-d056-5400-b46b-78aabaeeaff3', 'word_count': 216, 'uri': 'nyt://article/67226ed7-d056-5400-b46b-78aabaeeaff3'}, {'abstract': 'Where’s the improvement?', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/06/10/latvian-competitiveness/', 'snippet': 'Where’s the improvement?', 'lead_paragraph': 'As Simon Wren-Lewis says, there’s something about the Latvian experience that causes many people to leave their critical faculties at the immigration desk. I think it’s mainly the fact that austerians need a hero, and Ireland keeps failing to deliver. But whatever. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Latvian Competitiveness', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-06-10T12:09:32+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/afa80b69-d4ec-5bc2-a3ab-e7f2edf6df48', 'word_count': 295, 'uri': 'nyt://article/afa80b69-d4ec-5bc2-a3ab-e7f2edf6df48'}, {'abstract': 'Three young men, two of them with ties to Auburn’s football team, were killed and three others were wounded during a pool party on Saturday night.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/06/11/us/report-of-a-multiple-shooting-near-auburn-university.html', 'snippet': 'Three young men, two of them with ties to Auburn’s football team, were killed and three others were wounded during a pool party on Saturday night.', 'lead_paragraph': 'AUBURN, Ala. — Three young men, two of them with ties to Auburn University’s football team, were killed and three others were wounded during a pool party on Saturday night at an apartment complex near the campus here.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/11/us/AUBURN-2/AUBURN-2-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 965, 'subType': 'j