/bonnaroo-the-red-hot-chili-peppers-get-funky/', 'snippet': 'In their two-hour set, the Chili Peppers repeatedly knocked their music apart and slammed it back together.', 'lead_paragraph': 'With the Red Hot Chili Peppers playing the headlining set, one through-line for Bonnaroo on Saturday was funk. The festival offered up funk as motion, as repetition, as a mathematical pattern, as a process, as a communal project, as a yardstick of history and a reason to just dance. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Bonnaroo: The Red Hot Chili Peppers Get Funky', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Bonnaroo Festival (Manchester, Tenn)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kiedis, Anthony', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Red Hot Chili Peppers', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-10T19:23:59+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jon Pareles', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jon', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Pareles', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0baa4f2b-6a10-5767-bbf4-5ff62d6c73c8', 'word_count': 516, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0baa4f2b-6a10-5767-bbf4-5ff62d6c73c8'}, {'abstract': 'Matthew Weiner, the creator and show runner of “Mad Men,” answers questions about the characters and events of this season prior to Sunday night’s finale.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/06/10/mad-men-creator-matthew-weiner-reflects-on-the-season-so-far/', 'snippet': 'Matthew Weiner, the creator and show runner of “Mad Men,” answers questions about the characters and events of this season prior to Sunday night’s finale.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Warning: this post contains spoilers for the first 12 episodes of the “Mad Men” season.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '‘Mad Men’ Creator Matthew Weiner Reflects on the Season So Far', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Weiner, Matthew', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-10T19:28:19+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dave Itzkoff', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dave', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Itzkoff', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f4521e84-b9bd-56f1-9721-90d39a70618b', 'word_count': 2363, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f4521e84-b9bd-56f1-9721-90d39a70618b'}, {'abstract': 'Facing their first glimpse of adversity since the playoffs began, the Kings say they will stick with what has worked as they try to finish off the Devils.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/06/11/sports/hockey/kings-tripped-up-by-final-victory.html', 'snippet': 'Facing their first glimpse of adversity since the playoffs began, the Kings say they will stick with what has worked as they try to finish off the Devils.', 'lead_paragraph': 'NEWARK — In the end, as the minutes wound down in Game 5 on Saturday night, as they had in Game 4, the officials never had to think about pulling the Stanley Cup from its protective case. The Los Angeles Kings, who were poised to parade it around a rink in celebration, proved in two losses to the Devils that they may still be the better team, but that they are not ready to hoist the prize. They proved the theory that the final win is the hardest, and perhaps that the Stanley Cup will not be won — cannot be won? — until you have done something truly hard.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/11/sports/11onhockey-pic/11onhockey-pic-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 350, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/06/11/sports/11onhockey-pic/11onhockey-pic-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 350}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/11/sports/11onhockey-pic/11onhockey-pic-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/11/sports/11onhockey-pic/11onhockey-pic-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/11/sports/11onhockey-pic/11onhockey-pic-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/06/11/sports/11onhockey-pic/11onhockey-pic-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/11/sports/11onhockey-pic/11onhockey-pic-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Used to Winning With Ease, Kings Tripped Up by Final Victory', 'kicker': 'On Hockey', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Kings Learn Hardest Part Is Writing An Ending', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Brodeur, Martin', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hockey, Ice', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Los Angeles Kings', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Stanley Cup', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New Jersey Devils', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-10T19:39:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Hockey', 'byline': {'original': 'By Lynn Zinser', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Lynn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Zinser', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7cbf9f81-ced3-599c-9fe6-d8cd68aa1120', 'word_count': 906, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7cbf9f81-ced3-599c-9fe6-d8cd68aa1120'}, {'abstract': 'Charles Isherwood and Dave Itzkoff live blogged the 66th annual Tony Awards.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/06/10/tony-awards-2012-live-blog/', 'snippet': 'Charles Isherwood and Dave Itzkoff live blogged the 66th annual Tony Awards.', 'lead_paragraph': 'On Sunday night, The New York Times brought you live coverage of the Tony Awards with photos from the red carpet and the show, commentary from Charles Isherwood and Dave Itzkoff and behind-the-scenes reports.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '‘Once’ and ‘Clybourne Park’ Win Top 2012 Tony Awards', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-06-10T19:44:18+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5d9ab7da-d537-5652-8b84-e86afedbf895', 'word_count': 4870, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5d9ab7da-d537-5652-8b84-e86afedbf895'}, {'abstract': 'The New Jersey Symphony Orchestra, led by Jacques Lacombe, ended its season with performances of Mahler’s Ninth Symphony, a bold undertaking for a smallish ensemble.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/06/11/arts/music/jacques-lacombe-and-the-new-jersey-symphony-orchestra.html', 'snippet': 'The New Jersey Symphony Orchestra, led by Jacques Lacombe, ended its season with performances of Mahler’s Ninth Symphony, a bold undertaking for a smallish ensemble.', 'lead_paragraph': 'NEWARK — Mahler’s Ninth Symphony is a brave undertaking for a smallish band. The New Jersey Symphony Orchestra ended its season, its second with Jacques Lacombe as music director, with performances of that work over the weekend. (Mr. Lacombe closed his first season, last year, with Mahler’s Third Symphony, also a difficult but arguably less challenging assignment.)', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Mahler That Stands or Falls on Its Finale', 'kicker': 'Music Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Mahler Work That Stands or Falls on Its Finale', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lacombe, Jacques', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Mahler, Gustav', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Classical Music', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New Jersey Symphony Orchestra', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-10T20:10:02+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By James R. Oestreich', 'person': [{'firstname': 'James', 'middlename': 'R.', 'lastname': 'Oestreich', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/3f020218-ffd6-5c16-8618-7f82f7b13f1a', 'word_count': 475, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3f020218-ffd6-5c16-8618-7f82f7b13f1a'}, {'abstract': 'In “I Am a Tree,” Dulcy Rogers follows a family-centered trail to self-knowledge.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/06/11/theater/reviews/i-am-a-tree-dulcy-rogerss-solo-show-at-st-clements.html', 'snippet': 'In “I Am a Tree,” Dulcy Rogers follows a family-centered trail to self-knowledge.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Playwrights, be fearless about what you put into your plays. But then be ruthless about what you cut out of them. Even your best lines can be lost if they’re just tossed into an ocean of words.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/11/theater/11tree-web/11tree-web-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 330, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/06/11/theater/11tree-web/11tree-web-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 330}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/11/theater/11tree-web/11tree-web-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 680, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/11/theater/11tree-web/11tree-web-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1360, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/11/theater/11tree-web/11tree-web-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/06/11/theater/11tree-web/11tree-web-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/11/theater/11tree-web/11tree-web-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Excavating Family Truths', 'kicker': 'Theater Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': "Excavating A Family's Truths", 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Rogers, Dulcy', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-10T20:13:22+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Theater', 'subsection_name': 'Theater Reviews', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ken Jaworowski', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ken', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Jaworowski', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/b47e3e6c-524a-5907-a5d6-533ef9956f6e', 'word_count': 313, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b47e3e6c-524a-5907-a5d6-533ef9956f6e'}, {'abstract': 'Andrea Miller’s “Sit, Kneel, Stand” had its premiere with members of her Gallim Dance at the Joyce Theater.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/06/11/arts/dance/andrea-millers-sit-kneel-stand-at-joyce-theater.html', 'snippet': 'Andrea Miller’s “Sit, Kneel, Stand” had its premiere with members of her Gallim Dance at the Joyce Theater.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When Andrea Miller began choreographing her latest dance, “Sit, Kneel, Stand,” her point of inspiration was the myth of Sisyphus and his eternal task of rolling a boulder up a hill, only to watch it roll back down. For its premiere at the Joyce Theater on Friday night, what lingered from that initial impulse were the ideas of effort and resilience, both spiritual and physical. Members of Ms. Miller’s Gallim Dance compressed or stretched their bodies to extremes, returned to form and started all over again. Presented in conjunction with the Gotham Dance Festival that concluded over the weekend, the production did offer a glimpse into a new direction for Ms. Miller, whose dances, up to this point, have been energetic but unoriginal. They take much from Gaga, a movement practice created by the Israeli choreographer Ohad Naharin. There are traces of Gaga in “Sit, Kneel, Stand” but, encouragingly, the performance is more than a Gaga class. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/11/arts/gallim/gallim-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 643, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/11/arts/gallim/gallim-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1286, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/11/arts/gallim/gallim-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/06/11/arts/gallim/gallim-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/11/arts/gallim/gallim-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Movement, Interrupted and Extreme', 'kicker': 'Dance Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Movement, Interrupted and Extreme', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Gallim Dance', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Dancing', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Miller, Andrea J (1982- )', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-10T20:17:02+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Dance', 'byline': {'original': 'By Gia Kourlas', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Gia', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kourlas', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/53b1b017-2f1c-5c4a-a8e3-147fe0f1f43e', 'word_count': 438, 'uri': 'nyt://article/53b1b017-2f1c-5c4a-a8e3-147fe0f1f43e'}, {'abstract': 'A concert in the New York Philharmonic’s Contact! series at the Met offered a program of works by Elliott Carter, Michael Jarrell and Pierre Boulez.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/06/11/arts/music/new-york-philharmonics-contact-series-at-the-met.html', 'snippet': 'A concert in the New York Philharmonic’s Contact! series at the Met offered a program of works by Elliott Carter, Michael Jarrell and Pierre Boulez.', 'lead_paragraph': 'That David Robertson is a superb conductor of contemporary classical music hardly needs repeating. Evidence is everywhere in reviews worldwide and in the vivid memories of those who have heard concerts he has led. But what makes Mr. Robertson one of the most extraordinary musical advocates of our day has just as much to do with what he does off the podium.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Poems in Four Voices, a Concerto in Two', 'kicker': 'Music Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Presenting Poems in Four Voices, a Concerto in Two', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Carter, Elliott', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Philharmonic', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Boulez, Pierre', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Clas