in the background singing “You Only Live Twice,” Don Draper is approached by a young woman at a bar and asked, “Are you alone?” He considers the question, gives her a sexy sideways glance and — cut.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '‘Mad Men’ Recap: In Season 5 Finale,  Alone Again', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-11T04:50:11+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Mike Hale', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mike', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hale', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/abb94496-0c55-5caa-bfb6-ae8ad6cc2c8d', 'word_count': 1127, 'uri': 'nyt://article/abb94496-0c55-5caa-bfb6-ae8ad6cc2c8d'}, {'abstract': 'A senior executive with a Korean firm splits his time between Atlanta and Udaipur, where he runs a diabetes clinic.', 'web_url': 'https://india.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/06/11/two-jobs-but-half-a-world-apart/', 'snippet': 'A senior executive with a Korean firm splits his time between Atlanta and Udaipur, where he runs a diabetes clinic.', 'lead_paragraph': '“You could say that I was raised to be a nomad,” Anand Chaturvedi writes in The New York Times. “By the 10th grade, I had attended 10 schools throughout Rajasthan, my home state in northwestern India.” The experience turned out to be good training for my current working life, Mr. Chaturvedi writes; who juggles his time as a senior executive with a Korean firm in Atlanta and manages a clinic for diabetes patients in Udaipur, Rajasthan. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Two Jobs, but Half a World Apart', 'kicker': 'India Ink', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_