s.nytimes.com/2012/06/11/positive-ad-push-in-massachusetts-senate-race/', 'snippet': 'Senator Scott P. Brown looked to reach out to women voters, and his Democratic challenger, Elizabeth Warren, attempted to move on from weeks of controversy over her heritage.', 'lead_paragraph': 'BOSTON – Both candidates locked in the fierce Senate race in Massachusetts released positive, family-themed advertisements this week, with incumbent Senator Scott P. Brown looking to reach out to women voters and his Democratic challenger, Elizabeth Warren, attempting to move on from weeks of controversy over her heritage.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Positive Ad Push in Massachusetts Senate Race', 'kicker': 'The Caucus', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Elections', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Political Advertising', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Brown, Scott P', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Warren, Elizabeth', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Senate', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Massachusetts', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-11T15:53:35+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jess Bidgood', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jess', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bidgood', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3543a122-2bb3-50cb-946d-b398de71bd90', 'word_count': 665, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3543a122-2bb3-50cb-946d-b398de71bd90'}, {'abstract': 'The brand’s new resort collection inspires fantasies of sunshine, luxury and leisure.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/06/11/just-one-look-the-row-3/', 'snippet': 'The brand’s new resort collection inspires fantasies of sunshine, luxury and leisure.', 'lead_paragraph': 'This look from the Row is neither the collection’s most elaborate — a white-on-white coat embroidered with palms designed by the artist Whitney Bedford wins that prize — nor is it as novel as the group’s layered trousers or glossy hoods-as-rain-hats, either. What the striped, pajama-y dress does most effectively, however, is evoke fantasies of sunshine (or else why the hat?) and luxury via leisure. Buy it and you could be in a jasmine-scented canyon in Southern California drinking an Arnold Palmer with your feet in the pool, it says. Sold.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Just One Look | The Row', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-06-11T15:55:03+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jane Herman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jane', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Herman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/81ce3e21-7a6c-5e0d-9478-6c674ab7955d', 'word_count': 94, 'uri': 'nyt://article/81ce3e21-7a6c-5e0d-9478-6c674ab7955d'}, {'abstract': 'How narrow can a road be and still allow a three-point turn?', 'web_url': 'https://wordplay.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/06/11/numberplay-turn/', 'snippet': 'How narrow can a road be and still allow a three-point turn?', 'lead_paragraph': 'Welcome to Numberplay, the weeklong game of collaboration and discovery that also happens to be about math. If you’re new to the game, give it a try by leaving a comment at the end of this post. You don’t have to solve the problem; hunches are good. So are wild conjectures. At Numberplay, the standard answer is just the beginning.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Numberplay: Three-Point Turn', 'kicker': 'Wordplay', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mathematics', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-11T16:00:17+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Crosswords & Games', 'byline': {'original': 'By Gary Antonick', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Gary', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Antonick', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7c35e548-1848-5f92-973f-9aaa304ae2ec', 'word_count': 957, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7c35e548-1848-5f92-973f-9aaa304ae2ec'}, {'abstract': 'The trial of Jerry Sandusky, a former Penn State football coach, began Monday with the prosecution showing pictures of eight boys whom Sandusky is accused of sexually assaulting.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/06/12/sports/ncaafootball/jerry-sandusky-child-sexual-abuse-trial-begins.html', 'snippet': 'The trial of Jerry Sandusky, a former Penn State football coach, began Monday with the prosecution showing pictures of eight boys whom Sandusky is accused of sexually assaulting.', 'lead_paragraph': 'BELLEFONTE, Pa. — When Jerry Sandusky, the former Penn State football assistant coach, retired from Joe Paterno’s staff in 1999, he devoted himself full time to his charity, which tried to help children from disadvantaged families and broken homes.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/12/sports/12sandusky/12sandusky-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/12/sports/12sandusky/12sandusky-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1366, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/12/sports/12sandusky/12sandusky-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/06/12/sports/12sandusky/12sandusky-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/12/sports/12sandusky/12sandusky-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Sandusky’s Trial Begins With Graphic Testimony', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Sandusky’s Trial Begins With Graphic Testimony', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Sandusky, Jerry', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Child Abuse and Neglect', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sex Crimes', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Pennsylvania State University', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-11T16:03:10+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'College Football', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ken Belson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ken', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Belson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/1c9445d1-02a6-59b1-8494-1aa584283f1f', 'word_count': 1037, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1c9445d1-02a6-59b1-8494-1aa584283f1f'}, {'abstract': 'A new study compares same-sex parenting to two-parent biological families.', 'web_url': 'https://douthat.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/06/11/gay-parents-and-the-marriage-debate/', 'snippet': 'A new study compares same-sex parenting to two-parent biological families.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Slate is out with two pieces today on an attention-getting new study that looks at adult outcomes for children raised by parents in same-sex relationships — one by the study’s author, the University of Texas sociologist Mark Regnerus (who co-authored the excellent survey “Premarital Sex in America”), and a response by Will Saletan that tries to tease out some of the study’s implications for the gay marriage debate. The background to the study is the striking fact that even as traditional marriage has declined in the United States – marriage rates tumbling, out-of-wedlock childbirth climbing, and cohabitation becoming as respectable as wedlock – the evidence has mounted for the institution’s importance to the well-being of children. A happy childhood is possible in many different environments, of course. But a growing body of research indicates that no other parental arrangement, from single motherhood to cohabitation to shared custody, affords as many social, economic and emotional advantages as being raised by two biological parents joined in a lifelong commitment. (Isabel Sawhill from the Brookings Institution and Kay Hymowitz from the Manhattan Institute have both had good recent pieces elaborating on some of these themes.)', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Gay Parents and the Marriage Debate', 'kicker': 'Ross Douthat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-06-11T16:06:54+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ross Douthat', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ross', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Douthat', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'orga