pe': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/11/fashion/resort2013therow-slide-1JJK/resort2013therow-slide-1JJK-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1365, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Row', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Fashion and Apparel', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-11T16:59:52+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Fashion & Style', 'section_name': 'Fashion & Style', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Slideshow', '_id': 'nyt://slideshow/4ca98e40-8bd0-5022-b450-f18568100efe', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://slideshow/4ca98e40-8bd0-5022-b450-f18568100efe'}, {'abstract': 'Toasting the grains in a hot pan before cooking the quinoa gives this richly textured salad a deeper flavor.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/06/12/health/nutrition/rainbow-quinoa-salad-with-fava-beans-and-herbs-recipes-for-health.html', 'snippet': 'Toasting the grains in a hot pan before cooking the quinoa gives this richly textured salad a deeper flavor.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Each of the three varieties of quinoa in rainbow quinoa -- red, black and blond – has a different texture, which is one of the things I like about the blend. The black grains are the hardest and never splay, and the red grains are harder than the white grains. The mixture takes about 5 minutes longer to cook than blond quinoa alone. I like to toast the grains in a hot pan first, for a richer flavor.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/12/health/12recipehealth/12recipehealth-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/06/12/health/12recipehealth/12recipeh