-fees-have-risen-report-finds/', 'snippet': 'Checking account disclosures still need simplification, a Pew report finds.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Even as we all pay greater attention to bank overdraft policies, some types of overdraft fees actually have increased, according to a new analysis from an arm of the Pew Charitable Trusts.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Some Bank Overdraft Fees Have Risen, Report Finds', 'kicker': 'Bucks', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Some Fees Rise For Overdrafts', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Banking and Financial Institutions', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Pew Charitable Trusts', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-11T18:00:57+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Your Money', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ann Carrns', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ann', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Carrns', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/1debbcbc-aced-5b51-88d7-7b97f69422ee', 'word_count': 413, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1debbcbc-aced-5b51-88d7-7b97f69422ee'}, {'abstract': 'From crows that stick pellets of dung in deer ears, to robins that can hear for worms, two new books offer remarkable stories and insight on the winged creatures.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/06/12/science/gifts-of-the-crow-and-bird-sense-the-lives-of-the-winged-set.html', 'snippet': 'From crows that stick pellets of dung in deer ears, to robins that can hear for worms, two new books offer remarkable stories and insight on the winged creatures.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The extremes of animal behavior can be a source of endless astonishment. Books have been written about insect sex. The antics of dogs and cats are sometimes hard to believe. And birds, those amazing birds: They build elaborate nests, learn lyrical songs, migrate impossibly long distances. But “Gifts of the Crow,” by John N. Marzluff and Tony Angell, includes a description of one behavior that even Aesop never imagined.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/12/science/12SCIB1/12SCIB1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 819, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/12/science/12SCIB1/12SCIB1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1638, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/12/science/12SCIB1/12SCIB1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/06/12/science/12SCIB1/12SCIB1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/12/science/12SCIB1/12SCIB1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Games Crows Play, and Other Winged Tales', 'kicker': 'Books on Science', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Games Crows Play, and Other Winged Tales', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Marzluff, John N', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Birkhead, Tim R', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Birds', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Angell, Tony', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-11T18:02:02+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Science', 'section_name': 'Science', 'byline': {'original': 'By James Gorman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'James', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gorman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/a47c5cb2-0c42-5f1a-9c67-4ee34a723ff4', 'word_count': 868, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a47c5cb2-0c42-5f1a-9c67-4ee34a723ff4'}, {'abstract': 'What can we make of the quasi-pornographic, awkward sex portrayed on Lena Dunham’s “Girls”?', 'web_url': 'https://6thfloor.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/06/11/girls-and-bad-sex/', 'snippet': 'What can we make of the quasi-pornographic, awkward sex portrayed on Lena Dunham’s “Girls”?', 'lead_paragraph': 'If, like me, you’re still wondering\xa0what to make of the sex in Lena Dunham’s “Girls” — (even after\xa0her hilarious interview with Andrew Goldman in this week’s magazine) — read Elaine Blair’s post on The New York Review of Books’s blog. It offers a welcome corrective to what she calls the “limited and literal reading” of the show’s depiction of “bad sex” by making sense of the pained feelings of recognition that Hannah and Adam’s relationship can induce in a female viewer.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '‘Girls’ and ‘Bad Sex’', 'kicker': 'The 6th Floor', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Dunham, Lena', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-11T18:05:16+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sheila Glaser', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sheila', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Glaser', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/06405f6d-30f1-56bb-a260-b15a8dbe673f', 'word_count': 324, 'uri': 'nyt://article/06405f6d-30f1-56bb-a260-b15a8dbe673f'}, {'abstract': 'The peacock mantis shrimp uses its clubs to rain quick, powerful blows on its prey, with little damage to itself because of the claws’ complex three-layer structure.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/06/12/science/peacock-mantis-shrimp-has-a-knockout-punch.html', 'snippet': 'The peacock mantis shrimp uses its clubs to rain quick, powerful blows on its prey, with little damage to itself because of the claws’ complex three-layer structure.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The colorful Indo-Pacific creature known as the peacock mantis shrimp is not a true shrimp but a distantly related crustacean. And its name and coloring are not the only distinctive things about it.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/12/science/12OBSHRIMP_SPAN/12OBOX1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 360, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/06/12/science/12OBSHRIMP_SPAN/12OBOX1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 360}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/12/science/12OBSHRIMP_SPAN/12OBOX1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/12/science/12OBSHRIMP_SPAN/12OBOX1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/12/science/12OBSHRIMP_SPAN/12OBOX1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height'