t Was Gone', 'kicker': 'Frequent Flier', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Never a Stressed Traveler Until His Passport Was Gone', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'TomorrowVentures LLC', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'AIRLINES AND AIRPLANES', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Business Travel', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-11T20:53:18+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Court Coursey', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Court', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Coursey', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/715ad236-549c-5bbd-bfa0-47af1810a0d0', 'word_count': 605, 'uri': 'nyt://article/715ad236-549c-5bbd-bfa0-47af1810a0d0'}, {'abstract': 'A lab notebook discovered in a dusty archive at Rutgers may help settle a 70-year argument over credit for the Nobel-winning discovery of streptomycin.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/06/12/science/notebooks-shed-light-on-an-antibiotic-discovery-and-a-mentors-betrayal.html', 'snippet': 'A lab notebook discovered in a dusty archive at Rutgers may help settle a 70-year argument over credit for the Nobel-winning discovery of streptomycin.', 'lead_paragraph': 'NEW BRUNSWICK, N.J. — For as long as archivists at Rutgers University could remember, a small cardboard box marked with the letter W in black ink had sat unopened in a dusty corner of the special collections of the Alexander Library. Next to it were 60 sturdy archive boxes of papers, a legacy of the university’s most famous scientist: Selman A. Waksman, who won a Nobel Prize in 1952 for the discovery of streptomycin, the first antibiotic to cure tuberculosis. ', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': 