ine': {'original': 'By Charlie Savage', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Charlie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Savage', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/35cda2e2-1d3a-5d6d-a0b2-62ea8a37b450', 'word_count': 942, 'uri': 'nyt://article/35cda2e2-1d3a-5d6d-a0b2-62ea8a37b450'}, {'abstract': 'European Internet entrepreneurs are hoping their experience in foreign markets will help them compete with better-financed American rivals.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2012/06/11/companies-born-in-europe-but-based-on-the-planet/', 'snippet': 'European Internet entrepreneurs are hoping their experience in foreign markets will help them compete with better-financed American rivals.', 'lead_paragraph': 'LONDON — When Alex Ljung, a multilingual entrepreneur, co-founded the audio-sharing Web service SoundCloud in Stockholm in 2007, he knew the company needed an international expansion strategy right away. The company didn’t have a big domestic market or access to a large pool of world-class developers.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Companies Born in Europe, but Based on the Planet', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Companies Born in Europe, but Based on the Planet', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Entrepreneurship', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Start-ups', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Venture Capital', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'SoundCloud', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-11T21:00:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Mark Scott', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mark', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Scott', 'qua