y because of job loss.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Pensions Under Stress, Worldwide', 'kicker': 'Economix', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Pensions and Retirement Plans', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Organization for Economic Cooperation and Development', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-11T22:17:48+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Economy', 'byline': {'original': 'By Motoko Rich', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Motoko', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rich', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/bec9e5fb-f90d-5513-a72a-faa82e4b77ae', 'word_count': 493, 'uri': 'nyt://article/bec9e5fb-f90d-5513-a72a-faa82e4b77ae'}, {'abstract': 'A well-regarded restaurant in Williamsburg temporarily closed on Monday after its three top chefs left.', 'web_url': 'https://dinersjournal.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/06/11/isa-shuts-after-a-staff-shakeup/', 'snippet': 'A well-regarded restaurant in Williamsburg temporarily closed on Monday after its three top chefs left.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Isa, the high-profile restaurant that Taavo Somer opened last summer in Williamsburg, Brooklyn, closed on Monday after its chef, Ignacio Mattos; sous-chef, Jose Ramirez Ruiz; and pastry chef, Pam Yung, left the business. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Isa Shuts and Regroups After a Staff Shakeup', 'kicker': 'Diner&#039;s Journal', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Restaurants', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Isa (Brooklyn, NY, Restaurant)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Williamsburg (NYC)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-11T22:18:39+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Florence Fabricant', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Florence', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Fabricant', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/134bbd7e-224e-5a01-a662-ea59f04cad87', 'word_count': 312, 'uri': 'nyt://article/134bbd7e-224e-5a01-a662-ea59f04cad87'}, {'abstract': 'David Pogue learned a lot at the Apple Worldwide Developers’ Conference presentation Monday. He lays it all out here, including news about Airport Express and two photo programs.', 'web_url': 'https://pogue.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/06/11/an-explanation-of-the-news-from-apple/', 'snippet': 'David Pogue learned a lot at the Apple Worldwide Developers’ Conference presentation Monday. He lays it all out here, including news about Airport Express and two photo programs.', 'lead_paragraph': '5:04 p.m. | Updated Clarifying information on future MacPro computers.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'An Explanation of the News From Apple', 'kicker': 'Pogue&#039;s Posts', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Automobiles', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computers and the Internet', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'iPad', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'iPhone', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'iPod', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Maps', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Smartphones', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Voice Recognition Systems', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Apple Incorporated', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-11T22:20:32+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'byline': {'original': 'By David Pogue', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Pogue', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6777e2fa-be87-54b8-bbad-56924ac69470', 'word_count': 1763, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6777e2fa-be87-54b8-bbad-56924ac69470'}, {'abstract': 'David Morrissey plays a British detective in two episodes of “Thorne,” on Encore.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/06/12/arts/television/david-morrissey-plays-a-cop-in-thorne-on-encore.html', 'snippet': 'David Morrissey plays a British detective in two episodes of “Thorne,” on Encore.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Picture a bracket-style Fictional Police Brood-Off in which all those moody cops populating the international television landscape would compete to see who could convey the most while saying the least. The smart money would be on Tom Selleck’s growly Jesse Stone to come out of the American bracket. In the championship he might well meet David Morrissey’s Tom Thorne, who, based on the two intense dramas being offered this week on Encore, would have an excellent shot at being the British finalist.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/11/arts/thorne/thorne-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 330, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/06/11/arts/thorne/thorne-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 330}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/11/arts/thorne/thorne-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 6