', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jon Pareles', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jon', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Pareles', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/625ddfad-a456-5381-9a19-faba133d6d70', 'word_count': 1195, 'uri': 'nyt://article/625ddfad-a456-5381-9a19-faba133d6d70'}, {'abstract': 'During Monday’s Apple developer conference, Tim Cook, Apple’s chief executive, was no longer compared to Steven P. Jobs, the co-founder of Apple, who often commanded an Apple keynote as a rock star. But Mr. Cook had control and command, so much so that it went unremarked upon.', 'web_url': 'https://bits.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/06/11/at-apple-keynote-no-talk-of-steve-jobs-or-tim-cook/', 'snippet': 'During Monday’s Apple developer conference, Tim Cook, Apple’s chief executive, was no longer compared to Steven P. Jobs, the co-founder of Apple, who often commanded an Apple keynote as a rock star. But Mr. Cook had control and command, so much so...', 'lead_paragraph': 'Last year, when Tim Cook, Apple’s chief\xa0executive, took the stage in San Francisco to present the new iPad, he was watched like an alien who had just landed at Area 51.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'At Apple Keynote, Talk Is of Products, Not Steve Jobs or Tim Cook', 'kicker': 'Bits', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Blogs and Blogging (Internet)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cook, Timothy D', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Jobs, Steven P', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Apple Incorporated', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-11T22:46:46+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nick Bilton', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nick', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bilton', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c947216e-63b7-5dc3-b74c-6f7af860c5cb', 'word_count': 253, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c947216e-63b7-5dc3-b74c-6f7af860c5cb'}, {'abstract': 'During its presentation on Monday, Apple highlighted features that it made just for China. It shows just how important that market has become for the company.', 'web_url': 'https://bits.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/06/11/apple-china-software/', 'snippet': 'During its presentation on Monday, Apple highlighted features that it made just for China. It shows just how important that market has become for the company.', 'lead_paragraph': 'While getting seated at Apple’s keynote presentation at its developers’ conference on Monday, I noticed something peculiar compared to past events: an unusually large number of Chinese bloggers. As the event unraveled, it became clear why so many were invited. China is an extremely important market for Apple now.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Apple Doubles Down on China', 'kicker': 'Bits', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Chinese Language', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'iPhone', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Software', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Apple Incorporated', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-11T22:55:02+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'byline': {'original': 'By Brian X. Chen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Brian', 'middlename': 'X.', 'lastname': 'Chen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': '