'headline': {'main': 'Words We Love Too Much', 'kicker': 'After Deadline', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-06-12T12:00:58+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Times Topics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Philip B. Corbett', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Philip', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/479717ca-f854-518f-b920-8f4323f02d0c', 'word_count': 1231, 'uri': 'nyt://article/479717ca-f854-518f-b920-8f4323f02d0c'}, {'abstract': 'A company dreamed up a way of treating the alloy produced in the recycling process so that it would be more suitable for remaking the can.', 'web_url': 'https://green.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/06/12/toward-a-greener-soda-can/', 'snippet': 'A company dreamed up a way of treating the alloy produced in the recycling process so that it would be more suitable for remaking the can.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Of all the materials that are commonly dropped in recycling bins, aluminum is by far the most valuable. New aluminum sells for almost $2,000 a metric ton, so recycling old cans would seem to be profitable. It takes about 75,000 cans to make a metric ton, so each one should be worth about 2.5 cents. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Toward a Greener Soda Can', 'kicker': 'Green', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Aluminum', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Metals and Minerals', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Recycling of Waste Materials', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-12T12:01:41+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Science', 'subsection_name': 'Environment', 'byline': {'original': 'By Matthew L. Wald', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Matthew', 'middlename': 'L.', 'lastname': 'Wald', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/69cbcac3-adf1-53e2-b971-e1b58d14f808', 'word_count': 540, 'uri': 'nyt://article/69cbcac3-adf1-53e2-b971-e1b58d14f808'}, {'abstract': 'A summary of what’s on today’s editorial page.', 'web_url': 'https://takingnote.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/06/12/opinion-report-serial-bailouts/', 'snippet': 'A summary of what’s on today’s editorial page.', 'lead_paragraph': 'From the editorial page', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Opinion Report: Serial Bailouts', 'kicker': 'Taking Note', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-06-12T12:03:50+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andrew Rosenthal', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andrew', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rosenthal', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/329c4c1a-2c12-5bfb-a038-e7a70697b355', 'word_count': 86, 'uri': 'nyt://article/329c4c1a-2c12-5bfb-a038-e7a70697b355'}, {'abstract': 'Respect for water reduces the risk of drowning, family net worth drops to a level from the early 1990s, making greener soda cans and other consumer-focused news from The New York Times.', 'web_url': 'https://bucks.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/06/12/tuesday-reading-cutting-the-risk-of-drowning/', 'snippet': 'Respect for water reduces the risk of drowning, family net worth drops to a level from the early 1990s, making greener soda cans and other consumer-focused news from The New York Times.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A variety of consumer-focused articles appears daily in The New York Times and on our blogs. Each weekday morning, we gather them together here so you can quickly scan the news that could hit you in your wallet.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Tuesday Reading: Cutting the Risk of Drowning', 'kicker': 'Bucks', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-06-12T12:19:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Your Money', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ann Carrns', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ann', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Carrns', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/598c94c1-0a05-56d6-b150-905d6f617137', 'word_count': 130, 'uri': 'nyt://article/598c94c1-0a05-56d6-b150-905d6f617137'}, {'abstract': 'He’s not ecstatic.', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/06/12/blanchard-on-latvia/', 'snippet': 'He’s not ecstatic.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Olivier Blanchard, the IMF’s chief economist (and a former colleague of mine at MIT), has a post on Latvia’s economic experience. Given his position, and the sacred status Latvia has acquired among the austerians, he is necessarily cautious in his wording. But the substance is pretty much the same as what I said: one year of pretty good growth after an incredible decline — growth that still leaves you 15 percent below previous peak — is not exactly a proof of concept for fiscal austerity.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Blanchard on Latvia', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-06-12T12:25:44+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/bced6e1b-1b88-542b-a4de-8790cb86e211', 'word_count': 85, 'uri': 'nyt://article/bced6e1b-1b88-542b-a4de-8790cb86e211'}, {'abstract': 'Metropolitan Diary: At the end of her shift, a nurse gets a ride home from someone who already knows the way.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/06/12/a-regular-evening-escort/', 'snippet': 'Metropolitan Diary: At the end of her shift, a nurse gets a ride home from someone who already knows the way.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Dear Diary: As a very tired floor nurse at Mount Sinai Hospital in Harlem, I often took a cab home after work to my apartment in Inwood. Over the course of two years, a particular livery cab driver began to anticipate my schedule and would often wait for me outside the hospital around the end of my shift. I appreciated this, as it meant I did not have to haggle over the price of my ride with a different driver every night.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '21', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Regular Evening Escort', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Awaking to a Masked Visitor, and Other Reader Tales', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Taxicabs and Taxicab Drivers', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Mount Sinai Medical Center', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'HARLEM (NYC)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'INWOOD (NYC)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-12T12:30:38+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Rachel Brugger', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Rachel', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Brugger', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 