{'firstname': 'Mary', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Pilon', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/dbe9c03e-4ae8-53a3-92f4-7a3b08e40404', 'word_count': 356, 'uri': 'nyt://article/dbe9c03e-4ae8-53a3-92f4-7a3b08e40404'}, {'abstract': 'The big news on Monday was Apple’s announcement of new products at the opening day of its developers conference in San Francisco.', 'web_url': 'https://bits.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/06/12/daily-report-its-another-apple-day/', 'snippet': 'The big news on Monday was Apple’s announcement of new products at the opening day of its developers conference in San Francisco.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The big news on Monday was Apple’s announcement of new products at the opening day of its developers’ conference in San Francisco. As Brian X. Chen and Nick Wingfield report in Tuesday’s New York Times, Apple unveiled a new operating system for its iPhone and iPad, as well as an overhaul of its Mac line of laptops.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/11/30/technology/bits-daily-report/bits-daily-report-articleLarge-v4.png', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/11/30/technology/bits-daily-report/bits-daily-report-articleLarge-v4.png', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/11/30/technology/bits-daily-report/bits-daily-report-jumbo-v6.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/11/30/technology/bits-daily-report/bits-daily-report-superJumbo-v3.png', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/11/30/technology/bits-daily-report/bits-daily-report-thumbStandard-v5.png', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/11/30/technology/bits-daily-report/bits-daily-report-thumbStandard-v5.png', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/11/30/technology/bits-daily-report/bits-daily-report-thumbLarge-v5.png', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Daily Report: It’s Another Apple Day', 'kicker': 'Bits', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computers and the Internet', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mac OS (Operating System)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Apple Incorporated', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-12T13:35:55+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0d61e11d-26af-5b2e-b3a8-16d659e59025', 'word_count': 341, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0d61e11d-26af-5b2e-b3a8-16d659e59025'}, {'abstract': 'All 10 groups in the Standard & Poor’s 500-stock index rose, with commodity, financial and industrial shares gaining the most.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/06/13/business/daily-stock-market-activity.html', 'snippet': 'All 10 groups in the Standard & Poor’s 500-stock index rose, with commodity, financial and industrial shares gaining the most.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Stocks advanced on Tuesday, rebounding from a decline on Monday, amid speculation the Federal Reserve will act to stimulate the economy and after the European Central Bank endorsed a plan to guarantee bank deposits.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Wall Street Is Lifted by Speculation That the Federal Reserve Will Act to Spur the Economy', 'kicker': 'Stocks and Bonds', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Wall Street Is Lifted by Speculation That the Federal Reserve Will Act to Spur the Economy', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'European Sovereign Debt Crisis (2010- )', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Stocks and Bonds', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Dow Jones Stock Average', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-12T13:41:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Bloomberg News', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Bloomberg', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'News', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/eda72a9a-cab1-5338-93b7-d7eb94f5e648', 'word_count': 490, 'uri': 'nyt://article/eda72a9a-cab1-5338-93b7-d7eb94f5e648'}, {'abstract': 'Environmental groups had threatened to sue if the agency did not allot time for a full-scale public review and comment on the plan for leases in the Talladega National Forest.', 'web_url': 'https://green.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/06/12/on-our-radar-a-delay-in-federal-gas-leases/', 'snippet': 'Environmental groups had threatened to sue if the agency did not allot time for a full-scale public review and comment on the plan for leases in the Talladega National Forest.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Facing threats of lawsuits, the Bureau of Land Management delays the sale of oil and natural gas leases in the Talladega National Forest in Alabama and says it will seek public comment. The sale, covering 43,000 acres, had been scheduled to begin on Thursday. [Bureau of Land Management] ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'On Our Radar: A Delay in Federal Gas Leases', 'kicker': 'Green', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-06-12T13:47:38+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Science', 'subsection_name': 'Environment', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/18c604f3-8314-5d57-9c87-7c66675544ff', 'word_count': 207, 'uri': 'nyt://article/18c604f3-8314-5d57-9c87-7c66675544ff'}, {'abstract': 'The percentage of New York City students graduating from public high schools within four years was flat in 2011 compared with the year before, marking the first time the rate has not significantly increased in the last six years. Still, the mayor and schools chancellor hailed the new numbers -- though they also expressed concerns over what will happen when higher graduation standards take effect next year.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/schoolbook/2012/06/12/city-graduation-rate-barely-budges-from-last-year/', 'snippet': 'The percentage of New York City students graduating from public high schools within four years was flat in 2011 compared with the year before, marking the first time the rate has not significantly increased in the last six years. Still, the mayor ...', 'lead_paragraph': '', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'City Graduation Rate Barely Budges From Last Year ', 'kicker': 'SchoolBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-06-12T13:50:02+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Education', 'byline': {'original': 'By MARIA NEWMAN', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Maria', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'NEWMAN', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ac59244d-8248-5fac-97e0-249e921d45b2', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ac59244d-8248-5fac-97e0-249e921d45b2'}, {'abstract': 'A collection of links from the reporters and editors of the Dining section.', 'web_url': 'https://dinersjournal.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/06/12/what-were-reading-455/', 'snippet': 'A collection of links from the reporters and editors of the Dining section.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Trader Joe’s Secrets: An advertising executive, one Mark Gardiner, was curious about how Trader Joe’s became so successful without any advertising. So he went undercover as an entry-level employee when the chain opened its first store in Kansas City. — Julia Moskin', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'What We’re Reading', 'kicker': 'Diner&#039;s Journal', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-06-12T13:53:18+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/baad1ddb-dd59-5242-8854-dc1ec268b37d', 'word_count': 542, 'uri': 'nyt://article/baad1ddb-dd59-5242-8854-dc1ec268b37d'}, {'abstract': 'Two new books, Paul Krugman’s “End This Depression Now!” and Timothy Noah’s “Great Divergence,” are an attempt to disabuse the rich of the idea that we’re in a sustainable recovery.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/06/13/books/paul-krugman-and-timothy-noah-on-the-economy.html', 'snippet': 'Two new books, Paul Krugman’s “End This Depression Now!” and Timothy Noah’s “Great Divergence,” are an attempt to disabuse the rich of the idea that we’re in a sustainable recovery.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Rich people have more power than poor people, and they use that power to get what they want — which is, normally, more wealth and more power. Across America, politicians invariably reflect the views of their richest constituents. And the Federal Reserve, too, appears to have been captured by the rich: It seems much more worried about the specter of possible future inflation (which might be bad for the rich) than it is about the tragedy of present-day unemployment (which is calamitous for today’s jobless). ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/13/arts/13book-cnd/13book-cnd-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 725, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/13/arts/13book-cnd/13book-cnd-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1450, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/13/arts/13book-cnd/13book-cnd-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/06/13/arts/13book-cnd/13book-cnd-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/13/arts/13book-cnd/13book-cnd-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 212, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Speaking to the Haves, in a Plea to Consider All the Have-Nots', 'kicker': 'Books of The Times', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Speaking to the Haves, In a Plea to Consider All The Have-Nots', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Economic Conditions and Trends', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': "The Great Divergence: America's Growing Inequality Crisis and What We Can Do about It (Book)", 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Krugman, Paul', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Economy', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Noah, Timothy', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Income Inequality', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-12T13:54:23+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Books', 'byline': {'original': 'By Felix Salmon', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Felix', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Salmon', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/0c920a1e-9dad-5db4-875b-6234c34b8da2', 'word_count': 1236, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0c920a1e-9dad-5db4-875b-6234c34b8da2'}, {'abstract': 'A new by-appointment-only boutique pairs small-scale pieces alongside boldface names.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/06/12/artisanal-threads-graymarket-in-williamsburg/', 'snippet': 'A new by-appointment-only boutique pairs small-scale pieces alongside boldface names.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Williamsburg is already recognized as a center of artisanal pickle, jam and cheese production. With the recent opening of Graymarket, a by-appointment-only boutique-cum-gallery dedicated to dead-stock clothing made by designers like Paul Harnden, Carpe Diem, Carol Christian Poell and Katie Gallagher, it’s time to add artisanal clothing to that list. The Graymarket founders, Wei Du and Alex Kasavin, met online, where artisanal clothing fans buy, trade and spend many, many characters analyzing the provenance and lineage of their limited-edition obsessions. “Buttonholes, stitching, dye lots — these kind of things are taken very seriously,” Du says. “People try to find out exactly what tannery the leather in a jacket comes from.” Many artisanal clothing designers work in black, the better to emphasize the tailoring and technique that goes into each garment, and that’s reflected in the goth cast of many of the clothes on Graymarket’s racks. But Due and Kasavin stress that they’re not purists, a stance they’ve underlined both in the tongue-in-cheek name they gave their shop — it’s a reference to goods being traded legally but not via the distribution channels intended by their manufacturers — and in the stock they carry, which includes the work of both small-scale, cult studios and boldface names like Balenciaga, Givenchy and Alaïa, designers that not all artisanal enthusiasts would accept as legit. “We don’t want to be about labels,” Kasavin says. “To us, it’s about technique and narrative, not about limiting what we can sell. We take something glam, like a jacket by Givenchy, and hang it next to something with a more street feel. We want to open up the possibilities.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Artisanal Threads | Graymarket in Williamsburg', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-06-12T14:00:16+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nancy MacDonell', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nancy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'MacDonell', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/12005b4e-5f0e-55f3-a944-7a41921c09be', 'word_count': 275, 'uri': 'nyt://article/12005b4e-5f0e-55f3-a944-7a41921c09be'}, {'abstract': 'Las Vegas casinos are turning to electronic dance music in a big way, and journalists return to produce a special election edition of a Greek newspaper that hasn’t published since December.', 'web_url': 'https://mediadecoder.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/06/12/the-breakfast-meeting-apple-ditches-google-maps-and-you-really-liked-cop-and-a-half/', 'snippet': 'Las Vegas casinos are turning to electronic dance music in a big way, and journalists return to produce a special election edition of a Greek newspaper that hasn’t published since December.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Apple on Monday introduced its own mapping system for iPhones and iPads, which will replace Google’s, in another sign that Apple is distancing itself from a company it once considered a close partner, Brian X. Chen and Nick Wingfield write. The announcement came during a developer conference, and reflects Apple’s efforts to catch up with Google on maps, including acquiring three mapping companies in three years. Also, they write,', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Breakfast Meeting: Apple Ditches Google Maps, and You Really Liked ‘Cop and a Half’?', 'kicker': 'Media Decoder', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-06-12T14:09:39+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Media', 'byline': {'original': 'By Noam Cohen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Noam', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cohen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/489bb1f0-3a6e-58e0-a9df-dc1383e8e106', 'word_count': 447, 'uri': 'nyt://article/489bb1f0-3a6e-58e0-a9df-dc1383e8e106'}, {'abstract': 'Apple’s tools for iPhone tracking and security can lock up the data on a lost or stolen iPhone — and may help locate a missing mobile.', 'web_url': 'https://gadgetwise.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/06/12/qa-tracking-a-missing-iphone/', 'snippet': 'Apple’s tools for iPhone tracking and security can lock up the data on a lost or stolen iPhone — and may help locate a missing mobile.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Q.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Q&A: Tracking a Missing iPhone', 'kicker': 'Gadgetwise', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Q&A: Tracking an iPhone That’s Gone Missing', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computer Security', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'iPhone', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mobile Applications', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Apple Incorporated', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'iCloud', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-12T14:14:55+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'subsection_name': 'Personal Tech', 'byline': {'original': 'By J.d. Biersdorfer', 'person': [{'firstname': '', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3b014ed8-a32b-5e85-a76e-6fd571e42f2f', 'word_count': 272, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3b014ed8-a32b-5e85-a76e-6fd571e42f2f'}, {'abstract': 'The N.C.A.A. president answered questions about new academic standards, conference realignment and the B.C.S. system.', 'web_url': 'https://thequad.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/06/12/qa-with-mark-emmert-n-c-a-a-president/', 'snippet': 'The N.C.A.A. president answered questions about new academic standards, conference realignment and the B.C.S. system.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In the 20 months since he took over as president of the N.C.A.A., Mark Emmert has dealt with scandals, witnessed realignment, and been held powerless as the conferences negotiated their own lucrative TV deals and now work to decide how to decide a national champion in college football. All while publicly lauding an ideal that is outdated for some, a truism for others: the student-athlete.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Q. and A. With Mark Emmert, N.C.A.A. President', 'kicker': 'The Quad', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'College Athletics', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Emmert, Mark', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Collegiate Athletic Assn', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-12T14:21:29+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Pro Basketball', 'byline': {'original': 'By Tim Rohan', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Tim', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rohan', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/13eb311f-a23e-5751-bf8b-90f7467f96c3', 'word_count': 2150, 'uri': 'nyt://article/13eb311f-a23e-5751-bf8b-90f7467f96c3'}, {'abstract': 'My daughter’s dyslexia may mean that she never learns to share my love of language and literature. What will we share instead?', 'web_url': 'https://parenting.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/06/12/can-a-daughter-with-dyslexia-learn-to-love-words/', 'snippet': 'My daughter’s dyslexia may mean that she never learns to share my love of language and literature. What will we share instead?', 'lead_paragraph': 'My heart has been heavy recently with a sense of loss. I’m grieving, but over something that hasn’t actually been taken — and perhaps won’t ever be completely lost at all.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'head