bears down once again, there are scores of gadgets for chilling wines. But instead of frozen gels and wraps and whatnot, these glass carafes rely on the simplest method: ice. You pour in the wine, then fill an insert with ice cubes, cooling the wine from the inside out. A bottle of room-temperature white can be a palatable 45 to 50 degrees in about 40 minutes. (You may have to refill the ice holder to maintain the chill during serving.) The insert in the Metrokane model (below, left) is metal, so the ice does not melt quite as fast as in a glass insert.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/13/dining/13STUFF_CHILLERS/13STUFF_CHILLERS-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1853, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/13/dining/13STUFF_CHILLERS/13STUFF_CHILLERS-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1132, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/13/dining/13STUFF_CHILLERS/13STUFF_CHILLERS-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/06/13/dining/13STUFF_CHILLERS/13STUFF_CHILLERS-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/13/dining/13STUFF_CHILLERS/13STUFF_CHILLERS-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 225, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Reverting to Ice to Chill the Wine', 'kicker': 'Food Stuff', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Reverting to Ice To Chill the Wine', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Wines', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-12T16:56:38+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Dining', 'section_name': 'Food', 'byline': {'original': 'By Florence Fabricant', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Florence', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Fabricant', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2f96978a-50a5-5a23-9e66-85f99ded7ac2', 'word_count': 152, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2f96978a-50a5-5a23-9e66-85f99ded7ac2'}, {'abstract': 'The documentary “Marina Abramovic the Artist Is Present” is a portrait more celebratory than analytical of the Yugoslav-born performance artist, directed by Matthew Akers.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/06/13/movies/marina-abramovic-the-artist-is-present-a-documentary-on-her-life.html', 'snippet': 'The documentary “Marina Abramovic the Artist Is Present” is a portrait more celebratory than analytical of the Yugoslav-born performance artist, directed by Matthew Akers.', 'lead_paragraph': '“The Artist Is Present,” the centerpiece of an identically named 2010 retrospective of work by the performance artist Marina Abramovic at the Museum of Modern Art in New York, was one of those rare events that breach the wall dividing the art world from popular culture. Over 90 days — from the beginning of March through the end of May — hundreds of thousands of people streamed through MoMA, lining up (sometimes after camping out on 53rd Street the night before) for the opportunity to see Ms. Abramovic face to face. She sat in a plain wooden chair, gazing straight ahead, silently, into the eyes of whichever museum patron happened to be seated across from her. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': '