ocument_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By David Barboza', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Barboza', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e78bd8bb-8e9c-502a-89cf-45167c21de06', 'word_count': 424, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e78bd8bb-8e9c-502a-89cf-45167c21de06'}, {'abstract': 'The Heat-Thunder matchup pits a turnover-prone offense against a stifling defense.', 'web_url': 'https://offthedribble.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/06/12/finals-present-classic-clash-of-offense-vs-defense/', 'snippet': 'The Heat-Thunder matchup pits a turnover-prone offense against a stifling defense.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Given the participants, these N.B.A. finals will likely be remembered for something spectacular. Perhaps there will be some breathtaking dunks from Dwyane Wade and LeBron James, or late-game heroics from one of the Thunder’s young stars. Certainly the first championship for either James or Kevin Durant will be one for the history books. For statisticians, however, it will be another case of great offense versus great defense. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Finals Present Classic Clash of Offense vs. Defense', 'kicker': 'Off the Dribble', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Basketball', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bosh, Chris', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Durant, Kevin', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Howard, Juwan', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'James, LeBron', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Perkins, Kendrick', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Wade, Dwyane', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Westbrook, Russell', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Miami Heat', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Oklahoma City Thunder', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-12T17:41:42+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Pro Basketball', 'byline': {'original': 'By Mike Kurylo', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mike', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kurylo', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/37c7e67d-b8d1-53de-a8c3-92cdad13a96e', 'word_count': 442, 'uri': 'nyt://article/37c7e67d-b8d1-53de-a8c3-92cdad13a96e'}, {'abstract': 'Thomas Rinaldi, an architectural designer, is on a mission to catalog the city’s neon signs, before they disappear.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/06/12/capturing-the-glow-of-old-new-york/', 'snippet': 'Thomas Rinaldi, an architectural designer, is on a mission to catalog the city’s neon signs, before they disappear.', 'lead_paragraph': 'One hundred and fourteen years ago, an experiment in a London laboratory helped shape the face of modern New York City. On June 12, 1898, two British chemists discovered neon.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '24', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Capturing the Glow of Old New York', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Capturing City’s Glow Before It Fades', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Lighting', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-12T17:50:12+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Aidan Gardiner', 'person': [{'f