 retail offerings at the airport.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Travelers who pass through La Guardia Airport’s terminal D will likely do a double take when, in place of the usual newsstand, coffee shop or kiosk selling noise-canceling headphones, they spot the new boutique Zona. The shop delivers a major upgrade to the retail offerings at the airport; it’s stocked with beauty products, jewelry and accessories for men and women, stationery and children’s toys and gifts, all by a roster of innovative designers and manufacturers. “Our first sale, at 4:30 in the morning while we were still setting up the space, was a $60 John Derian paperweight,” says Jesse James, the founder and creative director of Aesthetic Movement, the company that was hired to merchandise and brand the store. (It is run by the airport retailer A.E.I. Retail.) Prices range from $4.99 for Hurraw! Lip balms to $100 for a handmade necktie by Forage to $150 for a Thomaspaul wool shawl, and top out at $425 for a pearl and pyrite necklace by Carolina. Besides carrying items a traveler would expect, like a stylish toothbrush, James said it was important to “bring in a few things that made no sense, like a gold leather Webster’s dictionary or Harry Allen’s gold pig bank — things that are interesting and lovely, but we thought, honestly, Who will buy this in an airport?” Plenty of people, apparently. Journal sets inscribed with Oscar Wilde quotes, $109, have already sold out.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'La Guardia’s Retail Therapy', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-06-12T21:03:46+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Rima Suqi', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Rima', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Suqi', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4c1290df-1b45-5a14-b9e0-6f4961982f67', 'word_count': 246, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4c1290df-1b45-5a14-b9e0-6f4961982f67'}, {'abstract': 'Two summery gin cocktails are a refreshing change from your usual gin and tonic.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/06/13/dining/a-couple-of-gin-cocktails.html', 'snippet': 'Two summery gin cocktails are a refreshing change from your usual gin and tonic.', 'lead_paragraph': 'What to pair with gin? Back in the day it would have been a cigarette. Instead, in place of a food pairing, here are a couple of cocktails to mix with the more flavorful American gins, provided by the drinks historian David Wondrich, one of our panelists. His cocktails are light and summery, and a refreshing change from your usual gin and tonic. The punch recipe makes 20 servings; assume that refills are likely to be in demand. The other recipe was adapted by Mr. Wondrich from one by the 19th-century bartender Jerry Thomas. The optional sparkling rosé is my own idea, a felicitously chic addition that turns the drink into something close to a French 75.\xa0', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/13/dining/13PAIR_SPAN/13PAIR_SPAN-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 390, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/06/13/dining/13PAIR_SPAN/13PAIR_SPAN-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 390}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/13/dining/13PAIR_SPAN/13PAIR_SPAN-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 665, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/13/dining/13PAIR_SPAN/13PAIR_S