+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Azam Ahmed and Peter Lattman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Azam', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ahmed', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Peter', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Lattman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6819c36c-e0d4-5b8e-b0d6-ca6ea8d62347', 'word_count': 956, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6819c36c-e0d4-5b8e-b0d6-ca6ea8d62347'}, {'abstract': 'Ethan Lipton’s wine-dark satire, “Luther,” looks at a society in which Americans adopt military veterans.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/06/14/theater/reviews/luther-by-ethan-lipton-at-here-arts-center.html', 'snippet': 'Ethan Lipton’s wine-dark satire, “Luther,” looks at a society in which Americans adopt military veterans.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Can a man who kills other men for money retain his humanity? What if his employer is the United States government?', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/14/arts/luther/luther-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 344, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/06/14/arts/luther/luther-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 344}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/14/arts/luther/luther-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 616, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/14/arts/luther/luther-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1231, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/14/arts/luther/luther-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/06/14/arts/luther/luther-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/14/arts/luther/luther-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Hire a Vet? Forget That. Adopt One Instead.', 'kicker': 'Theater Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Hire a Vet? Forget That. Adopt One Instead.', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Clubbed Thumb', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Schmoll, Ken Rus', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Frazier, Gibson', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lipton, Ethan', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-13T19:11:34+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Theater', 'subsection_name': 'Theater Reviews', 'byline': {'original': 'By Catherine Rampell', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Catherine', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rampell', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/6ea1a419-7a08-5b97-a4de-c0c5bd83392c', 'word_count': 352, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6ea1a419-7a08-5b97-a4de-c0c5bd83392c'}, {'abstract': 'In this week’s links: theories of addiction; Rorty’s legacy; Darwin’s literary ambitions, and more', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/06/13/stone-links-addiction-and-agency/', 'snippet': 'In this week’s links: theories of addiction; Rorty’s legacy; Darwin’s literary ambitions, and more', 'lead_paragraph': 'This week The Stone featured an essay by Peg O’Connor on addiction, the brain and the will. Philosophy Now has more on the topic. Addictive actions are often thought to undermine the will. But can we think of them more simply as representing nothing more than capricious changes of mind? There is a view of temptation and the will, tracing back to Plato’s “Protagoras,” that takes them to be just that. It is an elegant theory, as it makes no use of the thorny notion of akrasia, or weakness of will. Alan Brody argues at Philosophy Now, though, that we can find another view in Plato that also refuses the concept of akrasia while still respecting the way addiction disrupts agency, in particular our capacity to regulate our actions. The view is this: when one acts on an addictive impulse, one’s will remains intact. It is, rather, the capacity to judge what is the best course of action that fails one; the addictive course momentarily appears to be the best available.What Rorty Wrought: Santiago Zabala marks the five year anniversary of Richard Rorty’s passing with this tribute in Al-Jazeera. Rorty ought to be the model for 21st century philosophy, Zabala argues, owing to his eclectic approach to philosophical method and the capacity it gave him to transcend narrow debates within Anglo-American philosophy and see the way philosophy might figure productively in the broader intellectual and political life of America and Europe. But politics should also inform philosophy: “If you take care of freedom,” Rorty says, “truth will take care of itself.” For Rorty, truth ought to be conceived not as correspondence to a mind-independent reality, but pragmatically, as consisting in those conclusions an open and free community might arrive at in the long run of inquiry. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Stone Links: Addiction and Agency', 'kicker': 'The Stone', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Drones (Pilotless Planes)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Philosophy', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Brody, Alan', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Rorty, Richard', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-13T19:18:41+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Mark de Silva', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mark', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'de Silva', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f00ffaca-ebfa-5faf-b117-350df4e18afb', 'word_count': 486, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f00ffaca-ebfa-5faf-b117-350df4e18afb'}, {'abstract': 'The Heat-Thunder game on Tuesday was the highest-rated Game 1 of the finals since ESPN/ABC acquired N.B.A. rights in 2002-03.', 'web_url': 'https://offthedribble.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/06/13/heat-thunder-gets-a-big-number/', 'snippet': 'The Heat-Thunder game on Tuesday was the highest-rated Game 1 of the finals since ESPN/ABC acquired N.B.A. rights in 2002-03.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Game 1 of the N.B.A. finals Tuesday night proved that a small-market team like the Oklahoma City Thunder can resonate with fans beyond its region. The Thunder’s 105-94 over the Miami Heat was seen by an average of 16.2 million viewers, the most for a Game 1 since ESPN/ABC acquired N.B.A. rights in 2002-03.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Heat-Thunder Gets a Big Number', 'kicker': 'Off the Dribble', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Strong Ratings for Game 1 of Finals', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Basketball', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'ABC INC', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Los Angeles Lakers', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Oklahoma City Thunder', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-13T19:21:59+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Pro Basketball', 'byline': {'original': 'By Richard Sandomir', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Richard', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Sandomir', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/39b935a0-7b13-5ab6-862a-ad83f218cfc1', 'word_count': 144, 'uri': 'nyt://article/39b935a0-7b13-5ab6-862a-ad83f218cfc1'}, {'abstract': 'Florida’s former governor can look skeptically at his party only because he’s on the sidelines and not in the fray.', 'web_url': 'https://bruni.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/06/13/jeb-bushs-candor/', 'snippet': 'Florida’s former governor can look skeptically at his party only because he’s on the sidelines and not in the fray.', 'lead_paragraph': 'As you no doubt read or heard, Jeb Bush made news earlier this week, saying that neither his father nor Ronald Reagan would have found a comfortable place in the Republican Party of today. This is hardly a revelatory observation. But it’s an essential one, presented in this case by a member of one of the party’s ruling clans (at least once upon a time), as my colleague Jim Rutenberg noted in his terrific news article about Bush’s comments.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Jeb Bush’s Candor', 'kicker': 'Frank Bruni', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bush, Jeb', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Florida', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-13T19:29:40+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Frank Bruni', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Frank', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bruni', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/29a1d71e-d02e-549b-b745-8d47f8d8f525', 'word_count': 823, 'uri': 'nyt://article/29a1d71e-d02e-549b-b745-8d47f8d8f525'}, {'abstract': 'The architect David Rockwell designed the pavilion for Southampton Center.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/06/13/pavilion-planned-for-new-arts-center-in-southampton-village/', 'snippet': 'The architect David Rockwell designed the pavilion for Southampton Center.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The architect David Rockwell has designed a pavilion for the Southampton Center, a new cultural and artistic organization that will occupy the Parrish Art Museum’s former site on Jobs Lane in Southampton Village, N.Y. (The Parrish is moving to a building designed by Herzog & de Meuron in Watermill that is due to open in November.)', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Pavilion Planned for New Arts Center in Southampton Village', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'New Arts Pavilion For the Hamptons', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ARCHITECTURE', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Rockwell, David', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Rockwell Group', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Southampton (NY)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-13T19:31:37+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Robin Pogrebin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Robin', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Pogrebin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a3511499-813c-5842-bfc7-c5f983818139', 'word_count': 185, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a3511499-813c-5842-bfc7-c5f983818139'}, {'abstract': 'Indian safari meets colonial in Altuzarra’s second resort collection.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/06/13/just-one-look-joseph-altuzarra/', 'snippet': 'Indian safari meets colonial in Altuzarra’s second resort collection.', 'lead_paragraph': 'For Joseph Altuzarra’s second resort collection, the CFDA winner held a seated presentation in his Tribeca studio. “I don’t really know how to do this,” he said with a laugh as models began walking in safari-inspired looks. The outerwear was Indian safari meets colonial capes and jackets; underneath were peasant tops and dresses in linen or bright ikat cotton. “I watched a Bob Marley movie while working on this collection, so the colors took on a reggae feel,” Altuzarra explained. North African bead work embellished the collars of blouses and the hems of peplum pencil skirts. Newness arrived in billowing (but belted) gypsy dresses. “Everyone in the office tried them on and they really flatter every body type,” Altuzarra said of the very un-body-con silhouette. “So that’s a new direction we’re trying.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Just One Look | Altuzarra', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Fashion and Apparel', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Altuzarra, Joseph', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Council of Fashion Designers of America', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-13T19:38:25+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Chelsea Zalopany', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Chelsea', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Zalopany', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/85a44c39-2c69-5e85-8584-a2e947293d8b', 'word_count': 135, 'uri': 'nyt://article/85a44c39-2c69-5e85-8584-a2e947293d8b'}, {'abstract': 'The English soccer club is considering listing in the United States instead of Singapore, according to people with knowledge of the matter.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2012/06/13/manchester-united-said-to-consider-moving-stock-sale-to-u-s/', 'snippet': 'The English soccer club is considering listing in the United States instead of Singapore, according to people with knowledge of the matter.', 'lead_paragraph': 'LONDON — Manchester United may have fallen short of an English soccer championship last month, but the United States stock market is emerging as a new goal. ', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Manchester United Said to Consider Moving Stock Sale to U.S.', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Manchester United Is Said to Weigh I.P.O. in United States', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Initial Public Offerings', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Glazer, Malcolm', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Manchester United (Soccer Team)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-13T19:41:22+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Mark Scott and Michael J. de la Merced', 'person': 