nt_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Photography', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Iraq', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Myanmar', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-13T21:18:11+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Multimedia/Photos', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/acdf098c-629f-5395-b64c-5bea9d47461a', 'word_count': 15, 'uri': 'nyt://article/acdf098c-629f-5395-b64c-5bea9d47461a'}, {'abstract': 'The choreographer David Wampach uses John Waters and Brian De Palma films as grist in the Queer New York Festival.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/06/14/arts/dance/david-wampach-in-queer-new-york-festival-at-abrons-center.html', 'snippet': 'The choreographer David Wampach uses John Waters and Brian De Palma films as grist in the Queer New York Festival.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It’s not surprising that John Waters, a director long\xa0celebrated and reviled for his trashy, outré, queer films, would be represented in a queer arts festival. \xa0 And there he — or, rather, an extended excerpt from his sordid, classic film “Desperate Living” — was on Saturday night at the Abrons Arts Center, when the French choreographer David Wampach performed two works as part of the Queer New York International Arts Festival.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Seeing Reflections in John Waters and ‘Carrie’', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Seeing Reflections in John Waters and ‘Carrie’', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Dancing', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-13T21:19:12+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Dance', 'byline': {'original': 'By Claudia La Rocco', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Claudia', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'La Rocco', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/ca5582d1-d4ae-5034-9c7f-8c7faff3ee89', 'word_count': 499, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ca5582d1-d4ae-5034-9c7f-8c7faff3ee89'}, {'abstract': 'The Giants were taken by surprise when the Patriots claimed tight end Jake Ballard off waivers.', 'web_url': 'https://fifthdown.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/06/13/with-ballard-gone-the-giants-must-adjust/', 'snippet': 'The Giants were taken by surprise when the Patriots claimed tight end Jake Ballard off waivers.', 'lead_paragraph': 'EAST RUTHERFORD, N.J. — Jake Ballard’s locker at the Giants’ training facility looked the same Wednesday morning, as if it still belonged to him. Jackets hung underneath a shelf holding his red, white and blue Giants bag, which was underneath his shoulder pads. Below that was a pile of mismatched shoes. On the wall inside the locker hung a tribute to his alma mater, Ohio State: a rag with “Ohio” scripted in scarlet and gray. Above it was a picture of the Giants tight ends from last season: Travis Beckum, Bear Pascoe and Ballard.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'With Ballard Gone, the Giants Must Adjust', 'kicker': 'The Fifth Down', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Football', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ballard, Jake', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Coughlin, Tom', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New England Patriots', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Giants', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-13T21:19:40+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Pro Football', 'byline': {'original': 'By Tim Rohan', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Tim', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rohan', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d1e306d9-9b2c-5612-a0e9-2f191aa87f27', 'word_count': 825, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d1e306d9-9b2c-5612-a0e9-2f191aa87f27'}, {'abstract': 'Freddie Freeman was hitting .302 for the Atlanta Braves on May 5 when he scratched the cornea of his right eye while changing his contact lenses.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/06/14/sports/baseball/freddie-freemans-vision-problem-subsides-but-hitting-problem-lingers.html', 'snippet': 'Freddie Freeman was hitting .302 for the Atlanta Braves on May 5 when he scratched the cornea of his right eye while changing his contact lenses.', 'lead_paragraph': 'ATLANTA — A hitter is nothing without his eyes, which is why anxiety gripped Atlanta’s Freddie Freeman last month when his vision started blurring. What if his career was being sideswiped? A hitter might as well have a Wiffle ball bat in his hands if he goes to the plate and his vision is not right. Freeman, 22 and in his second season as the Braves’ regular first baseman, was scared, and rightfully so. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/14/sports/14freeman1/14freeman1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/14/sports/14freeman1/14freeman1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1363, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/14/spor