google-wants-love-and-90-other-things/', 'snippet': 'Google proved to be one of the more ambitious applicants for new top level domain names, announced today. It applied for more than 100 domains, including .google, .youtube, .goog and .plus. It was the only applicant for .fly, .new and .eat. But it...', 'lead_paragraph': 'Google wants love. But so do six other companies. They are after .love, actually, a new “top-level domain” that could catch on as .com, .org and .net did.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/13/technology/13google-doodle/13google-doodle-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 142, 'width': 326, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/13/technology/13google-doodle/13google-doodle-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 142, 'width': 326, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/13/technology/13google-doodle/13google-doodle-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/06/13/technology/13google-doodle/13google-doodle-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/13/technology/13google-doodle/13google-doodle-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 65, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Google Wants Love and 100 Other Things', 'kicker': 'Bits', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Imagining Dot Magic In a Name', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computers and the Internet', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Domain Names (Internet)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'AMAZON.COM INC', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Apple Incorporated', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Google Inc', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Internet Corp for Assigned Names and Numbers', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Johnson & Johnson', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Microsoft Corporation', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-13T21:37:35+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nicole Perlroth and Eric Pfanner', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nicole', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Perlroth', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Eric', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Pfanner', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f4821d21-c801-50d1-93e8-d0174961338c', 'word_count': 697, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f4821d21-c801-50d1-93e8-d0174961338c'}, {'abstract': 'Dave Eggers has written a new novel set in Saudi Arabia that introduces a distinctive voice.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/06/14/books/dave-eggerss-new-novel-a-hologram-for-the-king.html', 'snippet': 'Dave Eggers has written a new novel set in Saudi Arabia that introduces a distinctive voice.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The hero of Dave Eggers’s absorbing new novel “A Hologram for the King” is a penny-ante Job named Alan Clay, who finds himself in an absurd situation. Alan is deeply in debt, unable to pay his daughter’s college tuition and plagued by a scary golf-ball-size lump on the back of his neck. He’s betting everything on a last-ditch chance at a big payday, hoping he can sell the Saudi king, Abdullah, on a lucrative technology contract — a contract that depends on Alan’s going to a remote real estate development in Saudi Arabia and making an elaborate holographic presentation to the king, who may or may not even show up. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/14/arts/BOOK/BOOK-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 634, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/14/arts/BOOK/BOOK-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2030, 'width': 1256, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/14/arts/BOOK/BOOK-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/06/14/arts/BOOK/BOOK-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/14/arts/BOOK/BOOK-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Joe Average, Just Waiting for Salvation', 'kicker': 'Books of The Times', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Joe Average, Just Waiting For Salvation', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Eggers, Dave', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-13T21:38:26+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Books', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michiko Kakutani', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michiko', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kakutani', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/2d156045-0e0e-56d2-b896-9a9819a75991', 'word_count': 1192, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2d156045-0e0e-56d2-b896-9a9819a75991'}, {'abstract': 'Natalia Almada’s documentary “El Velador” (“The Night Watchman”) photographs a cemetery for those killed in Mexico’s drug wars.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/06/14/movies/el-velador-the-night-watchman-mexicos-drug-war-cemetery.html', 'snippet': 'Natalia Almada’s documentary “El Velador” (“The Night Watchman”) photographs a cemetery for those killed in Mexico’s drug wars.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When Wallace Stevens wrote, “death is the mother of beauty,” he probably wasn’t imagining anything like “El Velador” (“The Night Watchman”), Natalia Almada’s eloquent documentary portrait of a sprawling graveyard in Culiacán, Mexico, in the northwestern state of Sinaloa. The rapidly expanding cemetery has become the burial ground of choice for the country’s slain drug lords. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/14/arts/14velador/14velador-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 330, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/06/14/arts/14velador/14velador-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 330}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/14/arts/14velador/14velador-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 629, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/14/arts/14velador/14velador-superJumb