e': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/5abcac8f-7f73-57a1-8f12-ec2a5bd0bac2', 'word_count': 828, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5abcac8f-7f73-57a1-8f12-ec2a5bd0bac2'}, {'abstract': 'Zhang Ziyi, sued Apple Daily, a well-known tabloid newspaper, and Next Magazine Publishing, over articles saying she was paid to have sex with Bo Xilai, the deposed Communist Party official.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/06/14/world/asia/china-actress-sues-publications-over-bo-allegations.html', 'snippet': 'Zhang Ziyi, sued Apple Daily, a well-known tabloid newspaper, and Next Magazine Publishing, over articles saying she was paid to have sex with Bo Xilai, the deposed Communist Party official.', 'lead_paragraph': 'One of China’s most famous actresses has filed a libel suit against two prominent Hong Kong news organizations over articles saying she was paid to have sex with Bo Xilai, the deposed Communist Party official. The actress, Zhang Ziyi, sued Apple Daily, a well-known tabloid newspaper, and Next Magazine Publishing, both of which are owned by Next Media. Executives at the companies have declined to comment. Apple Daily reported this spring that Ms. Zhang made $110 million by sleeping with Mr. Bo and other officials in recent years; the article said she was introduced to Mr. Bo by Xu Ming, a tycoon who has been detained in the Bo investigation. Mr. Bo, a former Politburo member, is being investigated for abuse of power. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'China: Actress Sues Publications Over Bo Allegations', 'kicker': 'World Briefing | Asia', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'China: Actress Sues Publications Over Bo Allegations', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Libel and Slander', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Zhang Ziyi', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bo Xilai', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'China', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'APPLE DAILY', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Magazines', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-14T02:28:40+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Edward Wong', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Edward', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Wong', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/cdecee8b-7727-59b8-80c7-4290b4bcb742', 'word_count': 127, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cdecee8b-7727-59b8-80c7-4290b4bcb742'}, {'abstract': 'With R.A. Dickey facing David Price, two Nashville pitchers squared off at Tampa Bay. Fred Russell would have loved it.', 'web_url': 'https://bats.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/06/13/a-battle-of-tennessee-titans/', 'snippet': 'With R.A. Dickey facing David Price, two Nashville pitchers squared off at Tampa Bay. Fred Russell would have loved it.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The fabulous pitching matchup at Tropicana Field Wednesday was special enough on its own. The Mets’ R.A. Dickey, who had nine victories, faced the Rays’ David Price, who had eight. Dickey dares hitters with a knuckleball. Price is a premier power pitcher, although the Mets figured him out.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Battle of Tennessee Titans', 'kicker': 'Bats', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Baseball', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Dickey, R A', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Price, David Taylor', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Mets', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Tampa Bay Rays', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'University of Tennessee', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Vanderbilt University', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-14T02:31:20+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Baseball', 'byline': {'original': 'By Tyler Kepner', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Tyler', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kepner', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/688caa93-94e6-5046-8d22-96b6aab37dda', 'word_count': 353, 'uri': 'nyt://article/688caa93-94e6-5046-8d22-96b6aab37dda'}, {'abstract': 'Pakistan closed NATO’s supply routes across its border in November after an American airstrike mistakenly killed 24 Pakistani soldiers at a frontier outpost.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/06/14/world/asia/pakistan-supply-detour-costs-100-million-a-month.html', 'snippet': 'Pakistan closed NATO’s supply routes across its border in November after an American airstrike mistakenly killed 24 Pakistani soldiers at a frontier outpost.', 'lead_paragraph': ' Pakistan’s closing of NATO’s ground supply routes to Afghanistan has added about $100 million a month to the costs of the Afghan war, Defense Secretary Leon E. Panetta told Congress on Wednesday. Mr. Panetta, left, said the added expenses were a result of using the longer northern route to supply the military and carry out the American drawdown in Afghanistan. Pakistan closed NATO’s supply routes across its border in November after an American airstrike mistakenly killed 24 Pakistani soldiers at a frontier outpost. Talks with Pakistan to reopen the route have stalemated. Mr. Panetta also said he would advise Congress on recommendations to deal with Pakistan in the future, including on the question of American financial assistance to Islamabad. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Pakistan: Supply Detour Costs $100 Million a Month', 'kicker': 'World Briefing | Asia', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Pakistan: Supply Detour Costs $100 Million a Month', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Pakistan', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Panetta, Leon E', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Defense and Military Forces', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Afghanistan War (2001- )', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'North Atlantic Treaty Organization', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-14T02:36:38+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Thom Shanker', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Thom', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Shanker', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/7b279813-3b09-5cf5-b355-fa99d82fae9d', 'word_count': 122, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7b279813-3b09-5cf5-b355-fa99d82fae9d'}, {'abstract': 'The first six holes of the Lake Course at the Olympic Club, site of the 112th United States Open, will present a difficult challenge for the field of 156.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/06/14/sports/golf/us-open-from-first-hole-accuracy-and-nerves-will-be-tested.html', 'snippet': 'The first six holes of the Lake Course at the Olympic Club, site of the 112th United States Open, will present a difficult challenge for the field of 156.', 'lead_paragraph': 'SAN FRANCISCO — On the first hole, Rickie Fowler hit an errant approach from the fairway, plunked down a second ball and took another swing. On the second tee, he did the same. Multiple shots were the norm on Wednesday, the final day of practice before the 112th United States Open.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '13', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/14/sports/Y-JP-OPEN/Y-JP-OPEN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 350, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/06/14/sports/Y-JP-OPEN/Y-JP-OPEN-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 350}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/14/sports/Y-JP-OPEN/Y-JP-OPEN-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 669, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/14/sports/Y-JP-OPEN/Y-JP-OPEN-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1338, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/14/sports/Y-JP-OPEN/Y-JP-OPEN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/06/14/sports/Y-JP-OPEN/Y-JP-OPEN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/14/sports/Y-JP-OPEN/Y-JP-OPEN-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'From First Hole, Accuracy and Nerves Will Be Tested', 'kicker': 'U.S. Open', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'From First Hole, Accuracy and Nerves Will Be Tested', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Olympic Club (San Francisco, Calif)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Mickelson, Phil', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Golf', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Fowler, Rickie', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kuchar, Matt', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Open (Golf)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-14T02:41:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Golf', 'byline': {'original': 'By Karen Crouse', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Karen', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Crouse', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/58183de7-e0ed-58b7-b136-7f15a3a8b354', 'word_count': 971, 'uri': 'nyt://article/58183de7-e0ed-58b7-b136-7f15a3a8b354'}, {'abstract': 'In arguments made against the Obama administration’s health care law, a thread that runs through many of them is an analogy between the health care law and broccoli.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/06/14/business/how-broccoli-became-a-symbol-in-the-health-care-debate.html', 'snippet': 'In arguments made against the Obama administration’s health care law, a thread that runs through many of them is an analogy between the health care law and broccoli.', 'lead_paragraph': 'What does broccoli have to do with health insurance?', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/14/business/BROCCOLI-6/BROCCOLI-6-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 370, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/06/14/business/BROCCOLI-6/BROCCOLI-6-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlarge