shows.', 'web_url': 'https://mediadecoder.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/06/14/dallas-returns-after-20-years-to-solid-ratings/', 'snippet': 'The audience on Wednesday night for the drama — whose original run ended in 1991 — was 6.9 million viewers on TNT, enough to beat all broadcast network shows.', 'lead_paragraph': 'TNT managed to pull in a big audience by cable standards for its much-talked-about revival of the classic TV soap of the 1980s, “Dallas,” though the show was no “Hatfields & McCoys.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '‘Dallas’ Returns After 20 Years to Solid Ratings', 'kicker': 'Media Decoder', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Turner Network Television', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-14T17:55:41+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Media', 'byline': {'original': 'By Bill Carter', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Bill', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Carter', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e2854a12-93da-5301-880c-2692c5d10aab', 'word_count': 230, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e2854a12-93da-5301-880c-2692c5d10aab'}, {'abstract': 'A French clothing label makes its first foray into design with a three-print collection of kitchenware.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/06/14/ooh-la-la-maison-paul-joe-comes-home/', 'snippet': 'A French clothing label makes its first foray into design with a three-print collection of kitchenware.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Sophie Albou-Mechaly, the designer for the French clothing label Paul & Joe, doesn’t claim to be an avid chef, but delights in collecting china — from the high-end Hungarian manufacturer Herend to kitsch treasures like Ridgewood — at the Paris flea markets. “I love to dress a dinner table in colorful prints the way I dress myself,” says Albou-Mechaly, who is now expanding the brand to include home goods. “My idea of fashion is not to be only about clothes but about living well.” Paul & Joe La Maison is making its first foray into design with a three-print collection of kitchenware: cherry blossoms, kittens and Asian-inspired koi motifs. “The brand’s DNA is all about the happiness in colors and prints, so this feels like a natural extension,” she says, which is another way of saying that La Maison’s prints are derived from the current season and past collection hit makers. “As a woman and a mother, I am constantly thinking about women and the clothes and things that they need in their lives: practicality and appeal,” she adds. The collection includes everything from porcelain dinner plates and linen dishcloths to butter dishes, egg cups and even charming lunch boxes. And, with everything priced between $30 and $150 (similar to its sold-out 2006 Target collaboration), customers can afford the luxury of changing their home décor as often as they change their mood and clothes. “There’s no trend to it,” Albou-Mechaly says. “Mixing pieces and decorating your kitchen with them is fun.” The designer also suggests swapping tired hostess gifts (wine, flowers, desserts) with a La Maison item, since each piece comes beautifully packaged in a matching print box. Aren’t American kitchens’ needs different from those of French kitchens’? Albou-Mechaly begs to differ: “We are the American kitchen, English kitchen, French kitchen, Italian kitchen — it’s just the taste that is different.” Expect La Maison to take over every inch of your home, maison, flat or casa.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Ooh La La Maison | Paul & Joe Comes Home', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Fashion and Apparel', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Herend Porcelain', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Paris (France)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-14T18:02:35+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Chelsea Zalopany', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Chelsea', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Zalopany', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/989b435c-23d8-5609-b794-a49903a3bd40', 'word_count': 342, 'uri': 'nyt://article/989b435c-23d8-5609-b794-a49903a3bd40'}, {'abstract': 'The need to accommodate bigger airplanes and a growing number of foreign carriers has helped spur many of the new investments.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/06/15/business/building-boom-takes-hold-at-us-airports.html', 'snippet': 'The need to accommodate bigger airplanes and a growing number of foreign carriers has helped spur many of the new investments.', 'lead_paragraph': 'At a time when federal and state public works programs are stalled, the nation’s biggest airports are in the midst of major renovations or expansions that, taken together, amount to some of the largest infrastructure projects in the country.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/15/business/15airports/15airports-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 330, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/06/15/business/15airports/15airports-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 330}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/15/business/15airports/15airports-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 683, 'w