Chi (Oriental Exercise)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Exercise', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Pizzuti, Silvana', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-14T19:12:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Daniel Krieger', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Daniel', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krieger', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/bdacbf98-e9a2-5bf6-b40f-c9e399dfc189', 'word_count': 573, 'uri': 'nyt://article/bdacbf98-e9a2-5bf6-b40f-c9e399dfc189'}, {'abstract': 'The police took at least a half-dozen people into custody Wednesday night as protesters marched from Washington Square in what they said was a show of solidarity with striking Canadian students.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/06/14/protesters-arrested-in-quebec-solidarity-march/', 'snippet': 'The police took at least a half-dozen people into custody Wednesday night as protesters marched from Washington Square in what they said was a show of solidarity with striking Canadian students.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Updated, June 15 | The police took into custody at least half a dozen people on Wednesday night as hundreds of protesters, for the third week in a row, marched from Washington Square in what they described as an expression of solidarity with striking students in Quebec. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Protesters Arrested in Quebec Solidarity March', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Demonstrations, Protests and Riots', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Occupy Wall Street', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Police Department (NYC)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Greenwich Village (NYC)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-14T19:13:17+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Colin Moynihan', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Colin', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Moynihan', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/10d687be-4d23-531e-a209-8ded13110c56', 'word_count': 481, 'uri': 'nyt://article/10d687be-4d23-531e-a209-8ded13110c56'}, {'abstract': 'Like many aspects of the Swedish automaker’s acquisition by an investment group, it is unclear who might provide the batteries and motors to propel its proposed electric vehicles.', 'web_url': 'https://wheels.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/06/14/an-electric-future-for-saab-but-who-provides-the-power/', 'snippet': 'Like many aspects of the Swedish automaker’s acquisition by an investment group, it is unclear who might provide the batteries and motors to propel its proposed electric vehicles.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Like a punch-drunk prize fighter rising from the mat, Saab Automobile appears to have some rounds left. As The Times reported, the company was acquired on Wednesday by National Electric Vehicle Sweden, representing Chinese, Japanese and Swedish stakeholders, and could produce cars again at its base in Trollhattan, Sweden, as early as late next year, according to the new owners.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'An Electric Future for Saab, but Who Provides the Power?', 'kicker': 'Wheels', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Automobiles', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Electric and Hybrid Vehicles', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Electric Vehicle Sweden', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Saab Automobile AB', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Sweden', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-14T19:20:54+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Automobiles', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jim Motavalli', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jim', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Motavalli', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/95d20915-88a3-51c8-8ae4-d13d25a60ec2', 'word_count': 454, 'uri': 'nyt://article/95d20915-88a3-51c8-8ae4-d13d25a60ec2'}, {'abstract': 'The police officers took off their badges and laced up gloves for a “grudge match,” an unofficial but officiated method for dealing with bad blood within the force.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/06/14/taking-off-badges-and-lacing-up-gloves-to-settle-a-score/', 'snippet': 'The police officers took off their badges and laced up gloves for a “grudge match,” an unofficial but officiated method for dealing with bad blood within the force.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The older officer had taken a few boxing lessons at a gym not far from where his daughter went to gymnastics class. The younger officer had arms that seemed to belong to a man who weighed twice his 166 pounds.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '36', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Taking Off Badges and Lacing Up Gloves to Settle a Score', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Taking Off Badges and Lacing Up Gloves to Settle a Score', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Boxing', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Police Department (NYC)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-14T19:23:27+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Joseph Goldstein', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Joseph', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Goldstein', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/26931600-3ea3-5d3e-8fdf-15c3deca2293', 'word_count': 738, 'uri': 'nyt://article/26931600-3ea3-5d3e-8fdf-15c3deca2293'}, {'abstract': 'Celebrating its 50th anniversary, the American Symphony Orchestra looks back to its founder, Leopold Stokowski, and some of his achievements and adds new adventures in keeping with the revivalist mission of its present conductor, Leon Botstein.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/06/14/the-american-symphony-at-50-harks-back-to-stokowski/', 'snippet': 'Celebrating its 50th anniversary, the American Symphony Orchestra looks back to its founder, Leopold Stokowski, and some of his achievements and adds new adventures in keeping with the revivalist mission of its present conductor, Leon Botstein.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When Leopold Stokowski formed the American Symphony Orchestra 50 years ago, he wanted to create, in part, a training ground for young musicians. Its ambition was to be second only to the New York Philharmonic in New York. Fifty years later, its ranks are dominated by veteran New York freelancers, and a claim on that No. 2 spot is unlikely. (The Met Orchestra? Orpheus? Orchestra of St. Luke’s?) But the American Symphony is still around, again playing at Carnegie Hall, with a focus on less frequently performed works and a didactic mission. It has announced an ambitious season in the fall and spring to celebrate its 50th anniversary.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The American Symphony, at 50, Harks Back to Stokowski', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_h