e9873bb37aa'}, {'abstract': 'The average credit card fee for foreign transactions has fallen from a year ago, a Card Hub analysis finds.', 'web_url': 'https://bucks.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/06/14/no-foreign-fee-cards-still-best-for-overseas-spending/', 'snippet': 'The average credit card fee for foreign transactions has fallen from a year ago, a Card Hub analysis finds.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Credit cards that don’t carry foreign transaction fees are generally the best option for spending while traveling internationally. But even if you’re stuck with a card that charges the fees, you can still save more by using the card rather than by exchanging cash at banks and airport terminals.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'No-Foreign-Fee Cards Still Best for Overseas Spending', 'kicker': 'Bucks', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Banking and Financial Institutions', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Credit Cards', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Travelex', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'United States', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-14T20:09:48+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Your Money', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ann Carrns', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ann', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Carrns', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d40664dd-384c-5d87-b95d-a99dd2b14891', 'word_count': 546, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d40664dd-384c-5d87-b95d-a99dd2b14891'}, {'abstract': '“Art of Another Kind,” at the Guggenheim Museum, looks at global 1950s avant-garde movements.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/06/15/arts/design/art-of-another-kind-at-guggenheim-museum.html', 'snippet': '“Art of Another Kind,” at the Guggenheim Museum, looks at global 1950s avant-garde movements.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The excellent collection show “Art of Another Kind” finds the Guggenheim tunneling back in time to 1959 — when its Frank Lloyd Wright building first opened — and returning with a treasure trove of postwar abstraction, much of it made by artists you have probably never heard of.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '28', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/15/arts/GUGGENHEIM/GUGGENHEIM-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 781, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/15/arts/GUGGENHEIM/GUGGENHEIM-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1562, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Outsiders as Trendsetters', 'kicker': 'Art Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Outsiders as Trendsetters', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Guggenheim, Solomon R, Museum', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-14T20:12:15+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Art & Design', 'byline': {'original': 'By Karen Rosenberg', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Karen', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rosenberg', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/336af27b-69d6-5425-b000-b69b1ae90e54', 'word_count': 784, 'uri': 'nyt://article/336af27b-69d6-5425-b000-b69b1ae90e54'}, {'abstract': 'A scene from “Extraterrestrial,” Nacho Vigalondo’s film that blends romantic comedy with science fiction.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/video/movies/100000001607031/clip-extraterrestrial.html', 'snippet': 'A scene from “Extraterrestrial,” Nacho Vigalondo’s film that blends romantic comedy with science fiction.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A scene from “Extraterrestrial,” Nacho Vigalondo’s film that blends romantic comedy with science fiction.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/14/arts/video-extraterrestrial/video-extraterrestrial-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 338, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/06/14/arts/video-extraterrestrial/video-extraterrestrial-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 338}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/14/arts/video-extraterrestrial/video-extraterrestrial-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/06/14/arts/video-extraterrestrial/video-extraterrestrial-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Clip: ‘Extraterrestrial’', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-06-14T20:16:08+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'Focus World', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Focus', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'World', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Video', '_id': 'nyt://video/b4ae3735-3af0-519a-95b6-0fe577cddc23', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://video/b4ae3735-3af0-519a-95b6-0fe577cddc23'}, {'abstract': 'Forecasting the recriminations from an Obama defeat.', 'web_url': 'https://douthat.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/06/14/tomorrows-recriminations-today/', 'snippet': 'Forecasting the recriminations from an Obama defeat.', 'lead_paragraph': 'If Barack Obama loses his re-election bid, there will be a host of liberals ready to argue — in a mirror-image version of the argument many conservatives made about John McCain after 2008 — that he only lost because he wasn’t liberal enough. (Mickey Kaus pre-emptively summarizes this narrative: “He’ll have lost because he wasn’t populist enough, he demoralized his base, he didn’t pursue single-payer, he foolishly tried to win over swing voters instead of registering new voters, he was too nice to the Republicans. Rachel Maddow and both her guests will nod sagely.”) There will also be a group of liberals (smaller, but perhaps more influential) who argue that Obama mostly made the right choices, but fell victim to economic forces beyond any president’s control. And then there will be the liberals (joined by some conservatives) who focus intently on monetary policy, and argue among themselves over whether the White House could have exerted more pressure on Ben Bernanke and the Federal Reserve.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Tomorrow’s Recriminations Today', 'kicker': 'Ross Douthat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-06-14T20:19:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ross Douthat', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ross', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Douthat', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/bcc90808-62fc-57b2-be0f-eadefed03fd8', 'word_count': 1055, 'uri': 'nyt://article/bcc90808-62fc-57b2-be0f-eadefed03fd8'}, {'abstract': 'A reader responds to a recent review of Andrea Camilleri’s “Age of Doubt.”', 'web_url'