playing in and outside the New York area.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Movies', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '17', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Movie Listings for June 15-21', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Listings', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-14T22:16:45+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Schedule', '_id': 'nyt://article/3473a24c-4cc9-57e4-b78e-c788160a204e', 'word_count': 3950, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3473a24c-4cc9-57e4-b78e-c788160a204e'}, {'abstract': 'The Untitled Theater Company #61 adapts “The Lathe of Heaven,” Ursula K. Le Guin’s classic dystopian novel, at 3LD Art and Technology.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/06/15/theater/reviews/the-lathe-of-heaven-at-3ld.html', 'snippet': 'The Untitled Theater Company #61 adapts “The Lathe of Heaven,” Ursula K. Le Guin’s classic dystopian novel, at 3LD Art and Technology.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It’s oddly encouraging that a theater only blocks away from ground zero has become a home for apocalyptic plays about damaged landscapes. New York has come a long way since Sept. 11.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/15/arts/15STAGEROUNDUP3_SPAN/15STAGEROUNDUP3_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 380, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/06/15/arts/15STAGEROUNDUP3_SPAN/15STAGEROUNDUP3_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 380}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/15/arts/15STAGEROUNDUP3_SPAN/STAGE