Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/e0da9de5-3563-560a-a969-f53bb7a32ad6', 'word_count': 379, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e0da9de5-3563-560a-a969-f53bb7a32ad6'}, {'abstract': 'In “The Woman in the Fifth,” Ethan Hawke plays an American novelist in Paris who takes on an odd job and takes up with a mysterious woman.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/06/15/movies/the-woman-in-the-fifth-starring-ethan-hawke.html', 'snippet': 'In “The Woman in the Fifth,” Ethan Hawke plays an American novelist in Paris who takes on an odd job and takes up with a mysterious woman.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Ethan Hawke, his angular features slicing across the screen, is one reason to see “The Woman in the Fifth.” Another is that this psychological thriller (or perhaps, psychothriller) about an American writer down on his luck in Paris and maybe out of his mind creates a sense of dread that builds and builds though never resolves, even in the final fade out. It doesn’t have the kind of payoff that details who did what and why, which is a problem only if you demand tidy endings. If you’re up for some narrative drift, you can just go with the ambiguous flow created by the Polish-born filmmaker Pawel Pawlikowski, who I suspect is a fan of Antonioni’s “Passenger.”  ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/15/arts/15WOMAN_SPAN/15WOMAN_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 360, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/06/15/arts/15WOMAN_SPAN/15WOMAN_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 360}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/15/arts/15WOMAN_SPAN/WOMAN-1339699248235-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'captio