'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Slideshow', '_id': 'nyt://slideshow/8cd35290-566e-52d4-a579-4f984b402b12', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://slideshow/8cd35290-566e-52d4-a579-4f984b402b12'}, {'abstract': 'New York Hospital’s soaring white 1932 skyscraper, though added to and encroached upon, is still something to see.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/06/17/realestate/a-hospital-with-fine-bones.html', 'snippet': 'New York Hospital’s soaring white 1932 skyscraper, though added to and encroached upon, is still something to see.', 'lead_paragraph': 'CHARTERED in 1771, New York Hospital is the city’s oldest, having made peripatetic progress up Manhattan Island. In 1932 it opened a broad new campus at 68th Street and the East River, with a central tower rising in an upward cascade of almost-white masonry, Gothic arches and intricate brickwork 350 feet high. Generally, medical institutions and architectural sensibility do not coexist well, but New York Hospital, today NewYork-Presbyterian Hospital/Weill Cornell Medical Center, has managed, through circumstance and design, to keep much of its architectural character in good health. ', 'print_section': 'RE', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/17/realestate/sub-streets-1/sub-streets-1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 779, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/17/realestate/sub-streets-1/sub-streets-1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1558, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/17/realestate/sub-streets-1/sub-streets-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name':