line': {'main': 'Opinion Report: Prison Sentences', 'kicker': 'Taking Note', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-06-15T12:07:43+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andrew Rosenthal', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andrew', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rosenthal', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/20e8acac-181d-581f-842c-35c33ff4aaf5', 'word_count': 103, 'uri': 'nyt://article/20e8acac-181d-581f-842c-35c33ff4aaf5'}, {'abstract': 'If elections go through this weekend, Egypt will have a president whose powers are not spelled out, and who will take office without the checks-and-balances of a parliament.', 'web_url': 'https://latitude.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/06/15/egypt-goes-back-on-the-revolution/', 'snippet': 'If elections go through this weekend, Egypt will have a president whose powers are not spelled out, and who will take office without the checks-and-balances of a parliament.', 'lead_paragraph': 'CAIRO — Egypt’s revolution is back to square one. The political forces currently at play are the same ones that struggled against each other back in prerevolutionary Egypt: the conservative Islamist group and former President Hosni Mubarak’s National Democratic Party. This has been the case since the results of the first round of presidential elections were announced, but this week the country’s beleaguered transition to democracy was dealt a body blow.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Outrage in Egypt', 'kicker': 'Latitude', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Demonstrations, Protests, and Riots', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Elections', '