': 'Morsi, Mohamed', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Shafik, Ahmed', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Elections', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-15T13:00:46+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Middle East', 'byline': {'original': 'By David D. Kirkpatrick', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d67ebf8e-5905-5615-907b-9e4c286852be', 'word_count': 1100, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d67ebf8e-5905-5615-907b-9e4c286852be'}, {'abstract': 'In 1976, the East German women won every swimming event in Montreal except one–and the U.S. team eventually had their suspicions confirmed about how that happened.', 'web_url': 'https://london2012.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/06/15/friday-flashback-against-east-german-swimmers-in-76-a-medal-and-a-lot-of-questions/', 'snippet': 'In 1976, the East German women won every swimming event in Montreal except one–and the U.S. team eventually had their suspicions confirmed about how that happened.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Wendy Boglioli once was a kid who learned how to swim in a lake in her native Land O’Lakes, Wis., with her dad coaching her alongside while paddling a rowboat.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Friday Flashback: Against East German Swimmers in ’76, a Medal and a Lot of Questions', 'kicker': '2012 London Olympics', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Olympic Games', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Swimming', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-15T13:12:47+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Olympics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Joanne C. Gerstner', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Joanne', 'middlename': 'C.', 'lastname': 'Gerstner', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6a0809ff-b1a8-5371-ae9a-620929ecd186', 'word_count': 724, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6a0809ff-b1a8-5371-ae9a-620929ecd186'}, {'abstract': 'Also, Facebook begins to deal with shareholder lawsuits in wake of its initial public offering, and AOL shareholders reject an attempt to unseat three directors.', 'web_url': 'https://mediadecoder.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/06/15/the-breakfast-meeting-painful-online-updates-and-ending-a-what-i-ate-at-school-blog/', 'snippet': 'Also, Facebook begins to deal with shareholder lawsuits in wake of its initial public offering, and AOL shareholders reject an attempt to unseat three directors.', 'lead_paragraph': '11:02 a.m. | Updated Facebook and other social media services have been credited with maintaining ties that would normally have faded away — grade school friends, say — but that advantage can turn cruel for families that have become estranged, Catherine Saint Louis writes. It often prevents a clean break, with parents keeping up with an estranged child from afar. She writes:', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Breakfast Meeting: Painful Online Updates, and Ending a What-I-Ate-at-School Blog', 'kicker': 'Media Decoder', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-06-15T13:13:18+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Media', 'byline': {'original': 'By Noam Cohen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Noam', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cohen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0ccad168-03d6-516f-8be3-0ced0d387fb3', 'word_count': 340, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0ccad168-03d6-516f-8be3-0ced0d387fb3'}, {'abstract': 'Facebook can complicate family estrangements, job forecast steady but stay tuned, government expands inquiry into Jeep Grand Cherokee fire risk and other consumer-focused news from The New York Times.', 'web_url': 'https://bucks.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/06/15/friday-reading-facebook-complicates-family-estrangements/', 'snippet': 'Facebook can complicate family estrangements, job forecast steady but stay tuned, government expands inquiry into Jeep Grand Cherokee fire risk and other consumer-focused news from The New York Times.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A variety of consumer-focused articles appears daily in The New York Times and on our blogs. Each weekday morning, we gather them together here so you can quickly scan the news that could hit you in your wallet.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Friday Reading: Facebook Complicates Family Estrangements', 'kicker': 'Bucks', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-06-15T13:14:18+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Your Money', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ann Carrns', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ann', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Carrns', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9d8dac9b-cb29-5e78-b0c6-8fe405ea16ef', 'word_count': 96, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9d8dac9b-cb29-5e78-b0c6-8fe405ea16ef'}, {'abstract': 'The episodes, including cases of rape against young male recruits, date back to the 1950s, and has prompted Prime Minister Julia Gillard to raise the possibility of a high-level public inquiry.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/06/16/world/asia/report-details-history-of-abuse-in-australian-military.html', 'snippet': 'The episodes, including cases of rape against young male recruits, date back to the 1950s, and has prompted Prime Minister Julia Gillard to raise the possibility of a high-level public inquiry.', 'lead_paragraph': 'SYDNEY, Australia — Newly released documents detail numerous cases of physical and sexual abuse of minors in Australia’s armed forces dating back to the 1950s, prompting Prime Minister Julia Gillard on Friday to raise the possibility of a high-level public inquiry.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Report Details History of Abuse in Australian Military', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Report Details History of Abuse in Australian Military', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Australia', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sex Crimes', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Child Abuse and Neglect', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Defense and Military Forces', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-15T13:27:13+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Matt Siegel', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Matt', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Siegel', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/05ef4613-731b-5b8e-8385-e68045f8e7ea', 'word_count': 629, 'uri': 'nyt://article/05ef4613-731b-5b8e-8385-e68045f8e7ea'}, {'abstract': 'Runaway inflation, still not happening.', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/06/15/still-a-phantom-menace/', 'snippet': 'Runaway inflation, still not happening.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The policy response to financial crisis has, in effect, given us a great natural experiment in macroeconomics — an experiment that can and should be viewed as a test of two views of the economy. One view — which includes both freshwater macro and much of what Austrians say — is in effect classical macro as Keynes described it, in which the economy is always constrained by supply. The other is a more or less Keynesian view in which a depressed economy is constrained by demand, not supply. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Still A Phantom Menace', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-06-15T13:31:56+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/74fea600-be18-534a-966f-b7438cbe354f', 'word_count': 496, 'uri': 'nyt://article/74fea600-be18-534a-966f-b7438cbe354f'}, {'abstract': 'The man self-immolated in front of a police compound in China’s northwest Qinghai Province, according to Free Tibet and Radio Free Asia.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/06/16/world/asia/tibetan-herder-self-immolates-in-government-protest.html', 'snippet': 'The man self-immolated in front of a police compound in China’s northwest Qinghai Province, according to Free Tibet and Radio Free Asia.', 'lead_paragraph': 'BEIJING — A Tibetan herder in China’s northwest Qinghai Province died on Friday after setting himself on fire to protest government policies in the region, according to exile groups and Radio Free Asia.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Tibetan Herder Dies After Setting Himself on Fire in Government Protest', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Tibetan Herder Dies After Setting Himself on