 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-15T14:49:06+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Julia Preston and John H. Cushman Jr', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Julia', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Preston', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'John', 'middlename': 'H.', 'lastname': 'Cushman', 'qualifier': 'Jr', 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/fec48290-1a53-5997-8c0f-0197a90dc4d8', 'word_count': 1261, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fec48290-1a53-5997-8c0f-0197a90dc4d8'}, {'abstract': 'The newest addition to tandem aerial sports is BASE jumping off a cliff.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/video/sports/100000001565507/over-the-edge.html', 'snippet': 'The newest addition to tandem aerial sports is BASE jumping off a cliff.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The newest addition to tandem aerial sports is BASE jumping off a cliff.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/05/27/sports/video-tandemjump/video-tandemjump-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 338, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/05/27/sports/video-tandemjump/video-tandemjump-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 338}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/05/27/sports/video-tandemjump/video-tandemjump-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/05/27/sports/video-tandemjump/video-tandemjump-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Over the Edge', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Parachutes and Parachute Jumping', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Athletics and Sports', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-15T14:52:10+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'byline': {'original': 'David Frank', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Frank', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Video', '_id': 'nyt://video/3c36b4b6-e9d6-56af-b233-240cf10dc82b', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://video/3c36b4b6-e9d6-56af-b233-240cf10dc82b'}, {'abstract': 'A Middle East scholar offers historical perspective on Syria’s yearlong uprising.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/06/17/books/review/the-syrian-rebellion-by-fouad-ajami.html', 'snippet': 'A Middle East scholar offers historical perspective on Syria’s yearlong uprising.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Fourteen months ago, as ordinary Syrians were just beginning to gather in large numbers to call for their leaders to quit power, a cherub-faced 13-year-old named Hamza Ali al-Khateeb was taken into custody after he’d been caught scrawling an antigovernment slogan on a wall at a protest in the town of Jiza. A month later, when Hamza’s body was returned to his parents, it bore signs of the most hideous torture. His face had been beaten purple, his jaw and kneecaps pulverized, his body stabbed and torched. His penis had been chopped off. ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/17/books/review/17FILKINS/17FILKINS-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 379, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/06/17/books/review/17FILKINS/17FILKINS-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 379}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/17/books/review/17FILKINS/17FILKINS-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 649, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/17/books/review/17FILKINS/17FILKINS-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1298, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/17/books/review/17FILKINS/17FILKINS-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/06/17/books/review/17FILKINS/17FILKINS-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/17/books/review/17FILKINS/17FILKINS-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Coming Apart', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Coming Apart', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Syria', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'AJAMI, FOUAD', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Middle East and North Africa Unrest (2010- )', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-15T14:52:29+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dexter Filkins', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dexter', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Filkins', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/131df800-c8d1-521c-b700-423aeed8a4ac', 'word_count': 1211, 'uri': 'nyt://article/131df800-c8d1-521c-b700-423aeed8a4ac'}, {'abstract': 'Alex Witchel on the “laser-sharp eye” of Michael Frayn.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/06/17/books/review/up-front.html', 'snippet': 'Alex Witchel on the “laser-sharp eye” of Michael Frayn.', 'lead_paragraph': 'As a staff writer for The New York Times Magazine, Alex Witchel has profiled enough celebrities to populate a season of A-list dinner parties: from the American actress Ellen Barkin to well-connected Britons like Rupert Everett and Julian Fellowes, not to mention serious literary types like Colm Toibin. She’s spent time with Queen Latifah and Norris Church Mailer. But in “Feed Me,” the column she wrote for the Times Dining section for six years, she was more apt to celebrate kitchen-table pleasures, interviewing a homesick Texan cooking in a tiny Manhattan apartment or trying to replicate her own grandmother’s borscht.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/06/books/review/0617-Upfront/0617-Upfront-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 628, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/06/books/review/0617-Upfront/0617-Upfront-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1256, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Up Front', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Up Front', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-15T14:59:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Editors', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Editors'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/8f41a42b-ca91-50e7-88f0-4086493eca9f', 'word_count': 273, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8f41a42b-ca91-50e7-88f0-4086493eca9f'}, {'abstract': 'Recently reviewed books of particular interest.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/06/17/books/review/editors-choice.html', 'snippet': 'Recently reviewed books of particular interest.', 'lead_paragraph': 'CANADA,  by Richard Ford. (Ecco/HarperCollins, $27.99.) A boy whose parents rob a bank in North Dakota in 1960 takes refuge across the border in this mesmerizing novel, driven by fully realized characters and an accomplished prose style.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '24', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Editors’ Choice', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Editors’ Choice | Recent Books of Particular Interest', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-15T14:59:33+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'List', '_id': 'nyt://article/03dc03d0-3fc3-5ef3-9d93-85da0b346e5d', 'word_count': 281, 'uri': 'nyt://article/03dc03d0-3fc3-5ef3-9d93-85da0b346e5d'}, {'abstract': 'Paperback books of particular interest.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/06/17/books/review/paperback-row.html', 'snippet': 'Paperback books of particular interest.', 'lead_paragraph': 'NOTHING DAUNTED: The Unexpected Education of Two Society Girls in the West,  by Dorothy Wickenden. (Scribner, $15.) Mining a trove of letters, oral histories and period documents, Wickenden recounts how her grandmother and a friend — Smith graduates from blue-blooded East Coast families — embarked on a life-changing adventure in 1916, teaching the children of struggling homesteaders.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '28', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Paperback Row', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Paperback Row', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-15T15:00:02+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ihsan Taylor', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ihsan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Taylor', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'List', '_id': 'nyt://article/b9c2d9e6-d4a9-5a3c-bdae-54d6c362cd16', 'word_count': 502, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b9c2d9e6-d4a9-5a3c-bdae-54d6c362cd16'}, {'abstract': 'House Republicans have proposed appropriating $1.6 billion to the S.B.A. — about $40 million more than the Obama administration has sought.', 'web_url': 'https://boss.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/06/15/partisan-divide-in-d-c-not-over-the-s-b-a/', 'snippet': 'House Republicans have proposed appropriating $1.6 billion to the S.B.A. — about $40 million more than the Obama administration has sought.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In this election season, the leitmotif to the story taking shape in Washington is that the partisan divide is vast and growing vaster — if last year it was difficult to pass bills that were once routine, now it has become impossible. But there appears to be at least one exception to the emerging rule: the Small Business Administration. Last week, the Republican-led House Appropriations Committee unveiled its bill (pdf) to finance the S.B.A. and several other government agencies, and what is most notable about the four pages dealing with the S.B.A. is the prospect for comity, not conflict.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Partisan Divide in D.C.? Not Over the S.B.A.', 'kicker': 'You&#039;re the Boss', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Small Business', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'House of Representatives', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Internal Revenue Service', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Small Business Administration', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-15T15:00:24+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Entrepreneurship', 'byline': {'original': 'By Robb Mandelbaum', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Robb', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Mandelbaum', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b048682c-d1b0-5a5f-a6b4-c4b1ca1879e6', 'word_count': 616, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b048682c-d1b0-5a5f-a6b4-c4b1ca1879e6'}, {'abstract': 'Playful stripes on a ribbed dress gives a simple shape equal parts structure and ease.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/06/15/just-one-look-valentino-4/', 'snippet': 'Playful stripes on a ribbed dress gives a simple shape equal parts structure and ease.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Most great dresses have all of the following characteristics: 1. Balanced proportions on a flattering fit. 2. An element of intrigue via print or embellishment. 3. A hint of comfort, physical or emotional, but preferably both. Witness a perfect example, from Valentino. Playful jailhouse stripes on a ribbed duchesse dress draw the eye to all of the right places (and help minimize the other ones), giving a simple shape (just cap sleeves atop a flouncy skirt) equal parts structure and ease.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Just One Look | Valentino', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-06-15T15:00:56+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jane Herman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jane', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Herman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5e55303f-3cf3-58a4-9c11-c4424c9a1873', 'word_count': 84, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5e55303f-3cf3-58a4-9c11-c4424c9a1873'}, {'abstract': 'With a crucial decision on President Obama’s health care law due soon, a book by Supreme Court Justice, Antonin Scalia, on how he interprets law is well-timed.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/06/16/us/in-scalias-new-book-hints-of-health-ruling.html', 'snippet': 'With a crucial decision on President Obama’s health care law due soon, a book by Supreme Court Justice, Antonin Scalia, on how he interprets law is well-timed.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WASHINGTON — With a Supreme Court decision on the fate of President Obama’s health care law expected in the next two weeks, every wisp of a hint about the justices’ thinking is getting the scrutiny usually reserved for CAT scans.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/16/us/SCALIA/SCALIA-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 726, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/16/us/SCALIA/SCALIA-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1452, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/16/us/SCALIA/SCALIA-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/06/16/us/SCALIA/SCALIA-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/16/us/SCALIA/SCALIA-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Hints in New Scalia Book of Views on Health Law', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Hints in New Scalia Book Of Views on Health Law', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Patient Protection and Affordable Care Act (2010)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Health Insurance and Managed Care', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Supreme Court', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Scalia, Antonin', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-15T15:01:03+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Adam Liptak', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Adam', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Liptak', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f9e53167-b40b-5790-a9b5-f6d08c5c91c6', 'word_count': 946, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f9e53167-b40b-5790-a9b5-f6d08c5c91c6'}, {'abstract': 'Gillian Flynn, whose novel “Gone Girl” enters the hardcover list at No. 2, has said, “Female violence is a specific brand of ferocity. It’s invasive.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/06/24/books/review/inside-the-list.html', 'snippet': 'Gillian Flynn, whose novel “Gone Girl” enters the hardcover list at No. 2, has said, “Female violence is a specific brand of ferocity. It’s invasive.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'SCENES FROM A MARRIAGE: “The female / Is a chaos,” Ezra Pound wrote, but if the savage women of Gillian Flynn’s critically acclaimed novels are anything to go by, chaos is only the half of it. They’re women with dark pasts and big appetites — for booze, men and mutilation. In “Gone Girl,” Flynn’s third and latest novel, which enters the hardcover fiction list at No. 2, a happy housewife goes missing on the morning of her fifth anniversary, but naturally, nothing is what it seems. Both husband and wife are skillful liars, and the book turns out to be “a two-sided contest,” Janet Maslin wrote recently in the daily New York Times. “Each addresses the reader: Nick in the present tense, and Amy by way of an italics-filled, giddily emotional diary about the marriage. . . . They lied to each other a lot. Now they will lie to you.” Nick starts looking guilty pretty quickly, and flighty Amy turns out to be harboring some secrets of her own.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '26', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/24/books/review/24flynn/24flynn-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 290, 'width': 287, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/24/books/review/24flynn/24flynn-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 290, 'width': 287, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/24/books/review/24flynn/24flynn-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/06/24/books/review/24flynn/24flynn-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/24/books/review/24flynn/24flynn-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Inside the List', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'TBR: Inside the List', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-15T15:01:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Parul Sehgal', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Parul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Sehgal', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d2a297e1-faca-5b58-a21b-a401e0085132', 'word_count': 614, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d2a297e1-faca-5b58-a21b-a401e0085132'}, {'abstract': 'For 14 years, volunteers have annually mapped wet and dry areas of Arizona’s San Pedro River basin. Their results guide water conservation efforts.', 'web_url': 'https://green.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/06/15/by-mapping-a-river-volunteers-take-a-stake-in-its-future/', 'snippet': 'For 14 years, volunteers have annually mapped wet and dry areas of Arizona’s San Pedro River basin. Their results guide water conservation efforts.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Dodging quicksand and rattlesnakes, Ted Mouras will spend Saturday morning walking along a five-mile stretch of a remote section of southeastern Arizona’s San Pedro River Basin in triple-digit heat for the annual wet/dry mapping of its water levels. A retired Army officer, he has volunteered annually to help the Nature Conservancy and its partners determine how the prolonged drought in the Southwest and the depletion of aquifers from local use affect the river. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'By Mapping a River, Volunteers Take a Stake in Its Future', 'kicker': 'Green', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Birds', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Conservation of Resources', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Drought', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Rivers', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Volunteers and Community Service', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Water', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Bureau of Land Management', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Nature Conservancy', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'ARIZONA', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-15T15:02:54+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Science', 'subsection_name': 'Environment', 'byline': {'original': 'By Glenn Swain', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Glenn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Swain', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ad90b595-1ee2-5f39-8975-237d4e78477a', 'word_count': 636, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ad90b595-1ee2-5f39-8975-237d4e78477a'}, {'abstract': 'In Alyson Hagy’s novel, a young ranchman dreams of training a bay filly for a life in polo.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/06/17/books/review/boleto-a-novel-by-alyson-hagy.html', 'snippet': 'In Alyson Hagy’s novel, a young ranchman dreams of training a bay filly for a life in polo.', 'lead_paragraph': 'With his graceful depictions of the embattled American West, Wallace Stegner remains unrivaled in his ability to humanize the clash between frontier beauty and “progress.” But there is always more gold in those hills, and Alyson Hagy isn’t a writer to mistake pyrite for something precious. In her third novel, “Boleto,” the alluring wilds of Wyoming and the horses wrangled there come with price tags affixed. The question is: Are we buying?', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/17/books/review/17MACHART/17MACHART-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1019, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/17/books/review/17MACHART/17MACHART-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2038, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/17/books/review/17MACHART/17MACHART-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/06/17/books/review/17MACHART/17MACHART-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/17/books/review/17MACHART/17MACHART-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Sport of Kings', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Sport of Kings', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hagy, Alyson', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-15T15:04:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Bruce Machart', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Bruce', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Machart', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/389e4df4-137b-595b-8d17-af40c344ef88', 'word_count': 880, 'uri': 'nyt://article/389e4df4-137b-595b-8d17-af40c344ef88'}, {'abstract': 'In Michael Frayn’s novel, set in Greece, a charming sociopath assumes the identity of a famous lecturer. Mayhem ensues.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/06/17/books/review/skios-a-novel-by-michael-frayn.html', 'snippet': 'In Michael Frayn’s novel, set in Greece, a charming sociopath assumes the identity of a famous lecturer. Mayhem ensues.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Can the hoary trope of mistaken identity still play in the age of Google images? Especially if it’s a young, handsome womanizer with tousled blond hair being mistaken for the middle-aged, balding keynote speaker at the meticulously planned conference of a prestigious foundation?', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '13', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/17/books/review/17frayn/17frayn-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 908, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/17/books/review/17frayn/17frayn-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1817, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/17/books/review/17frayn/17frayn-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/06/17/books/review/17frayn/17frayn-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/17/books/review/17frayn/17frayn-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Fantasy Island', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Fantasy Island', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Frayn, Michael', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-15T15:04:40+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Alex Witchel', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Alex', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Witchel', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/882bd31a-325e-5b90-ac5e-ea7d64025970', 'word_count': 1176, 'uri': 'nyt://article/882bd31a-325e-5b90-ac5e-ea7d64025970'}, {'abstract': 'To the afflicted at a Karachi hospital, the arrival of Mohammed Hanif’s heroine is a sign of salvation.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/06/17/books/review/our-lady-of-alice-bhatti-by-mohammed-hanif.html', 'snippet': 'To the afflicted at a Karachi hospital, the arrival of Mohammed Hanif’s heroine is a sign of salvation.', 'lead_paragraph': 'We need to talk about Alice. \xadAlice, with her black hair and big mouth. With her beautiful body and poor impulse control. Alice, criminal and savior, the victim and heroine of “Our Lady of Alice Bhatti,” a deft, evil little novel of comic genius by Mohammed Hanif, author of the prizewinning “Case of Exploding Mangoes.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/17/books/review/17SEHGAL/17SEHGAL-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 978, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/17/books/review/17SEHGAL/17SEHGAL-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1956, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/17/books/review/17SEHGAL/17SEHGAL-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/06/17/books/review/17SEHGAL/17SEHGAL-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/17/books/review/17SEHGAL/17SEHGAL-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Interpreter of Maladies', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Interpreter of Maladies', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hanif, Mohammed', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-15T15:05:09+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Parul Sehgal', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Parul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Sehgal', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/f494e46c-10c8-5897-a2af-460f4632be9e', 'word_count': 1235, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f494e46c-10c8-5897-a2af-460f4632be9e'}, {'abstract': 'In Gillian Flynn’s “Gone Girl,” a young woman disappears on her fifth wedding anniversary — and her husband is suspected of murder.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/06/17/books/review/gillian-flynns-gone-girl-and-more.html', 'snippet': 'In Gillian Flynn’s “Gone Girl,” a young woman disappears on her fifth wedding anniversary — and her husband is suspected of murder.', 'lead_paragraph': '“This is the hardest part,” confides one of the untrustworthy narrators in GONE GIRL (Crown, $25), “waiting for stupid people to figure things out.” There’s no need to rub it in, because Gillian Flynn’s latest novel of psychological suspense will confound anyone trying to keep up with her quicksilver mind and diabolical rules of play. Not that there’s anything underhanded about her intentions: she promises to deliver an account of the troubled marriage of Nick and Amy Dunne, who alternate as narrators, and so she does. The trickery is in the devilish way she tells their story.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '15', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/17/books/review/Crime/Crime-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 702, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/17/books/review/Crime/Crime-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1404, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/17/books/review/Crime/Crime-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/06/17/books/review/Crime/Crime-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/17/books/review/Crime/Crime-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Avengers', 'kicker': 'Crime', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Avengers', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Todd, Charles (Author)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Desai, Kishwar', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Flynn, Gillian', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Piccirilli, Tom', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-15T15:05:40+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Marilyn Stasio', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Marilyn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Stasio', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/557412db-1a25-528d-8db6-2d5d7b7b6f45', 'word_count': 903, 'uri': 'nyt://article/557412db-1a25-528d-8db6-2d5d7b7b6f45'}, {'abstract': 'The conventional wisdom in America and Europe is making things worse, Paul Krugman says.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/06/17/books/review/end-this-depression-now-by-paul-krugman.html', 'snippet': 'The conventional wisdom in America and Europe is making things worse, Paul Krugman says.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Nearly four years on from the financial meltdown that plunged it into recession, the global economy remains fragile. The latest green shoots of recovery in the United States already show signs of turning brown. It is touch and go whether Barack Obama will enter the polls with unemployment above or below the 8 percent rate that usually means defeat for an incumbent president. Across the Atlantic, the euro zone stumbles from crisis to crisis; the continuing problems of the heavily indebted PIIGS (Portugal, Ireland, Italy, Greece and Spain) remain the basis for many a nightmare, some of the worst involving the banks around the world that own much of the debt.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '20', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/17/books/review/0617-BISHOP/0617-BISHOP-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 996, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/17/books/review/0617-BISHOP/0617-BISHOP-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1993, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/17/books/review/0617-BISHOP/0617-BISHOP-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/06/17/books/review/0617-BISHOP/0617-BISHOP-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/17/books/review/0617-BISHOP/0617-BISHOP-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'What Would Keynes Do?', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'What Would Keynes Do?', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Economy', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Economic Conditions and Trends', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Recession and Depression', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Krugman, Paul', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-15T15:06:10+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Matthew Bishop', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Matthew', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bishop', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/834dc3bf-ff81-5db5-8a01-dd754d651db0', 'word_count': 1224, 'uri': 'nyt://article/834dc3bf-ff81-5db5-8a01-dd754d651db0'}, {'abstract': 'The suburbs, a minivan, Costco. Dan Zevin’s comic journey through adulthood continues.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/06/17/books/review/dan-gets-a-minivan-by-dan-zevin.html', 'snippet': 'The suburbs, a minivan, Costco. Dan Zevin’s comic journey through adulthood continues.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The subtitle of “Dan Gets a Minivan” — “Life at the Intersection of Dude and Dad” — nicely encapsulates a genre that has provided rich fodder for former dudes like Bill Cosby (“Fatherhood”), Robert Wilder (“Daddy Needs a Drink”) and Dave Barry (take your pick). It has also spawned a lucrative second career for the actor Paul Reiser (“Couplehood,” “Babyhood,” “Familyhood”). ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '30', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/17/books/review/17SALTZSTEIN2/17SALTZSTEIN2-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 721, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/17/books/review/17SALTZSTEIN2/17SALTZSTEIN2-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1441, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/17/books/review/17SALTZSTEIN2/17SALTZSTEIN2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/06/17/books/review/17SALTZSTEIN2/17SALTZSTEIN2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/17/books/review/17SALTZSTEIN2/17SALTZSTEIN2-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Changing Lanes', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Changing Lanes', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Zevin, Dan', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-15T15:06:37+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dan Saltzstein', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Saltzstein', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/7fb029db-5569-564a-84ee-c5fae69b937d', 'word_count': 641, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7fb029db-5569-564a-84ee-c5fae69b937d'}, {'abstract': 'In this historical novel, April Bernard imagines confessional letters from Margaret Fuller to Nathaniel Hawthorne’s wife, Sophia.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/06/17/books/review/miss-fuller-by-april-bernard.html', 'snippet': 'In this historical novel, April Bernard imagines confessional letters from Margaret Fuller to Nathaniel Hawthorne’s wife, Sophia.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The poet April Bernard’s latest work of fiction, “Miss Fuller,” opens in 1850, on the day the translator, literary critic, essayist, political activist and journalist Margaret Fuller died in a shipwreck off the coast of Long Island. Fuller, who had been in Italy reporting for Horace Greeley’s New-York Tribune, was returning home to the United States with her husband and young son when the ship foundered during a storm. Most of the novel is seen from the point of view of a woman named Anne, a fictitious sister of Henry David Thoreau; other parts are narrated by Fuller herself, via fabricated letters supposedly found by \xadThoreau on the day he visited the site of the wreck.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '30', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Transcendentalist', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Transcendentalist', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Fuller, Margaret', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bernard, April', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-15T15:07:21+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sarah Fay', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sarah', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Fay', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/d22770ff-4ed6-571d-83cb-28555477192f', 'word_count': 536, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d22770ff-4ed6-571d-83cb-28555477192f'}, {'abstract': 'Three new picture books help young readers explore their emotions.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/06/17/books/review/happy-how-do-you-feel-and-fish-on-a-walk.html', 'snippet': 'Three new picture books help young readers explore their emotions.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Americans love to talk about feelings. We do it almost compulsively and generally go about with the assumption that expressing emotion is a good idea. We are always articulating anger, jealousy and anxiety while people from more decorous cultures know when to keep their inner lives and dirty laundry to themselves.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '16', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/17/books/review/617-bks-Jenkins-sub/617-bks-Jenkins-sub-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 834, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/17/books/review/617-bks-Jenkins-sub/617-bks-Jenkins-sub-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1668, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/17/books/review/617-bks-Jenkins-sub/617-bks-Jenkins-sub-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/06/17/books/review/617-bks-Jenkins-sub/617-bks-Jenkins-sub-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/17/books/review/617-bks-Jenkins-sub/617-bks-Jenkins-sub-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Express Yourself', 'kicker': "Children's Books", 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Express Yourself', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Muggenthaler, Eva', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Browne, Anthony', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'van Hout, Mies', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-15T15:08:11+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Emily Jenkins', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Emily', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Jenkins', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/137cff77-61ce-5f0a-8c0d-65d41636cabf', 'word_count': 945, 'uri': 'nyt://article/137cff77-61ce-5f0a-8c0d-65d41636cabf'}, {'abstract': 'A teenager faces complications from siblings older and younger.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/06/17/books/review/see-you-at-harrys-by-jo-knowles.html', 'snippet': 'A teenager faces complications from siblings older and younger.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Adolescence is — for almost every\xadone, at least some of the time — excruciating. (Those few for whom this is not so, beware: there can be few things more dismaying than to find that adolescence was your zenith.)', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '17', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Third Child', 'kicker': "Children's Books", 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Third Child', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Knowles, Jo', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-15T15:08:36+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'sec