hout Canada were searching Friday afternoon for a security company employee after three of his co-workers were fatally shot and a fourth was critically wounded during an early morning robbery attempt at a university residence and student services center in Edmonton, Alberta.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Security Worker Is Sought in Shootings on Canadian Campus', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Canada: 3 Killed in Campus Shooting', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Canada', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Deaths (Fatalities)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Edmonton (Alberta)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'School Shootings', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Colleges and Universities', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Robberies and Thefts', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'University of Alberta', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Murders and Attempted Murders', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-15T15:16:31+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Americas', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ian Austen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ian', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Austen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/40ebe52b-6f08-5787-addf-67b9b93754c9', 'word_count': 387, 'uri': 'nyt://article/40ebe52b-6f08-5787-addf-67b9b93754c9'}, {'abstract': 'Berlin’s reluctance to aid its euro zone brethren has many supporters, but saying “no” too many times could have global repercussions.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/06/16/business/german-rectitude-has-its-risks.html', 'snippet': 'Berlin’s reluctance to aid its euro zone brethren has many supporters, but saying “no” too many times could have global repercussions.', 'lead_paragraph': 'As markets pummeled Spanish and Italian government bonds this week after Europe’s latest effort to contain the euro crisis, Germany seemed increasingly isolated in its demands for more austerity. The June 9 cover of The Economist portrayed the world economy as a sinking oil tanker with the caption, “Please can we start the engines now, Mrs. Merkel?” ', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/25/business/james-stewart/james-stewart-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/06/25/business/james-stewart/james-stewart-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/25/business/james-stewart/james-stewart-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/25/business/james-stewart/james-stewart-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1333, 'width': 2000, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/25/business/james-stewart/james-stewart-thumbStandard-v3.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/06/25/business/james-stewart/james-stewart-thumbStandard-v3.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/06/25/business/james-stewart/james-stewart-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'German Rectitude Has Its Risks', 'kicker': 'Common Sense', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'German Rectitude Has Its Risks', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Germany', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'European Sovereign Debt Crisis (2010- )', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-15T15:17:25+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By James B. Stewart', 'person': [{'firstname': 'James', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d28647b3-863e-5787-869a-692617da1bd2', 'word_count': 1196, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d28647b3-863e-5787-869a-692617da1bd2'}, {'abstract': 'Clarence Ditlow, executive director of the Center for Auto Safety and the person who pushed for the federal investigation, said he was “certain” there would be a recall of the S.U.V.', 'web_url': 'https://wheels.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/06/15/investigation-of-jeep-grand-cherokee-portends-a-recall-safety-advocate-says/', 'snippet': 'Clarence Ditlow, executive director of the Center for Auto Safety and the person who pushed for the federal investigation, said he was “certain” there would be a recall of the S.U.V.', 'lead_paragraph': 'With the National Highway Traffic Safety Administration having decided to upgrade its investigation of rear-impact fires involving Jeep Grand Cherokees, a recall of millions of those vehicles is “certain,” said Clarence Ditlow, the executive director of the Center for Auto Safety, the organization whose work prompted the federal inquiry.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Investigation of Jeep Grand Cherokee Portends a Recall, Safety Advocate Says', 'kicker': 'Wheels', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Automobile Safety Features and Defects', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Automobiles', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Center for Auto Safety', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Chrysler Group LLC', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Jeep Division of Chrysler', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Highway Traffic Safety Administration', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-15T15:17:52+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Automobiles', 'byline': {'original': 'By Christopher Jensen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Christopher', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Jensen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b898d550-587f-5330-8ad5-e65ff5b7bbc1', 'word_count': 716, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b898d550-587f-5330-8ad5-e65ff5b7bbc1'}, {'abstract': 'Seven tips for easier family travel.', 'web_url': 'https://parenting.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/06/15/practical-travelers-tips-for-summer-travel-with-kids/', 'snippet': 'Seven tips for easier family travel.', 'lead_paragraph': 'If you’re planning a trip involving flights with children this summer, you’ll want to check out Practical Traveler Michelle Higgins’s advice for traveling families. “At a minimum, especially if you’re traveling with small children, take a change of clothes onboard for the child and yourself. That way, when she spills cranberry juice all over you, or her diaper leaks, you have options.” My oldest son once soaked himself at the infinity fountain in the Detroit airport …', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Practical Traveler’s Tips for Summer Travel With Kids', 'kicker': 'Motherlode', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-06-15T15:20:53+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': "By Kj Dell'Antonia", 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kj', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': "Dell'Antonia", 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/80a9f393-ed0c-550f-8253-34f4e8740142', 'word_count': 77, 'uri': 'nyt://article/80a9f393-ed0c-550f-8253-34f4e8740142'}, {'abstract': 'Three books for children — and adults — who love to cook.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/06/17/books/review/minettes-feast-bon-appetit-and-tyler-makes-pancakes.html', 'snippet': 'Three books for children — and adults — who love to cook.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I like to claim I grew up watching Julia Child on TV, though what really happened is, I watched my parents watch her. I also like to believe “Mastering the Art of French Cooking” was a huge influence on my own, though I’m not sure I’ve ever even looked at the vegetable chapter. I was there for Julia when she got all Californian. (A recipe for pita? Come on.) I’m here for her even now that she’s no longer around. We all love Julia and the stories from her wonderful life: the O.S.S., the sophisticated husband who introduced her to sole meunière, the Cordon Bleu, “The French Chef,” Paris, Provence, the fact that she was over six feet tall and was said to have dropped a chicken on live television. (Alas, it was only a potato pancake.)', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/17/books/review/Hodgman-1338835555981/Hodgman-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1014, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/17/books/review/Hodgman-1338835555981/Hodgman-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2029, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/17/books/review/Hodgman-1338835555981/Hodgman-1338835555981-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/06/17/books/review/Hodgman-1338835555981/Hodgman-1338835555981-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/17/books/review/Hodgman-1338835555981/Hodgman-1338835555981-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Let’s Eat!', 'kicker': "Children's Books", 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Let’s Eat!', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Florence, Tyler', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Reich, Susanna', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bates, Amy', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hartland, Jessie', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-15T15:22:24+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ann Hodgman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ann', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hodgman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/eeb8cfd6-bb05-5b92-939a-fc933f38302b', 'word_count': 923, 'uri': 'nyt://article/eeb8cfd6-bb05-5b92-939a-fc933f38302b'}, {'abstract': 'A study finds that even successful business owners can make poor decisions when it comes to so-called passion investments like vineyards, baseball teams and racehorses.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/06/16/your-money/in-investments-as-in-life-passion-can-cloud-judgment.html', 'snippet': 'A study finds that even successful business owners can make poor decisions when it comes to so-called passion investments like vineyards, baseball teams and racehorses.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I’VE been talking to a lot of wealthy people in the last few weeks who have followed their passions into a variety of investments: vineyards, baseball teams, restaurants and racehorses. But I couldn’t help wondering what it would take for these people, who had made the money for such investments by being shrewd in other lines of work, to sell their stake in a fascinating second career. ', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/16/your-money/16wealth/16wealth-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 330, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/06/16/your-money/16wealth/16wealth-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 330}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/16/your-money/16wealth/16wealth-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/16/your-money/16wealth/16wealth-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'In Investments, as in Life, Passion Can Cloud Judgment', 'kicker': 'Wealth Matters', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'In Investments, As in Life, Passion Can Cloud Judgment', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hobbies', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Asset Allocation (Personal Finances)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Personal Finances', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'High Net Worth Individuals', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-15T15:22:27+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Your Money', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Sullivan', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Sullivan', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/71fbc106-15da-506d-9374-5392b2e40749', 'word_count': 1290, 'uri': 'nyt://article/71fbc106-15da-506d-9374-5392b2e40749'}, {'abstract': 'A heroine’s ability to summon light is just what her world sorely needs.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/06/17/books/review/shadow-and-bone-by-leigh-bardugo.html', 'snippet': 'A heroine’s ability to summon light is just what her world sorely needs.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Some fantasy novels deal out the tropes of the genre like cards from a dog-eared deck. Others affirm the elemental power of these tropes, reminding us not only why we read fantasy, but also why we read at all. There may be nothing new under the sun, but a good story makes you just not care. Like the expert strike of a reflex hammer, it hits precisely the right spot.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '19', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Gleam Off the Samovar', 'kicker': "Children's Books", 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Gleam Off the Samovar', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bardugo, Leigh', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-15T15:22:52+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Laini Taylor', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Laini', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Taylor', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/4e689a64-01d1-5bf0-8875-2dda8c976848', 'word_count': 910, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4e689a64-01d1-5bf0-8875-2dda8c976848'}, {'abstract': 'Not all gossip is bad, according to researchers, who say it can be useful in maintaining social norms and keeping people in line.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/06/16/your-money/studies-find-gossip-isnt-just-loose-talk.html', 'snippet': 'Not all gossip is bad, according to researchers, who say it can be useful in maintaining social norms and keeping people in line.', 'lead_paragraph': 'GOSSIP. Almost all of us do it, most of us are embarrassed about it, and sometimes, to our horror, we get caught.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Studies Find That Gossip Isn’t Just Loose Talk', 'kicker': 'Shortcuts', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Studies Find That Gossip Isn’t Just Loose Talk', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Gossip', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Psychology and Psychologists', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sociology', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Journal of Personality and Social Psychology', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-15T15:24:33+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Your Money', 'byline': {'original': 'By Alina Tugend', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Alina', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Tugend', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/43adad9f-4ea0-508a-ba72-ca31eeaca4ac', 'word_count': 1213, 'uri': 'nyt://article/43adad9f-4ea0-508a-ba72-ca31eeaca4ac'}, {'abstract': 'Chinese writers employ stealth to elude the censors.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/06/17/books/review/how-chinese-writers-elude-censors.html', 'snippet': 'Chinese writers employ stealth to elude the censors.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Two months ago at the London Book Fair, where China was this year’s “guest of honor,” Ma Jian, the exiled author of the Tiananmen-era novel “Beijing Coma,” inked a red X across his face in an emotional protest against Chinese censorship. It may be a sign of the times that while drawing attention from the Western press, the move went almost unnoticed by other Chinese authors present, many of whom still live in China and find ways to circumvent the authorities.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '31', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/17/books/review/17backpage/17backpage-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 759, 'width': 625, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/17/books/review/17backpage/17backpage-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 759, 'width': 625, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'i