ravel and Vacations', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Palm Springs (Calif)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-15T20:27:34+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Travel', 'section_name': 'Travel', 'byline': {'original': 'By CARRIE NIEMAN CULPEPPER', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Carrie', 'middlename': 'Nieman', 'lastname': 'CULPEPPER', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Slideshow', '_id': 'nyt://slideshow/ecf89579-dfc3-50f4-a5e1-6d2aa7ef0226', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://slideshow/ecf89579-dfc3-50f4-a5e1-6d2aa7ef0226'}, {'abstract': 'A pilot program gives Philippine fishermen an incentive to collect worn-out gear that can be turned into something useful instead of dumping it in the ocean.', 'web_url': 'https://green.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/06/15/a-second-life-for-discarded-fishing-nets/', 'snippet': 'A pilot program gives Philippine fishermen an incentive to collect worn-out gear that can be turned into something useful instead of dumping it in the ocean.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Abandoned and lost fishing gear makes up about 10 percent of the trash that collects in the world’s oceans, according to a report from the United Nations. Much of this debris is lost in storms, vandalized or simply discarded. It piles up on beaches, creates a navigational hazard for boats or settles to the bottom, where it can damage sensitive ecosystems. Discarded nets can cause a particular problem as they continue to “ghost fish,” trapping fish and other sea animals like turtles, seabirds and dolphins.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Second Life for Discarded Fishing Nets', 'kicker': 'Green', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-06-15T20:30:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Science', 'subsection_name': 'Environment', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jim Witkin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jim', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Witkin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/848116c2-0891-5267-8eb7-7754d72cbce4', 'word_count': 463, 'uri': 'nyt://article/848116c2-0891-5267-8eb7-7754d72cbce4'}, {'abstract': 'Golden beets and their flavorful greens, paired with yogurt, mint and dill, make for a rich summer salad with an Eastern Mediterranean slant.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/06/20/dining/off-with-the-tops-off-with-your-head-making-use-of-the-whole-beet-from-greens-to-root.html', 'snippet': 'Golden beets and their flavorful greens, paired with yogurt, mint and dill, make for a rich summer salad with an Eastern Mediterranean slant.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I SPEND a lot of time meandering through the greenmarkets. When planning my pedestrian itinerary, I always make a deliberate detour, and not just for research, but also to pick up something for dinner.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/20/dining/20KITCH1_SPAN/20KITCH1_SPAN-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 399, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/06/20/dining/20KITCH1_SPAN/20KITCH1_SPAN-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 399}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/20/dining/20KITCH1_SPAN/20KITCH1_SPAN-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/20/dining/20KITCH1_SPAN/20KITCH1_SPAN-superJumbo-v2.jpg', '