 setting himself on fire to protest government policies in the region, according to exile groups and Radio Free Asia. The herder, Tamdin Thar, thought to be in his early 60s, set himself on fire in Markethang, in the Huangnan Tibetan Autonomous Prefecture, said the group Free Tibet. Since 2009, at least 39 Tibetans have set themselves on fire. Of those, 30 have died, according to the International Campaign for Tibet, an advocacy group in Washington. “If the human rights situation doesn’t improve, such tragic incidents will keep happening,” said Tsering Woeser, a Tibetan writer in Beijing. “Many Tibetans think the pain of self-immolation is nothing compared to the pain of living without religious freedom.” ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'China: Tibetan Dies in Protest Fire', 'kicker': 'World Briefing | Asia', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'China: Tibetan Dies in Protest Fire', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Human Rights and Human Rights Violations', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Deaths (Fatalities)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Qinghai Province (China)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Demonstrations, Protests, and Riots', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Immolation', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Tibet', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-16T00:08:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andrew Jacobs', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andrew', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Jacobs', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/5c6d2a2f-7c7e-556e-bf87-a8acb5116434', 'word_count': 130, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5c6d2a2f-7c7e-556e-bf87-a8acb5116434'}, {'abstract': 'Three days after it was reported that Brett Anderson, the well-regarded restaurant critic for The Times-Picayune, was among about 200 employees being laid off, the paper said Friday that it had offered Mr. Anderson a job.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/06/16/us/louisiana-dining-critic-has-a-job-paper-says.html', 'snippet': 'Three days after it was reported that Brett Anderson, the well-regarded restaurant critic for The Times-Picayune, was among about 200 employees being laid off, the paper said Friday that it had offered Mr. Anderson a job.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Three days after it was reported that Brett Anderson, the well-regarded restaurant critic for The Times-Picayune in New Orleans, was among about 200 employees being laid off, the paper said Friday that it had offered Mr. Anderson “a position writing about restaurants and dining.” Mr. Anderson, 41, is headed to Harvard University in the fall on a journalism fellowship that he landed before the layoffs took place. “We told Brett then that he would have a job with us when the fellowship ended,” Jim Amoss, the paper’s editor, said in a report on the newspaper’s Web site. “We should have communicated that this week when we announced the changes.” Mr. Anderson had said earlier that he was told the fellowship was the reason for his dismissal. Mr. Anderson said that he did not have to make a decision about the job until his fellowship nears its end in 2013. “I don’t know yet,” he said. “I’m going to kick that can down the road a ways as to whether or not I’ll take it.” ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '13', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Louisiana: Dining Critic Has a Job, Paper Says ', 'kicker': 'National Briefing | South', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Louisiana: Dining Critic Has a Job, Paper Says', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Times-Picayune (New Orleans)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Layoffs and Job Reductions', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Anderson, Brett', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-16T00:08:52+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jeff Gordinier', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jeff', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gordinier', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/93356669-c800-5033-87c9-0ffee0d2c13b', 'word_count': 176, 'uri': 'nyt://article/93356669-c800-5033-87c9-0ffee0d2c13b'}, {'abstract': 'People, what is it about our love for wide open spaces? It seems to be playing a big role in Campaign 2012.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/06/16/opinion/collins-running-on-empty.html', 'snippet': 'People, what is it about our love for wide open spaces? It seems to be playing a big role in Campaign 2012.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Our biggest political division is the war between the empty places and the crowded places. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '23', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/03/opinion/gail-collins/gail-collins-articleLarge.png', 'height': 300, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/04/03/opinion/gail-collins/gail-collins-articleLarge.png', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 300}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/03/opinion/gail-collins/gail-collins-jumbo.png', 'height': 512, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': N