'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Video', '_id': 'nyt://video/2084dbe3-051e-5677-8189-eb1d42f7c0bf', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://video/2084dbe3-051e-5677-8189-eb1d42f7c0bf'}, {'abstract': 'The first two games of the N.B.A. Finals have so far attracted more viewers than any finals since ESPN and ABC’s current deal with the league began in 2002-03, according to Nielsen.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/06/16/sports/basketball/nba-finals-tv-ratings-up-seven-percent-from-last-year.html', 'snippet': 'The first two games of the N.B.A. Finals have so far attracted more viewers than any finals since ESPN and ABC’s current deal with the league began in 2002-03, according to Nielsen.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Oklahoma City Thunder and Miami Heat have split the first two games of the N.B.A. finals — exciting contests that have so far attracted more viewers than any finals since ESPN and ABC’s current deal with the league began in 2002-03, according to Nielsen. Game 2, won by the Heat, 100-96, was seen by an average of 16.7 million viewers on ABC, 7 percent better than the comparable broadcast last year between the Heat and the Dallas Mavericks. Through two games, the average viewership of 16.4 million is also up 7 percent. ¶ Tina Charles had 20 points and 8 rebounds to lead the host Connecticut Sun to a 97-55 victory over the Liberty. Connecticut led by 61-27 at halftime, setting the league record for the largest lead at the break. Essence Carson scored 14 points for New York (3-7). (AP)', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'T.V. Ratings for Finals Up Seven Percent From Last Year', 'kicker': 'Sports Briefing | Pro Basketball', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Ratings Up 7 Percent', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Basketball', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'ABC INC', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Miami Heat', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Oklahoma City Thunder', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'ESPN', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-16T01:26:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Pro Basketball', 'byline': {'original': 'By Richard Sandomir', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Richard', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Sandomir', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/8a0449ad-f743-542d-9565-f21f8c5ae25c', 'word_count': 144, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8a0449ad-f743-542d-9565-f21f8c5ae25c'}, {'abstract': 'Taxpayers should love Citigroup even if shareholders don’t. And, on the bank’s 200th birthday, here’s why.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/06/16/opinion/nocera-the-safest-bank.html', 'snippet': 'Taxpayers should love Citigroup even if shareholders don’t. And, on the bank’s 200th birthday, here’s why.', 'lead_paragraph': 'We’re counterprogramming today.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '23', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/11/01/opinion/nocera-circular/nocera-circular-jumbo-v2.png', 'height': 228, 'width': 228, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/11/01/opinion/nocera-circular/nocera-circular-superJumbo-v2.png', 'height': 228, 'width': 228, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/11/01/opinion/nocera-circul