'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Toronto (Ontario)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-16T22:35:33+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By James C. McKinley Jr', 'person': [{'firstname': 'James', 'middlename': 'C.', 'lastname': 'McKinley', 'qualifier': 'Jr', 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d76e0d00-3c15-5d79-830d-fbbb8aa5d4de', 'word_count': 386, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d76e0d00-3c15-5d79-830d-fbbb8aa5d4de'}, {'abstract': 'Abby Wambach scored on a 35-yard half-volley to lead the United States women’s national team to a 3-1 victory over Sweden in an Olympic tuneup.', 'web_url': 'https://goal.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/06/16/wambach-stunner-helps-u-s-women-beat-sweden/', 'snippet': 'Abby Wambach scored on a 35-yard half-volley to lead the United States women’s national team to a 3-1 victory over Sweden in an Olympic tuneup.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The United States women’s national team began a two-game trip to Sweden on Saturday with a 3-1 victory over the host country. Alex Morgan scored in the first half and Tobin Heath in the second, but the goal of the day — and that includes the Euro 2012 action — was the 35-yard, first-touch half-volley scored by Abby Wambach in the eighth minute.', 'print_section': 'SP', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Wambach Stunner Helps U.S. Women Beat Sweden', 'kicker': 'Goal', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'U.S. Women Beat Sweden', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Olympic Games (2012)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Soccer', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Wambach, Abby', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-16T22:55:41+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Soccer', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andrew Das', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andrew', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Das', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/8cfe096c-52dd-51eb-98fe-ab104e8ffcb1', 'word_count': 230, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8cfe096c-52dd-51eb-98fe-ab104e8ffcb1'}, {'abstract': 'With a record-breaking heat wave fresh in Texans’ memory, and summer fast approaching, here are ways to keep cool.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/06/17/us/beating-the-texas-heat-without-having-to-go-too-far.html', 'snippet': 'With a record-breaking heat wave fresh in Texans’ memory, and summer fast approaching, here are ways to keep cool.', 'lead_paragraph': 'This time last year was the beginning of a record-busting heat wave that gripped Texas, with several cities experiencing 50-plus consecutive days of 100-degree-plus temperatures and an average 2011 summer temperature of 86.7 degrees. The rains this spring have cooled things off, but the first day of summer is approaching, and with it expect above-average temperatures, says John Nielsen-Gammon, the state’s climatologist. While some people flee the oppressive heat and vacation in cooler climates in the North, there are ways to stay in state and keep cool. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '25B', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/17/us/17TTTravel/17TTTravel-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 255, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/06/17/us/17TTTravel/17TTTravel-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 255}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/17/us/17TTTravel/17TTTravel-jumbo.jpg', 'hei