, 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/17/blogs/greece-vote-opens-rdv/greece-vote-opens-rdv-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1421, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Decision Day for Greece and the Euro', 'kicker': 'IHT Rendezvous', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Elections', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'European Sovereign Debt Crisis (2010- )', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Europe', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Greece', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-17T05:58:52+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'World', 'byline': {'original': 'By The International Herald Tribune', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The International Herald Tribune'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/811b00a9-8312-529a-aba4-dce78b5b5fee', 'word_count': 607, 'uri': 'nyt://article/811b00a9-8312-529a-aba4-dce78b5b5fee'}, {'abstract': 'Top magazine and long-form articles this week.', 'web_url': 'https://india.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/06/17/newswallah-long-reads-edition-31/', 'snippet': 'Top magazine and long-form articles this week.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The cover story in Tehelka magazine, entitled “Jailhouse Rock,” uncovers the continuing “goonda raj” — or thugs’ reign — in the state of Uttar Pradesh despite assurances from the newly elected chief minister, Akhilesh Yadav, of stringent action against criminals in the state. The author, Ashish Khaitan, cites several examples of convicted gangsters, incarcerated for a broad range of crimes including murder, extortion, abduction, assault and others. Abhay Singh, a notorious don, is “back in business” under the new administration in the state, the article notes. “On 24 May, the Samajwadi government…took the decision to withdraw a case registered in Faizabad district court under the U.P. Gangster Act – the only case out of 18 pending cases in which [Abhay] Singh had not got a bail,” thus allowing Mr. Singh to go free. The district police signed off on this. Incidentally, “exactly two years ago, the same district police had booked Mr. Singh under the U.P. Gangster Act,” Mr. Khaitan reported. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Newswallah: Long Reads Edition', 'kicker': 'India Ink', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Mumbai (India)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-17T09:57:10+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nikhila Gill', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nikhila', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gill', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/32d77f72-ed69-5340-8a29-f47ba8026055', 'word_count': 565, 'uri': 'nyt://article/32d77f72-ed69-5340-8a29-f47ba8026055'}, {'abstract': 'When I came out, I believed I was giving up the one thing I had always wanted since I was a kid: being a dad.', 'web_url': 'https://parenting.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/06/17/the-gift-of-being-gay-and-a-dad/', 'snippet': 'When I came out, I believed I was giving up the one thing I had always wanted since I was a kid: being a dad.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I’m in my mid-40s. Growing up, there was no such thing as gay parenting. I had a vague notion, I think, probably from television or a movie, that some kids’ fathers or mothers could end up running off with someone of the same sex. So, technically, they were a parent and they were gay – but it was a roundabout, devastating way to do it, kind of like demolishing a building by using a hurricane. It gets the job done but you wouldn’t want to see it.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Gift of Being Gay and a Dad', 'kicker': 'Motherlode', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Children and Childhood', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Families and Family Life', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': "Father's Day", 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Homosexuality', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Parenting', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-17T10:21:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Marcus Mabry', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Marcus', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Mabry', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/175c6330-22dd-577e-81cf-f8b40e092ed4', 'word_count': 695, 'uri': 'nyt://article/175c6330-22dd-577e-81cf-f8b40e092ed4'}, {'abstract': 'The Muslim Brotherhood projected its candidate as the winner of Egypt’s first competitive presidential election, hours after the ruling military council granted itself broad power over the future government.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/06/18/world/middleeast/egyptian-presidential-vote-enters-second-day.html', 'snippet': 'The Muslim Brotherhood projected its candidate as the winner of Egypt’s first competitive presidential election, hours after the ruling military council granted itself broad power over the future government.', 'lead_paragraph': 'CAIRO — The Muslim Brotherhood early Monday projected its candidate, an Islamist, as the winner of Egypt’s first competitive presidential election, hours after the ruling military council issued an interim constitution granting itself broad power over the future government, all but eliminating the president’s authority in an apparent effort to guard against just such a victory.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/18/world/EGYPT-2/EGYPT-2-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 360, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/06/18/world/EGYPT-2/EGYPT-2-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 360}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/18/world/EGYPT-2/EGYPT-2-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/18/world/EGYPT-2/EGYPT-2-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/18/world/EGYPT-2/EGYPT-2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/06/18/world/EGYPT-2/EGYPT-2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/18/world/EGYPT-2/EGYPT-2-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Egypt’s Military Cements Its Powers as Voting Ends', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Islamists Claim Victory In Egypt as the Military Moves to Cement Power', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Muslim Brotherhood (Egypt)', 'rank': 1, '