e home he shared with his fiancée in Rialto, Calif. He was 47.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/18/us/king-Obit/king-Obit-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 330, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/06/18/us/king-Obit/king-Obit-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 330}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/18/us/king-Obit/king-Obit-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 922, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/18/us/king-Obit/king-Obit-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1843, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/18/us/king-Obit/king-Obit-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/06/18/us/king-Obit/king-Obit-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/18/us/king-Obit/king-Obit-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Rodney King Dies at 47; Police Beating Victim Who Asked ‘Can We All Get Along?’', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Police Beating Victim Who Asked ‘Can We All Get Along?’', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'King, Rodney Glen', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Police Brutality and Misconduct', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Demonstrations, Protests, and Riots', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Race and Ethnicity', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Los Angeles (Calif)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Deaths (Obituaries)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-17T16:11:39+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jennifer Medina', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jennifer', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Medina', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Obituary (Obit)', '_id': 'nyt://article/a3bf64cd-3552-5d79-91b8-64b7b4b1cb7c', 'word_count': 1236, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a3bf64cd-3552-5d79-91b8-64b7b4b1cb7c'}, {'abstract': 'Appearing on CBS’s ‘Face the Nation’ on Sunday, Mitt Romney was equivocal about overturning President Obama’s order.', 'web_url': 'https://thecaucus.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/06/17/romney-doesnt-say-whether-he-will-overturn-obama-immigration-order/', 'snippet': 'Appearing on CBS’s ‘Face the Nation’ on Sunday, Mitt Romney was equivocal about overturning President Obama’s order.', 'lead_paragraph': ' ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Romney Doesn’t Say Whether He Would Overturn Obama Immigration Order', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'On TV, Romney Doesn’t Show Immigration Hand', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Presidential Election of 2012', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Romney, Mitt', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-17T16:22:52+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Trip Gabriel', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Trip', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gabriel', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c0fa06b1-a12a-5370-a02d-cbaf784c7fd6', 'word_count': 699, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c0fa06b1-a12a-5370-a02d-cbaf784c7fd6'}, {'abstract': 'The story of London’s men’s wear is often set up as a tension between the elder statesmen of traditional tailoring and the wild, independent designers of today, but the reality is that the two sides are beginning to blend quite nicely.', 'web_url': 'https://runway.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/06/17/traditional-tailoring-meets-the-younger-pack/', 'snippet': 'The story of London’s men’s wear is often set up as a tension between the elder statesmen of traditional tailoring and the wild, independent designers of today, but the reality is that the two sides are beginning to blend quite nicely.', 'lead_paragraph': 'After the E. Tautz show, in a wharf building on the east side of London, I stopped with Jonathan Player, who is shooting the men’s collections for The Times this weekend, for a coffee at an Italian place nearby. The clearly fashionable couple at the next table struck up a conversation, and, as it turned out, they were Alex and Zofia Torun-Shaw, the founders of Laird & Co., a young company making gentlemen’s hats here in London’s grand millinery tradition. They made the hats for some of the cool shows this weekend, including a pastel pink crusher for E. Tautz, which is a recently revitalized, more contemporary division of the Savile Row tailor Norton & Sons. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Traditional Tailoring Meets the Younger Pack', 'kicker': 'On the Runway', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-06-17T16:34:14+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Eric Wilson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Eric', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Wilson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3ff7be07-5b6c-5cdc-b0b2-e0de888bde4e', 'word_count': 586, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3ff7be07-5b6c-5cdc-b0b2-e0de888bde4e'}, {'abstract': 'Adam Sandler has now suffered two duds in the span of seven months (the last was “Jack & Jill”), leading to concern that his reign as a comedic must-see for many moviegoers is ending.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/06/17/box-office-busts-for-sandler-and-cruise-movies/', 'snippet': 'Adam Sandler has now suffered two duds in the span of seven months (the last was “Jack & Jill”), leading to concern that his reign as a comedic must-see for many moviegoers is ending.', 'lead_paragraph': 'LOS ANGELES — The weekend’s two new wide-release movies — “Rock of Ages” and Adam Sandler’s ultracrude “That’s My Boy” — both flopped, continuing Hollywood’s boom-or-bust summer.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Box Office Busts for Sandler and Cruise Movies', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Sandler and Cruise Films Flop at Box Office', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cruise, Tom', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Sandler, Adam', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-17T16:47:57+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Brooks Barnes', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Brooks', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Barnes', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/cbc59871-7958-5488-bd03-fcaa74e4ddb3', 'word_count': 222, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cbc59871-7958-5488-bd03-fcaa74e4ddb3'}, {'abstract': 'Better than you think.', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/06/17/long-run-greek-competitiveness/', 'snippet': 'Better than you think.', 'lead_paragraph': 'One of the things you keep hearing about Greece is that if it exits the euro one way or another there will be no gains, because Greece basically can’t export — so structural reform is the only way forward.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Long Run Greek Competitiveness', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-06-17T16:58:13+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/fb623678-5a59-5f71-806b-dbcfc9c77b31', 'word_count': 164, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fb623678-5a59-5f71-806b-dbcfc9c77b31'}, {'abstract': 'The Automats featured gleaming machines that were successfully marketed as newfangled gadgets that dispensed fresh food barely touched by human hands.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/06/17/revisiting-the-era-of-automatic-dining/', 'snippet': 'The Automats featured gleaming machines that were successfully marketed as newfangled gadgets that dispensed fresh food barely touched by human hands.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Audrey Meadows worked there in “That Touch of Mink.” Marlo Thomas met a photographer there in an episode of “That Girl.” Joan Crawford longs for a fellow patron’s pie in “Sadie McKee.” In “Easy Living,” Jean Arthur calmly digests her beef potpie while cafeteria chaos ensues. Neeley ate there in “A Tree Grows in Brooklyn.” An Irving Berlin ditty evokes the place (“let’s have another cup of coffee”) as do the lyrics of “Gentlemen Prefer Blondes.” The advertising slogan “Less work for mother” became a middle-class mantra.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '20', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Revisiting the Era of Automatic Dining', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Future Of Lunch, As Seen By the Past', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cafeterias', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Horn, Joseph', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Automats', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Public Library', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-17T17:00:15+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sam Roberts', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sam', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Roberts', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d5245ac9-734e-51c8-9867-4e5be65ef75b', 'word_count': 902, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d5245ac9-734e-51c8-9867-4e5be65ef75b'}, {'abstract': 'Club W.i.P, which is on Vandam Street in SoHo, was closed on Saturday night because of code violations, days after it was the scene of a fight between Chris Brown and people associated with Drake.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/06/17/police-shutter-club-in-aftermath-of-celebrity-brawl/', 'snippet': 'Club W.i.P, which is on Vandam Street in SoHo, was closed on Saturday night because of code violations, days after it was the scene of a fight between Chris Brown and people associated with Drake.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The police have shut down a nightclub where the R&B singer Chris Brown and people associated with the rapper Drake were involved in a brawl early Thursday morning, The Associated Press reported.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Police Close SoHo Club After a Celebrity Brawl', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Brown, Chris', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Drake', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Rihanna', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-17T17:00:58+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By James C. McKinley Jr', 'person': [{'firstname': 'James', 'middlename': 'C.', 'lastname': 'McKinley', 'qualifier': 'Jr', 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e888e118-94f6-5877-8cb9-002fb16a3165', 'word_count': 265, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e888e118-94f6-5877-8cb9-002fb16a3165'}, {'abstract': 'Thierry Roland, one of the most famous sports journalists in France, if not the most famous, died on Saturday.', 'web_url': 'https://goal.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/06/17/tribute-thierry-roland-the-legendary-voice-of-soccer-in-france/', 'snippet': 'Thierry Roland, one of the most famous sports journalists in France, if not the most famous, died on Saturday.', 'lead_paragraph': 'You probably don’t know him, but he was a legendary TV sportscaster who lived his dream and passion until the end. He died at age 74 , in the early morning of June 16.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Tribute: Thierry Roland, the Legendary Voice of Soccer in France', 'kicker': 'Goal', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Soccer', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Blatter, Sepp', 'rank'