icle', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Soccer', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jack Bell', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jack', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bell', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/073501ca-a306-51fa-bb1c-27549e924dbf', 'word_count': 1322, 'uri': 'nyt://article/073501ca-a306-51fa-bb1c-27549e924dbf'}, {'abstract': 'Eli Manning throws out the first pitch at the Mets game.', 'web_url': 'https://bats.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/06/17/manning-throws-out-first-pitch/', 'snippet': 'Eli Manning throws out the first pitch at the Mets game.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In honor of Fathers’ Day, Eli Manning threw out the first pitch at the Mets game Sunday afternoon while cradling his daughter, Ava, in his left arm. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Manning Throws Out First Pitch', 'kicker': 'Bats', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Baseball', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Manning, Eli', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Mets', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-17T17:42:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Baseball', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andrew Keh', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andrew', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Keh', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4e64a269-b071-5372-bdf9-31cbaf5b7ea7', 'word_count': 150, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4e64a269-b071-5372-bdf9-31cbaf5b7ea7'}, {'abstract': 'The man killed when a stage roof collapsed Saturday afternoon just before a Radiohead concert in Toronto has been indentified as Scott Johnson from South Yorkshire, England, a 33-year-old a drum technician for the band.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/06/17/victim-of-radiohead-stage-collapse-identified/', 'snippet': 'The man killed when a stage roof collapsed Saturday afternoon just before a Radiohead concert in Toronto has been indentified as Scott Johnson from South Yorkshire, England, a 33-year-old a drum technician for the band.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The man killed when a stage roof collapsed Saturday afternoon just before a Radiohead concert in Toronto has been identified as Scott Johnson, 33, from South Yorkshire, England. He was a drum technician for the band, the BBC and The Globe and Mail reported. Mr. Johnson also worked for Keane, another British band.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Victim of Radiohead Stage Collapse Identified as Drum Technician', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Radiohead', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-17T18:02:02+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By James C. McKinley Jr', 'person': [{'firstname': 'James', 'middlename': 'C.', 'lastname': 'McKinley', 'qualifier': 'Jr', 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/824a1a91-15aa-5571-bdf7-0fb63b00a887', 'word_count': 214, 'uri': 'nyt://article/824a1a91-15aa-5571-bdf7-0fb63b00a887'}, {'abstract': 'Speaking at a church in Brooklyn, Mayor Bloomberg reiterated his message that the city’s stop-and-frisk policy needed to be “mended, not ended.”', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/06/17/in-brooklyn-another-church-receives-the-mayors-gospel-on-stop-and-frisk/', 'snippet': 'Speaking at a church in Brooklyn, Mayor Bloomberg reiterated his message that the city’s stop-and-frisk policy needed to be “mended, not ended.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'Updated 4:20 p.m. | For the second consecutive Sunday, Mayor Michael R. Bloomberg took to the pulpit at a large, predominantly African-American church and defended the Police Department’s controversial stop-and-frisk program, saying it needed to be “mended, not ended.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'In Brooklyn, Another Church Receives the Mayor’s Gospel on Stop and Frisk', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-06-17T18:06:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nate Schweber', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nate', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Schweber', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/eff861e7-60d9-5cc6-afc5-8a2b90279e6f', 'word_count': 599, 'uri': 'nyt://article/eff861e7-60d9-5cc6-afc5-8a2b90279e6f'}, {'abstract': 'An election that settled nothing.', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/06/17/and-then-what/', 'snippet': 'An election that settled nothing.', 'lead_paragraph': 'So it appears that the governing coalition in Greece has pulled out a narrow victory — winning only a minority of votes, but getting a narrow majority in the parliament thanks to the 50-seat bonus New Democracy gets for coming in first.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'And Then What?', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-06-17T18:41:10+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/51551f7a-d8c2-52bd-8243-29065a9c8952', 'word_count': 189, 'uri': 'nyt://article/51551f7a-d8c2-52bd-8243-29065a9c8952'}, {'abstract': 'A new radio adaptation, with Stephen Rea as the narrator and Henry Goodman among those lending their voices, is available to American listeners on the BBC Web site.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/06/17/a-ulysses-for-those-unable-to-make-bloomsday-on-broadway/', 'snippet': 'A new radio adaptation, with Stephen Rea as the narrator and Henry Goodman among those lending their voices, is available to American listeners on the BBC Web site.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Fans of James Joyce who were unable to attend the annual “Bloomsday on Broadway” readings at Symphony Space on Saturday – along with anyone who appreciates the sound of famous literature read aloud by trained actors – will want to sample the BBC Radio 4 production “James Joyce’s Ulysses,” currently accessible to American listeners on the BBC Web site. Broadcast on Saturday – June 16 being the day when the action of “Ulysses” takes place – this new radio adaptation consisted of segments scattered across the day’s schedule, at times roughly corresponding to the movements of Leopold Bloom and Stephen Dedalus across Dublin in the novel.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A ‘Ulysses’ for Those Unable to Make ‘Bloomsday on Broadway’', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Joyce’s Words Waft Across the Water', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Goodman, Henry', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Joyce, James', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Rea, Stephen', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Symphony Space', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-17T19:10:29+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Mike Hale', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mike', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hale', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/25200208-047b-5363-b3bf-f829dfdc16f5', 'word_count': 191, 'uri': 'nyt://article/25200208-047b-5363-b3bf-f829dfdc16f5'}, {'abstract': 'The three dope-dealing Laguna Beach hipsters from “Savages” return, in a book going back in time to account for their pasts.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/06/18/books/the-kings-of-cool-by-don-winslow.html', 'snippet': 'The three dope-dealing Laguna Beach hipsters from “Savages” return, in a book going back in time to account for their pasts.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It was not possible to finish Don Winslow’s lean, mean, piercingly funny 2010 “Savages” without wanting more. But there was a problem. By the time Mr. Winslow finished his tale about three dope-dealing Laguna Beach hipsters — two guys and a girl: Ben, Chon and O — they were floating out to sea on God’s great cosmic surfboard. None of them was available for an encore. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/18/arts/jpbook/jpbook-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 936, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/18/arts/jpbook/jpbook-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1872, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'image