'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/06/18/us/southern-baptists-set-to-elect-their-first-black-leader.html', 'snippet': 'The Rev. Fred Luter Jr. of New Orleans is expected to become the leader of the convention, which has 16 million members and roots in the segregationist era.', 'lead_paragraph': 'NEW ORLEANS — The Southern Baptist Convention, a denomination born in 1845 in defense of slavery and a spiritual home to white supremacists for much of the 20th century, is poised to elect its first African-American president.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '11', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/18/us/BAPTIST/BAPTIST-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 340, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/06/18/us/BAPTIST/BAPTIST-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 340}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/18/us/BAPTIST/BAPTIST-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 728, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/18/us/BAPTIST/BAPTIST-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1456, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/18/us/BAPTIST/BAPTIST-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/06/18/us/BAPTIST/BAPTIST-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/18/us/BAPTIST/BAPTIST-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Southern Baptists Set for a Notable First', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Southern Baptists Set for a Notable First', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New Orleans (La)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Southern Baptist Convention', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Blacks', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Black People', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Ministers (Protestant)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Race and Ethnicity', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Christians and Christianity', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Luter, Fred Jr', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-17T22:05:02+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Erik Eckholm', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Erik', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Eckholm', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c86ecff8-3222-5511-b8b9-4b216176ea2c', 'word_count': 1177, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c86ecff8-3222-5511-b8b9-4b216176ea2c'}, {'abstract': 'The Australian Ballet celebrated its 50th anniversary with performances of “Swan Lake” at the Koch Theater.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/06/18/arts/dance/swan-lake-by-australian-ballet-at-koch-theater.html', 'snippet': 'The Australian Ballet celebrated its 50th anniversary with performances of “Swan Lake” at the Koch Theater.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It’s best to get disappointments over with before a show starts. As my companion to watch the Australian Ballet’s “Swan Lake” I invited an old friend who has known “Swan Lake” for decades. Two hours before Saturday’s performance at the David H. Koch Theater she e-mailed me to say that she was looking forward to it because Australia has real black swans, and she was eager to see the connection onstage. I informed her hurriedly that this “Swan Lake” was the one about Diana, Charles and Camilla, and that Australian black swans were not on offer. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/18/arts/swan/swan-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 984, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/18/arts/swan/swan-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1967, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/18/arts/swan/swan-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/06/18/arts/swan/swan-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/18/arts/swan/swan-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Relationships, Flawed and Fragile, in an Updated Classic', 'kicker': 'Dance Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Relationships, Flawed and Fragile, in an Updated Classic', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Australian Ballet', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Swan Lake (Ballet)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Dancing', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-17T22:05:39+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Dance', 'byline': {'original': 'By Alastair Macaulay', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Alastair', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Macaulay', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/85b1de27-ea6c-5ca8-b7e5-9dcad882ee08', 'word_count': 840, 'uri': 'nyt://article/85b1de27-ea6c-5ca8-b7e5-9dcad882ee08'}, {'abstract': 'Readers discuss Mayor Michael R. Bloomberg’s proposal.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/06/18/opinion/is-a-ban-on-large-sodas-the-answer-to-obesity.html', 'snippet': 'Readers discuss Mayor Michael R. Bloomberg’s proposal.', 'lead_paragraph': 'To the Editor:', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Is a Ban on Large Sodas the Answer to Obesity?', 'kicker': 'Letters', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Is a Ban on Large Sodas the Answer to Obesity?', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Obesity', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Soft Drinks', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Diabetes', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bloomberg, Michael R', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-17T22:06:24+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Letters', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Letter', '_id': 'nyt://article/520de88f-d00d-5b67-8ef6-d8afa1fb8c14', 'word_count': 509, 'uri': 'nyt://article/520de88f-d00d-5b67-8ef6-d8afa1fb8c14'}, {'abstract': 'The chairman of the National Bankruptcy Conference responds to an editorial.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/06/18/opinion/bankruptcy-lawyers-fees.html', 'snippet': 'The chairman of the National Bankruptcy Conference responds to an editorial.', 'lead_paragraph': 'To the Editor:', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Bankruptcy Lawyers’ Fees', 'kicker': 'Letter', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Bankruptcy Lawyers’ Fees', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Bankruptcies', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Prices (Fares, Fees and Rates)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Legal Profession', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-17T22:07:43+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Letters', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Letter', '_id': 'nyt://article/653d2615-b07b-5184-9814-567af5436765', 'word_count': 136, 'uri': 'nyt://article/653d2615-b07b-5184-9814-567af5436765'}, {'abstract': 'Kurt Rosenwinkel and his Standards Trio played the Jazz Standard on Saturday with the singer Johnny O’Neal, whose style balanced plush tone and jittery improvising.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/06/18/arts/music/kurt-rosenwinkel-trio-with-johnny-oneal-at-jazz-standard.html', 'snippet': 'Kurt Rosenwinkel and his Standards Trio played the Jazz Standard on Saturday with the singer Johnny O’Neal, whose style balanced plush tone and jittery improvising.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The guitarist Kurt Rosenwinkel played on Saturday night at the Jazz Standard with a consistent tone and sound, dry and slightly reverbed, chords harmonically colored or extended, or replaced altogether by quick and startling runs that rippled the surface. It was sturdy, organized, meticulous, but still basically inward; he’s evolved within the academy-oriented air of jazz-guitar playing over the last 20 years. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/18/arts/trio/trio-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 839, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/18/arts/trio/trio-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1678, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/18/arts/trio/trio-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/06/18/arts/trio/trio-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/18/arts/trio/trio-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Revisiting Jazz Repertory, With Speedy Scat', 'kicker': 'Music Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Revisiting Jazz Repertory, With Speedy Scat', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Standards Trio', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Jazz', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Rosenwinkel, Kurt', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': "O'Neal, Johnny", 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-17T22:08:16+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ben Ratliff', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ben', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ratliff', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/e922aca2-eec1-5b59-ab23-69ddff55afb6', 'word_count': 588, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e922aca2-eec1-5b59-ab23-69ddff55afb6'}, {'abstract': 'The third part of the Opera Moderne series the Red Scare focused on music reflecting World War II and its aftermath, presented in Deutsches Haus at New York University.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/06/18/arts/music/opera-moderne-completes-its-red-scare-series.html', 'snippet': 'The third part of the Opera Moderne series the Red Scare focused on music reflecting World War II and its aftermath, presented in Deutsches Haus at New York University.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The young company Opera Moderne wants to be the go-to source of programs for sophisticated listeners, featuring music a little off the beaten path. This season it has put on varied song programs, Wolf-Ferrari’s charming opera “Le Donne Curiose” and an acclaimed production of Britten’s “Turn of the Screw.”', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/18/arts/OPERA/OPERA-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 775, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/18/arts/OPERA/OPERA-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1551, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Blacklists and Shadows Looming Over Composers', 'kicker': 'Music Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Blacklists and Shadows Looming Over Composers', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Opera', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Opera Moderne', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Classical Music', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-17T22:13:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Zachary Woolfe', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Zachary', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Woolfe', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/d941913e-5f31-5932-bbe2-c49a5473a1e4', 'word_count': 434, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d941913e-5f31-5932-bbe2-c49a5473a1e4'}, {'abstract': 'Glass Farm Ensemble led off its program at Symphony Space with “Credo in Us,” a 1942 piece by John Cage.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/06/18/arts/music/glass-farm-ensemble-plays-john-cage-at-symphony-space.html', 'snippet': 'Glass Farm Ensemble led off its program at Symphony Space with “Credo in Us,” a 1942 piece by John Cage.', 'lead_paragraph': 'One reason “Credo in Us,” a 1942 composition by John Cage, never grows old is that, by design, it never fails to surprise. The piece, played at the start of a concert by the Glass Farm Ensemble in the Leonard Nimoy Thalia at Symphony Space on Friday evening, incorporates bursts of other composers’ music produced with a radio, a phonograph or both, amid a frolicsome tumult of lively rhythms played on piano, tin cans and muted gongs. An electric buzzer keeps the piece from getting too cozy.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/18/arts/jpfarm/jpfarm-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 737, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/18/arts/jpfarm/jpfarm-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1474, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Like a Sampling D.J., John Cage Weaves a Piece From the Works of Others', 'kicker': 'Music Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Like a Sampling D.J., John Cage Weaves a Piece From the Works of Others', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Glass Farm Ensemble', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cage, John', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-17T22:14:36+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Steve Smith', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Steve', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Smith', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/34d1083e-04e8-5681-9bfa-cc4d2e2f686c', 'word_count': 443, 'uri': 'nyt://article/34d1083e-04e8-5681-9bfa-cc4d2e2f686c'}, {'abstract': 'A specialist in fighting cyberfraud says small businesses need to stay a step ahead of the criminals.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/06/18/opinion/cyberfraud-and-businesses.html', 'snippet': 'A specialist in fighting cyberfraud says small businesses need to stay a step ahead of the criminals.', 'lead_paragraph': 'To the Editor:', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Cyberfraud and Businesses', 'kicker': 'Letter', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Cyberfraud and Businesses', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computer Security', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cyberattacks and Hackers', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Small Business', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-17T22:14:51+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Letters', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Letter', '_id': 'nyt://article/2fe50e91-940a-54cf-9816-99dd6e1c5f20', 'word_count': 259, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2fe50e91-940a-54cf-9816-99dd6e1c5f20'}, {'abstract': 'To raise money and awareness for African-American theater and playwrights, Project1Voice, a nonprofit organization, will stage a one-night-only benefit reading of James Baldwin’s 1954 play, “The Amen Corner,” in 18 cities and 28 theaters across the country.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/06/18/theater/a-baldwin-play-reading-will-benefit-theaters.html', 'snippet': 'To raise money and awareness for African-American theater and playwrights, Project1Voice, a nonprofit organization, will stage a one-night-only benefit reading of James Baldwin’s 1954 play, “The Amen Corner,” in 18 cities and 28 theaters across th...', 'lead_paragraph': 'To raise money and awareness for African-American theater and playwrights, Project1Voice, a nonprofit organization, will stage a one-night-only benefit reading of James Baldwin’s 1954 play, “The Amen Corner,” in 18 cities and 28 theaters across the country. Part of Project1Voice’s second annual event 1 Voice, 1 Play, 1 Day, the reading was chosen to commemorate the 25th anniversary of Baldwin’s death. Set in Harlem in the 1950s, “The Amen Corner,” like Baldwin’s first novel, “Go Tell It on the Mountain,” explores the role Christianity plays in the lives of African-Americans. The New York reading, which will be performed on Monday at 7 p.m at the Gerald W. Lynch Theater at John Jay College, is to be hosted by Norm Lewis (below, of “The Gershwins’ Porgy and Bess”) and directed by Walter Dallas with Lillias White, Chuck Cooper and Adriane Lenox starring. Speaking in a telephone interview, the Project1Voice founder Erich McMillan-McCall said that the goal of the annual reading was to help “preserve the legacy and tradition of black theaters for future generations,” adding, “By creating awareness and reigniting what I call lesser-known classics by African-American playwrights we can get people to realize that there is a voice that is missing, and that is the voice of many minority professionals.” Tickets are available through (631) 464-7421 or project1voice.org.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Baldwin Play Reading Will Benefit Theaters', 'kicker': 'Arts, Briefly', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Baldwin Play Reading Will Benefit Theaters', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Project1Voice', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Blacks', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Black People', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Baldwin, James', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-17T22:19:31+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Theater', 'byline': {'original': 'Compiled by Adam W. Kepler', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Adam', 'middlename': 'W.', 'lastname': 'Kepler', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/f400747b-561c-5989-9eaa-f76c141ed816', 'word_count': 221, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f400747b-561c-5989-9eaa-f76c141ed816'}, {'abstract': 'According to The New York Post, an administrator was accused of faking professors’ signatures to improve grades of students seeking executive master’s degrees.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/06/18/nyregion/baruch-college-finds-problems-in-business-program.html', 'snippet': 'According to The New York Post, an administrator was accused of faking professors’ signatures to improve grades of students seeking executive master’s degrees.', 'lead_paragraph': 'An internal investigation at the Zi