k Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'An Immigration Order in June, With an Eye to November', 'kicker': 'IHT Rendezvous', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-06-18T05:29:47+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'World', 'byline': {'original': 'By Luisita Lopez Torregrosa', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Luisita', 'middlename': 'Lopez', 'lastname': 'Torregrosa', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/cb835369-e5c2-5b15-b45d-3dce070989b4', 'word_count': 645, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cb835369-e5c2-5b15-b45d-3dce070989b4'}, {'abstract': 'Media attention to female feticide contrasts with the subject’s marginal treatment in mainstream Indian literature and cinema.', 'web_url': 'https://india.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/06/18/films-literature-devote-little-attention-to-female-feticide/', 'snippet': 'Media attention to female feticide contrasts with the subject’s marginal treatment in mainstream Indian literature and cinema.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The actor Aamir Khan opened a significant chapter in gender politics in the first episode of his show, “Satyamev Jayate” (Truth Triumphs), last month when he took on the topic of female feticide. Media coverage of the subject exploded afterward, highlighting India’s shocking record on female feticide — 10 million aborted over two decades, according to a 2006 study by the British medical journal The Lancet.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Films, Literature Devote Little Attention to Female Feticide', 'kicker': 'India Ink', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Women and Girls', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'na