stname': 'Dan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bilefsky', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d17d014f-3661-55a5-9fe2-7d6617ad28c7', 'word_count': 1237, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d17d014f-3661-55a5-9fe2-7d6617ad28c7'}, {'abstract': 'Electronic medical records, by uploading data automatically to state health departments, are enabling officials to identify and track outbreaks more quickly than ever.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/06/19/health/states-using-electronic-medical-records-to-track-epidemics.html', 'snippet': 'Electronic medical records, by uploading data automatically to state health departments, are enabling officials to identify and track outbreaks more quickly than ever.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Public health departments around the country have long scrutinized data from local hospitals for indications that diseases like influenza, tuberculosis, AIDS, syphilis and asthma might be on the rise, and to monitor the health consequences of heat waves, frigid weather or other natural phenomena. In the years since 9/11, this scrutiny has come to include signs of possible bioterrorism.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Fast Access to Records Helps Fight Epidemics', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Fast Access To Records Helps Fight Epidemics', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Electronic Health Records', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Medicine and Health', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Epidemics', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-18T20:06:38+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Science', 'section_name': 'Health', 'byline': {'original': 'By Milt Freudenheim', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Milt', 'middlename': N