rtunate enough to have work are struggling as well, which is why Sherry Woods, a 59-year-old van driver from Atlanta, found herself standing in line at a jobs fair this month, with her résumé tucked inside a Bible.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '11', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/19/us/JOBS-1/JOBS-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 330, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/06/19/us/JOBS-1/JOBS-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 330}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/19/us/JOBS-1/JOBS-1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 681, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/19/us/JOBS-1/JOBS-1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1362, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Lost in Recession, Toll on Underemployed and Underpaid', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Lost in Recession, Toll on Underemployed and Underpaid', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Labor and Jobs', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Economy', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Economic Conditions and Trends', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Recession and Depression', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Economic Policy Institute', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-19T01:49:25+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael Cooper', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cooper', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d6c22a7c-1f8c-5ed8-969a-a54a4a4e0d61', 'word_count': 1250, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d6c22a7c-1f8c-5ed8-969a-a54a4a4e0d61'}, {'abstract': 'The Supreme Court retreated from a 2009 decision that said crime lab reports cannot be used against criminal defendants unless the analysts responsible for them are subject to cross-examination.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/06/19/opinion/the-confrontation-clause-confused.html', 'snippet': 'The Supreme Court retreated from a 2009 decision that said crime lab reports cannot be used against criminal defendants unless the analysts responsible for them are subject to cross-examination.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Supreme Court ruled in 2009 that crime lab reports cannot be used against criminal defendants unless the analysts responsible for them are subject to cross-examination. On Monday, the court’s 5-to-4 majority sharply departed from that clear precedent, without overruling it, in a fractured decision that will sow confusion about how such reports can be used in criminal trials. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Confrontation Clause, Confused', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Confrontation Clause, Confused', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Supreme Court', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Forensic Science', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Decisions and Verdicts', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sixth Amendment (US Constitution)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'ALITO, SAMUEL A JR', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kagan, Elena', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Editorials', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Crime and Criminals', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-19T01:49:57+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/aee5aed1-9cf2-5b81-98af-6838ac9ac1b2', 'word_count': 360, 'uri': 'nyt://article/aee5aed1-9cf2-5b81-98af-6838ac9ac1b2'}, {'abstract': 'The calendar wants to apportion just so much summer and no more, but we’ll take whatever of the warm, sunny season we can find.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/06/19/opinion/well-take-summer.html', 'snippet': 'The calendar wants to apportion just so much summer and no more, but we’ll take whatever of the warm, sunny season we can find.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It’s easy to forget that we live in a world of temporal abstractions. The sun reaches its zenith — local noon — a little earlier in New York City than it does in Buffalo, but, according to the clocks, “noon” comes at exactly the same moment in both places. The world has been organized and synchronized for very good reasons, but it’s worth remembering that, only a couple of centuries ago, time was utterly local. Noon here — wherever you happened to be — was never the same as noon farther east or west. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'We’ll Take Summer', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'We’ll Take Summer', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Summer (Season)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Seasons and Months', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Editorials', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-19T01:52:23+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/3bce7bdf-b642-5c93-8fc2-0ad485c6bb72', 'word_count': 281, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3bce7bdf-b642-5c93-8fc2-0ad485c6bb72'}, {'abstract': 'From euro uncertainty to our own debt battles, we’re too used to hovering over near catastrophe.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/06/19/opinion/bruni-dont-look-down.html', 'snippet': 'From euro uncertainty to our own debt battles, we’re too used to hovering over near catastrophe.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Somewhere between Nik Wallenda’s first step onto a tightrope over Niagara Falls and Greece’s most recent retreat from the brink, it hit me: teetering needlessly on the precipice of disaster wasn’t just the story of the weekend. It’s the story of our days.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '23', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/03/opinion/frank-bruni/frank-bruni-articleLarge.png', 'height': 300, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/04/03/opinion/frank-bruni/frank-bruni-articleLarge.png', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 300}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/03/opinion/frank-bruni/frank-bruni-jumbo.png', 'height': 512, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/03/opinion/frank-bruni/frank-bruni-superJumbo.png', 'height': 1000, 'width': 2000, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/03/opinion/frank-bruni/frank-bruni-thumbStandard.png', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2018/04/03/opinion/frank-bruni/frank-bruni-thumbStandard.png', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/03/opinion/frank-bruni/frank-bruni-thumbLarge.png', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Don’t Look Down', 'kicker': 'Op-Ed Columnist', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Don’t Look Down', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Forest and Brush Fires', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Greece', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'European Sovereign Debt Crisis (2010- )', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Federal Budget (US)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Republican Party', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Colorado', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Wallenda, Nik', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-19T01:58:56+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Frank Bruni', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Frank', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bruni', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/bf83bd8a-0398-59be-9930-6c2665ada4c3', 'word_count': 837, 'uri': 'nyt://article/bf83bd8a-0398-59be-9930-6c2665ada4c3'}, {'abstract': 'An uncle and his niece find an emotional connection in the jungle, once she has poured out her heart to him, in Greg Pierce’s play “Slowgirl.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/06/19/theater/reviews/slowgirl-by-greg-pierce-at-the-claire-tow-theater.html', 'snippet': 'An uncle and his niece find an emotional connection in the jungle, once she has poured out her heart to him, in Greg Pierce’s play “Slowgirl.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'The scrabbling of iguanas across a metal roof, the squawk of a parrot and other colorful noises are resounding throughout the Claire Tow Theater, the handsome new auditorium built atop Lincoln Center Theater’s Broadway home, the Vivian Beaumont Theater. But the truly exotic note in the jungle symphony of Greg Pierce’s play “Slowgirl” is the adrenaline chatter of the 17-year-old American girl who finds herself in the alien environment of rural Costa Rica. Her anxious, endless nattering reverberates like a constant klaxon horn amid the silence inhabited by her uncle, who has retreated from the world to live in solitude with nature. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/19/arts/SLOW/SLOW-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 344, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/06/19/arts/SLOW/SLOW-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 344}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/19/arts/SLOW/SLOW-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 606, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/19/arts/SLOW/SLOW-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1213, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/19/arts/SLOW/SLOW-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': '