 over Mohamed Morsi, their newly elected leader whose accidental candidacy thrust him to the center of a tense national debate about citizenship, religion and politics.', 'lead_paragraph': 'CAIRO — The first celebrations for the new president were joyous but spare, a party overwhelmed by traffic and heat. In Tahrir Square, flags waved and boys set off fireworks that disappeared in the midday sun.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/19/world/CAIRO/CAIRO-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 350, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/06/19/world/CAIRO/CAIRO-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 350}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/19/world/CAIRO/CAIRO-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/19/world/CAIRO/CAIRO-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/19/world/CAIRO/CAIRO-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/06/19/world/CAIRO/CAIRO-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/19/world/CAIRO/CAIRO-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Uncertainties Underlie the Celebrations in Cairo', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Uncertainties Underlie the Celebrations in Cairo', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Muslim Brotherhood (Egypt)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Middle East and North Africa Unrest (2010- )', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Egypt', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Morsi, Mohamed', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Muslims and Islam', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Shafik, Ahmed', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Elections', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Tahrir Square (Cairo)', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-19T02:00:41+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Middle East', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kareem Fahim and Dina Salah Amer', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kareem', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Fahim', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Dina', 'middlename': 'Salah', 'lastname': 'Amer', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/403fc144-9168-5568-8e28-d467f9c774bc', 'word_count': 887, 'uri': 'nyt://article/403fc144-9168-5568-8e28-d467f9c774bc'}, {'abstract': 'Oh oh, Steven Riley is back.', 'web_url': 'https://wordplay.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/06/18/allo-allo/', 'snippet': 'Oh oh, Steven Riley is back.', 'lead_paragraph': 'TUESDAY’S PUZZLE — If the Monday through Saturday New York Times crossword puzzles ran with a title, today’s would have to be something along the lines of “‘Allo, ‘Allo!” because, and I’m sure you’ve sussed this out already, the grid contains all O’s. Sixty nine of them. That’s the only vowel in the whole puzzle. O is a very common letter, but it’s still quite a feat to pull this off. Steven Riley has done it, and with the exception of some less than stellar areas, he’s done it well.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '‘Allo, ‘Allo!', 'kicker': 'Wordplay', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Crossword Puzzles', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-19T02:00:58+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Crosswords & Games', 'byline': {'original': 'By Deb Amlen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Deb', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Amlen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/fb8bab47-dd3e-5c74-95d2-3e6d9757f9b8', 'word_count': 423, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fb8bab47-dd3e-5c74-95d2-3e6d9757f9b8'}, {'abstract': 'Corrections appearing in print for June 19, 2012.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/06/19/pageoneplus/corrections-june-19.html', 'snippet': 'Corrections appearing in print for June 19, 2012.', 'lead_paragraph': 'INTERNATIONAL', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Corrections: June 19', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Corrections', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-06-19T02:01:21+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Summary', 'section_name': 'Corrections', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Correction', '_id': 'nyt://article/d7cc8e8f-cf10-567a-af0a-d66e9fdd177a', 'word_count': 568, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d7cc8e8f-cf10-567a-af0a-d66e9fdd177a'}, {'abstract': 'With a police force thought too stressed by a rise in crime to enforce headgear rules for bicyclists, a struggling city opte