ole': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Twentieth', 'middlename': 'Century', 'lastname': 'Fox', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Video', '_id': 'nyt://video/690c9c33-4371-5b04-851e-5ee4ab5bdf22', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://video/690c9c33-4371-5b04-851e-5ee4ab5bdf22'}, {'abstract': 'Mark Leong’s cinematic photos reveal an uneasy relationship between old and new, order and chaos in Hong Kong, 15 years after its handover from Britain.', 'web_url': 'https://lens.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/06/19/in-post-colonial-hong-kong-finding-whimsy-and-menace/', 'snippet': 'Mark Leong’s cinematic photos reveal an uneasy relationship between old and new, order and chaos in Hong Kong, 15 years after its handover from Britain.', 'lead_paragraph': 'HONG KONG – During the months he spent trekking through this city for National Geographic magazine, Mark Leong was on a hunt for glimpses of its underground. Years of Hong Kong cinema had colored his view of the place, rendering it a seedy scene beneath a vibrant subtropical metropolis.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'In Post-Colonial Hong Kong, Finding Whimsy and Menace', 'kicker': 'Lens', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Photography', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Leong, Mark', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Paterniti, Michael', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'To, Johnnie', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Wong Kar-Wai', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Geographic', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'China', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Hong Kong', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-19T09:00:34+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Multimedia/Photos', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kerri MacDonald', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kerri', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'MacDonald', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/54ea68b3-1ee4-57f2-8fd3-6d900800c1e9', 'word_count': 702, 'uri': 'nyt://article/54ea68b3-1ee4-57f2-8fd3-6d900800c1e9'}, {'abstract': 'After “You Can Count on Me,” Lonergan made what may be the best film of the decade. So why did you never get to see it?', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/06/24/magazine/kenneth-lonergans-thwarted-masterpiece.html', 'snippet': 'After “You Can Count on Me,” Lonergan made what may be the best film of the decade. So why did you never get to see it?', 'lead_paragraph': 'Think back on the last time you saw Kenneth Lonergan’s 2000 film, “You Can Count On Me.” Do you remember how good it was? The intellectual and emotional complexity of the script? Those remarkable performances by Mark Ruffalo and Laura Linney? That scene — to choose one among many — between Terry (Ruffalo) and his 8-year-old nephew, Rudy (Rory Culkin), in which the drunk Terry sits next to Rudy’s bed late at night, smoking a cigarette and telling Rudy why his dad is such a jerk and why the town he lives in sucks so much? (“Fortunately for you, though, your mom is like, the greatest. So you had some bad luck, and you had some good luck.”) It was a modest story of a brother and sister whose parents die when they’re kids and whose lives are blown in different directions and who, years later, come to some almost-peace about what they can and can’t be for each other. But there was such intense realness about it, the way people really talk, the way lives are actually lived, that was unlike anything else on screen, radical almost, in its attention to the genuine messiness of human lives.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '20', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/24/magazine/24lonergan1/24lonergan1-articleLarge-v3.jpg', 'height': 454, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/06/24/magazine/24lonergan1/24lonergan1-articleLarge-v3.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 454}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/24/magazine/24lonergan1/24lonergan1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 683, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/24/magazine/24lonergan1/24lonergan1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1500, 'width': 1000, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/24/magazine/24lonergan1/24lonergan1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/06/24/magazine/24lonergan1/24lonergan1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/24/magazine/24lonergan1/24lonergan1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Kenneth Lonergan’s Thwarted Masterpiece', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'How to Thwart a Masterpiece', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Margaret (Movie)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lonergan, Kenneth', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 