est happenings in Washington.', 'web_url': 'https://thecaucus.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/06/19/the-early-word-middle-ground/', 'snippet': 'Political news from today’s Times and around the Web, plus a look at the latest happenings in Washington.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Today’s Times', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Early Word: Middle Ground', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-06-19T10:00:10+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jada F. Smith', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jada', 'middlename': 'F.', 'lastname': 'Smith', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/8a6893b3-3207-5a3b-9913-8d6468baf53a', 'word_count': 310, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8a6893b3-3207-5a3b-9913-8d6468baf53a'}, {'abstract': 'Turnover in the different income brackets — some wealthy slide down, some lower-income people climb — helps mitigate the impact of income inequality, an economist writes.', 'web_url': 'https://economix.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/06/19/as-income-inequality-grows-some-movement-at-the-top-and-bottom/', 'snippet': 'Turnover in the different income brackets — some wealthy slide down, some lower-income people climb — helps mitigate the impact of income inequality, an economist writes.', 'lead_paragraph': '7:28 p.m. | Updated to correct figures.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'As Income Inequality Grows, Some Movement at the Top and Bottom', 'kicker': 'Economix', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Income Inequality', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Tax Foundation', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-19T10:00:12+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Economy', 'byline': {'original': 'By Bruce Bartlett', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Bruce', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bartlett', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ea02ee17-7930-502d-92ca-3a10c4b07323', 'word_count': 883, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ea02ee17-7930-502d-92ca-3a10c4b07323'}, {'abstract': 'The current season offers numerous arguments against spending any of your early preseason picks on catchers.', 'web_url': 'https://bats.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/06/19/fantasy-focus-doldrums-behind-the-plate/', 'snippet': 'The current season offers numerous arguments against spending any of your early preseason picks on catchers.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The value of the catcher position varies depending on format, as it increases if your league uses two spots in the starting lineup. However, even in two-catcher formats, few catchers are drafted highly for two reasons. First, the position’s taxing physical demand limits their plate appearances, reducing their upside simply through lack of volume. Second, during the past 10 seasons, just eight catchers have finished with more than 10 steals, making it a position that inherently helps you in four categories at best.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Fantasy Focus: Doldrums Behind the Plate', 'kicker': 'Bats', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Baseball', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'McCann, Brian', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Napoli, Mike', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Santana, Carlos', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-19T10:00:22+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Baseball', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dalton Del Don', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dalton', 'middlename': 'Del', 'lastname': 'Don', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/62a7219b-fc74-573d-86e7-815aa09c60b1', 'word_count': 649, 'uri': 'nyt://article/62a7219b-fc74-573d-86e7-815aa09c60b1'}, {'abstract': 'A green blob appeared on the screen of the MacBook. What was it? Could we get rid of it before it ate Cleveland?', 'web_url': 'https://pogue.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/06/19/the-case-of-the-screens-green-blob/', 'snippet': 'A green blob appeared on the screen of the MacBook. What was it? Could we get rid of it before it ate Cleveland?', 'lead_paragraph': 'You think you’re good at technology? O.K., let’s see you troubleshoot this one.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Case of the Screen’s Green Blob', 'kicker': 'Pogue&#039;s Posts', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computers and the Internet', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Apple Incorporated', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-19T10:43:35+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'byline': {'original': 'By David Pogue', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Pogue', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/da0b56b7-ddf5-5431-b15d-899d6bfc9d47', 'word_count': 300, 'uri': 'nyt://article/da0b56b7-ddf5-5431-b15d-899d6bfc9d47'}, {'abstract': "Adrian Allannic, a third grader, has learned the lessons of healthy eating at home, but at his school, Public School 11 William T. Harris, he gets to live his healthy habits all day. A growing number of more affluent parents and a motivated principal have brought a new emphasis on fresh foods to the school of almost 700 students in Chelsea, Manhattan. Many schools in New York City have taken steps to provide healthier foods for students in their cafeterias, but P.S. 11 has gone further than most. Elbert Chu's multimedia report shows another way in which New York City's parents are Paying for Public School.", 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/schoolbook/2012/06/19/with-parents-support-a-chelsea-school-goes-healthy/', 'snippet': 'Adrian Allannic, a third grader, has learned the lessons of healthy eating at home, but at his school, Public School 11 William T. Harris, he gets to live his healthy habits all day. A growing number of more affluent parents and a motivated princi...', 'lead_paragraph': '', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': "With Parents' Support, a Chelsea School Goes Healthy", 'kicker': 'SchoolBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-06-19T11:00:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Education', 'byline': {'original': 'By ELBERT CHU', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Elbert', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'CHU', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/fa300141-45f2-5326-97ee-8b5d56667380', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fa300141-45f2-5326-97ee-8b5d56667380'}, {'abstract': 'Microsoft unveiled its new tablet, the Surface. There are many unanswered questions about it and some tough sledding ahead, but for the moment, we can celebrate the innovation.', 'web_url': 'https://pogue.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/06/19/the-surface-a-new-tablet-from-microsoft/', 'snippet': 'Microsoft unveiled its new tablet, the Surface. There are many unanswered questions about it and some tough sledding ahead, but for the moment, we can celebrate the innovation.', 'lead_paragraph': 'On Monday at a Los Angeles media event that had been veiled in secrecy, Microsoft announced that it was going to make a gorgeous touchscreen tablet like the iPad. It’s called the Surface tablet. Its main differentiators from the iPad: It has a kickstand, it has real PC ports and it will run Windows 8.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Surface: Celebrate the Competition, Question the Premise', 'kicker': 'Pogue&#039;s Posts', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-06-19T11:00:15+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'byline': {'original': 'By David Pogue', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Pogue', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/fe437d18-e8da-562f-aa8a-39192b4676cb', 'word_count': 494, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fe437d18-e8da-562f-aa8a-39192b4676cb'}, {'abstract': 'It offers many good programs for small businesses, but the S.B.A. is widely misunderstood.', 'web_url': 'https://boss.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/06/19/the-three-biggest-myths-about-the-s-b-a/', 'snippet': 'It offers many good programs for small businesses, but the S.B.A. is widely misunderstood.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I want to share with you what I consider to be the three biggest myths about the Small Business Administration. But let me be clear: I am a big supporter of the S.B.A. My company, MultiFunding, brokers loans, and we put about 25 percent of our clients into S.B.A. loans. I think it’s a good program with a lot of economic benefits. It’s also terribly misunderstood by many of the small-business owners we talk to.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Three Biggest Myths About the S.B.A.', 'kicker': 'You&#039;re the Boss', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Banking and Financial Institutions', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Regulation and Deregulation of Industry', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Small Business', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Mills, Karen', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Small Business Administration', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-19T11:00:43+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Entrepreneurship', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ami Kassar', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ami', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kassar', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4ac9a7f2-6e35-5dc5-bccd-da0668fb377d', 'word_count': 723, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4ac9a7f2-6e35-5dc5-bccd-da0668fb377d'}, {'abstract': 'The Consumer Financial Protection Bureau says it has been able to resolve four out of every five complaints it received in its first 10 months.', 'web_url': 'https://bucks.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/06/19/consumer-bureau-says-it-resolved-4-of-every-5-complaints/', 'snippet': 'The Consumer Financial Protection Bureau says it has been able to resolve four out of every five complaints it received in its first 10 months.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In its first 10 months on the job, the Consumer Financial Protection Bureau has been able to resolve roughly four out of every five consumer complaints to the apparent satisfaction of the consumer involved, according to statistics released by the bureau on Tuesday.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'hea