See what you know about the news below. To prepare, you might scan the articles or summaries on today’s paper. Good luck!', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'News Quiz | June 20, 2012', 'kicker': 'The Learning Network', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-06-20T05:30:29+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Education', 'byline': {'original': 'By Anastasia Economides', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Anastasia', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Economides', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/aee94248-14a3-5a27-ad2b-2ebe012891a9', 'word_count': 22, 'uri': 'nyt://article/aee94248-14a3-5a27-ad2b-2ebe012891a9'}, {'abstract': 'Scores of of songs in the Bhojpuri language, commonly used in north India, celebrate the mobile phone both as a fashion statement and a handy tool for romance.', 'web_url': 'https://india.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/06/20/colloquial-music-spawns-a-culture-of-romance/', 'snippet': 'Scores of of songs in the Bhojpuri language, commonly used in north India, celebrate the mobile phone both as a fashion statement and a handy tool for romance.', 'lead_paragraph': 'As the nation’s 919 million mobile connections grow by nearly 1 percent a month, scores of songs in the Bhojpuri language, commonly used in north India, have come to celebrate the mobile phone both as a fashion statement and a handy tool for romance. Their lyrics – and the cheaply shot videos that illustrate them, like the one above – demonstrate how the tiny digital device is cutting through the thicket of rural conventions that can make liaisons difficult and often dangerous in their rigidly conservative societies. (The lyrics of this song roughly translate to: “The fancy madam has got a mobile and started going on dates.”)', 'sou