aragraph': 'TOP STORIES ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Morning Take-Out', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-06-20T11:54:16+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By William Alden', 'person': [{'firstname': 'William', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Alden', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/fae86d97-9936-5357-abdf-cc247bf2a7bd', 'word_count': 2289, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fae86d97-9936-5357-abdf-cc247bf2a7bd'}, {'abstract': 'An Austrian who spent his youth agitating about the environment reflects on what’s missing at the big sustainability meeting in Rio.', 'web_url': 'https://dotearth.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/06/20/dispatches-from-rio-and-beyond-young-people-reflect-on-whats-missing/', 'snippet': 'An Austrian who spent his youth agitating about the environment reflects on what’s missing at the big sustainability meeting in Rio.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Here’s two fresh dispatches from young people reflecting on Rio+20, the United Nations Conference on Sustainable Development, which is approaching its conclusion in Rio de Janeiro in the next few days. You can best track developments via the #rioplus20 tag on Twitter. Here’s the final draft of “The Future We Want,” the conference declaration heading to heads of state for signature and upsetting environmental campaigners. The fight over the formal proceedings, which were bound to be underwhelming, distracts from some significant developments that I’ll be covering in subsequent posts.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Dispatches From Rio (and Beyond): Young People Reflect on What’s Missing', 'kicker': 'Dot Earth', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Environment', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sustainable Living', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'United Nations', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Rio de Janeiro (Brazil)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-20T11:55:33+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andrew C. Revkin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andrew', 'middlename': 'C.', 'lastname': 'Revkin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c7227b8a-389a-5ab9-98c1-eb7e3d42827a', 'word_count': 1073, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c7227b8a-389a-5ab9-98c1-eb7e3d42827a'}, {'abstract': 'Julian Assange, the Wikileaks founder, is waiting for a decision on his request for asylum in Ecuador, a country whose president is accused of muzzling free speech and hounding the press.', 'web_url': 'https://rendezvous.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/06/20/asylum-for-assange-whats-in-it-for-ecuador/', 'snippet': 'Julian Assange, the Wikileaks founder, is waiting for a decision on his request for asylum in Ecuador, a country whose president is accused of muzzling free speech and hounding the press.', 'lead_paragraph': 'LONDON — A decision by Ecuador to grant political asylum to Julian Assange, the Wikileaks founder facing extradition to Sweden, was almost a done deal, one Quito newspaper said on Wednesday. “It is a matter of hours,” El Comercio said. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/20/blogs/rdv-assange/rdv-assange-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 630, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/20/blogs/rdv-assange/rdv-assange-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1260, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Asylum for Assange: What’s in It for Ecuador?', 'kicker': 'IHT Rendezvous', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-06-20T11:57:54+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'World', 'byline': {'original': 'By Harvey Morris', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Harvey', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Morris', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ea40d9fa-4437-5a03-831b-31540900e026', 'word_count': 541, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ea40d9fa-4437-5a03-831b-31540900e026'}, {'abstract': 'A television critic monitors “Today” for a month to test the theory that the end of its 16-year run atop the morning-show ratings was the fault of its co-host Ann Curry.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/06/24/arts/television/ann-curry-and-the-ratings-for-today.html', 'snippet': 'A television critic monitors “Today” for a month to test the theory that the end of its 16-year run atop the morning-show ratings was the fault of its co-host Ann Curry.', 'lead_paragraph': 'IT’S a windy May morning on the Rockefeller Center plaza where the stars of NBC’s “Today” periodically gather to perform in the open air, and for some reason Kathie Lee Gifford and Hoda Kotb are wearing hospital gowns, like a latter-day Lucy and Ethel embarked on some crazy scheme. Ms. Gifford’s explanation — it has something to do with a segment on plastic surgery — requires dramatic gestures, and with one of them she knocks a batch of notecards out of the hands of the show’s co-host Ann Curry.', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multime