rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Gaza Strip', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Hamas', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Terrorism', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-20T12:47:37+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Middle East', 'byline': {'original': 'By Isabel Kershner', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Isabel', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kershner', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/aa9d4ba7-27aa-5bee-a102-6b9bddebc2e6', 'word_count': 769, 'uri': 'nyt://article/aa9d4ba7-27aa-5bee-a102-6b9bddebc2e6'}, {'abstract': 'Before you jettison any expression of emotion, consider shelling out some money for eye primers; they’re well worth the price.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/06/20/samurai-shopper-prize-for-the-eyes/', 'snippet': 'Before you jettison any expression of emotion, consider shelling out some money for eye primers; they’re well worth the price.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Samurai Shopper is particularly delighted this week with eye primers. What a difference they make in the general scheme of looking good. Eye primers are not pudding-y and slick like eye creams, nor are they powdery, dry, greasy or thick like concealers: they’re the intermediate step between those two. Some are tinted to match your skin color, some have a nacreous luster, one or two terrible ones are pasty and glittery. The better primers disappear but leave a film that acts like a two-sided piece of tape: the primer adheres to eyelids while keeping eye makeup on top from huddling in a corner or forming a wobbly line on your eyelid crease. A good eye primer can replace concealers entirely over the lid, evening out skin tone. The very, very good ones can even be swept under the eye, where the bags might be.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Samurai Shopper | Prize for the Eyes', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Shopping and Retail', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Skin', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-20T13:00:53+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By S.S. Fair', 'person': [{'firstname': 'S.', 'middlename': 'S.', 'lastname': 'Fair', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9b9e9967-bc96-53e4-a832-d9fa860e0da7', 'word_count': 683, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9b9e9967-bc96-53e4-a832-d9fa860e0da7'}, {'abstract': 'Recent attacks against Israel from Sinai — a byproduct of political change in Egypt — are a reminder to be wary of peace deals and democracy in the Middle East.', 'web_url': 'https://latitude.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/06/20/trouble-in-sinai/', 'snippet': 'Recent attacks against Israel from Sinai — a byproduct of political change in Egypt — are a reminder to be wary of peace deals and democracy in the Middle East.', 'lead_paragraph': 'JERUSALEM — It was in ancient Kadesh Barnea that after the Jews’ exodus from Egypt spies sent out by Moses reported back that the land of Canaan would not easily be conquered. And it was from near modern-day Kadesh Barnea, a small village by Israel’s southern border with Egypt, that on Monday another event proved the enduring difficulty of ruling this land.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Trouble in Sinai', 'kicker': 'Latitude', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Defense and Military Forces', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'International Relations', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Middle East and North Africa Unrest (2010- )', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Muslim Brotherhood (Egypt)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Egypt', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Israel', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Sinai Peninsula (Egypt)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-20T13:29:26+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Shmuel Rosner', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Shmuel', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rosner', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/05a1746b-595c-5d26-ba9e-64dc528de2e8', 'word_count': 589, 'uri': 'nyt://article/05a1746b-595c-5d26-ba9e-64dc528de2e8'}, {'abstract': 'Traders responded tepidly to the Federal Reserve’s announcement to extend a program intended to drive down long-term interest rates.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/06/21/business/daily-stock-market-activity.html', 'snippet': 'Traders responded tepidly to the Federal Reserve’s announcement to extend a program intended to drive down long-term interest rates.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Wall Street gave a tepid response on Wednesday to the Federal Reserve’s decision to extend a program intended to drive down long-term interest rates, as analysts questioned just how far the measure would go in bolstering economic growth.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Wall Street Dips Slightly on Fed Decision', 'kicker': 'Stocks and Bonds', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Wall Street Dips Slightly on Fed Decision', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Stocks and Bonds', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Economic Conditions and Trends', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-20T13:46:22+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Christine Hauser', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Christine', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hauser', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/df352834-549d-58c2-b8b5-f0fb738eff60', 'word_count': 686, 'uri': 'nyt://article/df352834-549d-58c2-b8b5-f0fb738eff60'}, {'abstract': 'Despite corroborating medical evidence, Bangalore police took five days to arrest the diplomat charged with raping his 3-year-old daughter.', 'web_url': 'https://india.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/06/20/french-diplomat-rape-case/', 'snippet': 'Despite corroborating medical evidence, Bangalore police took five days to arrest the diplomat charged with raping his 3-year-old daughter.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The boundaries of diplomatic immunity were brought into sharp relief this past week with news that a French diplomat had been accused by his wife of raping their 3-year-old daughter. Despite the wife’s testimony and some corroborating medical evidence, Bangalore police took five days to arrest the diplomat because of worries over his diplomatic status.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Confusion Over Diplomatic Immunity Delays Arrest in Rape Case', 'kicker': 'India Ink', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Domestic Violence', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sex Crimes', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Traffic and Parking Violations', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Police Department (NYC)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Bangalore (India)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'France', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Karnataka (India)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-20T13:51:12+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Hari Kumar', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Hari', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kumar', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3c031b19-6a99-5ae0-be2c-18f3b11d4df8', 'word_count': 528, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3c031b19-6a99-5ae0-be2c-18f3b11d4df8'}, {'abstract': 'Presenting 17 case studies, researchers write that some investments in biofuels, ecotourism and other green initiatives amount to land grabs that displace the poor.', 'web_url': 'https://green.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/06/20/q-and-a-the-dark-side-to-green-transactions/', 'snippet': 'Presenting 17 case studies, researchers write that some investments in biofuels, ecotourism and other green initiatives amount to land grabs that displace the poor.', 'lead_paragraph': 'At Rio+20, the global conference on sustainable development that got under way Wednesday morning in Brazil, discussions abound on advancing environmental goals in a way that will benefit local and national economies. But development experts say there is a dark side to some ostensibly “green” market initiatives: the appropriation of resources for biofuels production, carbon offsets, ecotourism and so on can have devastating consequences for local people. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Q.&A.: The Underside of ‘Green’ Transactions', 'kicker': 'Green', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Agriculture and Farming', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Biofuels', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Forests and Forestry', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Greenhouse Gas Emissions', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Land Use Policies', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Pearce, David', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Brazil', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Great Britain', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Guatemala', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Tanzania', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-20T14:00:13+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Science', 'subsection_name': 'Environment', 'byline': {'original': 'By Joanna M. Foster', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Joanna', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/59f7d34a-02bd-5fdd-bc62-6191fa68a21f', 'word_count': 1297, 'uri': 'nyt://article/59f7d34a-02bd-5fdd-bc62-6191fa68a21f'}, {'abstract': 'Chinese officials denied that the newly issued white paper had been issued as part of its defense against charges filed by other nations at the World Trade Organization.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/06/21/business/global/china-vows-tighter-controls-over-rare-earth-mining.html', 'snippet': 'Chinese officials denied that the newly issued white paper had been issued as part of its defense against charges filed by other nations at the World Trade Organization.', 'lead_paragraph': 'HONG KONG — China’s cabinet issued on Wednesday its first white paper on rare earth industry policies, acknowledging that poorly regulated mining of rare earth metals had caused widespread environmental damage and promising an extensive cleanup and a crackdown on illegal mines.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'China, Citing Errors, Vows to Overhaul Rare Earth Industry', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'China, Citing Errors, Vows to Overhaul Rare Earth Industry', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'China', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Rare Earths', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mines and Mining', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'World Trade Organization', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Environment', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-20T14:06:20+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Global Business', 'byline': {'original': 'By Keith Bradsher', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Keith', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bradsher', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/45a8f090-8e62-5bcc-a663-37f7c8611aa1', 'word_count': 517, 'uri': 'nyt://article/45a8f090-8e62-5bcc-a663-37f7c8611aa1'}, {'abstract': 'First published in 1978, “Life in Hell” played a crucial role in the creation of “The Simpsons” while it acquainted readers with Matt Groening’s sardonic perspectives on childhood, dating, family and rock-music criticism.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/06/20/matt-groening-ends-his-life-in-hell-the-comic-strip-that-is/', 'snippet': 'First published in 1978, “Life in Hell” played a crucial role in the creation of “The Simpsons” while it acquainted readers with Matt Groening’s sardonic perspectives on childhood, dating, family and rock-music criticism.', 'lead_paragraph': 'While his animated comedy “The Simpsons” has been on the air a mere 22 seasons and counting, series creator Matt Groening has spent those years – and then some – drawing his syndicated comic strip “Life in Hell.” But soon that streak and the comic itself, which ran more than 30 years and played a crucial role in the creation of “The Simpsons,” will be no more: Mr. Groening has said he is ending the strip.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Matt Groening Ends His ‘Life in Hell’ (The Comic Strip, That Is)', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Matt Groening Ends His ‘Life in Hell’', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cartoons and Cartoonists', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Comic Books and Strips', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Brooks, James L', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Groening, Matt', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ullman, Tracey', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-20T14:11:46+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dave Itzkoff', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dave', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Itzkoff', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/15c35c80-1e87-570b-bbb7-b509ecf6b7fa', 'word_count': 255, 'uri': 'nyt://article/15c35c80-1e87-570b-bbb7-b509ecf6b7fa'}, {'abstract': 'Our lessons are on summer vacation, but here are links to every science, health, technology and math lesson we published this school year.', 'web_url': 'https://learning.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/06/20/year-end-roundup-science-health-technology-and-math-2/', 'snippet': 'Our lessons are on summer vacation, but here are links to every science, health, technology and math lesson we published this school year.', 'lead_paragraph': 'We used this video and the related article in one of our science lessons this year. Go to related lesson »', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Year-End Roundup | Science, Health, Technology and Math', 'kicker': 'The Learning Network', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-06-20T14:14:55+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Education', 'byline': {'original': 'By Daniel E. Slotnik', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Daniel', 'middlename': 'E.', 'lastname': 'Slotnik', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4316c71f-566c-5020-9a23-b8154c30de6e', 'word_count': 397, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4316c71f-566c-5020-9a23-b8154c30de6e'}, {'abstract': 'A collection of links from the reporters and editors of the Dining section.', 'web_url': 'https://dinersjournal.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/06/20/what-were-reading-461/', 'snippet': 'A collection of links from the reporters and editors of the Dining section.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Gray Report: Noilly Prat is bringing back the dry vermouth it used to market just in the United States but discontinued in 2009. The lighter, simpler vermouth, which was sold from 1979 to 2009, was replaced by the straw-colored, spicier Noilly Prat vermouth that is the norm in the rest of the world. But American sales dipped, so the company reversed itself, and will sell the product under a different name: Noilly Prat Extra Dry. — Robert Simonson', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'What We’re Reading', 'kicker': 'Diner&#039;s Journal', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-06-20T14:17:29+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f7f99698-4152-5abe-94b8-1e783691b069', 'word_count': 562, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f7f99698-4152-5abe-94b8-1e783691b069'}, {'abstract': 'Egyptian bloggers mocked a flurry of conflicting reports about the health of former President Hosni Mubarak, who was first pronounced, “clinically dead” by a state news agency and then said to be “improving” by government officials.', 'web_url': 'https://thelede.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/06/20/reports-of-mubaraks-death-clinically-exaggerated-egyptian-bloggers-say/', 'snippet': 'Egyptian bloggers mocked a flurry of conflicting reports about the health of former President Hosni Mubarak, who was first pronounced, “clinically dead” by a state news agency and then said to be “improving” by government officials.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Last Updated, 12:13 p.m. CAIRO — The exact condition of former President Hosni Mubarak’s health remains a mystery, as government officials variously described him to the media as comatose, on life support, and much improved, just hours after Egypt’s state news agency pronounced him “clinically dead,” in a report on his late-night transfer from prison to a hospital in an upscale district of the capital. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Reports of Mubarak’s Death Clinically Exaggerated, Egyptian Bloggers Say', 'kicker': 'The Lede', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hospitals', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Middle East and North Africa Unrest (2010- )', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Prisons and Prisoners', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Mubarak, Hosni', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Cairo (Egypt)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Egypt', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-20T14:20:46+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Blogs', 'byline': {'original': 'By Robert Mackey', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Robert', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Mackey', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/cd1955c7-72f6-5237-b641-b9991fb9e001', 'word_count': 599, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cd1955c7-72f6-5237-b641-b9991fb9e001'}, {'abstract': 'So far this year, the total value of mergers and acquisitions on the Continent by foreign companies has reached $101 billion, well ahead of the combined $73 billion spent in the United States by international suitors.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2012/06/20/amid-debt-crisis-overseas-buyers-seek-european-companies/', 'snippet': 'So far this year, the total value of mergers and acquisitions on the Continent by foreign companies has reached $101 billion, well ahead of the combined $73 billion spent in the United States by international suitors.', 'lead_paragraph': 'LONDON – Europe’s capital markets are in the doldrums. Uncertainty caused by the Continent’s financial crisis has led to a big fall in initial public offerings, while investors remain wary of buying sovereign and company debt.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Amid Debt Crisis, Overseas Buyers Seek European Companies', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mergers, Acquisitions and Divestitures', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Europe', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-20T14:26:49+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Mark Scott', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mark', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Scott', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0c59c97f-3e93-55a0-a1c9-d73755c5b3dd', 'word_count': 497, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0c59c97f-3e93-55a0-a1c9-d73755c5b3dd'}, {'abstract': 'Looking to take an “authentic” or “sustainable” trip? Here are red flags to watch for, and low-impact options to consider.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/06/24/travel/up-close-but-doing-no-harm-while-traveling.html', 'snippet': 'Looking to take an “authentic” or “sustainable” trip? Here are red flags to watch for, and low-impact options to consider.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WHITE-WATER rafting in Costa Rica. Riding an elephant in Thailand. Communing with indigenous people in Ecuador. Tour operators are marketing activities like these to travelers in search of “authentic” experiences, often using buzzwords like “eco-friendly” or “sustainable.” But how do you know which tour operators are truly responsible or simply exploitive?', 'print_section': 'TR', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/24/travel/24SUBPRAC/SUB24PRAC-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 380, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/06/24/travel/24SUBPRAC/SUB24PRAC-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 380}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/24/travel/24SUBPRAC/SUB24PRAC-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 822, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/24/travel/24S