stname': 'Dan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bilefsky', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/96a9735a-8f2a-5988-a754-2b3efa1026d9', 'word_count': 399, 'uri': 'nyt://article/96a9735a-8f2a-5988-a754-2b3efa1026d9'}, {'abstract': 'The Kenyan Riders, who one day hope to compete in the Tour de France, are a squad of competitive cyclists who have managed to introduce cycling to a country with virtually no history of it.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/06/21/sports/cycling/in-country-of-runners-kenyan-cycling-team-faces-uphill-climb.html', 'snippet': 'The Kenyan Riders, who one day hope to compete in the Tour de France, are a squad of competitive cyclists who have managed to introduce cycling to a country with virtually no history of it.', 'lead_paragraph': 'ITEN, Kenya — Five years ago, Sammy Ekiru was negotiating traffic on his bicycle taxi for roughly three dollars a day in Kitale, a remote Kenyan town 20 miles from the Ugandan border. Today, he and his bike remain inseparable. His labor and his livelihood, however, have taken a substantially different course. ', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/21/sports/JPyCYCLING/JPyCYCLING-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 350, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/06/21/sports/JPyCYCLING/JPyCYCLING-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 350}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/21/sports/JPyCYCLING/JPyCYCLING-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 669, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': '