ial Election of 2012', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': "O'Brien, Conan", 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Romney, Mitt', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Romney, Tagg', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-21T04:12:53+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ashley Parker', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ashley', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Parker', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/bbddcd94-8ed4-57fe-bfb1-4c383f7b5dfe', 'word_count': 269, 'uri': 'nyt://article/bbddcd94-8ed4-57fe-bfb1-4c383f7b5dfe'}, {'abstract': 'Police Chief Bill R. Lee Jr. of Sanford, who drew criticism for his department’s handling of Trayvon Martin’s killing, lost his job on Wednesday.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/06/21/us/florida-sanfords-embattled-police-chief-loses-his-job.html', 'snippet': 'Police Chief Bill R. Lee Jr. of Sanford, who drew criticism for his department’s handling of Trayvon Martin’s killing, lost his job on Wednesday.', 'lead_paragraph': ' Police Chief Bill R. Lee Jr. of Sanford, who drew criticism for his department’s handling of Trayvon Martin’s killing, lost his job on Wednesday. In a news release, City Manager Norton N. Bonaparte Jr. announced that Mr. Lee was immediately relieved of his duties. “We need to move forward with a police chief that all the citizens of Sanford can support,” Mr. Bonaparte said. In April, he announced that the chief had agreed to resign, but the City Commission rejected the resignation. Mr. Lee has been on administrative leave. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Florida: Sanford’s Embattled Police Chief Loses His Job', 'kicker': 'National Briefing | South', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Florida: Sanford’s Embattled Police Chief Loses His Job', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Martin, Trayvon', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Sanford (Fla)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lee, Bill Jr', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-21T04:16:30+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/565a22ee-8b01-5266-9887-8ee79bda9094', 'word_count': 91, 'uri': 'nyt://article/565a22ee-8b01-5266-9887-8ee79bda9094'}, {'abstract': 'Lanai, the smallest publicly accessible inhabited island in Hawaii, has been sold, the governor’s office announced Wednesday.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/06/21/us/hawaii-island-of-lanai-sold-to-chief-executive-of-oracle.html', 'snippet': 'Lanai, the smallest publicly accessible inhabited island in Hawaii, has been sold, the governor’s office announced Wednesday.', 'lead_paragraph': ' Lanai, the smallest publicly accessible inhabited island in Hawaii, has been sold, the governor’s office announced Wednesday. Castle & Cooke, the company that owned 98 percent of the 141-square-mile island, agreed to sell that land to Lawrence J. Ellison, chief executive of Oracle, the software giant. The price was not known, and neither Castle & Cooke nor Oracle responded to a request for comment. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Hawaii: Island of Lanai Sold to Chief Executive of Oracle', 'kicker': 'National Briefing | West', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Hawaii: Island of Lanai Sold To Chief Executive of Oracle', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Lanai (Hawaii)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Oracle Corporation', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ellison, Lawrence J', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-21T04:22:11+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ian Lovett', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ian', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Lovett', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/1b4ce29e-bb3a-5db0-9d38-9b99b3bcfb7d', 'word_count': 69, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1b4ce29e-bb3a-5db0-9d38-9b99b3bcfb7d'}, {'abstract': 'At least 11 animals at the Lake Superior Zoo in Duluth drowned when severe storms caused major flooding in northeastern Minnesota on Wednesday.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/06/21/us/minnesota-zoo-animals-drown-in-flooding-in-duluth.html', 'snippet': 'At least 11 animals at the Lake Superior Zoo in Duluth drowned when severe storms caused major flooding in northeastern Minnesota on Wednesday.', 'lead_paragraph': 'At least 11 animals at the Lake Superior Zoo in Duluth drowned when severe storms caused major flooding in northeastern Minnesota on Wednesday. Much of the zoo was under water, and firefighters and police officers were dispatched to help round up a polar bear and two seals that escaped from their exhibits. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Minnesota: Zoo Animals Drown in Flooding in Duluth', 'kicker': 'National Briefing | Midwest', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Minnesota: Zoo Animals Drown In Flooding In Duluth', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Floods', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Minnesota', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Zoos', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-21T04:28:10+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Steven Yaccino', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Steven', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Yaccino', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/707ae1aa-c414-52b3-9374-76796fbdd49d', 'word_count': 53, 'uri': 'nyt://article/707ae1aa-c414-52b3-9374-76796fbdd49d'}, {'abstract': 'Contaminated milk, cabbage, pork, soft drinks, baby formula, cooking oil, even “artificial peas” — there seems to be no end to the food-safety issues in China. And a new investigative report says regulators and inspectors are not keeping up with the cheaters.', 'web_url': 'https://rendezvous.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/06/21/from-milk-to-peas-a-chinese-food-safety-mess/', 'snippet': 'Contaminated milk, cabbage, pork, soft drinks, baby formula, cooking oil, even “artificial peas” — there seems to be no end to the food-safety issues in China. And a new investigative report says regulators and inspectors are not keeping up with t...', 'lead_paragraph': 'HONG KONG — There’s mercury in the baby formula. Cabbages are sprayed with formaldehyde. Gelatin capsules for pills, tens of millions of them, are laced with chromium. Used cooking oil is scooped out of gutters for recycling, right along with the sewage.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/21/blogs/milkchina-rdv/milkchina-rdv-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 688, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/21/blogs/milkchina-rdv/milkchina-rdv-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1376, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'From Milk to Peas, a Chinese Food-Safety Mess', 'kicker': 'IHT Rendezvous', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Baby Foods', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Dairy Products', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Fast Food Industry', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Food', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Food Contamination and Poisoning', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Foreign Investments', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hazardous and Toxic Substances', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Infant Formulas', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Melamine', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Milk', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Caixin', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Carlyle Group', 'rank': 12, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': "McDonald's Corporation", 'rank': 13, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Beijing (China)', 'rank': 14, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'China', 'rank': 15, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-21T04:28:51+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'World', 'byline': {'original': 'By Mark McDonald', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mark', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'McDonald', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0bba0f65-585b-553f-bd65-1359e43791a1', 'word_count': 951, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0bba0f65-585b-553f-bd65-1359e43791a1'}, {'abstract': '22-year-old Tarun Sehrawat died of malaria while on assignment for Tehelka magazine in the forest-dense state of Chhattisgarh.', 'web_url': 'https://india.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/06/21/37179/', 'snippet': '22-year-old Tarun Sehrawat died of malaria while on assignment for Tehelka magazine in the forest-dense state of Chhattisgarh.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Tarun Sehrawat was a 22-year-old photographer for Tehelka magazine who returned after an assignment deep in the heart of India’s forests where Maoists battle security forces and indigenous groups often get caught in the crossfire. He had gone with a reporter, Tusha Mittal, into the forest-dense state of Chhattisgarh in central India in May. He had been unwell during the trip and felt uneasy upon his return but continued to work. (The pictures that he took during the trip appeared in the magazine’s May 12 issue.)', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Young Photographer’s Death Has Some Worried', 'kicker': 'India Ink', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Accidents and Safety', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Malaria', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'News and News Media', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Mittal, Tusha', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Sehrawat, Tarun', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Tehelka', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'India', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-21T05:48:10+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Salil Tripathi', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Salil', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Tripathi', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a3b6b50d-1b22-5060-9470-28f47a58d8af', 'word_count': 1338, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a3b6