1+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Education', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ben Murray', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ben', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Murray', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c1785bb0-c6ab-5af8-9aaa-78c201555b0a', 'word_count': 181, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c1785bb0-c6ab-5af8-9aaa-78c201555b0a'}, {'abstract': 'Rebecca Norris Webb had begun a photography project in South Dakota, the land of her youth, when her brother died unexpectedly. All of a sudden, “My Dakota” became a process of grieving, rediscovery and poetry.', 'web_url': 'https://lens.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/06/21/grieving-a-loss-with-prairie-and-poetry/', 'snippet': 'Rebecca Norris Webb had begun a photography project in South Dakota, the land of her youth, when her brother died unexpectedly. All of a sudden, “My Dakota” became a process of grieving, rediscovery and poetry.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Rebecca Norris Webb’s book “My Dakota” is a personal meditation on the land where she came of age and an elegy for her brother who died in the middle of her project. James Estrin interviewed Ms. Norris Webb via e-mail.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Prairie, Poetry and Loss', 'kicker': 'Lens', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Grief (Emotion)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Photography', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Poetry and Poets', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Weather', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'South Dakota', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-21T09:00:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Multimedia/Photos', 'byline': {'original': 'By James Estrin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'James', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Estrin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ba5e15ce-f06d-5c90-8d35-2ecb9e95b361', 'word_count': 1471, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ba5e15ce-f06d-5c90-8d35-2ecb9e95b361'}, {'abstract': 'The underlying problem in the European financial crisis is that the creation of the euro was a bet that all participating nations would increase their productivity, and that bet has been lost, an economist writes.', 'web_url': 'https://economix.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/06/21/the-end-of-the-euro-is-not-about-austerity/', 'snippet': 'The underlying problem in the European financial crisis is that the creation of the euro was a bet that all participating nations would increase their productivity, and that bet has been lost, an economist writes.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Simon Johnson is the Ronald A. Kurtz Professor of Entrepreneurship at the M.I.T. Sloan School of Management and co-author of “White House Burning: The Founding Fathers, Our National Debt, and Why It Matters to You.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The End of the Euro Is Not About Austerity', 'kicker': 'Economix', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'European Sovereign Debt Crisis (2010- )', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'European Monetary Union', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Group of Twenty', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-21T09:00:19+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Economy', 'byline': {'original': 'By Simon Johnson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Simon', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Johnson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/fe85c4fa-4566-51d5-bfb8-19fb52ca36c5', 'word_count': 1087, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fe85c4fa-4566-51d5-bfb8-19fb52ca36c5'}, {'abstract': 'Across Indian cities, the state often comes in conflict with fun.', 'web_url': 'https://india.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/06/21/a-generation-gap-cleaved-apart-with-hockey-sticks/', 'snippet': 'Across Indian cities, the state often comes in conflict with fun.', 'lead_paragraph': '“The field hockey stick is not among the weapons that the Mumbai police force issues its officers,” Manu Joseph wrote in The International Herald Tribune. But that is the “implement” an assistant commissioner of police, Vasant Dhoble, “carried with him at least once this month as he and his team raided Mumbai’s pubs and nightclubs, making boys with gelled hair and girls in high heels flee inelegantly into the night,” Mr.Joseph wrote.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Generation Gap Cleaved Apart With Hockey Sticks', 'kicker': 'India Ink', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hockey, Field', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'NIGHTCLUBS AND CABARETS', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Joseph, Manu', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'International Herald Tribune', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Mumbai (India)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-21T09:20:40+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b1f526cb-cfd3-5ecc-b304-447b5bf74279', 'word_count': 292, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b1f526cb-cfd3-5ecc-b304-447b5bf74279'}, {'abstract': 'Two authors share advice for incoming college freshmen, including what is expected of college papers and when a student should declare a major.', 'web_url': 'https://thechoice.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/06/21/7-things-graduating-seniors-should-know-about-college/', 'snippet': 'Two authors share advice for incoming college freshmen, including what is expected of college papers and when a student should declare a major.', 'lead_paragraph': 'As graduating seniors prepare for their freshman year in college, we’ve asked Lynn F. Jacobs and Jeremy S. Hyman, authors of “The Secrets of College Success,” to provide tips for incoming college freshmen. What follows are excerpts. — Tanya Caldwell', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '7 Things Graduating Seniors Should Know About College', 'kicker': 'The Choice', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Colleges and Universities', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Education (K-12)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-21T09:58:48+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Lynn F. Jacobs and Jeremy S. Hyman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Lynn', 'middlename': 'F.', 'lastname': 'Jacobs', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Jeremy', 'middlename': 'S.', 'lastname': 'Hyman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/770c2e7a-e3e9-57f7-9a35-c72efa9d9247', 'word_count': 828, 'uri': 'nyt://article/770c2e7a-e3e9-57f7-9a35-c72efa9d9247'}, {'abstract': 'Political news from today’s Times and around the Web, plus a look at what’s happening in Washington.', 'web_url': 'https://thecaucus.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/06/21/the-early-word-cited/', 'snippet': 'Political news from today’s Times and around the Web, plus a look at what’s happening in Washington.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Today’s Times', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Early Word: Cited', 'kicker': 'The Caucus', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-06-21T10:00:11+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jada F. Smith', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jada', 'middlename': 'F.', 'lastname': 'Smith', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/1d1242e2-0913-59c3-8540-d1b8e4f4b2db', 'word_count': 429, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1d1242e2-0913-59c3-8540-d1b8e4f4b2db'}, {'abstract': 'A conversation with Anne-Marie Slaughter about why women can’t juggle a high-powered job and being fully present as parents — at least not the way America’s economy and society are structured today.', 'web_url': 'https://parenting.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/06/21/talking-about-why-women-cant-have-it-all/', 'snippet': 'A conversation with Anne-Marie Slaughter about why women can’t juggle a high-powered job and being fully present as parents — at least not the way America’s economy and society are structured today.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The cover story for the July/August issue of The Atlantic is one bound to stir conversation — as it was meant to. In “Why Women Still Can’t Have It All,” Anne-Marie Slaughter, the director of policy planning at the State Department under Hillary Rodham Clinton from 2009 to 2011, writes from personal experience about the reasons women can’t juggle both the demands of a high-powered job and being fully present as parents — at least not with the way America’s economy and society are structured today. After two years in Washington doing work she loved, she returned to academia at Princeton University, not because she didn’t want to stay in public service, but because “juggling high-level government work with the needs of two teenage boys was not possible.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Talking About Why Women Can’t Have It All', 'kicker': 'Motherlode', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-06-21T10:00:36+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': "By Kj Dell'Antonia", 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kj', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': "Dell'Antonia", 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c436b8fc-7d8b-5523-aa9a-e7fadf1b32d9', 'word_count': 1444, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c436b8fc-7d8b-5523-aa9a-e7fadf1b32d9'}, {'abstract': 'The potential cooling demands of upwardly mobile Mumbai have been estimated to be a quarter of those of the United States.', 'web_url': 'https://india.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/06/21/relief-in-every-window-but-global-worry-too/', 'snippet': 'The potential cooling demands of upwardly mobile Mumbai have been estimated to be a quarter of those of the United States.', 'lead_paragraph': '“In the ramshackle apartment blocks and sooty concrete homes that line the dusty roads of urban India, there is a new status symbol on proud display,” Elisabeth Rosenthal and Andrew W. Lehren wrote in The New York Times. “An air-conditioner has become a sign of middle-class status in developing nations, a must-have dowry item.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Relief in Every Window, but Global Worry, Too', 'kicker': 'India Ink', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': Non