name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Jordan', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Assad, Bashar al-', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-21T10:57:36+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Middle East', 'byline': {'original': 'By Rick Gladstone and Alan Cowell', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Rick', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gladstone', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Alan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cowell', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d05bd027-5834-57ec-b6dd-368274bbd1da', 'word_count': 1067, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d05bd027-5834-57ec-b6dd-368274bbd1da'}, {'abstract': 'Peaches, cherries, plums — poached, stewed, sautéed.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/interactive/2012/06/24/magazine/stone-fruit-recipes.html', 'snippet': 'Peaches, cherries, plums — poached, stewed, sautéed.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Peaches, cherries, plums — poached, stewed, sautéed.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Twelve Stone Fruit Recipes', 'kicker': 'Eat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Recipes', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cooking and Cookbooks', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Fruit', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Peaches', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cherries', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Plums', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-21T10:58:01+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Interactive Feature', '_id': 'nyt://interactive/94098e3e-5064-55a2-ba62-c38b7cfd2a8a', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://interactive/94098e3e-5064-55a2-ba62-c38b7cfd2a8a'}, {'abstract': 'In this week’s links: Massimo Pigliucci on ignorance and humor, children and gay parents, Alan Turing’s legacy, and more.', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/06/21/stone-links-the-ethics-of-ignorance-and-humor/', 'snippet': 'In this week’s links: Massimo Pigliucci on ignorance and humor, children and gay parents, Alan Turing’s legacy, and more.', 'lead_paragraph': ' What do Jenny McCarthy and Socrates have in common? According to a post by Massimo Pigliucci at his blog Rationally Speaking, they each exemplifie a certain understanding of ignorance. Inspired by Katja Vogt, Pigliucci uses the Platonic concept of “transferred ignorance,” to distinguish between a healthy Socratic, “self-aware” kind of ignorance, and the kind of “willful ignorance” that allows “feelings” to “somehow trump actual scientific evidence,” as in the case of McCarthy’s campaign against childhood vaccination. Pigliucci argues, after Plato, that this latter type of ignorance can be “morally problematic,” and that its recent prevelance has led our political discourse into “never before achieved peaks (or, rather, valleys).”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Stone Links: The Ethics of Ignorance (and Humor)', 'kicker': 'The Stone', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Children and Childhood', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computers and the Internet', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Philosophy', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Same-Sex Marriage, Civil Unions and Domestic Partnerships', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Turing, Alan M', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-21T11:00:00+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By A.c. Lee', 'person': [{'firstname': '', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/cd01cf5e-421f-5d61-87ba-cdd835f484df', 'word_count': 650, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cd01cf5e-421f-5d61-87ba-cdd835f484df'}, {'abstract': 'Holley Mangold goes for glory in the scruffiest Olympic sport.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/06/24/magazine/shes-350-pounds-and-olympics-bound.html', 'snippet': 'Holley Mangold goes for glory in the scruffiest Olympic sport.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When you’re a big, strong girl — a really big, strong girl — it’s easier to pull the table to your chair than to scoot your chair toward the table. Doorknobs need to be tightened, or you might pull them off. Holley Mangold, 22 and a superheavyweight Olympic weight lifter, is 5-foot-8 and weighs 350 pounds. When she squats, which she can do with 525 pounds on her shoulders, she looks almost wholly like an egg. “I get a lot of Creepy McCreepersons interested in me because I’m so big it’s not normal, it’s like a fetish,” she told me one day in the gym. “And I don’t like to sit outside. Not because I don’t like to be outside, but usually there are plastic chairs. Once you break a couple plastic chairs, you’re afraid of them all.”', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '36', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/24/magazine/24mangold1/24mangold1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 713, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/06/24/magazine/24mangold1/24mangold1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 713}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': 