erson': [{'firstname': 'Lillian', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'MONGEAU', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/29f6a74a-8f72-53e3-8f56-90da72ef614a', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://article/29f6a74a-8f72-53e3-8f56-90da72ef614a'}, {'abstract': 'Now that Thomas Pynchon novels are available as e-books, why not take “Mason & Dixon” to the beach?', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/06/22/books/thomas-pynchons-mason-dixon.html', 'snippet': 'Now that Thomas Pynchon novels are available as e-books, why not take “Mason & Dixon” to the beach?', 'lead_paragraph': 'Fifteen years after it was first published, Thomas Pynchon’s “Mason & Dixon” remains his most emotionally affecting novel and a dazzling, encyclopedic showcase for his talents: his ability to mix the philosophical with the slapstick, the playfully postmodern with the old-fashioned picaresque; his delight in language and narrative sleight of hand; his Swiftian knack for satire; and his storyteller’s ability to fuse history and science and fable with ardor and panache. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '28', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/22/arts/22KAKUTANI/22KAKUTANI-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 679, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/22/arts/22KAKUTANI/22KAKUTANI-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 679, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/22/arts/22KAKUTANI/22KAKUTANI-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/06/22/arts/22KAKUTANI/22KAKUTANI-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/22/arts/22KAKUTANI/22KAKUTANI-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 225, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Vintage Pynchon Goes Electronic', 'kicker': 'The Hot List | Books', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Vintage Pynchon Goes Electronic', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'E-Books and Readers', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Pynchon, Thomas', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-21T19:33:49+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Books', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michiko Kakutani', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michiko', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kakutani', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0dacc752-7db9-5f72-8550-b6b46b7491d4', 'word_count': 352, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0dacc752-7db9-5f72-8550-b6b46b7491d4'}, {'abstract': 'The Department of Energy is weighing the possibility of developing a market for carbon dioxide as a commercial product.', 'web_url': 'https://green.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/06/21/dreaming-up-a-whole-new-carbon-market/', 'snippet': 'The Department of Energy is weighing the possibility of developing a market for carbon dioxide as a commercial product.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Normally, when supporters of renewable energy talk about carbon markets, they’re referring to a system of traded credits that industries buy to compensate for their emissions of greenhouse gases, like the ones already sold in Europe and under development in California and Canada. But at the Renewable Energy Finance Forum-Wall Street, an annual conference organized by the American Council on Renewable Energy, the talk on Wednesday was of developing a different kind of carbon market where the gas would be bought and sold like a commodity to encourage carbon capture.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Dreaming Up Whole New Carbon Markets', 'kicker': 'Green', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-06-21T19:40:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Science', 'subsection_name': 'Environment', 'byline': {'original': 'By Diane Cardwell', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Diane', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cardwell', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2bf8261c-c390-58b7-8b7b-00d232e37445', 'word_count': 414, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2bf8261c-c390-58b7-8b7b-00d232e37445'}, {'abstract': 'What President Obama might have said had he gone to the Rio environment summit.', 'web_url': 'https://dotearth.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/06/21/dispatches-from-rio-none-from-obama/', 'snippet': 'What President Obama might have said had he gone to the Rio environment summit.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I understand why President Obama is not going to the United Nations Conference on Sustainable Development in Rio, which is a reprise of the 1992 Earth Summit there at which President George H. W. Bush and nearly all the world’s leaders appeared. (He’s skipping the conference despite taking direct heat in a YouTube message to Rio from Senator James Inhofe, the Republican of Oklahoma and steadfast ally of big coal, and indirect heat from Senator John Kerry, the Democrat of Massachusetts who gave a long floor speech on global warming and the Rio meeting yesterday, decrying “the conspiracy of silence 