n ‘Today’', 'kicker': 'Media Decoder', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'News and News Media', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Curry, Ann', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': "Ladies' Home Journal", 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Broadcasting Co', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-21T20:56:51+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Media', 'byline': {'original': 'By Brian Stelter', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Brian', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Stelter', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/bec425b9-4fa7-5ee3-9bf6-a7815a1eb985', 'word_count': 494, 'uri': 'nyt://article/bec425b9-4fa7-5ee3-9bf6-a7815a1eb985'}, {'abstract': 'In Alberto Manguel’s “All Men Are Liars,” lovers, friends and rivals talk about a famed writer, and violently disagree about him.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/06/22/books/all-men-are-liars-a-novel-by-alberto-manguel.html', 'snippet': 'In Alberto Manguel’s “All Men Are Liars,” lovers, friends and rivals talk about a famed writer, and violently disagree about him.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The novelist Edmund White has called biography “the most middle class of all forms, the judgment of little people avenging themselves on the great.” ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/22/arts/22BOOK/SUBBOOK-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/22/arts/22BOOK/SUBBOOK-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1363, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/22/arts/22BOOK/22BOOK-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/06/22/arts/22BOOK/22BOOK-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/22/arts/22BOOK/22BOOK-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 100, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Truth Is Out There, but Maybe Not in Here', 'kicker': 'Books of The Times', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Truth Is Out There, But Maybe Not in Here', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Manguel, Alberto', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-21T20:57:23+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Books', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dwight Garner', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dwight', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Garner', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/073a231d-66f3-5cd3-a86e-1f84dfcff0af', 'word_count': 794, 'uri': 'nyt://article/073a231d-66f3-5cd3-a86e-1f84dfcff0af'}, {'abstract': 'Los Angeles complements the summer blockbuster output with revival movies from varied cinemas and programs.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/06/22/movies/oscar-outdoors-and-other-los-angeles-screenings.html', 'snippet': 'Los Angeles complements the summer blockbuster output with revival movies from varied cinemas and programs.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Each summer Hollywood swamps the country’s movie theaters with its biggest, often most blockheaded blockbusters, flooding thousands of screens with the usual loud bangs, broad laughs, high concepts, low returns, remakes, reboots and foolishly resurrected bad ideas. The summer can feel especially endless in Los Angeles, where I live, because the entire city and its environs — from car-clogged street to street, from looming billboard to billboard — transform into one continuous advertisement for new releases that you’re not sure you want to see but probably will (even if you don’t make a living watching them). ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '30', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/22/arts/22DARGIS_SPAN/0622WKDARGAS-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 380, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/06/22/arts/22DARGIS_SPAN/0622WKDARGAS-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 380}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/22/arts/22DARGIS_SPAN/0622WKDARGAS-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 699, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/22/arts/22DARGIS_SPAN/0622WKDARGAS-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 1500, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/22/arts/22DARGIS_SPAN/0622WKDARGAS-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/06/22/arts/22DARGIS_SPAN/0622WKDARGAS-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Cures for Blockbusteritis, Even in the Land of Hollywood', 'kicker': 'The Hot List | Film', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Cures for Blockbusteritis, Even in the Land of Hollywood', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Los Angeles (Calif)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theaters (Buildings)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Hollywood (Calif)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'ACADEMY OF MOTION PICTURE ARTS AND SCIENCES', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-21T21:00:55+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'By Manohla Dargis', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Manohla', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Dargis', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f87e7eb8-0006-51e4-8ba4-29ce96a7c81b', 'word_count': 355, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f87e7eb8-0006-51e4-8ba4-29ce96a7c81b'}, {'abstract': 'A treasure trove of finished but unreleased tapes by the German experimental-rock group has been released as a three-CD box set by Mute Records.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/06/21/can-come-back/', 'snippet': 'A treasure trove of finished but unreleased tapes by the German experimental-rock group has been released as a three-CD box set by Mute Records.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The German experimental-rock group Can has never been obscure. Though not quite a household name here in the States, Can recorded several European hits in the years they were together, 1968 to 1979. They have also had an impact on a diverse group of musicians, from David Bowie to Brian Eno to Radiohead, and pioneered whole styles of music that their followers later expanded upon. This week, Mute Records releases “The Lost Tapes,” a three-CD box set containing over 180 minutes of previously unissued material. More than a great listen, the set solidifies both Can’s oeuvre and their approach to music making as far-reaching influences.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Can Come Back', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-06-21T21:01:50+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Diego Hadis', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Diego', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hadis', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/85d3a8df-2911-500a-8f15-92bdf9a614a5', 'word_count': 906, 'uri': 'nyt://article/85d3a8df-2911-500a-8f15-92bdf9a614a5'}, {'abstract': 'Three ballerinas are playing Juliet in Kenneth MacMillan’s “Romeo and Juliet”.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/slideshow/2012/06/22/arts/dance/20120622_ABT_SS.html', 'snippet': 'Three ballerinas are playing Juliet in Kenneth MacMillan’s “Romeo and Juliet”.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Three ballerinas are playing Juliet in Kenneth MacMillan’s “Romeo and Juliet”.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/22/arts/dance/20120622_ABT_SS-slide-H1LX/20120622_ABT_SS-slide-H1LX-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 640, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/22/arts/dance/20120622_ABT_SS-slide-H1LX/20120622_ABT_SS-slide-H1LX-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1281, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/22/arts/dance/20120622_ABT_SS-slide-H1LX/20120622_ABT_SS-slide-H1LX-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/06/22/arts/dance/20120622_ABT_SS-slide-H1LX/20120622_ABT_SS-slide-H1LX-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/22/arts/dance/20120622_ABT_SS-slide-H1LX/20120622_ABT_SS-slide-H1LX-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Romeos and Juliets', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-06-21T21:06:08+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Arts', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Dance', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Slideshow', '_id': 'nyt://slideshow/7d182c7a-755d-5786-86de-66c24ac28eac', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://slideshow/7d182c7a-755d-5786-86de-66c24ac28eac'}, {'abstract': 'Christer Strömholm’s work, at the International Center of Photography, documents the subculture of cross-dressing young men in Paris in the mid-1950s.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/06/22/arts/design/christer-stromholm-at-international-center-of-photography.html', 'snippet': 'Christer Strömholm’s work, at the International Center of Photography, documents the subculture of cross-dressing young men in Paris in the mid-1950s.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In Paris in the mid-1950s the Swedish photographer Christer Strömholm (1918-2002) gravitated to the red-light district around the Place Blanche, where he was drawn to a subculture of cross-dressing young men, many of whom worked as prostitutes to earn enough money for sex-change operations. The stocky, middle-aged photographer with a beard and military haircut hung out with them for more than a decade and photographed them on the street at night and in bars and hotel rooms with a coolly affectionate eye.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '27', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/22/arts/22STROMHOLM4_SPAN/22STROMHOLM4_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/06/22/arts/22STROMHOLM4_SPAN/22STROMHOLM4_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/22/arts/22STROMHOLM4_SPAN/22STROMHOLM4_SPAN-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 686, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/22/arts/22STROMHOLM4_SPAN/22STROMHOLM4_SPAN-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1372, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/22/arts/22STROMHOLM4_SPAN/22STROMHOLM4_SPAN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/06/22/arts/22STROMHOLM4_SPAN/22STROMHOLM4_SPAN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/22/arts/22STROMHOLM4_SPAN/22STROMHOLM4_SPAN-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 