ry Woodard drew on the songs of Ethel Merman, Sammy Davis Jr. and others in their show, “Glamorous Summer Nights,” at the Laurie Beechman Theater.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/06/22/arts/music/k-t-sullivan-and-larry-woodard-at-laurie-beechman-theater.html', 'snippet': 'K T Sullivan and Larry Woodard drew on the songs of Ethel Merman, Sammy Davis Jr. and others in their show, “Glamorous Summer Nights,” at the Laurie Beechman Theater.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When K T Sullivan swept onto the stage of the Laurie Beechman Theater on Wednesday evening with mischief in her eyes and a swivel in her hips, the image of Mae West flashed in my mind. Like West, Ms. Sullivan is voluptuous, blond and funny — and ageless. But Ms. Sullivan has a softer edge. Overt carnality is smothered in soap bubbles. Widening her saucer eyes and wearing a half-smile, a zany hat tilted just so, she projected an ebullient va-va-voom that camouflaged the vigilance of a performer on whom nothing is lost. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/22/arts/22SULLIVAN_SPAN/22SULLIVAN_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 360, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/06/22/arts/22SULLIVAN_SPAN/22SULLIVAN_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 360}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/22/arts/22SULLIVAN_SPAN/SULLIVAN-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/22/arts/22SULLIVAN_SPAN/SULLIVAN-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/22/arts/22SULLIVAN_SPAN/22SULLIVAN_SPAN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/06/22/arts/22SULLIVAN_SPAN/22SULLIVAN_SPAN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/22/arts/22SULLIVAN_SPAN/22SULLIVAN_SPAN-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 100, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Respectful Heirs Together Invoking Playful Spirits', 'kicker': 'Music Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Respectful Heirs Together Invoking Playful Spirits', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Sullivan, K T', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Woodard, Larry', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Beechman, Laurie, Theater', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-21T21:35:10+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Stephen Holden', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Stephen', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Holden', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/f032b806-29e8-5df0-9c02-e01377663054', 'word_count': 366, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f032b806-29e8-5df0-9c02-e01377663054'}, {'abstract': 'In “Abraham Lincoln: Vampire Hunter,” where the vampires are white Southerners whose cause is enslaving human beings, there are enough bodies to paint the screen red.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/06/22/movies/abraham-lincoln-vampire-hunter.html', 'snippet': 'In “Abraham Lincoln: Vampire Hunter,” where the vampires are white Southerners whose cause is enslaving human beings, there are enough bodies to paint the screen red.', 'lead_paragraph': '“Abraham Lincoln: Vampire Hunter” is such a smashing title it’s too bad someone had to spoil things by making a movie to go with it. Then again, a big-screen version of Seth Grahame-Smith’s comic novel was doubtless inevitable, given the delectable absurdity of the 16th president of the United States’ going all Buffy on a vamp army, splitting heads like rails. That sounds funny, and for a while it plays like head-exploding gangbusters on screen, particularly when the young Mr. Lincoln (a solid Benjamin Walker), in his grasshopper stage, begins learning how to swing a silver-tipped ax like a kung fu master. (As the great man said: “Whatever you are, be a good one.”) ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/22/arts/22ABRAHAM_SPAN/JPABRAHAM-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 360, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/06/22/arts/22ABRAHAM_SPAN/JPABRAHAM-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 360}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/22/arts/22ABRAHAM_SPAN/JPABRAHAM-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 576, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/22/arts/22ABRAHAM_SPAN/JPABRAHAM-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1151, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/22/arts/22ABRAHAM_SPAN/JPABRAHAM-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height'