eb_url': 'https://india.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/06/22/leaving-the-figure-behind/', 'snippet': '“Approaching Abstraction,” an art exhibition in New York City showcases Indian art of the 1960s and ’70s.', 'lead_paragraph': '“‘Approaching Abstraction,’ at the Rubin Museum, tries to put an Eastern phenomenon in a Western box,” Karen Rosenberg wrote in The New York Times. “It doesn’t always work, but there’s much to be learned from the effort.” ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Leaving the Figure Behind', 'kicker': 'India Ink', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-06-22T12:56:50+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/72d3dae4-237f-5588-b431-5f07d3e4c189', 'word_count': 168, 'uri': 'nyt://article/72d3dae4-237f-5588-b431-5f07d3e4c189'}, {'abstract': 'Sure, they try to eat in a healthy way. But eliminating bacon? Not going to happen.', 'web_url': 'https://london2012.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/06/22/for-top-athletes-the-side-order-they-cant-give-up/', 'snippet': 'Sure, they try to eat in a healthy way. But eliminating bacon? Not going to happen.', 'lead_paragraph': 'EUGENE, Ore. – Forget quinoa or lentils: Is it possible that bacon is the secret super food for the top United States athletes?', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'For Top Athletes, the Side Order They Can’t Give Up', 'kicker': '2012 London Olympics', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Olympic Games', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gatlin, Justin', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Burger King Corp', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-22T12:57:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Olympics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sam Borden', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sam', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Borden', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f2c83195-e575-59a8-a661-7b8ed0c6e55d', 'word_count': 249, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f2c83195-e575-59a8-a661-7b8ed0c6e55d'}, {'abstract': 'The reporter, Joe Williams, had a history of describing Mr. Romney and other conservatives in provocative terms on his Twitter feed.', 'web_url': 'https://thecaucus.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/06/22/politico-suspends-reporter-over-romney-comments/', 'snippet': 'The reporter, Joe Williams, had a history of describing Mr. Romney and other conservatives in provocative terms on his Twitter feed.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Politico, the Web site devoted to covering all facets of the political news cycle, said late on Thursday that it had suspended one of its White House reporters for making numerous disparaging and vulgar comments about Mitt Romney.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '11', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Politico Suspends Reporter Over Romney Comments', 'kicker': 'The Caucus', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Politico Suspends Reporter', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-06-22T12:58:00+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jeremy W. Peters', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jeremy', 'middlename': 'W.', 'lastname': 'Peters', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3729e68e-d0ba-573c-b830-8b47c5f69144', 'word_count': 714, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3729e68e-d0ba-573c-b830-8b47c5f69144'}, {'abstract': 'A Paris label’s inspiration? The lazily fashionable young men of Bedford Avenue.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/06/22/paris-does-brooklyn/', 'snippet': 'A Paris label’s inspiration? The lazily fashionable young men of Bedford Avenue.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Growing up in the Paris suburbs, David Obadia, 23, was obsessed with Nike Air Jordans and the American street-wear brands Supreme and Stussy. Meanwhile, his best friend, Nelson Hassan, 22, was more drawn to the avant-garde looks of Comme des Garçons and Raf Simons. No wonder, then, that upon graduation from business school and fashion school, respectively, the duo started their own label, Brooklyn We Go Hard, named for the Jay-Z song. “We like everything about Brooklyn, including Bedford Avenue in Williamsburg, Dr. Brown’s soda and the Nets,” Obadia explains. (He assures that they’ve both been there several times.) “Brooklyn brings together the mainstream and the underground, which is what we want to do with the clothes.” They started in 2010 selling simple white T-shirts embossed with gritty images of New York taken by a handful of Paris photographers, but they gained steam last year when they introduced an elbow-padded sweatshirt (about $140), soft cotton on the inside and nubby wool on the outside, with the motto “Brooklyn Parle Français” embroidered across the chest. The sweatshirt got picked up by Colette in Paris, Antonioli in Milan, Urban Research in Tokyo and Opening Ceremony in the United States, and has been worn by the likes of Kid Cudi, who sported it at the “Hunger Games” premiere, as well as Olivier Nakache, the director of the recent French box-office record-breaker “Les Intouchables.” (“Can you help us get the sweatshirt on Jay-Z or anyone from the Nets?” Hassan asks.) Later this month, they’ll show their first full collection at the Paris men’s Capsule show; the collection includes pieces that would look right at home on the lazily fashionable young men of Bedford Avenue, including chambray shirts with baseball stripes ($100) and reversible cotton knit sweaters that droop on a skinny torso as raffishly as a cigarette from the lips of a Marais bobo ($164). The guys are making the clothes in conjunction with a magazine they publish that, in charmingly bad English, helps foreigners understand such Brooklyn characters as boricuas and Lubavitchers. “People in Paris want to be in New York and Tokyo, and vice versa and vice versa,” Obadia says. “Everybody doesn’t want to be where they are.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Paris Does Brooklyn', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-06-22T13:00:24+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Tim Murphy', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Tim', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Murphy', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b34dda5d-c2ab-5d84-86dd-6b11b953cd23', 'word_count': 366, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b34dda5d-c2ab-5d84-86dd-6b11b953cd23'}, {'abstract': 'What Anne-Marie Slaughter gets right, and very wrong, about work-life balance.', 'web_url': 'https://douthat.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/06/22/having-it-all/', 'snippet': 'What Anne-Marie Slaughter gets right, and very wrong, about work-life balance.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Anne-Marie Slaughter’s long Atlantic essay on work-life balance, “Why Women Still Can’t Have It All,” is a striking mix of important truth and wishful thinking. The important truth is that there really is more than our society could do to help women (and men) balance their careers and their family lives. Both our workplaces and our public policy arrangements have not kept up with the decline of the single-breadwinner household, and an era of massive female labor force participation requires greater accommodations and adjustments to the reality of childbearing than American society has yet made.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Having It All', 'kicker': 'Ross Douthat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-06-22T13:07:41+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ross Douthat', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ross', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Douthat', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0bbcf5e6-a3fb-5a8e-8911-69be143ec9ad', 'word_count': 1275, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0bbcf5e6-a3fb-5a8e-8911-69be143ec9ad'}, {'abstract': 'A new survey offers a list of the 10 best small and mid-size soccer cities in the U.S.', 'web_url': 'https://goal.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/06/22/listmania-top-10-u-s-small-to-midsize-soccer-cities/', 'snippet': 'A new survey offers a list of the 10 best small and mid-size soccer cities in the U.S.', 'lead_paragraph': 'If there were ever doubts that soccer in the United States has found a home far beyond the big urban centers, a new survey released by Livability.com seems to put them to rest.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Listmania: Top 10 U.S. (Small- to Midsize) Soccer Cities', 'kicker': 'Goal', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Soccer', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'United States', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-22T13:12:11+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'S