subject', 'value': 'Factories and Manufacturing', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Land Use Policies', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Banerjee, Mamata', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'All India Trinamool Congress', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Tata Group', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Tata Motors Ltd', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'India', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Kolkata (India)', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'West Bengal (India)', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-22T13:41:12+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Hari Kumar', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Hari', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kumar', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d41b7b40-792d-529b-b14f-6e818916233a', 'word_count': 457, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d41b7b40-792d-529b-b14f-6e818916233a'}, {'abstract': 'The French director André Téchiné is fascinated by individuals stepping into new identities, a preoccupation evident in his latest movie, “Unforgivable.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/06/24/movies/andre-techine-director-of-unforgivable.html', 'snippet': 'The French director André Téchiné is fascinated by individuals stepping into new identities, a preoccupation evident in his latest movie, “Unforgivable.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'ANDRÉ TÉCHINÉ, who turns 70 next year, makes impossibly elegant movies about very messy lives. All the people in his films are in one way or another wanderers, existential transients drifting from relationship to relationship, identity to identity, unable somehow to settle into themselves, and that’s just the way Mr. Téchiné likes them. Movies like “Wild Reeds” (1994), “Thieves” (1996), “The Girl on the Train” (2009) and the new “Unforgivable” — his 19th feature, opening Friday — are filled with characters who seem not to have been created but simply found, strays picked up off the street. He offers them a place to rest while they, and he, try to figure out who they are and where they’re going: the house is rambling but beautifully appointed, and the host is gracious, tolerant, tactful. At this point Mr. Téchiné has been putting up lost souls for nearly 40 years; he knows what he’s doing.', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/24/arts/24TECHINE1_SPAN/24TECHINE1_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 360, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/06/24/arts/24TECHINE1_SPAN/24TECHINE1_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 360}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/24/arts/24TECHINE1_SPAN/24TECHINE1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/24/arts/24TECHINE1_SPAN/24TECHINE1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/24/arts/24TECHINE1_SPAN/24TECHINE1_SPAN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/06/24/arts/24TECHINE1_SPAN/24TECHINE1_SPAN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/24/arts/24TECHINE1_SPAN/24TECHINE1_SPAN-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 100, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Savoring Human Nature in Random Flux', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Savoring Human Nature In Random Flux', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Unforgivable (Movie)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Techine, Andre', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-22T14:00:02+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'By Terrence Rafferty', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Terrence', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rafferty', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9d260a03-e240-508d-b7ec-3870785b2345', 'word_count': 1222, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9d260a03-e240-508d-b7ec-3870785b2345'}, {'abstract': 'How much economic mobility do Americans really have? Are they stuck in the income class they were born to? The answers to these questions depend largely on whether they went to college.', 'web_url': 'https://economix.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/06/22/how-to-escape-a-lowly-socioeconomic-class-go-to-college/', 'snippet': 'How much economic mobility do Americans really have? Are they stuck in the income class they were born to? The answers to these questions depend largely on whether they went to college.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The latest installment in our Is College Worth It? series comes courtesy of the Treasury Department, which on Thursday released a report titled “The Economic Case for Higher Education.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'How to Escape a Lowly Socioeconomic Class: Go to College', 'kicker': 'Economix', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-06-22T14:00:21+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Economy', 'byline': {'original': 'By Catherine Rampell', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Catherine', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rampell', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/590641de-1901-592b-9f9c-3ec8c4688092', 'word_count': 276, 'uri': 'nyt://article/590641de-1901-592b-9f9c-3ec8c4688092'}, {'abstract': 'A collection of links from the reporters and editors of the Dining section.', 'web_url': 'https://dinersjournal.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/06/22/what-were-reading-463/', 'snippet': 'A collection of links from the reporters and editors of the Dining section.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Esquire: Apparently canned beer is gearing up for a comeback …', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'What We’re Reading', 'kicker': 'Diner&#039;s Journal', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-06-22T14:02:20+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6fa7353a-7a0a-5f6e-b8fc-26a4a73f9fd2', 'word_count': 406, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6fa7353a-7a0a-5f6e-b8fc-26a4a73f9fd2'}, {'abstract': 'Also, Facebook will let users opt out of becoming part of advertisements, and a promotional tie-in between “Madagascar 3″ and a children’s medication draws fire.', 'web_url': 'https://mediadecoder.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/06/22/the-breakfast-meeting-on-having-it-all-or-some-and-cracks-at-nbc-news/', 'snippet': 'Also, Facebook will let users opt out of becoming part of advertisements, and a promotional tie-in between “Madagascar 3″ and a children’s medication draws fire.', 'lead_paragraph': '11:38 a.m. | Updated An article in The Atlantic just posted online has renewed an intense discussion among women about how best – and whether – to balance family and career, Jodi Kantor writes. It is a discussion, admittedly, among elite women who are at the top of their fields, including the comedian Tina Fey, the Facebook executive Sheryl Sandberg and the author of the Atlantic article, Anne-Marie Slaughter, a Princeton professor who recently left a job at the State Department. The pace of the arguments about the article, “Why Women Still Can’t Have It All,” reflects the Internet age, Ms. Kantor writes:', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Breakfast Meeting: On ‘Having It All’ (or Some), and Cracks at NBC News', 'kicker': 'Media Decoder', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-06-22T14:09:54+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Media', 'byline': {'original': 'By Noam Cohen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Noam', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cohen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/18d8527d-16ee-5efc-9067-d8026dbc1104', 'word_count': 693, 'uri': 'nyt://article/18d8527d-16ee-5efc-9067-d8026dbc1104'}, {'abstract': 'The Goal blog will provide live analysis of the Germany-Greece Euro 2012 game on Friday.', 'web_url': 'https://goal.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/06/22/live-coverage-friday-of-germany-vs-greece/', 'snippet': 'The Goal blog will provide live analysis of the Germany-Greece Euro 2012 game on Friday.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Goal blog will provide live coverage of the Euro 2012 game between Germany and Greece on Friday at 2:45 PM ET. Jack Bell will offer commentary and analysis of the quarterfinal match.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Live Coverage Friday of Germany vs. Greece', 'kicker': 'Goal', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Soccer', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-22T14:16:02+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Soccer', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b197ed2b-f16d-5b84-857e-857b3b476439', 'word_count': 33, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b197ed2b-f16d-5b84-857e-857b3b476439'}, {'abstract': 'Previewing the critic Neil Genzlinger’s adventures in reality shows and the Louisiana bayou.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/06/22/take-one-critic-add-mud-mix/', 'snippet': 'Previewing the critic Neil Genzlinger’s adventures in reality shows and the Louisiana bayou.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When TNT announced that it would be rolling out its first attempt at reality television, a competition show called “The Great Escape,” starting Sunday, it seemed as if it might be an opportunity to explore two long-standing questions I’ve had about the genre: Are these competitions real, and, more important, could I do this stuff? My editor felt the same way, especially about Question 2, since she’s a bit of an “Amazing Race” addict. So the two of us asked TNT and the creators of the show, Elise Doganieri and Bertram van Munster (who are also behind “The Amazing Race”), if we might come to a shooting location for “The Great Escape” and try out the course.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Take One Critic, Add Mud, Mix', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-06-22T14:19:25+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Neil Genzlinger', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Neil', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Genzlinger', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/dc30ced7-746a-5fe0-8455-6e1cddd1d6b3', 'word_count': 254, 'uri': 'nyt://article/dc30ced7-746a-5fe0-8455-6e1cddd1d6b3'}, {'abstract': 'An actor stumbles into the clutches of Nazi conspirators in Alan Furst’s thriller.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/06/24/books/review/mission-to-paris-by-alan-furst.html', 'snippet': 'An actor stumbles into the clutches of Nazi conspirators in Alan Furst’s thriller.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Alan Furst’s Web site lists his current residence as Sag Harbor, Long Island. But, as legions of readers know, Furst obviously dwells in another time and place, somewhere between Paris and the Carpathian Mountains, sometime between the blindly tragic years of 1933 and 1941, when the world, as he says in an earlier book, “drifted idly toward blood and fire.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/24/books/review/0624-BYRD/0624-BYRD-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 922, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/24/books/review/0624-BYRD/0624-BYRD-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1844, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/24/books/review/06