Gov. Andrew M. Cuomo is expected to sign into law.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'New York Mandates Alerts on Sewage Discharges', 'kicker': 'Green', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Law and Legislation', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sewers and Sewage', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Swimming', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Waste Materials and Disposal', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Water Pollution', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cuomo, Andrew M', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Department of Environmental Protection (NYC)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'North River Wastewater Treatment Plant', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Riverkeeper', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'HARLEM (NYC)', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Hudson River', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York State', 'rank': 12, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-22T14:22:35+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Science', 'subsection_name': 'Environment', 'byline': {'original': 'By Mireya Navarro', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mireya', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Navarro', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/8d4c9959-6f25-5d32-a64c-bc988b510e1e', 'word_count': 446, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8d4c9959-6f25-5d32-a64c-bc988b510e1e'}, {'abstract': 'A Nobel laureate reimagines the life of the human rights advocate and Irish nationalist Roger Casement.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/06/24/books/review/the-dream-of-the-celt-by-mario-vargas-llosa.html', 'snippet': 'A Nobel laureate reimagines the life of the human rights advocate and Irish nationalist Roger Casement.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Joseph Conrad wasn’t the only literary European who traveled through the Congo basin during the 19th-century rush to harvest the region’s ivory, animal hides, minerals and especially its “black gold” — the liquid latex that welled in its rubber trees. While journeying by caravan to Léopoldville in 1890 to take charge of the merchant ship Le Roi des Belges, Conrad met Roger Casement, a handsome 25-year-old Irish do-\xadgooder who had lived and worked in the Congo Free State for the past few years. Like Conrad, Casement had come to Africa buoyed by the conviction that European colonization would bring moral and social progress to the continent and free its inhabitants “from slavery, paganism and other barbarities.” Each man would soon learn the gravity of his error.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '11', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/24/books/review/24Schillinger/24Schillinger-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 693, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/24/books/review/24Schillinger/24Schillinger-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1387, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/24/books/review/24Schillinger/24Schillinger-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/06/24/books/review/24Schillinger/24Schillinger-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/24/books/review/24Schillinger/24Schillinger-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Traitor, Martyr, Liberator', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Traitor, Martyr, Liberator', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Grossman, Edith', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Casement, Roger David', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Llosa, Mario Vargas', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-22T14:22:49+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Liesl Schillinger', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Liesl', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Schillinger', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/2f750109-7c7a-59c9-ba54-12289bfbe4bb', 'word_count': 1233, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2f750109-7c7a-59c9-ba54-12289bfbe4bb'}, {'abstract': 'A WASP clan performs its tribal rituals in this first novel.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/06/24/books/review/seating-arrangements-by-maggie-shipstead.html', 'snippet': 'A WASP clan performs its tribal rituals in this first novel.', 'lead_paragraph': '“Seating Arrangements,” Maggie Shipstead’s smart and frothy debut novel, is set on a perfect John Cheever island — the kind where old-money families gather to drink gin and nurture loyalties. Beneath the surface of this summery romp, however, lie animosities, well-paced sexual suspense and a clash between appearances and authenticity.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '13', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/24/books/review/0624-LANDIS/0624-LANDIS-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 281, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/06/24/books/review/0624-LANDIS/0624-LANDIS-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 281}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/24/books/review/0624-LANDIS/0624-LANDIS-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 479, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/24/books/review/0624-LANDIS/0624-LANDIS-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 959, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/24/books/review/0624-LANDIS/0624-LANDIS-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/06/24/books/review/0624-LANDIS/0624-LANDIS-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/24/books/review/0624-LANDIS/0624-LANDIS-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Members of the Wedding', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Members of the Wedding', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Shipstead, Maggie', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-22T14:23:29+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dylan Landis', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dylan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Landis', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/b8071a91-bbef-5372-8540-b1fe32524d9d', 'word_count': 626, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b8071a91-bbef-5372-8540-b1fe32524d9d'}, {'abstract': 'The second volume of Sontag’s diaries looks behind the mask.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/06/24/books/review/the-second-volume-of-susan-sontags-diaries.html', 'snippet': 'The second volume of Sontag’s diaries looks behind the mask.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When she was 13, Susan Sontag “made a rule”: “No daydreaming.” The habit of idle reflection, to most writers part of the job, seemed to her not so much a waste of time as a threat to stability: “Being intelligent isn’t, for me, like doing something ‘better,’ ” she confided to her journal in 1973. “It’s the only way I exist. If I’m not [being] intelligent, I hover near being catatonic.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '15', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/24/books/review/0624-Campbell-SUB/0624-Campbell-SUB-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 1000, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/24/books/review/0624-Campbell-SUB/0624-Campbell-SUB-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 2001, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/24/books/review/0624-Campbell-SUB/0624-Campbell-SUB-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/06/24/books/review/0624-Campbell-SUB/0624-Campbell-SUB-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/24/books/review/0624-Campbell-SUB/0624-Campbell-SUB-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Seeking Susan', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Seeking Susan', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Writing and Writers', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Sontag, Susan', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Rieff, David', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-22T14:24:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By James Campbell', 'person': [{'firstname': 'James', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Campbell', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/758f804c-6d11-5f2a-b605-cac91f194edc', 'word_count': 1293, 'uri': 'nyt://article/758f804c-6d11-5f2a-b605-cac91f194edc'}, {'abstract': 'A legal philosopher urges Americans to punish hate speech.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/06/24/books/review/the-harm-in-hate-speech-by-jeremy-waldron.html', 'snippet': 'A legal philosopher urges Americans to punish hate speech.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The United States is almost alone among Western liberal democracies in not punishing what is called hate speech — oral or written messages that “incite hatred” against a person or group on the basis of their race, religion, sex, ethnicity or sexual orientation. Canada, Britain, Denmark, Germany and New Zealand have such laws, and the International Covenant on Civil and Political Rights purports to require signatory nations to pass them. In the American constitutional tradition, by contrast, even detestable speech is permitted so long as the speaker does not threaten violence or incite others to it. The Supreme Court’s recent decision upholding the right of the Westboro Baptist Church to engage in hateful picketing of military funerals in opposition to toleration of homosexuality (“God Hates Fags,” one sign said) is a ready example. That case would almost certainly have come out differently in other liberal democracies.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/24/books/review/24McConnell/24McConnell-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 896, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/24/books/review/24McConnell/24McConnell-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1793, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/24/books/review/24McConnell/24McConnell-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/06/24/books/review/24McConnell/24McConnell-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/24/books/review/24McConnell/24McConnell-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 229, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'You Can’t Say That', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'You Can’t Say That', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Law and Legislation', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Waldron, Jeremy', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-22T14:24:37+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael W. McConnell', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': 'W.', 'lastname': 'McConnell', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/2eb6ee38-15b8-517d-8117-8660fdca7a5e', 'word_count': 1234, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2eb6ee38-15b8-517d-8117-8660fdca7a5e'}, {'abstract': 'The way to a stronger economy is to encourage the financial industry, Robert J. Shiller argues.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/06/24/books/review/finance-and-the-good-society-by-robert-j-shiller.html', 'snippet': 'The way to a stronger economy is to encourage the financial industry, Robert J. Shiller argues.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Robert J. Shiller made his name in 2000 with “Irrational Exuberance,” a book that drew on behavioral economics to explain why financial markets overshoot. Shiller concentrated on the boom-bust cycle in technology stocks, but a later edition published while real estate was taking off correctly anticipated the popping of that bubble, too. With his latest volume, “Finance and the Good Society,” Shiller is coming at us from the opposite direction. Quoting Walt Whitman, who found “the eternal meanings” in muscular commerce, he celebrates finance’s “genuine beauty” and exhorts idealistic young students to pursue careers in derivatives, insurance and related fields.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/24/books/review/24MALLABY/24MALLABY-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 281, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/06/24/books/review/24MALLABY/24MALLABY-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 281}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/24/books/review/24MALLABY/24MALLABY-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 479, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/24/books/review/24MALLABY/24MALLABY-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 958, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/24/books/review/24MALLABY/24MALLABY-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/06/24/books/review/24MALLABY/24MALLABY-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/24/books/review/24MALLABY/24MALLABY-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Boiler Rooms', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Boiler Rooms', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Shiller, Robert J', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-22T14:25:10+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sebastian Mallaby', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sebastian', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Mallaby', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/4bdcea4b-7a06-5566-a54d-6d6ecfe04072', 'word_count': 1181, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4bdcea4b-7a06-5566-a54d-6d6ecfe04072'}, {'abstract': 'The long-suppressed account of life in Auschwitz by a Polish officer.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/06/24/books/review/the-auschwitz-volunteer-by-witold-pilecki.html', 'snippet': 'The long-suppressed account of life in Auschwitz by a Polish officer.', 'lead_paragraph': 'One man volunteered for Ausch\xadwitz, and now we have his story. In September 1940 the 39-year-old Polish cavalry officer Witold Pilecki deliberately walked into a German roundup in Warsaw, and was sent by train to the new German camp. His astounding choice was made within, and for, Poland’s anti-Nazi underground.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '20', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/24/books/review/24SNYDER/24SNYDER-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 735, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/24/books/review/24SNYDER/24SNYDER-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1469, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/24/books/review/24SNYDER/24SNYDER-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/06/24/books/review/24SNYDER/24SNYDER-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/24/books/review/24SNYDER/24SNYDER-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '‘Were We All People?’', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '‘Were We All People?’', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Pilecki, Witold', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Holocaust and the Nazi Era', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Concentration Camps', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-22T14:25:53+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Timothy Snyder', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Timothy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Snyder', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/9c356aa1-7f53-56e1-a099-5bcde622bf48', 'word_count': 1668, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9c356aa1-7f53-56e1-a099-5bcde622bf48'}, {'abstract': 'An android of the author Philip K. Dick has a story of his own.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/06/24/books/review/how-to-build-an-android-by-david-f-dufty.html', 'snippet': 'An android of the author Philip K. Dick has a story of his own.', 'lead_paragraph': '“How to Build an Android” is the honest title of an earnest book, the first by David F. Dufty, a senior research officer at the Australian Bureau of Statistics. It explains how a team of researchers at the University of Memphis collaborated in 2005 with an artist and robotics expert, David Hanson, to create what was then the most sophisticated android anywhere, a replica of the science-fiction writer Philip K. Dick.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '17', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/24/books/review/24DOWNES/24DOWNES-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 731, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/24/books/review/24DOWNES/24DOWNES-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1463, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/24/books/review/24DOWNES/24DOWNES-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/06/24/books/review/24DOWNES/24DOWNES-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/24/books/review/24DOWNES/24DOWNES-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 226, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Talking Head', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Talking Head', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Artificial Intelligence', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Dick, Philip K', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Dufty, David F', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Robots and Robotics', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-22T14:26:36+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Lawrence Downes', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Lawrence', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Downes', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/ec774278-74b0-58b5-a484-1dd6e31e73ab', 'word_count': 1259, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ec774278-74b0-58b5-a484-1dd6e31e73ab'}, {'abstract': 'Egypt’s seemingly endless transition appears to have entered a bizarre state where things both are and are not.', 'web_url': 'https://latitude.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/06/22/egypts-quantum-entanglement/', 'snippet': 'Egypt’s seemingly endless transition appears to have entered a bizarre state where things both are and are not.', 'lead_paragraph': 'CAIRO — In the famous thought experiment by the Austrian physicist Erwin Schrodinger, a cat that is placed in a box, alongside a radioactive element whose decay will trigger the release of a deadly poison, is said to be at once alive and dead. According to the laws of quantum physics there is an equal chance that the radioactive element will decay and that it won’t. Thus Schrodinger posited that until the box is opened and the cat’s state observed, the animal is theoretically in a state where both possibilities co-exist.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Egypt’s Quantum Entanglement', 'kicker': 'Latitude', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Defense and Military Forces', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Middle East and North Africa Unrest (2010- )', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Mubarak, Hosni', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Shafik, Ahmed', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Muslim Brotherhood (Egypt)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Supreme Council of the Armed Forces', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Egypt', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-22T14:27:03+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Issandr El Amrani', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Issandr', 'middlename': 'El', 'lastname': 'Amrani', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/022e1b84-d991-5bac-a0ac-64c841044c25', 'word_count': 520, 'uri': 'nyt://article/022e1b84-d991-5bac-a0ac-64c841044c25'}, {'abstract': 'A. N. Wilson’s brief biography examines how a ludicrously run-of-the-mill man like Hitler rose to a position of such terrible power.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/06/24/books/review/hitler-by-a-n-wilson.html', 'snippet': 'A. N. Wilson’s brief biography examines how a ludicrously run-of-the-mill man like Hitler rose to a position of such terrible power.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Slender volumes on hefty topics have a natural appeal. Who would not like to master a complex subject in the space of a single afternoon? Yet small books on big things can also have their built-in hazards, as they run the risks of neglecting nuance and precision and ignoring valuable details. While it certainly offers numerous insights into Adolf Hitler and the wider context that made his monstrous career possible, A. N. Wilson’s “Hitler” ultimately falls short as a satisfactory substitute for something more substantial.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '21', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/24/books/review/24HERZOG/24HERZOG-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 461, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url':