, 'height': 587, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/24/books/review/0624-LAPIDOS/0624-LAPIDOS-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1173, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/24/books/review/0624-LAPIDOS/0624-LAPIDOS-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/06/24/books/review/0624-LAPIDOS/0624-LAPIDOS-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/24/books/review/0624-LAPIDOS/0624-LAPIDOS-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Dependents', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Dependents', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Dastgir, Rosie', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-22T14:28:33+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Juliet Lapidos', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Juliet', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Lapidos', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/5b2a2e4f-e204-54d1-b257-ffac7b11f756', 'word_count': 584, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5b2a2e4f-e204-54d1-b257-ffac7b11f756'}, {'abstract': 'A troubled 10-year-old hears a divine voice in Grace McCleen’s first novel.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/06/24/books/review/the-land-of-decoration-by-grace-mccleen.html', 'snippet': 'A troubled 10-year-old hears a divine voice in Grace McCleen’s first novel.', 'lead_paragraph': '“There is a world in my room,” says Judith McPherson, the mother\xadless 10-year-old narrator of Grace McCleen’s first novel, “The Land of Decoration.” “It is made from things no one else wanted and it is made with things that were my mother’s, that she left to me.” The world is Judith’s miniature re‑creation of the land of milk and honey that she believes will come after Armageddon. Touchingly, it is just a cleaned-up version of her working-class neighborhood, comprising tiny gardens and snowmen and clay model figures on which she eventually enacts a shameful voodoo. It’s no wonder Judith spends all day in her room. She is abnormally isolated — constrained by her father’s religious fundamentalism, which dictates that she sit out school assemblies and accept as her social circle the motley members of her church group, with whom she wanders through town trying to evangelize. Judith considers all of this within the range of normal. Only when she becomes the target of the class bully does the imminence of the End seem personal, and acute.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '23', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/24/books/review/0624-GAIGE/0624-GAIGE-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 243, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/06/24/books/review/0624-GAIGE/0624-GAIGE-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 243}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/24/books/review/0624-GAIGE/0624-GAIGE-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 415, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/24/books/review/0624-GAIGE/0624-GAIGE-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 831, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/24/books/review/0624-GAIGE/0624-GAIGE-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/06/24/books/review/0624-GAIGE/0624-GAIGE-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/24/books/review/0624-GAIGE/0624-GAIGE-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Child of God', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Child of God', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'McCleen, Grace', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-22T14:29:02+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Amity Gaige', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Amity', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gaige', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/6488973e-00c4-5350-bb0c-fa66c68923a5', 'word_count': 638, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6488973e-00c4-5350-bb0c-fa66c68923a5'}, {'abstract': 'Rich Benjamin on his relationship to the gay rights movement.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/06/24/books/review/up-front.html', 'snippet': 'Rich Benjamin on his relationship to the gay rights movement.', 'lead_paragraph': '“My relationship to the gay rights movement has been decidedly mixed,” said Rich Benjamin, who this week reviews Linda Hirshman’s “Victory,” a history of the movement. “I’m not an activist by nature. I’ve never been totally in the closet, but until recently I’d never been ‘out.’ I’m a fan of secrets. The less people know about me the better I can maneuver. There’s a beauty to mystery, to conversation, to discovery, to the exquisite pastimes through which people do a tango of distance and intimacy. And secrets are power.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/14/books/review/0624-UPFRONT/0624-UPFRONT-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 686, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/14/books/review/0624-UPFRONT/0624-UPFRONT-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1371, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Up Front', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Up Front', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-22T14:29:29+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Editors', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Editors'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/fcfc6bf3-5e87-5fe2-9558-cb389ba6fc64', 'word_count': 274, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fcfc6bf3-5e87-5fe2-9558-cb389ba6fc64'}, {'abstract': 'When the homey South Carolina novelist Dorothea Benton Frank signed books after a reading recently, one octogenarian in the audience asked if he could suck her toes.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/07/01/books/review/inside-the-list.html', 'snippet': 'When the homey South Carolina novelist Dorothea Benton Frank signed books after a reading recently, one octogenarian in the audience asked if he could suck her toes.', 'lead_paragraph': 'GUEST BOOK: Dorothea Benton Frank’s homey novels about family life in South Carolina’s Lowcountry — her latest, “Porch Lights,” hits the hardcover fiction list at No. 5 — are the kind you’re apt to discover on the bookshelf of a rented beach house, their swollen pages foxed and mildewed and maybe marked with a ring of vegetable oil, since Frank’s Web site provides recipes for her characters’ meals. (Grilled chicken, fried bananas, lots of seafood.)', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '26', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/07/01/books/review/01list-img/01list-img-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 403, 'width': 395, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/07/01/books/review/01list-img/01list-img-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 403, 'width': 395, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/07/01/books/review/01list-img/01list-img-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/07/01/books/review/01list-img/01list-img-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/07/01/books/review/01list-img/01list-img-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Inside the List', 'ki