s-choice.html', 'snippet': 'Recently reviewed books of particular interest.', 'lead_paragraph': 'BARACK OBAMA: The Story,  by David Maraniss. (Simon & Schuster, $32.50.) This huge and absorbing new biography, full of previously unexplored detail, shows that Obama’s saga is more surprising and gripping than the version we’re familiar with.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '26', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Editors’ Choice', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Editors’ Choice | Recent Books of Particular Interest', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-22T14:30:36+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'List', '_id': 'nyt://article/dd2ab6d4-660e-58da-880c-1cfd8b6a83bd', 'word_count': 269, 'uri': 'nyt://article/dd2ab6d4-660e-58da-880c-1cfd8b6a83bd'}, {'abstract': 'Paperback books of particular interest.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/06/24/books/review/paperback-row.html', 'snippet': 'Paperback books of particular interest.', 'lead_paragraph': 'TRAIN DREAMS,  by Denis Johnson. (Picador, $12.) The taming of the American West is encompassed in Johnson’s novella. Sent by train into the woods of the Idaho panhandle in the 1890s, his orphaned hero, Robert Grainier, works on logging gangs, falls in love and loses his wife and daughter to a devastating wildfire. “What Johnson builds from the ashes of Grainier’s life is a tender, lonesome and riveting story,” Anthony Doerr wrote here.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '28', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Paperback Row', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Paperback Row', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-22T14:31:13+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ihsan Taylor', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ihsan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Taylor', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'List', '_id': 'nyt://article/c764b4e2-e5e4-5f42-a3a1-368847b1b068', 'word_count': 520, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c764b4e2-e5e4-5f42-a3a1-368847b1b068'}, {'abstract': 'Marriage, in one form or another, has figured prominently in gay and lesbian fiction for quite some time.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/06/24/books/review/marriage-in-gay-and-lesbian-fiction.html', 'snippet': 'Marriage, in one form or another, has figured prominently in gay and lesbian fiction for quite some time.', 'lead_paragraph': '“When gay people everywhere can marry, will that mean the end of gay literature?” I was regularly asked this question last spring while on tour for my most recent book, an account of how novels and plays in the years after World War II shook up sexual attitudes. This was months before President Obama announced his support for gay marriage; the subject was already very much out there.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '31', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/24/books/review/0624-BRAM/0624-BRAM-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 614, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/24/books/review/0624-BRAM/0624-BRAM-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1229, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}