rs and administrators of the prestigious Horace Mann school in the Bronx.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The New York Times Sunday Magazine article on abuse by teachers at Horace Mann School (“Prep-School Predators,” June 10, 2012) was a highly unusual artifact of journalism, a journey into a black hole of a kind.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Horace Mann Abuse Article Plays Loose', 'kicker': 'The Public Editor&#039;s Journal', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-06-22T14:46:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Arthur S. Brisbane', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Arthur', 'middlename': 'S.', 'lastname': 'Brisbane', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/48aaa906-6a40-5f01-a49f-435ad098ad83', 'word_count': 1223, 'uri': 'nyt://article/48aaa906-6a40-5f01-a49f-435ad098ad83'}, {'abstract': 'French consumption of white and red wines – especially the latter – has been falling steadily in recent years, but sales of rosé continue to soar. And although most French rosé is drunk in France, exports have risen too.', 'web_url': 'https://rendezvous.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/06/22/france-and-the-world-drinks-more-pink/', 'snippet': 'French consumption of white and red wines – especially the latter – has been falling steadily in recent years, but sales of rosé continue to soar. And although most French rosé is drunk in France, exports have risen too.', 'lead_paragraph': 'PARIS–High-end Bordeaux and Burgundy may be all the rage in the new wine-drinking markets of Asia, especially China. Back home in France, the star of the wine scene is rosé.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/22/style/ihtrdvwine622/ihtrdvwine622-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/22/style/ihtrdvwine622/ihtrdvwine622-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'France, and the World, Drinks More Pink', 'kicker': 'IHT Rendezvous', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'International Trade and World Market', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Wines', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Bordeaux (France)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Burgundy (France)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'France', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Provence (France)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Rhone Valley (France)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-22T14:59:18+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'World', 'byline': {'original': 'By Eric Pfanner', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Eric', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Pfanner', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/01aac138-8fe4-582e-b8ed-9ed98ec9ece7', 'word_count': 342, 'uri': 'nyt://article/01aac138-8fe4-582e-b8ed-9ed98ec9ece7'}, {'abstract': 'This week, Linda Hirshman talks about “Victory,” her history of the gay rights movement; Julie Bosman has notes from the field; Lawrence Downes discusses the android Philip K. Dick; and Gregory Cowles has best-seller news. Sam Tanenhaus is the host.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/audio/2012/06/22/books/review/24books_pod.html', 'snippet': 'This week, Linda Hirshman talks about “Victory,” her history of the gay rights movement; Julie Bosman has notes from the field; Lawrence Downes discusses the android Philip K. Dick; and Gregory Cowles has best-seller news. Sam Tanenhaus is the hos...', 'lead_paragraph': 'This week, Linda Hirshman talks about “Victory,” her history of the gay rights movement; Julie Bosman has notes from the field; Lawrence Downes discusses the android Philip K. Dick; and Gregory Cowles has best-seller news. Sam Tanenhaus is the host.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Book Review Podcast', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-22T15:00:34+0000', 'document_type': 'audio', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://audio/ec8a1405-7990-5208-ad31-bb3c515ff565', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://audio/ec8a1405-7990-5208-ad31-bb3c515ff565'}, {'abstract': 'The New York Philharmonic and Park Avenue Armory build a program around Stockhausen’s expansive “Gruppen” to take advantage of the vast space.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/06/24/arts/music/stockhausens-gruppen-at-park-avenue-armory.html', 'snippet': 'The New York Philharmonic and Park Avenue Armory build a program around Stockhausen’s expansive “Gruppen” to take advantage of the vast space.', 'lead_paragraph': 'FOR the planners and builders of the Park Avenue Armory, the landmark structure that consumes a city block, bounded by Park and Lexington Avenues and 66th and 67th Streets, music was surely not among the chief concerns. Completed in 1881, the armory was intended to serve as a military installation and social club for the Seventh Regiment of the New York Na