de Maze” (June 10), I read that at the beginning of the fare games one of the major airlines believed its loyal customers would appreciate better service and amenities, even if they came at slightly higher fares. It lost customers in droves. As a traveler on a budget, I almost always choose the lowest fare, so I recognize that I’ve helped create this mess today.', 'print_section': 'TR', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Letters to the Editor', 'kicker': 'Letters', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Upgrade Maze', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Travel and Vacations', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-22T15:18:33+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Travel', 'section_name': 'Travel', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Letter', '_id': 'nyt://article/1f6604bc-526b-5b6d-92d4-b1e755351c0d', 'word_count': 77, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1f6604bc-526b-5b6d-92d4-b1e755351c0d'}, {'abstract': 'As a child, I spent a month with my father at a “fat farm” in Durham, N.C., and gained a new appreciation for what was missing from my family life.', 'web_url': 'https://well.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/06/22/going-to-the-fat-farm-with-dad/', 'snippet': 'As a child, I spent a month with my father at a “fat farm” in Durham, N.C., and gained a new appreciation for what was missing from my family life.', 'lead_paragraph': 'My dad was a brilliant ad man in the “Mad Men” era, responsible for such iconic slogans as “Coke Is It,” “Leggo My Eggo,” and “Fly the Friendly Skies of United.” He also was extremely overweight.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Going to the Fat Farm With Dad', 'kicker': 'Well', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Diet and Nutrition', 'rank': 1, 'major': '