mes', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e7f1289a-b62f-5318-a654-e0789bebc377', 'word_count': 33, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e7f1289a-b62f-5318-a654-e0789bebc377'}, {'abstract': 'The American premiere of “Por um Fio” (“By a Thread”), by Mimulus Dance Company at the Jacob’s Pillow Dance Festival, strikes a familiar, persistent chord.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/06/23/arts/dance/mimulus-dance-company-opens-jacobs-pillow-season.html', 'snippet': 'The American premiere of “Por um Fio” (“By a Thread”), by Mimulus Dance Company at the Jacob’s Pillow Dance Festival, strikes a familiar, persistent chord.', 'lead_paragraph': 'BECKET, Mass. — Not to steal the Jacob’s Pillow Dance Festival’s thunder on the opening night of its 80th-anniversary season, but as the Pillow presented its first concert, by Mimulus Dance Company of Brazil, on Wednesday, I kept thinking of its slightly younger counterpart, the American Dance Festival in North Carolina. Specifically, I was reminded of a destined-to-become-infamous incident from 1984, when the choreographer Mark Morris rose from the audience during a performance of Twyla Tharp’s “Nine Sinatra Songs,” yelled “No more rape!” and exited the theater.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/23/arts/MIMULUS/MIMULUS-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/06/23/arts/MIMULUS/MIMULUS-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/23/arts/MIMULUS/MIMULUS-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 792, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'cre