is sound installation, called it “a soundtrack to the sunshine,” an answer to the question: What does sunlight sound like?', 'print_section': 'WE', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/24/nyregion/24ARTSWE2_SPAN/24ARTSWE2_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 350, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/06/24/nyregion/24ARTSWE2_SPAN/24ARTSWE2_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 350}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/24/nyregion/24ARTSWE2_SPAN/24ARTSWE2_SPAN-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/24/nyregion/24ARTSWE2_SPAN/24ARTSWE2_SPAN-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/24/nyregion/24ARTSWE2_SPAN/24ARTSWE2_SPAN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/06/24/nyregion/24ARTSWE2_SPAN/24ARTSWE2_SPAN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/24/nyregion/24ARTSWE2_SPAN/24ARTSWE2_SPAN-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Finding in Sunlight a Sound and a Taste', 'kicker': 'Arts | Westchester', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Finding in Sunlight A Sound and a Taste', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Storm King Art Center', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Westchester County (NY)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-23T01:29:25+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Susan Hodara', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Susan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hodara', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/25db85b3-b9c7-5afe-b6e0-c92d0bf0c644', 'word_count': 1159, 'uri': 'nyt://article/25db85b3-b9c7-5afe-b6e0-c92d0bf0c644'}, {'abstract': 'Berkshire Hathaway said on Friday that it had agreed to buy The Waco Tribune-Herald, which has a daily circulation of 34,000, to expand in Texas.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/06/23/business/media/berkshire-hathaway-buys-the-waco-tribune-herald.html', 'snippet': 'Berkshire Hathaway said on Friday that it had agreed to buy The Waco Tribune-Herald, which has a daily circulation of 34,000, to expand in Texas.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Berkshire Hathaway said on Friday that it had agreed to buy The Waco Tribune-Herald to expand in Texas. Berkshire is buying the newspaper, which has a daily circulation of 34,000, from the Robinson family, the company said. Terms were not disclosed. Warren E. Buffett, Berkshire’s chief, has said that newspapers must rethink whether to offer free content on the Internet, and that publications with a community focus can thrive where there is little competition. ', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Berkshire Hathaway Buys a Newspaper in Texas', 'kicker': 'Business Briefing | Media', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Berkshire Hathaway Buys a Newspaper In Texas', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mergers, Acquisitions and Divestitures', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Berkshire Hathaway Inc', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Newspapers', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Texas', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Waco Tribune-Herald', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-23T01:29:28+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Media', 'byline': {'original': 'By Bloomberg News', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Bloomberg', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'News', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/0872bd95-d131-52b9-a857-06cdb31fa9a0', 'word_count': 77, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0872bd95-d131-52b9-a857-06cdb31fa9a0'}, {'abstract': 'How a Union general created the first regiment of fugitive slaves.', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/06/22/runaway-masters/', 'snippet': 'How a Union general created the first regiment of fugitive slaves.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Arago, a mail steamer, was scheduled to head north at dawn from the Union outpost on South Carolina’s Sea Islands. Any letters that missed the outgoing ship’s departure would wait for another 10 days. So it was that Maj. Gen. David Hunter stayed up all night on June 22, 1862, amid a violent thunderstorm, writing incessantly, assisted by his young aide, Maj. Charles Graham Halpine. Hunter, a West Pointer from the class of 1822, commanded “The Department of the South” — in Halpine’s words the “tortuous and broken sea-coast of Georgia, South Carolina, and Florida” that had proven vulnerable to Union naval probes. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Runaway Masters', 'kicker': 'Disunion', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Civil War (US) (1861-65)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Slavery', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hunter, David', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'South Carolina', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-23T01:30:40+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By daniel w. crofts', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7e6e728e-53ea-577a-a5d7-e35e4dee3939', 'word_count': 1561, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7e6e728e-53ea-577a-a5d7-e35e4dee3939'}, {'abstract': 'Why was the great general missing during one of the Civil War’s bloodiest engagements?', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/06/22/where-was-stonewall/', 'snippet': 'Why was the great general missing during one of the Civil War’s bloodiest engagements?', 'lead_paragraph': 'In the early spring of 1862, the Confederate general Thomas J. “Stonewall” Jackson won dazzling victories in Virginia’s Shenandoah Valley that made him “the hero of the South.” Combining incredible energy and audacity with a mastery of terrain and tactics, Jackson’s Valley Campaign is internationally famous and still studied today.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Where Was Stonewall?', 'kicker': 'Disunion', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Civil War (US) (1861-65)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Jackson, Stonewall', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lee, Robert E', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'McClellan, George B', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Seven Days', 'rank': 5, 'major': '