ount': 932, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ae229a0f-c141-597d-b69e-9e65959e5688'}, {'abstract': 'Karen Thompson Walker, whose first novel, “The Age of Miracles,” will be published Tuesday, loves to spend Sundays cocooning at home in Park Slope, Brooklyn.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/06/24/nyregion/the-novelist-karen-thompson-walker-holes-up-in-brooklyn-on-sundays.html', 'snippet': 'Karen Thompson Walker, whose first novel, “The Age of Miracles,” will be published Tuesday, loves to spend Sundays cocooning at home in Park Slope, Brooklyn.', 'lead_paragraph': 'As an editor at Simon & Schuster, Karen Thompson Walker used to spend her days polishing the prose of her roster of authors. But nearly every morning for more than three years, she was also rising at 7 to squeeze in an hour or two of writing before going to her day job in Midtown Manhattan. The product of that quiet endeavor is her debut novel, an apocalyptic tale titled “The Age of Miracles,” which will be published by Random House on Tuesday. Now a full-time writer, Ms. Walker, 32, lives in Park Slope, Brooklyn, with her husband, Casey, 32, who recently earned a Ph.D. in English literature from Princeton and has enrolled in the Iowa Writers’ Workshop in the fall. Until the couple pick up and move to Iowa City in August, they can be found wandering Brooklyn on Sundays.', 'print_section': 'MB', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/24/nyregion/24ROUTINE3_SPAN/24ROUTINE3_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 360, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/06/24/nyregion/24ROUTINE3_SPAN/24ROUTINE3_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 360}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/24/nyregion/24ROUTINE3_SPAN/24ROUTINE3-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 68