'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Schedule', '_id': 'nyt://article/836e1301-c3e7-5159-b4be-bcc84f1d9dd3', 'word_count': 3312, 'uri': 'nyt://article/836e1301-c3e7-5159-b4be-bcc84f1d9dd3'}, {'abstract': 'Derogatory remarks by a pair of Polish radio hosts about their Ukrainian housekeepers escalated into an international incident this week, forcing them to issue an on-air apology.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/06/23/world/europe/poland-radio-hosts-apologize.html', 'snippet': 'Derogatory remarks by a pair of Polish radio hosts about their Ukrainian housekeepers escalated into an international incident this week, forcing them to issue an on-air apology.', 'lead_paragraph': ' Derogatory remarks by a pair of Polish radio hosts about their Ukrainian housekeepers escalated into an international incident this week, forcing them to issue an on-air apology. On Friday, the hosts, Michal Figurski and Kuba Wojewodzki of Radio Eska, expressed “deep regret about our comments concerning Ukrainian women,” adding, “We personally believe that Ukrainian women possess exceptional beauty, which elicits both our admiration and respect,” according to the Unian news agency. The hosts’ offensive remarks last week prompted the Ukrainian Foreign Ministry to protest to the Polish National Council of Television and Radio. The ministry criticized the comments as “offending the honor and dignity of Ukrainians” and demanded a public apology. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Poland: Radio Hosts Apologize', 'kicker': 'World Briefing | Europe', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Poland: Radio Hosts Apologize', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Apologies', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Ukraine', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Radio', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Poland', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-23T05:16:59+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Europe', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andrew Roth', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andrew', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Roth', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/f75d40ad-1631-5d21-82c9-3663dfd85f1d', 'word_count': 112, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f75d40ad-1631-5d21-82c9-3663dfd85f1d'}, {'abstract': 'Mr. Adler co-wrote the music and lyrics for the Broadway hits “The Pajama Game” and “Damn Yankees.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/06/23/arts/music/richard-adler-collaborator-on-pajama-game-and-damn-yankees-dies-at-90.html', 'snippet': 'Mr. Adler co-wrote the music and lyrics for the Broadway hits “The Pajama Game” and “Damn Yankees.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'Richard Adler, a composer and lyricist whose towering early successes on Broadway in the 1950s — the smash hits “The Pajama Game” and “Damn Yankees” — were followed abruptly by the death of his creative collaborator, died on Thursday at his home in Southampton, N.Y. He was 90. ', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/23/arts/ADLER-obit/ADLER-obit-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 819, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/23/arts/ADLER-obit/ADLER-obit-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1638, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type'