nk': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/24/nyregion/HORACE1-sub/HORACE1-sub-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 941, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/24/nyregion/HORACE1-sub/HORACE1-sub-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1674, 'width': 1538, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Retired Horace Mann Teacher Admits to Sex With Students', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Retired Teacher At Mann Recalls Sex With Pupils', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Teachers and School Employees', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sex Crimes', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Bronx (NYC)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Child Abuse and Neglect', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lin, Tek Young', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Education (K-12)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Horace Mann School', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-23T20:58:12+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jenny Anderson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jenny', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Anderson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f7f3c2f9-6b45-533a-a2cf-f47e92138265', 'word_count': 1420, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f7f3c2f9-6b45-533a-a2cf-f47e92138265'}, {'abstract': 'Representative Darrell Issa, Senator Marco Rubio and Tim Pawlenty are among the guests on this week’s Sunday shows.', 'web_url': 'https://thecaucus.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/06/23/sunday-breakfast-menu-june-24/', 'snippet': 'Representative Darrell Issa, Senator Marco Rubio and Tim Pawlenty are among the guests on this week’s Sunday shows.', 'lead_paragraph': 'At the end of a week that saw tensions mount between the Obama administration and House Republicans, Representative Darrell Issa of California will appear on three shows to offer his perspective.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Sunday Breakfast Menu: June 24', 'kicker': 'The Caucus', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-06-23T20:58:26+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Emmarie Huetteman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Emmarie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Huetteman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0bf6c6c8-a451-5809-8049-b1adce57cd55', 'word_count': 475, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0bf6c6c8-a451-5809-8049-b1adce57cd55'}, {'abstract': 'Starting July 1, the making and selling of foie gras will be banned in California to prevent inhumane treatment of ducks and geese.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/06/24/opinion/sunday/foie-gras-a-faux-pas.html', 'snippet': 'Starting July 1, the making and selling of foie gras will be banned in California to prevent inhumane treatment of ducks and geese.', 'lead_paragraph': 'This is the last week for legal foie gras in California. A law to shut down the making, cooking and selling of super-fatty goose or duck liver takes effect July 1. Chowhounds from Chico to Chula Vista have been opening their wallets for restaurants’ foie gras menus, gorging themselves like birds before the winter migration. The law’s purpose is to end gavage, the ancient practice of forcing grain down the throat of a goose or duck until its liver is grossly enlarged. The law’s critics say gavage is hardly more stress-inducing than the many other things humans do to the animals they eat. The corporate operations that grow and slaughter pigs, poultry and cattle represent animal cruelty on an immense scale, they say, about which the foie gras ban does nothing.', 'print_section': 'SR', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/24/opinion/24sun-img/24sun-img-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 330, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/06/24/opinion/24sun-img/24sun-img-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 330}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/24/opinion/24sun-img/24sun-img-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 575, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/24/opinion/24sun-img/24sun-img-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1151, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/24/opinion/24sun-img/24sun-img-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/06/24/opinion/24sun-img/24sun-img-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/24/opinion/24sun-img/24sun-img-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Foie Gras a Faux Pas?', 'kicker': 'Editorial | Week Ahead', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Foie Gras a Faux Pas?', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Law and Legislation', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'California', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Foie Gras', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Editorials', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cruelty to Animals', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-23T20:58:33+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'subsection_name': 'Sunday Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Lawrence Downes', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Lawrence', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Downes', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/bfa2e862-ff30-5744-9db0-05b9151cf1aa', 'word_count': 294, 'uri': 'nyt://article/bfa2e862-ff30-5744-9db0-05b9151cf1aa'}, {'abstract': 'Important cases are awaiting decisions by the Supreme Court. At the top of that list is the challenge to the Affordable Care Act.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/06/24/opinion/sunday/the-courts-moment.html', 'snippet': 'Important cases are awaiting decisions by the Supreme Court. At the top of that list is the challenge to the Affordable Care Act.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The challenge to the Affordable Care Act  tops the list of Supreme Court rulings expected this week. The big question is whether Congress has the power to require that individuals buy or obtain health insurance as part of a comprehensive reform. Other issues are whether Congress can broaden Medicaid coverage of the poor and taxpayers can even challenge the individual mandate before it takes effect in 2014. The outcome — aside from determining whether millions of Americans will get health coverage — will show whether the Roberts court recognizes that constitutional law requires that it defer to Congress on matters affecting the national economy.', 'print_section': 'SR', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Court’s Moment', 'kicker': 'Editorial | Week Ahead', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Court’s Moment', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Patient Protection and Affordable Care Act (2010)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Editorials', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Supreme Court', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Decisions and Verdicts', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Arizona Immigration Law (SB 1070)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-23T20:59:40+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'subsection_name': 'Sunday Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Lincoln Caplan', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Lincoln', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Caplan', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/0f918ab2-75e5-5422-9133-f6346a7fa001', 'word_count': 308, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0f918ab2-75e5-5422-9133-f6346a7fa001'}, {'abstract': 'The Goal blog will provide live analysis of the England-Italy Euro 2012 game on Sunday.', 'web_url': 'https://goal.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/06/23/live-coverage-sunday-of-england-vs-italy/', 'snippet': 'The Goal blog will provide live analysis of the England-Italy Euro 2012 game on Sunday.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Goal blog will provide live coverage of the Euro 2012 game between England and Italy on Sunday at 2:45 p.m. ET. Jeffrey Marcus will offer commentary and analysis of the quarterfinal match.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Live Coverage Sunday of England vs. Italy', 'kicker': 'Goal', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Soccer', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-23T21:00:42+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Soccer', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/746e3e28-bcba-55e6-801e-29b649726e15', 'word_count': 33, 'uri': 'nyt://article/746e3e28-bcba-55e6-801e-29b649726e15'}, {'abstract': 'Senate negotiators need to fight back against the ideologically driven House version of the bill, which is loaded with anti-environmental riders.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/06/24/opinion/sunday/last-lap-for-transportation-bill.html', 'snippet': 'Senate negotiators need to fight back against the ideologically driven House version of the bill, which is loaded with anti-environmental riders.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Congressional negotiators are racing to agree on a transportation bill before the current law expires on June 30. The choices before them are wildly different: a Senate bill that would invest $109 billion over two years on critical road, bridge and mass transit projects, and an ideologically driven, short-term House bill that is loaded with anti-environmental riders and would extend current financing for only three months, leaving future financing up in the air.', 'print_section': 'SR', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Last Lap for Transportation Bill', 'kicker': 'Editorial | Week Ahead', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Last Lap for Transportation Bill', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Editorials', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Boxer, Barbara', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organization