ath for America from the article “Class of 2012 in Qatar Savors the College Station Connection.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Test Yourself | Math, June 25, 2012', 'kicker': 'The Learning Network', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-06-25T08:28:57+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Education', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Learning Network', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Learning Network'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/01e75315-75d3-5901-8635-60210235fbdd', 'word_count': 55, 'uri': 'nyt://article/01e75315-75d3-5901-8635-60210235fbdd'}, {'abstract': 'Hazel Hankin has been hooked on Mambo’s elegant, energetic rhythms since she first heard them. Combining two passions, she’s turned her camera on the vibrant Mambo subculture in New York’s dance clubs and studios.', 'web_url': 'https://lens.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/06/25/hazel-hankins-mambo-madness/', 'snippet': 'Hazel Hankin has been hooked on Mambo’s elegant, energetic rhythms since she first heard them. Combining two passions, she’s turned her camera on the vibrant Mambo subculture in New York’s dance clubs and studios.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Against a shimmering sonic backdrop of brassy horns and percussive beats, mambo masters know it is a dancer’s dream to lose themselves in each other and the music. This is not some quick one-two, but a complex symphony of steps that test the hardiest hoofer.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Hazel Hankin’s Mambo Madness', 'kicker': 'Lens', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Dancing', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Photography', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Salsa Dancing', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Puente, Tito', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Rodriguez, Tito', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Brooklyn (NYC)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-25T09:00:20+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Multimedia/Photos', 'byline': {'original': 'By David Gonzalez', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gonzalez', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/cb145811-54f9-5fee-add5-46cb1e4f1cd2', 'word_count': 508, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cb145811-54f9-5fee-add5-46cb1e4f1cd2'}, {'abstract': 'The author of ” The Moslems Are Coming,” an ethnic Indian, says that his book makes sense of currents affairs by using pop-cultural references.', 'web_url': 'https://india.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/06/25/a-conversation-with-author-azad-essa/', 'snippet': 'The author of ” The Moslems Are Coming,” an ethnic Indian, says that his book makes sense of currents affairs by using pop-cultural references.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It’s the kind of book cover guaranteed to generate stares when pulled out in public: the title, “The Moslems are Coming: Encounters with a Desktop Terrorist,” is emblazoned above a menacing image of a man brandishing a firearm, but a closer inspection shows a USB flash drive sticking out of it. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Conversation With: Author Azad Essa', 'kicker': 'India Ink', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Writing and Writers', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Essa, Azad', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {