e': 'organizations', 'value': 'Indy Racing League', 'rank': 12, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Assn of Stock Car Auto Racing', 'rank': 13, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Valencia (Spain)', 'rank': 14, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-25T14:45:41+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Automobiles', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jonathan Schultz', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jonathan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Schultz', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/8055fa76-5365-504d-a46f-f460555b9f83', 'word_count': 401, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8055fa76-5365-504d-a46f-f460555b9f83'}, {'abstract': 'The top of the lineup could have been booked a decade ago: Beck, Modest Mouse, Explosions in the Sky and Fiona Apple.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/06/25/governors-ball-day-2-turning-back-the-clock/', 'snippet': 'The top of the lineup could have been booked a decade ago: Beck, Modest Mouse, Explosions in the Sky and Fiona Apple.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The top of Sunday’s lineup at the Governors Ball — the rock festival that commandeered Randalls (not Governors) Island on the weekend — could have been booked a decade ago. The headliner was Beck, leading the band that made his album “Sea Change” with him in 2002, in a set full of songs from “Sea Change” and his\xa01990s albums.\xa0 Preceding him were Modest Mouse, which had its biggest hit in 2004 and hasn’t released a new album since 2007 (though it did have new material); the instrumental rock band Explosions in the Sky, formed in 2000; and Fiona Apple, who has just released a superb new album but who made her multimillion-selling debut in 1996. Earlier there was a set by Built to Spill, formed by the songwriter and guitarist Doug Martsch in 1992.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': 