 Arabia, Qatar and Brunei — that previously sent only men.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/06/26/sports/olympics/saudi-arabia-to-allow-female-athletes-at-london-games.html', 'snippet': 'Every participating nation is expected to field at least one female athlete, including the three Muslim countries — Saudi Arabia, Qatar and Brunei — that previously sent only men.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Saudi Arabia, a monarchy with severe restrictions on women in daily life, will allow female athletes to compete in the Olympics for the first time this summer if they qualify, according to a statement by the country’s embassy in London.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '11', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Women May Compete for Saudi Arabia', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Saudi Team Is Opened to Women', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Olympic Games (2012)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Saudi Arabia', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Women and Girls', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-25T16:31:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Olympics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Mary Pilon', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mary', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Pilon', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c22fa85c-ef06-5576-95cc-5bddafb6c62e', 'word_count': 288, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c22fa85c-ef06-5576-95cc-5bddafb6c62e'}, {'abstract': 'The justices ruled that such sentencing for those under 18 violated the Eighth Amendment’s ban on cruel and unusual punishment.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/06/26/us/justices-bar-mandatory-life-sentences-for-juveniles.html', 'snippet': 'The justices ruled that such sentencing for those under 18 violated the Eighth Amendment’s ban on cruel and unusual punishment.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WASHINGTON — Some 2,000 juvenile offenders serving life sentences without parole were given hope of eventual release by the Supreme Court on Monday. The court ruled that laws requiring youths convicted of murder to be sentenced to die in prison violate the Eighth Amendment’s ban on cruel and unusual punishment. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/26/us/26juvenile/26juvenile-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 1022, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/26/us/26juvenile/26juvenile-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 2043, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Justices Bar Mandatory Life Terms for Juveniles', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Mandatory Life Terms Barred For Juveniles in Murder Cases', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Juvenile Delinquency', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Children and Childhood', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'United States', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Murders and Attempted Murders', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Supreme Court', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Decisions and Verdicts', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mandatory Sentencing', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kagan, Elena', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Law and Legislation', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sentences (Criminal)', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Eighth Amendment (US Constitution)', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-25T16:31:58+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Adam Liptak and Ethan Bronner', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Adam', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Liptak', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Ethan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bronner', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/1bb7ee40-a8fe-5efc-bf9a-01c80f32c877', 'word_count': 1003, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1bb7ee40-a8fe-5efc-bf9a-01c80f32c877'}, {'abstract': 'In Spec Ops: The Line, Americans are the targets, and the sense of darkness harks back to Conrad.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/06/26/arts/video-games/spec-ops-the-line-from-2k-games-makes-killing-personal.html', 'snippet': 'In Spec Ops: The Line, Americans are the targets, and the sense of darkness harks back to Conrad.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Most military shooters — a subgenre of video games with conventions so rigid as to seem ritualistic — try to have it both ways when it comes to their relationship with actual warfare. Players are clearly supposed to find the combat, and the real-world settings, a viscerally exciting way to connect to current events. At the same time, far-fetched plotlines reassure us that this is all just a game; have fun and don’t think too much about what you’re doing. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/25/arts/spec/spec-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 330, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/0