mselves, a scientist suggests.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When the smoke finally clears and new plant life pokes up from the scorched earth after the wildfires raging in the southern Rockies, what emerges will look radically different than what was there just a few weeks ago. According to Craig Allen, a research ecologist with the United States Geological Survey in Los Alamos, N.M., forests in the region have not been regenerating after the vast wildfires that have been raging for the last decade and a half. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Goodbye to Mountain Forests?', 'kicker': 'Green', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Forest and Brush Fires', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Forests and Forestry', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mountains', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'National Parks, Monuments and Seashores', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Colorado', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New Mexico', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Southwestern States (US)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-25T20:14:25+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Science', 'subsection_name': 'Environment', 'byline': {'original': 'By Hillary Rosner', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Hillary', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rosner', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3e5f6a54-eb99-560f-b4a6-116b8a6ecfc6', 'word_count': 423, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3e5f6a54-eb99-560f-b4a6-116b8a6ecfc6'}, {'abstract': 'Sonny Vandevelde’s Milan fashion week photo diary.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/06/25/photos-of-the-moment-calvin-klein-coll