', '_id': 'nyt://interactive/ceafba27-3ca7-531a-a071-291d3decc5f7', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://interactive/ceafba27-3ca7-531a-a071-291d3decc5f7'}, {'abstract': 'There was surprise and a measured sense of victory from all sides over the Supreme Court’s mixed decision on Arizona’s immigration law, as well as questions about what could happen next.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/06/26/us/in-arizona-confusion-on-immigration-law-ruling.html', 'snippet': 'There was surprise and a measured sense of victory from all sides over the Supreme Court’s mixed decision on Arizona’s immigration law, as well as questions about what could happen next.', 'lead_paragraph': 'PHOENIX — In the place where it all began, there was confusion and consternation on Monday at the United States Supreme Court’s unanimous decision to uphold the most controversial portion of Arizona’s contentious immigration law, the part that requires law enforcement officers to check the immigration status of people in custody who they suspect are in the country illegally. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'In Arizona, Confusion on Ruling on Migrants', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'In Arizona, Confusion on Ruling on Migrants', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Brewer, Jan', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Arizona Immigration Law (SB 1070)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Immigration and Emigration', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'ARIZONA', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Supreme Court', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Illegal Immigrants', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Law and Legislation', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-26T01:56:18+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'new