2.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/06/26/pageoneplus/corrections-june-26.html', 'snippet': 'Corrections appearing in print on June 26, 2012.', 'lead_paragraph': 'FRONT PAGE', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Corrections: June 26', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Corrections', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-06-26T02:01:14+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Summary', 'section_name': 'Corrections', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/8670435f-82cc-55c5-b5a2-aec138c590fb', 'word_count': 697, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8670435f-82cc-55c5-b5a2-aec138c590fb'}, {'abstract': 'President Obama appealed to voters worried about racial profiling, while Mitt Romney defended states’ aggressive efforts to fight illegal immigration.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/06/26/us/politics/candidates-react-to-supreme-court-ruling-in-campaigns.html', 'snippet': 'President Obama appealed to voters worried about racial profiling, while Mitt Romney defended states’ aggressive efforts to fight illegal immigration.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Supreme Court’s decision on Arizona’s strict immigration law gave President Obama another shot at energizing Latino voters, while Mitt Romney defended states’ aggressive efforts to fight illegal immigration. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '13', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/26/us/26politics1/26politics1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 330, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/06/26/us/26politics1/26politics1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 330}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/26/us/26politics1/26politics1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/26/us/26politics1/26politics1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1366, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/26/us/26politics1/26politics1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/06/26/us/26politics1/26politics1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/26/us/26politics1/26politics1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'On the Campaign Trail, Obama and Romney React to the Justices’ Decision', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'On the Campaign Trail, Obama and Romney React to the Justices’ Decision', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Arizona Immigration Law (SB 1070)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hispanic-Americans', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'ARIZONA', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Presidential Election of 2012', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Supreme Court', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Romney, Mitt', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Obama, Barack', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Illegal Immigrants', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Law and Legislation', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-26T02:05:51+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Trip Gabriel and Helene Cooper', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Trip', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gabriel', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Helene', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cooper', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/18e47046-5744-578e-8683-951de8659094', 'word_count': 867, 'uri': 'nyt://article/18e47046-5744-578e-8683-951de8659094'}, {'abstract': 'In choosing to turn its back on the disastrous ruling, the justices allow elections to continue to be bought by the biggest check writers.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/06/26/opinion/the-court-citizens-united.html', 'snippet': 'In choosing to turn its back on the disastrous ruling, the justices allow elections to continue to be bought by the biggest check writers.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Supreme Court examined the Arizona immigration law in minute detail, but when it came to revisiting the damage caused by its own handiwork in the 2010 Citizens United case, it couldn’t be bothered. In a single dismissive paragraph on Monday, the court’s conservative majority refused to allow Montana or any other state to impose limits on corporate election spending and wouldn’t even entertain arguments on the subject.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Citizens United', 'kicker': 'Editorial | The Supreme Court Acts', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'And refuse to revisit the Citizens United disaster', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Citizens United v Federal Election Commission (Supreme Court Decision)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Supreme Court', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Campaign Finance', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Presidential Election of 2012', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Decisions and Verdicts', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Montana', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Editorials', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-26T02:06:11+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/d950a534-2a74-560e-b1a9-1742c119c237', 'word_count': 462, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d950a534-2a74-560e-b1a9-1742c119c237'}, {'abstract': 'The chief of Britain’s domestic security service, MI5, offered a guarded reassurance on Monday about the risk of terrorist attacks during the 17-day spectacle in Britain.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/06/26/world/europe/britain-security-chief-offers-assessment-about-olympics.html', 'snippet': 'The chief of Britain’s domestic security service, MI5, offered a guarded reassurance on Monday about the risk of terrorist attacks during the 17-day spectacle in Britain.', 'lead_paragraph': ' A month before the start of the Olympic Games, the chief of Britain’s domestic security service, MI5, offered a guarded reassurance on Monday about the risk of terrorist attacks during the 17-day spectacle in Britain. “No doubt some terrorists have thought about whether they could pull off an attack,” Jonathan Evans, MI5’s director general, told an audience in London. “But the Games are not an easy target, and the fact that we have disrupted multiple terrorist plots here and abroad in recent years demonstrates that the United Kingdom as a whole is not an easy target.” British officials will deploy a security force of more than 25,000 for the Games, and Mr. Evans cited the “extremely generous” security assistance given by “friendly countries,” an allusion to an Olympic security unit run by F.B.I. and C.I.A. officials. He said security officials had assessed the current threat of a terrorist strike in Britain as substantial, meaning that an attack is “a strong possibility.” But he noted that the threat assessment was “one notch lower” than it had been for most of the period since the 2001 attacks in the United States. “There is no such thing as guaranteed security,” he said. “But I think we shall see a successful and memorable Games.” ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Britain: Security Chief Offers Assessment About Olympics', 'kicker': 'World Briefing | Europe', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Britain: Security Chief Offers Assessment About Olympics', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Olympic Games (2012)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Terrorism', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Security Service (MI5)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Security and Warning Systems', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Olympic Games', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Evans, Jonathan', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'London (England)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-26T02:07:14+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Europe', 'byline': {'original': 'By John F. Burns', 'person': [{'firstname': 'John', 'middlename': 'F.', 'lastname': 'Burns', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/646b0408-1784-5bd0-bba1-b20a27fcbcd6', 'word_count': 214, 'uri': 'nyt://article/646b0408-1784-5bd0-bba1-b20a27fcbcd6'}, {'abstract': 'Baseball is not played on paper, which is a good thing for the Cleveland Indians, who were barely over .500 but a half-game out of first place in the American League Central before Monday night.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/06/26/sports/baseball/raw-numbers-cant-explain-how-indians-win.html', 'snippet': 'Baseball is not played on paper, which is a good thing for the Cleveland Indians, who were barely over .500 but a half-game out of first place in the American League Central before Monday night.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Cleveland Indians have lost their last three games by a collective score of 22-3. The most recent, a 7-1 defeat on Monday at Yankee Stadium, was over early. The Yankees scored two runs in each of the first three innings, and the Indians do not win slugfests.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/26/sports/YKEPNER/YKEPNER-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 330, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/06/26/sports/YKEPNER/YKEPNER-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 330}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/26/sports/YKEPNER/YKEPNER-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 819, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/26/sports/YKEPNER/YKEPNER-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1639, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'supe