rapes — harvested and pressed enough arbequina olives to bottle and sell the oil, which the state has certified as extra virgin. Chartreuse and medium-bodied, it is on the mellow side, hinting of herbs, and in the back of the palate, pepper. ', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/27/dining/27STUFF_OIL/27STUFF_OIL-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1913, 'width': 586, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/27/dining/27STUFF_OIL/27STUFF_OIL-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1913, 'width': 586, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/27/dining/27STUFF_OIL/27STUFF_OIL-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/06/27/dining/27STUFF_OIL/27STUFF_OIL-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/27/dining/27STUFF_OIL/27STUFF_OIL-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Olive Oil From a California Tribe', 'kicker': 'Food Stuff ', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A California Tribe Makes Olive Oil', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Native Americans', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Olives', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cooking and Cookbooks', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Oils and Fats', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Olive Oil', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-26T14:48:00+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Dining', 'section_name': 'Food', 'byline': {'original': 'By Florence Fabricant', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Florence', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Fabricant', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/15acbac1-7ee0-58bb-8c6d-ecd6d024ccaa', 'word_count': 111, 'uri': 'nyt://article/15acbac1-7ee0-58bb-8c6d-ecd6d024ccaa'}, {'abstract': 'Stem, a new spray nozzle from the company Quirky, draws juice directly from citrus fruit.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/06/27/dining/a-new-spray-tool-for-fresh-citrus-juice.html', 'snippet': 'Stem, a new spray nozzle from the company Quirky, draws juice directly from citrus fruit.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Love the convenience of those citrus-shaped spray bottles of lemon and lime juice, but also want the juice to be fresh? Both are possible with Stem, a spray nozzle that you insert into the fruit. Press the plunger to spritz fresh juice (even orange or grapefruit) onto fish, into a cocktail or over a salad. Stem is the latest product from Quirky, a collaborative New York company that specializes in developing “Why didn’t I think of that?” gadgets. Would-be inventors who join the Quirky community (easily done online at quirky.com) can submit ideas, and if they win the approval of other members, the company’s design and production team takes over. Quirky also handles distribution and shares profits with the inventor. ', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/27/dining/27STUFF_SPRITZ/27STUFF_SPRITZ-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1536, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/27/dining/27STUFF_SPRITZ/27STUFF_SPRITZ-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1365, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/27/dining/27STUFF_SPRITZ/27STUFF_SPRITZ-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/06/27/dining/27STUFF_SPRITZ/27STUFF_SPRITZ-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/27/dining/27STUFF_SPRITZ/27STUFF_SPRITZ-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'For a Fresh Burst of Citrus', 'kicker': 'Food Stuff', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'For a Fresh Burst of Citrus', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Citrus Fruits', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Quirky Inc', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-26T14:50:42+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Dining', 'section_name': 'Food', 'byline': {'original': 'By Florence Fabricant', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Florence', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Fabricant', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/226806a5-8b01-57fb-8a0c-cee7093611e1', 'word_count': 138, 'uri': 'nyt://article/226806a5-8b01-57fb-8a0c-cee7093611e1'}, {'abstract': 'In his latest sketch, Carl Richards makes the case for buying better things and hanging on to them for longer. It may save money in the long run.', 'web_url': 'https://bucks.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/06/26/the-case-for-spending-a-little-more-sometimes/', 'snippet': 'In his latest sketch, Carl Richards makes the case for buying better things and hanging on to them for longer. It may save money in the long run.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Carl Richards is a certified financial planner in Park City, Utah, and is the director of investor education at BAM Advisor Services. His book, “The Behavior Gap,” was published this year. His sketches are archived here on the Bucks blog. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Case for Spending a Little More Sometimes', 'kicker': 'Bucks', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Bicycles and Bicycling', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Fashion and Apparel', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Personal Finances', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-26T14:54:54+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Your Money', 'byline': {'original': 'By Carl Richards', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Carl', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Richards', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ec9476bc-b838-5695-9d13-3b991d93c07a', 'word_count': 572, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ec9476bc-b838-5695-9d13-3b991d93c07a'}, {'abstract': 'Today Well reintroduces its popular fitness training tool, Run Well, which allows readers to choose among several free running plans for long and short distances from some of the country’s top running coaches.', 'web_url': 'https://well.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/06/26/pick-your-race-and-train-with-run-well/', 'snippet': 'Today Well reintroduces its popular fitness training tool, Run Well, which allows readers to choose among several free running plans for long and short distances from some of the country’s top running coaches.', 'lead_para