': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/7404ea7d-8a64-5190-8bef-f834c2af7917', 'word_count': 1129, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7404ea7d-8a64-5190-8bef-f834c2af7917'}, {'abstract': 'In American Ballet Theater’s “Swan Lake,” Kevin McKenzie’s version casts two von Rothbarts, one in a seductive, human form and one who looks like Swamp Thing.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/06/27/arts/dance/american-ballet-theaters-swan-lake-at-met-opera-house.html', 'snippet': 'In American Ballet Theater’s “Swan Lake,” Kevin McKenzie’s version casts two von Rothbarts, one in a seductive, human form and one who looks like Swamp Thing.', 'lead_paragraph': '“Swan Lake” is about transformations. The prologue of Kevin McKenzie’s version for American Ballet Theater that began a week of performances at the Metropolitan Opera House on Monday, has several. To enchant the princess Odette and change her into a swan, the evil sorcerer von Rothbart, who looks like Swamp Thing, takes on a more seductive, human form. Mr. McKenzie manages this transformation by casting two von Rothbarts.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/27/arts/SWAN/SWAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 330, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/06/27/arts/SWAN/SWAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 330}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/27/arts/SWAN/SWAN-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 576, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/27/arts/SWAN/SWAN-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1152, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'c