ristin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Terry', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Pristin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ed57ea9c-3389-5704-8a93-f6b7fcf61293', 'word_count': 1281, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ed57ea9c-3389-5704-8a93-f6b7fcf61293'}, {'abstract': 'Another major Broadway musical is closing in what is shaping up to be a summer of housecleaning at New York theaters.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/06/27/arts/jesus-christ-superstar-and-memphis-closing.html', 'snippet': 'Another major Broadway musical is closing in what is shaping up to be a summer of housecleaning at New York theaters.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Another major Broadway musical is closing in what is shaping up to be a summer of housecleaning at New York theaters. “Memphis,” which won the Tony Award for best musical in 2010, will close at the Shubert Theater on Aug. 5 after 30 previews and 1,166 performances, the show’s producers announced on Tuesday. The musical will have had a fairly long run by today’s standards, but ticket sales have been uneven, with the show grossing a relatively modest $541,602 last week (or 44 percent of the maximum possible amount). Separately on Tuesday, a spokeswoman for the Broadway revival of “Jesus Christ Superstar” confirmed that that show would close on Sunday after a run of 24 previews and 116 performances. The musical opened in March to mixed reviews and never caught on at the box office.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '‘Jesus Christ Superstar’ and ‘Memphis’ Closing', 'kicker': 'Arts, Briefly', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '‘Jesus Christ Superstar’ And ‘Memphis’ Closing', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-26T22:34:20+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Patrick Healy', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Patrick', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Healy', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/40be323a-60ea-5b73-906d-e6ec0ef7bbaa', 'word_count': 136, 'uri': 'nyt://article/40be323a-60ea-5b73-906d-e6ec0ef7bbaa'}, {'abstract': 'The architecture firm Diller Scofidio & Renfro has designed a 14-story tower that will be Columbia University Medical Center’s new medical and graduate education building.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/06/27/arts/design/columbia-unveils-med-school-building.html', 'snippet': 'The architecture firm Diller Scofidio & Renfro has designed a 14-story tower that will be Columbia University Medical Center’s new medical and graduate education building.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The architecture firm Diller Scofidio & Renfro has designed a 14-story tower of study and social spaces that will be Columbia University Medical Center’s new medical and graduate education building in Washington Heights. The design, to be released on Wednesday, is unorthodox for a medical school not only in its verticality and sculptural treatment of exposed interiors but also, according to the architects, in its reflection of a more collaborative, team-based mode of teaching. Gensler is the architectural firm of record for the building, working with the Diller team on technical aspects of the project, including construction documents. Construction — to begin in early 2013 and take about three and a half years — will be supported by a previously announced lead gift of $50 million from Dr. P. Roy Vagelos, an alumnus of Columbia’s medical school, and his wife, Diana Vagelos. The design incorporates an auditorium; event areas; student lounges; cafes; and outdoor spaces, including a terrace with views of the Hudson River. The building is to be the central hub for the students pursuing medical and doctoral degrees at Columbia’s College of Physicians and Surgeons and will also serve Columbia’s three other medical schools and the biomedical departments of the Graduate School of Arts and Sciences.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Columbia Unveils Med School Building', 'kicker': 'Arts, Briefly', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Columbia Unveils Med School Building', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Columbia University', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Diller Scofidio & Renfro', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ARCHITECTURE', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Colleges and Universities', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-26T22:35:52+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Art & Design', 'byline': {'original': 'By Robin Pogrebin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Robin', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Pogrebin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/db4d43e2-ea6d-5473-b04d-43b50907e1fa', 'word_count': 212, 'uri': 'nyt://article/db4d43e2-ea6d-5473-b04d-43b50907e1fa'}, {'abstract': 'The country’s coal and mineral wealth is a mixed blessing: Landlocked Mongolia knows that prosperity depends on its neighbors.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/06/27/world/asia/mongolias-coal-deposits-draw-neighbors-attention.html', 'snippet': 'The country’s coal and mineral wealth is a mixed blessing: Landlocked Mongolia knows that prosperity depends on its neighbors.', 'lead_paragraph': 'TAVAN TOLGOI, Mongolia — “All you need to mine here is a shovel,” said an awe-struck Indian investment manager 