 was finally caught in 2004, the media called him the “Royal Library Man,” and his sensational crime and subsequent suicide became the subjects of a government inquiry, a radio documentary and, last year, a television mini-series.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/27/arts/ROYAL/ROYAL-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 842, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/27/arts/ROYAL/ROYAL-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1684, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/27/arts/ROYAL/ROYAL-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/06/27/arts/ROYAL/ROYAL-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/27/arts/ROYAL/ROYAL-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Swedes Find Stolen Atlas in New York', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Swedes Find Stolen Atlas In New York', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Royal Library', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Libraries and Librarians', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Sweden', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Arader, W Graham III', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Burius, Anders', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Robberies and Thefts', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-27T01:00:21+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Books', 'byline': {'original': 'By Patricia Cohen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Patricia', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cohen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/22fd73dd-2708-5bc8-b168-466e4d9a7fd0', 'word_count': 909, 'uri': 'nyt://article/22fd73dd-2708-5bc8-b168-466e4d9a7fd0'}, {'abstract': 'Using the Gilt Taste app for iPads, recipe pages can be turned with a wave of the hand.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/06/27/dining/gilt-taste-an-ipad-app-for-cooks.html', 'snippet': 'Using the Gilt Taste app for iPads, recipe pages can be turned with a wave of the hand.', 'lead_paragraph': 'SINCE tablet computers came on the market, there has been the kitchen equivalent of the space race: competition to develop technology that can keep a cook’s sticky, floury hands off the touch-screen. With its new app, the e-commerce site Gilt Groupe appears to have combined appealing recipes with a touchless interface that really works.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Help for the Gooey-Fingered', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Help for the Gooey-Fingered', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Gilt Groupe', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Recipes', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Tablet Computers', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'iPad', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cooking and Cookbooks', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'M