 {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'NBC News', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-27T01:12:35+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Media', 'byline': {'original': 'By Brian Stelter', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Brian', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Stelter', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0085b95d-9f0d-5f73-ac65-82fcf779fdc7', 'word_count': 268, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0085b95d-9f0d-5f73-ac65-82fcf779fdc7'}, {'abstract': 'The Supreme Court’s ruling prohibiting mandatory sentences of life without parole for juveniles convicted of murder is an important step in juvenile justice.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/06/27/opinion/children-in-prison-for-life.html', 'snippet': 'The Supreme Court’s ruling prohibiting mandatory sentences of life without parole for juveniles convicted of murder is an important step in juvenile justice.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Supreme Court’s ruling this week prohibiting mandatory sentences of life without parole for juveniles convicted of murder is a measured and important step in a trend in juvenile justice begun more than two decades ago. The court left open the possibility that minors under age 18 could be sentenced to life without parole — but only if the sentencing judge has made an individualized finding that such a penalty is appropriate, weighing the defendant’s characteristics and the details of the crime. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '26', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Children in Prison for Life', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Children in Prison for Life', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Juvenile Delinquency', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mandatory Sentencing', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Decisions and Verdicts', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Supreme Court', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kennedy, Anthony M', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kagan, Elena', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Roberts, John G Jr', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Editorials', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-27T01:16:00+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/d3af68a6-c4ad-5def-9756-de50529ceba6', 'word_count': 509, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d3af68a6-c4ad-5def-9756-de50529ceba6'}, {'abstract': 'Interviews with 100 people who said they had been stopped and frisked found that most felt the officers used demeaning language and tones.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/06/27/nyregion/new-york-police-leave-raw-feelings-in-stops.html', 'snippet': 'Interviews with 100 people who said they had been stopped and frisked found that most felt the officers used demeaning language and tones.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Most of the time, the officers swoop in, hornetlike, with a command to stop: “Yo! You, come here. Get against the wall.”', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/27/nyregion/27frisk-ss-slide-X3L3/27frisk-ss-slide-X3L3-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 399, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/06/27/nyregion/27frisk-ss-slide-X3L3/27frisk-ss-slide-X3L3-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 399}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'ima