BLEDON, England — Gilles Simon, a Frenchman seeded 13th and freshly elected to the ATP Player Council, told French reporters on Monday that he was opposed to women receiving equal prize money as men. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Simon, Citing Court Time, Says Women Should Not Receive Equal Prize Money', 'kicker': 'Straight Sets', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Tennis', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Open (Tennis)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Wimbledon Tennis Tournament', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Simon, Gilles', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-27T03:45:31+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Tennis', 'byline': {'original': 'By Christopher Clarey', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Christopher', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Clarey', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/25a401a8-fba1-5ffa-9a00-d88dfa6488be', 'word_count': 380, 'uri': 'nyt://article/25a401a8-fba1-5ffa-9a00-d88dfa6488be'}, {'abstract': 'Do not adjust your computer monitor. And don’t panic. This puzzle by Mike Buckley is a little … different.', 'web_url': 'https://wordplay.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/06/26/fresh-puzzle/', 'snippet': 'Do not adjust your computer monitor. And don’t panic. This puzzle by Mike Buckley is a little … different.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WEDNESDAY’S PUZZLE — O.K., so you turned to the puzzle page of your paper, or perhaps you called it up on your computer, and your first reaction was … somebody made a mistake. Am I right?', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'What Fresh Puzzle is This?', 'kicker': 'Wordplay', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Crossword Puzzles', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-27T03:58:16+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Crosswords & Games', 'byline': {'original': 'By Deb Amlen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Deb', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Amlen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9ee0ec0f-0896-50c0-a401-a19fa9e412d8', 'word_count': 290, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9ee0ec0f-0896-50c0-a401-a19fa9e412d8'}, {'abstract': 'Humans have attained a top speed of less than 28 miles per hour, while cheetahs can run more than twice as fast. Scientists set out to discover what is it about the cheetah and its particular physiology or running form that allows it to set such a blazing pace, and the lessons it can provide for athletes.', 'web_url': 'https://well.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/06/27/what-runners-can-learn-from-cheetahs/', 'snippet': 'Humans have attained a top speed of less than 28 miles per hour, while cheetahs can run more than twice as fast. Scientists set out to discover what is it about the cheetah and its particular physiology or running form that allows it to set such a...', 'lead_paragraph': 'Back in the 1960s, researchers in Africa clocked the wild cheetah as it ran and determined that at full gallop, a cheetah reached a top speed of about 65 miles per hour, making it easily the world’s fastest land mammal. No other quadruped or biped comes close. Galloping quarter horses top out around 47 miles per hour, while sluggish humans, in the person of the world record 100-meter sprinter Usain Bolt, have attained a top speed of less than 28 miles per hour. Even the bullet-trainlike greyhound, similar in build and running style to the cheetah, doesn’t surpass 40 miles per hour.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print