ad_paragraph': 'On May 3, the day after an artful deal to end the diplomatic crisis over Chen Guangcheng, China’s now-famous dissident, unraveled spectacularly, Hillary Rodham Clinton followed a scrum of Chinese ministers around an exhibition of clean cookstoves. These are safer, portable alternatives to the crude stoves used by hundreds of millions of women in the developing world — at grave risk to themselves, their children and the planet. Not long after becoming secretary of state in 2009, Clinton took up the cookstove cause, one of what she describes as “smart power” issues — though skeptical veterans of American foreign policy tend to deride them as soft more than smart.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/07/01/magazine/01clinton1/01clinton1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 401, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/07/01/magazine/01clinton1/01clinton1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 401}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/07/01/magazine/01clinton1/mag-01Clinton-t_CA0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/07/01/magazine/01clinton1/mag-01Clinton-t_CA0-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1000, 'width': 1500, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/07/01/magazine/01clinton1/01clinton1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/07/01/magazine/01clinton1/01clinton1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/07/01/magazine/01clinton1/01clinton1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Hillary Clinton’s Last Tour as a Rock-Star Diplomat', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Last Tour of the Rock-Star Diplomat', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Clinton, Hillary Rodham', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States International Relations', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'State Department', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Chen Guangcheng', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-27T09:00:13+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Steven Lee Myers', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Steven', 'middlename': 'Lee', 'lastname': 'Myers', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/67f4f135-a024-543d-99c3-49c49e9f275a', 'word_count': 5476, 'uri': 'nyt://article/67f4f135-a024-543d-99c3-49c49e9f275a'}, {'abstract': 'Alejandro Cartagena’s photos reveal the private worlds of the backs of flatbed trucks that take working Mexicans to their jobs — people seeking a normal life, and doing what it takes to get there.', 'web_url': 'https://lens.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/06/27/car-poolers/', 'snippet': 'Alejandro Cartagena’s photos reveal the private worlds of the backs of flatbed trucks that take working Mexicans to their jobs — people seeking a normal life, and doing what it takes to get there.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A bridge is situated on a highway that goes from the Mexican city of Nuevo Laredo — across the United States border in Laredo, Tex. — due south to Monterrey. In the early-morning hours last winter, Alejandro Cartagena stood there, pointing his lens down at the passing cars, like a distracted spy.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Piling in a Flatbed to Get By in the Suburbs', 'kicker': 'Lens', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Photography', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Suburbs', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Mexico', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Monterrey (Mexico)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-27T09:00:31+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Multimedia/Photos', 'byline': {'original': 'By Matt McCann', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Matt', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'McCann', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/43e5638b-92c5-5784-8815-211fed471ffc', 'word_count': 484, 'uri': 'nyt://article/43e5638b-92c5-5784-8815-211fed471ffc'}, {'abstract': 'The gesture was a remarkable sign of reconciliation for both figures, since the queen is the titular head of Britain’s armed forces — the sworn enemy of the Irish Republican Army.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/06/28/world/europe/queen-elizabeth-meets-former-ira-commander.html', 'snippet': 'The gesture was a remarkable sign of reconciliation for both figures, since the queen is the titular head of Britain’s armed forces — the sworn enemy of the Irish Republican Army.', 'lead_paragraph': 'LONDON — She wore bright green; he was somber-suited. Between them lay a gulf of history that had been beyond bridging, defined by faith and