ation targeting many of the world’s biggest banks.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Morning Take-Out', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-06-27T12:27:50+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By William Alden', 'person': [{'firstname': 'William', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Alden', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/084537fb-e819-534c-a0cb-26eaa27415d2', 'word_count': 2381, 'uri': 'nyt://article/084537fb-e819-534c-a0cb-26eaa27415d2'}, {'abstract': 'A $100 weekend in Oslo, Norway, DuPont says claims over its herbicide are rising, pick a race and train with the Run Well tool and other consumer-focused news from The New York Times.', 'web_url': 'https://bucks.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/06/27/wednesday-reading-a-100-weekend-in-oslo-norway/', 'snippet': 'A $100 weekend in Oslo, Norway, DuPont says claims over its herbicide are rising, pick a race and train with the Run Well tool and other consumer-focused news from The New York Times.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A variety of consumer-focused articles appears daily in The New York Times and on our blogs. Each weekday morning, we gather them together here so you can quickly scan the news that could hit you in your wallet.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Wednesday Reading: A $100 Weekend in Oslo, Norway', 'kicker': 'Bucks', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-06-27T12:36:37+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Your Money', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ann Carrns', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ann', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Carrns', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/667bae5f-b7fe-526f-9022-f56c139ca1df', 'word_count': 149, 'uri': 'nyt://article/667bae5f-b7fe-526f-9022-f56c139ca1df'}, {'abstract': 'The broadband era began with the expectation that Internet connections were like all-you-can-eat buffets. But as Brian Stelter reports in Wednesday’s New York Times, people are now being prodded to think about how much they’re consuming.', 'web_url': 'https://bits.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/06/27/daily-report-the-trouble-with-limits-on-broadband/', 'snippet': 'The broadband era began with the expectation that Internet connections were like all-you-can-eat buffets. But as Brian Stelter reports in Wednesday’s New York Times, people are now being prodded to think about how much they’re consuming.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The broadband era began with the expectation that Internet connections were like all-you-can-eat buffets. But as Brian Stelter reports in Wednesday’s New York Times, people are now being prodded to think about how much they’re consuming. In South Texas, Time Warner Cable customers have been given the online equivalent of a scale in the bathroom: a “usage tracker.” Customers who sign up for a light plan of 5 gigabytes of broadband are rewarded with a $5 discount each month if they don’t go over. If they do, they pay $1 for every additional gigabyte.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/11/30/technology/bits-daily-report/bits-daily-report-articleLarge-v4.png', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/11/30/technology/bits-daily-report/bits-daily-report-articleLarge-v4.png', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'ima