ain': 'How Foul is Your Favorite Beach?', 'kicker': 'Green', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sewers and Sewage', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Swimming', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Water', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Environmental Protection Agency', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Natural Resources Defense Council', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-27T14:00:55+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Science', 'subsection_name': 'Environment', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kelly Slivka', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kelly', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Slivka', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/de9e8d9b-5814-5b64-963c-0b4d94cd6385', 'word_count': 369, 'uri': 'nyt://article/de9e8d9b-5814-5b64-963c-0b4d94cd6385'}, {'abstract': 'The average per-child cost of a summer’s worth of swimming pools, day camps and babysitting? $601. How much will you spend?', 'web_url': 'https://parenting.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/06/27/16-billion-the-cost-of-summer/', 'snippet': 'The average per-child cost of a summer’s worth of swimming pools, day camps and babysitting? $601. How much will you spend?', 'lead_paragraph': 'To the agony of scheduling activities for your children this summer, add the cost: according to a survey of American families from the American Express Spending and Saving Tracker, the average per-child cost of summer fun (which can also mean child care) is $601. Affluent families (defined as those with a minimum household income of $100,000) will spend nearly double that: $1,116. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '$16 Billion: The Cost of Summer', 'kicker': 'Motherlode', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Children and Childhood', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Education (K-12)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Families and Family Life', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Parenting', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Summer (Season)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-27T14:08:50+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': "By Kj Dell'Antonia", 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kj', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': "Dell'Antonia", 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d66b1943-0269-50cf-964b-bddf9bc26fe2', 'word_count': 356, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d66b1943-0269-50cf-964b-bddf9bc26fe2'}, {'abstract': 'A collection of links from the reporters and editors of the Dining section.', 'web_url': 'https://dinersjournal.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/06/27/what-were-reading-466/', 'snippet': 'A collection of links from the reporters and editors of the Dining section.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Serious Eats: A tribute to the late Nora Ephron (right), both a greatly gifted storyteller and a lover of food. — Jeff GordinierThe New York Times: Marcus Samuelsson recounts his astonishing rise, from poverty in Ethiopia to an orphanage in Sweden to international acclaim in Manhattan — grappling with racism all along the way. “A hundred years ago,” he writes in “Yes, Chef,” his new memoir, “black men and women had to fight to get out of the kitchen. These days, we have to fight to get in.” …', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'What We’re Reading', 'kicker': 'Diner&#039;s Journal', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-06-27T14:12:39+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/43811f45-c2e0-51e8-b988-d24941ccdf46', 'word_count': 728, 'uri': 'nyt://article/43811f45-c2e0-51e8-b988-d24941ccdf46'}, {'abstract': 'Pele has called the deftly chipped penalty kick known as the Panenka the work of “either a genius or a madman,” and they have been tried by both over the years.', 'web_url': 'https://goal.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/06/27/farewell-floating-panenka-until-we-see-you-again/', 'snippet': 'Pele has called the deftly chipped penalty kick known as the Panenka the work of “either a genius or a madman,” and they have been tried by both over the years.', 'lead_paragraph': 'We have probably not seen the last of penalty kicks at Euro 2012, but it’s a good bet we’ve seen our last Panenka for a while. Because really: for all the courage and daring and confidence it took for Andrea Pirlo to pull one off against England’s Joe Hart on Sunday, it would take twice as much — five times, 10 times as much — for a player to try another less than a week later.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Farewell, Floating Panenka, Until We See You Again', 'kicker': 'Goal', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Soccer', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'UEFA European Football Championship', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-27T14:15:19+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Soccer', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andrew Das', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andrew', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Das', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 