ations', 'value': 'Berkshire Hathaway Inc', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-27T15:55:53+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By William Alden', 'person': [{'firstname': 'William', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Alden', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f429b3cb-68d7-5a94-a7d6-25e963c43139', 'word_count': 263, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f429b3cb-68d7-5a94-a7d6-25e963c43139'}, {'abstract': 'Regulators quote e-mails between traders and those who submitted rate information as part of settlements with Barclays.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2012/06/27/the-things-traders-say/', 'snippet': 'Regulators quote e-mails between traders and those who submitted rate information as part of settlements with Barclays.', 'lead_paragraph': 'How do traders talk? ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Things Traders Say', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Barclays PLC', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-27T15:56:20+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jeffrey Cane', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jeffrey', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cane', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/325ee835-4750-5e0a-873c-03b3274e52e2', 'word_count': 488, 'uri': 'nyt://article/325ee835-4750-5e0a-873c-03b3274e52e2'}, {'abstract': 'Cooper-Hewitt museum announces the design team that will help shape the visitor experience when the expanded museum re-opens in 2014.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/06/27/cooper-hewitt-picks-design-team-to-help-with-its-expansion/', 'snippet': 'Cooper-Hewitt museum announces the design team that will help shape the visitor experience when the expanded museum re-opens in 2014.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Diller Scofidio + Renfro, the architectural firm responsible for the redevelopment of Lincoln Center and the city’s High Line, has been chosen to design the gallery and visitor experience at the remodeled Cooper-Hewitt National Design Museum on the Upper East Side. Local Projects, a design firm, will also be part of the team and responsible for the museum’s digital offerings. The renovations of the museum’s Carnegie Mansion are part of a $64-million capital campaign. “This project gives Cooper-Hewitt the opportunity to re-invent itself, to rethink museum conventions and the entire museum ‘visit,’” said Caroline Baumann, associate director of the museum. “We’re delighted to be working with DS+R and Local Projects in creating a distinctly contemporary vision that will serve as a model for a new type of museum.” While the renovations are underway, Cooper-Hewitt, which is part of the Smithsonian Institution, is offering exhibitions and educational programs at the Cooper-Hewitt Design Center in Harlem, the Enid and Lester Morse Historic Design Lecture Series at the University Club, and elsewhere. The museum on East 91st Street at Fifth Avenue is scheduled to re-open in 2014. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Cooper-Hewitt Picks Design Team to Help With Its Expansion', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Cooper-Hewitt Picks Team for Expansion', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Museums', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Cooper-Hewitt National Design Museum', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Upper East Side (NYC)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-27T15:57:10+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Patricia Cohen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Patricia', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cohen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/8213c2e5-7b34-51eb-919f-18ece7351dea', 'word_count': 186, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8213c2e5-7b34-51eb-919f-18ece7351dea'}, {'abstract': 'Lynn A. Stout, a professor at Cornell Law School, says that the business principle that companies should be run for the sole purpose returning value to shareholders has worked out horribly and, as a matter of law, is not true.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2012/06/27/challenging-the-long-held-belief-in-shareholder-value/', 'snippet': 'Lynn A. Stout, a professor at Cornell Law School, says that the business principle that companies should be run for the sole purpose returning value to shareholders has worked out horribly and, as a matter of law, is not true.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It’s a bedrock principle of our era: Companies should be run for the sole purpose of increasing their stock prices, or returning “value” to shareholders, the ultimate “owners.”', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Challenging the Long-Held Belief in ‘Shareholder Value’', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Trade: Challenging the Long-Held Belief in ‘Shareholder Value’', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Boards of Directors', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Minow, Nell', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Stout, Lynn A', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-27T16:00:28+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jesse Eisinger', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jesse', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Eisinger', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3c62479d-0613-5041-b859-304f1e34c5af', 'word_count': 952, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3c62479d-0613-5041-b859-304f1e34c5af'}, {'abstract': 'Shades of gray might make a lot of difference in terms of the policy implications of the Supreme Court’s ruling, but the political implications might be different.', 'web_url': 'https://fivethirtyeight.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/06/27/details-of-health-care-ruling-could-be-lost-on-public/', 'snippet': 'Shades of gray might make a lot of difference in terms of the policy implications of the Supreme Court’s ruling, but the political implications might be different.', 'lead_paragraph': 'As The New Republic’s Jonathan Cohn notes, the Supreme Court has a wide menu of options for what it might do with President Obama’s health care bill, upon which it is expected to rule on Thursday.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Details of Health Care Ruling Could Be Lost on Public', 'kicker': 'FiveThirtyEight', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Decisions and Verdicts', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Patient Protection and Affordable Care Act (2010)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Supreme Court', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-27T16:04:11+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nate Silver', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nate', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Silver', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/426b3703-4c66-5351-818c-b21e03364538', 'word_count': 616, 'uri': 'nyt://article/426b3703-4c66-5351-818c-b21e03364538'}, {'abstract': 'A newlywed’s protest against the lack of toilets in her marital home in Uttar Pradesh.', 'web_url': 'https://india.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/06/27/image-of-the-day-june-27/', 'snippet': 'A newlywed’s protest against the lack of toilets in her marital home in Uttar Pradesh.', 'lead_paragraph': '', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Image of the Day: June 27', 'kicker': 'India Ink', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Bathrooms and Toilets', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Uttar Pradesh State (India)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-27T16:08:11+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f0615abf-f8c8-52d3-aefc-2b9c59913f40', 'word_count': 1, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f0615abf-f8c8-52d3-aefc-2b9c59913f40'}, {'abstract': 'The superhero movie is now a Hollywood staple, one corporations and advertisers want a piece of. But what is it selling?', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/07/01/movies/the-amazing-spider-man-and-the-modern-comic-book-movie.html', 'snippet': 'The superhero movie is now a Hollywood staple, one corporations and advertisers want a piece of. But what is it selling?', 'lead_paragraph': 'EVILDOERS, beware! You cannot hide from the modern-day superhero! And neither can anyone else heading to the movies this summer. “Marvel’s The Avengers” which opened on May 4, reached $300 million in domestic ticket revenue faster than any other movie in history and tied the record for fastest $1 billion worldwide gross. On Tuesday, Sony will unleash the newest version of another venerable Marvel property, “The Amazing Spider-Man,” followed later in the month by “The Dark Knight Rises,” a Warner Brothers release and the third (and possibly the last) of Christopher Nolan’s Batman movies. The summer of 2012 is hardly unusual. The current superhero boom dates to the dawn of the century — Bryan Singer’s “X-Men” came out in 2000, Sam Raimi’s “Spider-Man” in 2002 — and shows no sign of abating. The New York Times’s chief movie critics, A. O. Scott and Manohla Dargis, ponder the meaning of an apparently invincible genre. ', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/07/01/arts/01COMICS_SPAN/01JPCOMICS5-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 360, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/07/01/arts/01COMICS_SPAN/01JPCOMICS5-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 360}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/07/01/arts/01COMICS_SPAN/01JPCOMICS5-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/07/01/arts/01COMICS_SPAN/01JPCOMICS5-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1363, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/07/01/arts/01COMICS_SPAN/01JPCOMICS5-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/07/01/arts/01COMICS_SPAN/01JPCOMICS5-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/07/01/arts/01COMICS_SPAN/01JPCOMICS5-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 100, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Super-Dreams of an Alternate World Order', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Super-Dreams Of an Alternate World Order', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'The Avengers (Movie)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Comic Books and Strips', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'The Amazing Spider-Man (Movie)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-27T16:13:09+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'By Manohla Dargis and A.O. Scott', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Manohla', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Dargis', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'A.', 'middlename': 'O.', 'lastname': 'Scott', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/68721ff5-c476-5329-9a5a-d072a6096ef7', 'word_count': 2483, 'uri': 'nyt://article/68721ff5-c476-5329-9a5a-d072a6096ef7'}, {'abstract': 'Teresa A. Sullivan, the University of Virginia’s president, was reinstated.', 'web_url': 'https://thechoice.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/06/27/university-virginia-president-is-reinstated/', 'snippet': 'Teresa A. Sullivan, the University of Virginia’s president, was reinstated.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The University of Virginia’s board of trustees voted unanimously on Tuesday to reinstate president Teresa A. Sullivan, whom they had forced to resign 16 days earlier. The board had originally charged that Ms. Sullivan was not handling the escalating financial and technological pressures facing colleges rapidly enough.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'University of Virginia President Is Reinstated After Unanimous Vote', 'kicker': 'The Choice', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-06-27T16:21:54+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Daniel E. Slotnik', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Daniel', 'middlename': 'E.', 'lastname': 'Slotnik', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c85f2230-0f63-575e-bf51-a6761ce6f5e9', 'word_count': 334, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c85f2230-0f63-575e-bf51-a6761ce6f5e9'}, {'abstract': 'The Yankees’ ace C.C. Sabathia is sidelined with a strained groin muscle.', 'web_url': 'https://bats.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/06/27/sabathia-is-on-disabled-list/', 'snippet': 'The Yankees’ ace C.C. Sabathia is sidelined with a strained groin muscle.', 'lead_paragraph': 'If it were September or October, C.C. Sabathia might make his next two starts, but it was a Wednesday in June, the All-Star break less than two weeks away, so the Yankees practiced restraint and precaution and placed their ace on the 15-day disabled list.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Sabathia Is on Disabled List', 'kicker': 'Bats', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Baseball', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cashman, Brian', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Garcia, Freddy', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Girardi, Joe', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Sabathia, C C', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Mets', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Yankees', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-27T16:26:42+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Baseball', 'byline': {'original': 'By Tim Rohan', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Tim', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rohan', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/629e6d41-6e9a-5629-9d1d-e78274d9beaa', 'word_count': 337, 'uri': 'nyt://article/629e6d41-6e9a-5629-9d1d-e78274d9beaa'}, {'abstract': 'A New York regiment’s experience at the Battle of Gaines Mill, one of the bloodiest in the Civil War.', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/06/27/fighting-for-nightfall/', 'snippet': 'A New York regiment’s experience at the Battle of Gaines Mill, one of the bloodiest in the Civil War.', 'lead_paragraph': 'On the morning of June 27, 1862, the Union’s Fifth Army Corps retreated south from the hamlet of Mechanicsville, Va., and set up defensive positions near a mill owned by a local planter named Gaines. Rather than securing the rest they badly needed, the exhausted soldiers of Col. Elisha G. Marshall’s 13th New York Infantry began building breastworks. An overwhelming Confederate force was in pursuit, and Marshall’s troops steeled themselves for a delaying action that would allow the rest of the Army of the Potomac to escape.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Fighting for Nightfall', 'kicker': 'Disunion', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Civil War (US) (1861-65)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Defense and Military Forces', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lee, Robert E', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Army of the Potomac', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Rochester (NY)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-27T16:30:15+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Will Hickox', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Will', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hickox', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/543df4d5-e6f6-50bd-b8de-8412f0794e7d', 'word_count': 2066, 'uri': 'nyt://article/543df4d5-e6f6-50bd-b8de-8412f0794e7d'}, {'abstract': 'Thousands have been forced to flee the flames, which have burned 24 square miles and are only 5 percent contained.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/06/28/us/furious-wildfire-in-colorado-leaves-destruction-in-its-wake.html', 'snippet': 'Thousands have been forced to flee the flames, which have burned 24 square miles and are only 5 percent contained.', 'lead_paragraph': 'COLORADO SPRINGS — With wind and heat giving it new strength, the furious wildfire that has been burning in the foothills around Colorado’s second-largest city doubled in size by Wednesday, first roaring past containment lines and forcing tens of thousands of residents to flee, chased by billowing smoke, blowing ash and the fear that their homes might be burned to the ground.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/28/us/28wildfires2_337/28wildfires2_337-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 330, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/06/28/us/28wildfires2_337/28wildfires2_337-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 330}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credi