_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Media', 'byline': {'original': 'By Stuart Elliott', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Stuart', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Elliott', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4189e071-7259-5f69-bd8c-0f7603a1525c', 'word_count': 396, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4189e071-7259-5f69-bd8c-0f7603a1525c'}, {'abstract': 'In Michael Frayn’s novel, we are asked to believe that in the age of Google a handsome, charming gadabout could be confused, over and over, with an older, pudgy world-renowned scientist.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/06/28/books/skios-by-michael-frayn.html', 'snippet': 'In Michael Frayn’s novel, we are asked to believe that in the age of Google a handsome, charming gadabout could be confused, over and over, with an older, pudgy world-renowned scientist.', 'lead_paragraph': 'As his uproarious 1983 play “Noises Off” so nimbly demonstrated, Michael Frayn is a master of that most frantic of genres: the door-slamming, coincidence-splattered, slapstick-studded genre of farce. With his latest novel, “Skios,” Mr. Frayn tries to translate farce from the theater to the page — with somewhat mixed results. The novel is immensely entertaining, sometimes laugh-out-loud funny, but it’s also a pretty flimsy production, with an unsatisfying and jerry-built conclusion. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/28/arts/BOOK/BOOK-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 610, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/28/arts/BOOK/BOOK-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1220, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}