 Rolling Stones Tour Reveals Promoters’ Backbiting Ways', 'kicker': 'Media Decoder', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Azoff, Irving', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cohl, Michael', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Live Nation Entertainment Inc', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Rolling Stones', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-27T23:21:32+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Media', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ben Sisario', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ben', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Sisario', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/521ffb85-1e95-5dfb-8bba-0d546007a96e', 'word_count': 822, 'uri': 'nyt://article/521ffb85-1e95-5dfb-8bba-0d546007a96e'}, {'abstract': 'A coalition of labor unions and other groups critical of the practice called for an explicit ban on racial profiling and the establishment of an inspector general in the Police Department.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/06/27/citing-discrimination-in-stop-and-frisk-practice-coalition-calls-for-reforms/', 'snippet': 'A coalition of labor unions and other groups critical of the practice called for an explicit ban on racial profiling and the establishment of an inspector general in the Police Department.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Mayor Michael R. Bloomberg has recently adopted a somewhat conciliatory tone with critics of the police department’s stop-and-frisk practice, pledging that police officers will be trained to behave more courteously and promising that the number of stops will decline.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Citing Discrimination in Stop-and-Frisk Practice, Coalition Calls for Reforms', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Racial Profiling', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bloomberg, Michael R', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Police Department (NYC)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-27T23:22:37+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kate Taylor', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kate', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Taylor', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/20186c56-f2c7-5b91-ad45-b2e0f67804d2', 'word_count': 377, 'uri': 'nyt://article/20186c56-f2c7-5b91-ad45-b2e0f67804d2'}, {'abstract': 'Questions for an anthropologist and co-author of a study that documents the stresses of material culture.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/06/28/garden/an-anthropologist-on-hyper-abundance-and-the-american-home.html', 'snippet': 'Questions for an anthropologist and co-author of a study that documents the stresses of material culture.', 'lead_paragraph': 'From 2001 to 2005, a team of social scientists studied 32 middle-class families in Los Angeles, a project documenting every wiggle of life at home. The study was generated by the U.C.L.A. Center on the Everyday Lives of Families to understand how people handled what anthropologists call material culture — what we call stuff. These were dual-earner households in a range of ethnic groups, neighborhoods, incomes and occupations, with at least two children between the ages of 7 and 12 — in other words, households smack in the weeds of family life.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia':