Mining', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Glencore International AG', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Xstrata', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-28T12:21:28+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Julia Werdigier', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Julia', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Werdigier', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/821dc53b-5163-524d-96bf-1c73a73c01ca', 'word_count': 276, 'uri': 'nyt://article/821dc53b-5163-524d-96bf-1c73a73c01ca'}, {'abstract': 'Rupert Murdoch, the chairman and chief executive, confirmed Thursday that the company was spinning off its publishing division. He defended his newspaper assets, vowing to “unleash their real potential.”', 'web_url': 'https://mediadecoder.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/06/28/news-corporation-makes-it-official-two-companies/', 'snippet': 'Rupert Murdoch, the chairman and chief executive, confirmed Thursday that the company was spinning off its publishing division. He defended his newspaper assets, vowing to “unleash their real potential.”', 'lead_paragraph': '11:39 a.m. | Updated Rupert Murdoch confirmed Thursday morning that News Corporation, his $54 billion media conglomerate, will proceed with a plan to divide the company in two — separating newspapers like The Wall Street Journal, The New York Post and The Times of London from the fast-growing entertainment unit.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Murdoch, Announcing News Corp. Split, Calls Newspapers ‘Viable’ and ‘Undervalued’', 'kicker': 'Media Decoder', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mergers, Acquisitions and Divestitures', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'News and News Media', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Newspapers', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Carey, Chase', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Murdoch, Rupert', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'News Corporation', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Great Britain', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-28T12:21:55+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Media', 'byline': {'original': 'By Amy Chozick', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Amy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Chozick', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7646aada-d3cf-595a-8c99-b08f47ca89bb', 'word_count': 712, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7646aada-d3cf-595a-8c99-b08f47ca89bb'}, {'abstract': 'Metropolitan Diary: Witnessing a fight at the bus terminal, a father and daughter knew just what their priorities should be.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/06/28/dont-mess-with-the-cupcakes/', 'snippet': 'Metropolitan Diary: Witnessing a fight at the bus terminal, a father and daughter knew just what their priorities should be.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Dear Diary: My father and I recently spent a lovely day in Manhattan. We saw a matinee performance by the New York City Ballet and met good friends for an early dinner on the Upper West Side. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Don’t Mess With the Cupcakes', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Port Authority Bus Terminal (NYC)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Stations and Terminals (Passenger)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Magnolia Bakery', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Manhattan (NYC)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Upper West Side (NYC)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-28T12:30:19+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Christine Quinter', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Christine', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Quinter', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/908b717e-74a9-5a9c-a468-3532a5625ec5', 'word_count': 171, 'uri': 'nyt://article/908b717e-74a9-5a9c-a468-3532a5625ec5'}, {'abstract': 'Highlights from the DealBook newsletter.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2012/06/28/morning-take-out-515/', 'snippet': 'Highlights from the DealBook newsletter.', 'lead_paragraph': 'JPMorgan Trading Loss May Reach $9 Billion \xa0|\xa0 Losses on JPMorgan Chase’s bungled trade could total as much as $9 billion, far exceeding earlier public estimates, according to people who have been briefed on the situation.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Morning Take-Out', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-06-28T12:38:48+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By William Alden', 'person': [{'firstname': 'William', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Alden', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/391340c8-f5f9-53c7-883d-fc1666ccfac1', 'word_count': 2643, 'uri': 'nyt://article/391340c8-f5f9-53c7-883d-fc1666ccfac1'}, {'abstract': 'France pulls the plug on the Minitel, and the memoir of a receptionist for The New Yorker.', 'web_url': 'https://mediadecoder.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/06/28/the-breakfast-meeting-wow-the-world-as-two-and-romeo-in-stubble/', 'snippet': 'France pulls the plug on the Minitel, and the memoir of a receptionist for The New Yorker.', 'lead_paragraph': 'With a memo to the staff Thursday morning, Rupert Murdoch confirmed that News Corporation, the company he built from a single Australian newspaper, would split into two — as a publishing company and an entertainment company. In his memo he was seemingly ecstatic — “we will wow the world as two, as opposed to merely one.” Yet Mr. Murdoch was reluctant to make the move, Amy Chozick writes, and needed much persuasion. She writes about the logic that carried the day:', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Breakfast Meeting: ‘Wow the World as Two,’ and Romeo in Stubble', 'kicker': 'Media Decoder', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'News and News Media', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'News Corporation', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'France', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-28T13:01:40+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Media', 'byline': {'original': 'By Noam Cohen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Noam', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cohen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7555436c-58ae-57c3-9061-3f0af5159620', 'word_count': 485, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7555436c-58ae-57c3-9061-3f0af5159620'}, {'abstract': 'The Day: Since Nora Ephron’s death, the main focus has understandably been on her movies. Somewhat overlooked is what a first-rate reporter she was.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/06/28/remembering-nora-ephron-a-reporter-at-heart/', 'snippet': 'The Day: Since Nora Ephron’s death, the main focus has understandably been on her movies. Somewhat overlooked is what a first-rate reporter she was.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It was hard to resist a smile — a very slight smile, while keeping tears in check — on hearing Tuesday night that Nora Ephron had died, of an illness she hid from almost everyone.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Remembering Nora Ephron, a Reporter at Heart', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ephron, Nora', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Post', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-28T13:09:22+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Clyde Haberman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Clyde', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Haberman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/88b2d5b1-005b-5c14-a5e0-22541848053a', 'word_count': 782, 'uri': 'nyt://article/88b2d5b1-005b-5c14-a5e0-22541848053a'}, {'abstract': 'Virgin Mobile is offering the LG Optimus Elite with near-field communication, which means it can be used to pay for goods at more than 400,000 locations, at businesses ranging from gas stations to clothiers.', 'web_url': 'https://gadgetwise.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/06/28/a-phone-with-google-wallet-and-25-cash/', 'snippet': 'Virgin Mobile is offering the LG Optimus Elite with near-field communication, which means it can be used to pay for goods at more than 400,000 locations, at businesses ranging from gas stations to clothiers.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The announcement that Virgin Mobile would carry the iPhone on a no-contract plan overshadowed another nifty bit of technology the carrier was offering.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Phone With Google Wallet and $25 Cash', 'kicker': 'Gadgetwise', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Android (Operating System)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cellular Telephones', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-28T13:12:42+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'subsection_name': 'Personal Tech', 'byline': {'original': 'By Roy Furchgott', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Roy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Furchgott', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/41c79a34-5554-5200-981f-0478e782e1e4', 'word_count': 324, 'uri': 'nyt://article/41c79a34-5554-5200-981f-0478e782e1e4'}, {'abstract': 'How foul is your favorite beach, luxury doghouses, new detergent pods pose poisoning risks and other consumer-focused news from The New York Times.', 'web_url': 'https://bucks.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/06/28/thursday-reading-how-foul-is-your-favorite-beach/', 'snippet': 'How foul is your favorite beach, luxury doghouses, new detergent pods pose poisoning risks and other consumer-focused news from The New York Times.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A variety of consumer-focused articles appears daily in The New York Times and on our blogs. Each weekday morning, we gather them together here so you can quickly scan the news that could hit you in your wallet.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Thursday Reading: How Foul Is Your Favorite Beach?', 'kicker': 'Bucks', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-06-28