'person': [{'firstname': 'Carol', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Vogel', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/02639d7a-c617-580a-9d8f-aa2b2696e8da', 'word_count': 960, 'uri': 'nyt://article/02639d7a-c617-580a-9d8f-aa2b2696e8da'}, {'abstract': 'The decision by Chief Justice John G. Roberts Jr. was deeply pragmatic and saved the Affordable Health Care Act.', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/06/28/a-justice-in-chief/', 'snippet': 'The decision by Chief Justice John G. Roberts Jr. was deeply pragmatic and saved the Affordable Health Care Act.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The title “chief justice of the United States” is not in the Constitution, and neither was it in the first Judiciary Act by which Congress organized the federal courts. It came into use only casually and gradually, by the late 19th century replacing the favored “chief justice of the Supreme Court of the United States.” Even today, people often mangle the title as “chief justice of the Supreme Court.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Justice in Chief', 'kicker': 'Opinionator', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Constitution (US)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Patient Protection and Affordable Care Act (2010)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Roberts, John G Jr', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Federation of Independent Business', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Supreme Court', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'United States', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-28T21:19:46+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Linda Greenhouse', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Linda', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Greenhouse', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/82e54562-249e-59ba-b92a-44b7c28ef7a6', 'word_count': 1139, 'uri': 'nyt://article/82e54562-249e-59ba-b92a-44b7c28ef7a6'}, {'abstract': 'New York City\'s Department of Education is joining forces with 30 other school districts to demand textbooks that are in sync with the new Common Core standards. At a panel on Thursday, some school officials said companies were trying to slip materials by them, with stickers that say things like, "Common Core Ready."', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/schoolbook/2012/06/28/school-districts-team-up-to-demand-better-textbooks/', 'snippet': "New York City's Department of Education is joining forces with 30 other school districts to demand textbooks that are in sync with the new Common Core standards. At a panel on Thursday, some school officials said companies were trying to slip mate...", 'lead_paragraph': '', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'School Districts Team Up to Demand Better Textbooks', 'kicker': 'SchoolBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-06-28T21:20:03+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Education', 'byline': {'original': 'By BETH FERTIG', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Beth', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'FERTIG', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9a3e17ed-8d5c-57b7-a020-462fffb9d3b3', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9a3e17ed-8d5c-57b7-a020-462fffb9d3b3'}, {'abstract': 'The remaining mysteries of the Roberts vote and the Medicaid expansion.', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/06/28/the-two-big-questions-on-health-care/', 'snippet': 'The remaining mysteries of the Roberts vote and the Medicaid expansion.', 'lead_paragraph': 'THERE are two great mysteries about the Supreme Court’s ruling on the Affordable Care Act that befuddle commentators. First, why was Chief Justice John G. Roberts Jr. the crucial fifth vote upholding the requirement that individuals buy health insurance? And what will be the impact of declaring unconstitutional the penalty for states that decide not to expand their Medicaid programs to everyone who earns less than 133 percent of the income defined as the poverty level?', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Two Big Questions on Health Care', 'kicker': 'Opinionator', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Health Insurance and Managed Care', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Medicaid', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Medicare', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Breyer, Stephen G', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kagan, Elena', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Roberts, John G Jr', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Supreme Court', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'United States', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-28T21:20:25+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ezekiel J. Emanuel and THEODORE RUGER', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ezekiel', 'middlename': 'J.', 'lastname': 'Emanuel', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Theodore', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'RUGER', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': 