rl” is the third New York production in eight months for which the actress Sarah Steele has drawn critical acclaim playing adolescents.', 'lead_paragraph': 'AS Becky, the 17-year-old motormouth in the new Off Broadway play “Slowgirl,” Sarah Steele rips through dialogue with such scatterbrained delight — “Stuff comes into my head, and I just say it, you know? I don’t, like, check where it fits on the Appropriate Topics Scale or whatever.” — that this actress’s own poise at a recent interview was startling at first. But at 23, Ms. Steele is an old pro at exploring how troubled teenagers resort to exhibitionism to bury their vulnerabilities.', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/07/01/arts/01STEELE1/01STEELE1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 683, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/07/01/arts/01STEELE1/01STEELE1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1365, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/07/01/arts/01STEELE1/01STEELE1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/07/01/arts/01STEELE1/01STEELE1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/07/01/arts/01STEELE1/01STEELE1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 225, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Role After Role, She Cuts Years Off Her Age', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Role After Role, She Cuts Years Off Her Age', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Pierce, Greg', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ACTORS AND ACTRESSES', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Steele, Sarah', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-28T22:20:19+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Theater', 'byline': {'original': 'By Patrick Healy', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Patrick', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Healy', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/24bac8ed-ce56-5044-9bca-b412305c8b7e', 'word_count': 1072, 'uri': 'nyt://article/24bac8ed-ce56-5044-9bca-b412305c8b7e'}, {'abstract': 'The Martha Washington Hotel, built for women in 1903, was declared a landmark this year.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/07/01/realestate/streetscapes-the-martha-washington-hotel-a-hassle-free-haven.html', 'snippet': 'The Martha Washington Hotel, built for women in 1903, was declared a landmark this year.', 'lead_paragraph': 'AT the turn of the last century, single professional women had a difficult time finding places to live in which they were free from the suspicion of immoral behavior. A group of reformers responded in 1903 by building a hotel exclusively for women, the Martha Washington, at 30 East 30th Street through the block to 29 East 29th. Though quite average architecturally, it is one of New York’s newest landmarks. ', 'print_section': 'RE', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/07/01/realestate/01SCAPES/01SCAPES-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 330, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarg