p_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/29/arts/GRADY1-obit/GRADY1-obit-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/06/29/arts/GRADY1-obit/GRADY1-obit-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/29/arts/GRADY1-obit/GRADY1-obit-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Don Grady, Robbie on ‘My Three Sons,’ Is Dead at 68', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Don Grady, 68, Robbie on &#8216;My Three Sons&#8217;', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Grady, Don', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Deaths (Obituaries)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ACTORS AND ACTRESSES', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-29T00:50:21+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Television', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Vitello', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Vitello', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Obituary (Obit)', '_id': 'nyt://article/cd20ff0f-b236-577a-a2ec-11ae014eb4ec', 'word_count': 650, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cd20ff0f-b236-577a-a2ec-11ae014eb4ec'}, {'abstract': 'The soldier who killed the officer immediately turned the gun on himself and suffered a wound but was not killed and is in custody, said Col. Kevin Arata, a spokesman for the base.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/06/29/us/north-carolina-soldier-kills-officer-and-turns-gun-on-self-at-fort-bragg.html', 'snippet': 'The soldier who killed the officer immediately turned the gun on himself and suffered a wound but was not killed and is in custody, said Col. Kevin Arata, a spokesman for the base.', 'lead_paragraph': 'An Army officer was shot and killed by another soldier Thursday afternoon at the Fort Bragg military base in North Carolina, military officials said. The soldier who killed the officer immediately turned the gun on himself and suffered a wound but was not killed and is in custody, said Col. Kevin Arata, a spokesman for the base. Another soldier was also injured from a ricocheting bullet. All three are members of the 525 Battlefield Surveillance Brigade. The identities of the dead officer and the wounded soldiers were not released. Military officials said they did not yet know the motive for the shootings and did not release details about the kind of weapon that was used. Colonel Arata said security forces arrived at the scene minutes after shots were fired around 3:30 p.m. He said the base remains open. Fort Bragg, in Fayetteville, is one of the nation’s largest military bases, home to both the Army airborne forces and Special Forces. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'North Carolina: Soldier Kills Officer and Turns Gun on Self at Fort Bragg', 'kicker': 'National Briefing | South', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'North Carolina: Soldier Kills Officer And Turns Gun On Self at Fort Bragg', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Fort Bragg (NC)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Defense and Military Forces', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Murders and Attempted Murders', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Military Bases and Installations', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-29T00:53:58+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Marc Santora', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Marc', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Santora', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/cdc42a21-54e5-5860-bd1f-bc9baf0f19fa', 'word_count': 162, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cdc42a21-54e5-5860-bd1f-bc9baf0f19fa'}, {'abstract': 'Aly Raisman, who is competing for a spot on the United States women’s gymnastics team, has become a favorite of its coordinator, Martha Karolyi, because of her solid performances.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/06/29/sports/olympics/aly-raisman-us-gymnast-stays-calm-under-pressure.html', 'snippet': 'Aly Raisman, who is competing for a spot on the United States women’s gymnastics team, has become a favorite of its coordinator, Martha Karolyi, because of her solid performances.', 'lead_paragraph': 'SAN JOSE, Calif. — Four years before Aly Raisman was considered a lock for the United States Olympic gymnastics team, she was in the stands at the national championships when the M.C. asked her a question she will not soon forget: where are the Beijing Olympics going to be?', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/29/sports/sub-gymnastics/sub-gymnastics-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 677, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/29/sports/sub-gymnastics/sub-gymnastics-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1353, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/29/sports/sub-gy