ue': 'Ethics (Institutional)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'South Carolina', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Haley, Nikki R', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-29T02:19:20+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c9bea717-7743-525e-b120-f5cb24d745d3', 'word_count': 244, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c9bea717-7743-525e-b120-f5cb24d745d3'}, {'abstract': 'Iran said it had agreed with Britain to establish diplomatic interests sections in each other’s capitals, seven months after Britain nearly broke off relations with Iran.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/06/29/world/middleeast/iran-diplomatic-deal-with-britain.html', 'snippet': 'Iran said it had agreed with Britain to establish diplomatic interests sections in each other’s capitals, seven months after Britain nearly broke off relations with Iran.', 'lead_paragraph': ' Iran said it had agreed with Britain to establish diplomatic interests sections in each other’s capitals, seven months after Britain nearly broke off relations with Iran, withdrew its diplomatic staff and expelled all Iranian diplomats in response to the pillaging of the British Embassy in Tehran by Iranians protesting British economic sanctions. Iran’s foreign minister, Ali Akbar Salehi, was quoted by Press TV, a state-run Iranian broadcaster, as saying that Iran had agreed to establish an interests section at Oman’s embassy in London, and that Britain had agreed to establish an interests section at Sweden’s embassy in Tehran. A spokesman for Britain’s Foreign Office said he could not immediately confirm Mr. Salehi’s announcement. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Iran: Diplomatic Deal With Britain', 'kicker': 'World Briefing | Middle East', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Iran: Diplomatic Deal With Britain', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Great Britain', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'International Relations', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Embargoes and Economic Sanctions', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Iran', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Diplomatic Service, Embassies and Consulates', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-29T02:25:20+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Middle East', 'byline': {'original': 'By Rick Gladstone', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Rick', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gladstone', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/97990bc5-5e29-59b8-bab0-5587502ff830', 'word_count': 118, 'uri': 'nyt://article/97990bc5-5e29-59b8-bab0-5587502ff830'}, {'abstract': 'Charts of daily stock closes for some major health care businesses.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/interactive/2012/06/29/business/reaction-to-the-court.html', 'snippet': 'Charts of daily stock closes for some major health care businesses.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Charts of daily stock closes for some major health care businesses.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Reaction to the Court', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-06-29T02:36:32+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Business Day', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Interactive Feature', '_id': 'nyt://interactive/37dbe7db-a390-5f6e-9953-d40347c09b38', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://interactive/37dbe7db-a390-5f6e-9953-d40347c09b38'}, {'abstract': 'The Foreign Ministry has protested a Chinese state-owned oil company’s move to offer for exploration nine blocks in a disputed area of the South China Sea.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/06/29/world/asia/vietnam-chinas-claims-disputed.html', 'snippet': 'The Foreign Ministry has protested a Chinese state-owned oil company’s move to offer for exploration nine blocks in a disputed area of the South China Sea.', 'lead_paragraph': ' The Foreign Ministry has protested a Chinese state-owned oil company’s move to offer for exploration nine blocks in a disputed area of the South China Sea. Vietnamese officials say the area is inside Vietnam’s exclusive economic zone and is not part of China. The Chinese government has laid claim to much of the South China Sea, areas of which are believed to be rich in oil, gas and fish. The blocks were put up for bids from foreign companies last week by China National Offshore Oil Corporation, or Cnooc. Some of the blocks overlap with ones that PetroVietnam, a state-owned enterprise, has already delineated as areas under contract for exploration by foreign companies. An executive at PetroVietnam has said it will proceed with the contracts. China said this week that Cnooc had the right to offer the blocks. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Vietnam: China’s Claims Disputed', 'kicker': 'World Briefing | Asia', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Vietnam: China’s Claims Disputed', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'China National Offshore Oil', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Offshore Drilling and Exploration', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Vietnam', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Oil (Petroleum) and Gasoline', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'China', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'South China Sea', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-29T02:40:13+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Edward Wong', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Edward', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Wong', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/535432b7-057b-5cf8-bdf7-da9a690105fe', 'word_count': 141, 'uri': 'nyt://article/535432b7-057b-5cf8-bdf7-da9a690105fe'}, {'abstract': 'Israel on Thursday completed the evacuation of 30 families from five buildings in the disputed Ulpana section of Beit El, a settlement in the West Bank.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/06/29/world/middleeast/israel-settlers-evacuated.html', 'snippet': 'Israel on Thursday completed the evacuation of 30 families from five buildings in the disputed Ulpana section of Beit El, a settlement in the West Bank.', 'lead_paragraph': ' Israel on Thursday completed the evacuation of 30 families from five buildings in the disputed Ulpana section of Beit El, a settlement in the West Bank. About 15 right-wing activists barricaded themselves inside an apartment for several hours. The police removed them, arresting six, a police spokesman said. The families left peacefully after the government promised to build 300 more homes in Beit El and 500 in other West Bank settlements. The Supreme Court had ordered the buildings to be demolished by July 1 because they sit on private Palestinian land. The state has requested an extension of several months. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Israel: Settlers Evacuated', 'kicker': 'World Briefing | Middle East', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Israel: Settlers Evacuated', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'West Bank', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Palestinians', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Israel', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Israeli Settlements', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-29T02:40:14+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Middle East', 'byline': {'original': 'By Isabel Kershner', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Isabel', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kershner', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/2b7b3f14-fd49-5d7f-8415-0cb4da6672ec', 'word_count': 102, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2b7b3f14-fd49-5d7f-8415-0cb4da6672ec'}, {'abstract': 'Changing levels of support for health-care reform in Massachusetts give us some idea of what to expect now.', 'web_url': 'https://campaignstops.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/06/28/will-time-heal-health-care-wounds/', 'snippet': 'Changing levels of support for health-care reform in Massachusetts give us some idea of what to expect now.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When the Supreme Court declared the Affordable Care Act’s provision for an individual mandate to buy medical insurance constitutional, the majority did so by placing the mandate within Congress’s power to tax rather than under the Commerce clause. While the decision keeps most of the law intact, what does it mean politically? And how will the characterization of the mandate as a tax play among supporters and opponents of the legislation?', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Will Time Heal Health Care Wounds?', 'kicker': 'Campaign Stops', 'content_ki