ganizations', 'value': 'Senate', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'House of Representatives', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Roberts, John G Jr', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ginsburg, Ruth Bader', 'rank': 12, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Romney, Mitt', 'rank': 13, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Editorials', 'rank': 14, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Medicaid', 'rank': 15, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-29T03:08:56+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/7cfec1f6-ae72-52e6-b5cc-9c9486860e39', 'word_count': 1177, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7cfec1f6-ae72-52e6-b5cc-9c9486860e39'}, {'abstract': 'The 36-year-old Jason Lezak got a spot in the finals at the Olympic trials, thanks to a decision by Ryan Lochte.', 'web_url': 'https://london2012.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/06/28/swim-veteran-lezak-still-has-a-chance/', 'snippet': 'The 36-year-old Jason Lezak got a spot in the finals at the Olympic trials, thanks to a decision by Ryan Lochte.', 'lead_paragraph': '5:48 p.m. | Updated OMAHA — Outside of Michael Phelps and Ryan Lochte, arguably the most recognizable swimmer at this week’s United States Olympic trials is Jason Lezak, who snatched a victory from apparent defeat in the 400-meter freestyle relay at the 2008 Olympics with a sizzling anchor leg.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Swim Veteran Lezak Still Has a Chance', 'kicker': '2012 London Olympics', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Olympic Games (2012)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Swimming', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lezak, Jason', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lochte, Ryan', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Omaha (Neb)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-29T03:10:34+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Olympics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Karen Crouse', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Karen', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Crouse', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/1429b4ab-e232-5a46-ba8a-2b12d2f7ee88', 'word_count': 303, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1429b4ab-e232-5a46-ba8a-2b12d2f7ee88'}, {'abstract': 'After claiming that the health care law is terrible for the country, Mitt Romney didn’t give much of an explanation of what he’d do differently other than repeal it.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/06/29/opinion/the-medical-care-ruling-the-right-responds.html', 'snippet': 'After claiming that the health care law is terrible for the country, Mitt Romney didn’t give much of an explanation of what he’d do differently other than repeal it.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The future of the health care system is now back where it should be, out of the hands of judges and into those of voters. With the constitutional question about President Obama’s health care law now settled, the public will have a chance to examine Mitt Romney’s claims that it is terrible for the country. Based on his remarks on Thursday, they will not have much to go on.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '24', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Muddled Political Response From the Right', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Court and Medical Care: A muddled political response from the right', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Supreme Court', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'na