 China’s elderly rights law that would require children to “often visit,” or “send respects to,” aging parents.', 'lead_paragraph': 'BEIJING — When did you last call your mom and dad? How often do you visit? ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Visit Your Parents — In China, It Could Soon Be the Law', 'kicker': 'IHT Rendezvous', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-06-29T05:44:36+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'World', 'byline': {'original': 'By DIDI KIRSTEN TATLOW', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Didi', 'middlename': 'Kirsten', 'lastname': 'TATLOW', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f99a4d04-55bf-5494-95d5-da504ec4de0f', 'word_count': 664, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f99a4d04-55bf-5494-95d5-da504ec4de0f'}, {'abstract': 'A dispatch from Kashmir from a photographer chronicling both sides of conflicts around the world.', 'web_url': 'https://dotearth.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/06/29/postcard-from-kashmir/', 'snippet': 'A dispatch from Kashmir from a photographer chronicling both sides of conflicts around the world.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Nelson Guda, a world-wandering photographer, artist and writer (with a doctorate in biology), sent me a note early this week from Kashmir, the beautiful and turbulent patch of territory caught for decades in a border dispute between India and Pakistan. Tensions were heightened when a revered Muslim shrine was destroyed in a fire on Monday. (The cause is still in dispute.) Guda is there for his Enemies Project, in which he is photographing people on opposite sides of conflicts around the world. I invited him to send a Dot Earth “Postcard,” which is posted below with more background and news links: ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Postcard from Kashmir', 'kicker': 'Dot Earth', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Muslims and Islam', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Photography', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Qadir, Abdul', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'India', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Kashmir and Jammu (India)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Pakistan', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-29T05:48:37+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andrew C. Revkin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andrew', 'middlename': 'C.', 'lastname': 'Revkin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9772dc95-85eb-52bd-97ea-fb89b9400c01', 'word_count': 896, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9772dc95-85eb-52bd-97ea-fb89b9400c01'}, {'abstract': 'Parents in India work hard to get their children vaccinated, while in the U.S. some resist vaccines.', 'web_url': 'https://india.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/06/29/seeking-and-resisting-vaccines/', 'snippet': 'Parents in India work hard to get their children vaccinated, while in the U.S. some resist vaccines.', 'lead_paragraph': '“It’s hard to argue with the premise that in the United States, some parents take lightly their ability to vaccinate children against childhood diseases,” KJ Dell’antonia wrote in the Motherlode blog of The New York Times. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Seeking, and Resisting, Vaccines', 'kicker': 'India Ink', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Children and Childhood', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Parenting', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': "United Nations Children's Fund", 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'India', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-29T05:58:26+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/41814109-26cb-5af8-ae7d-92337a8e32f8', 'word_count': 265, 'uri': 'nyt://article/41814109-26cb-5af8-ae7d-92337a8e32f8'}, {'abstract': 'Paul Swangard, the “Voice of Heyward Field,” discussed some of the finer points of track and field announcing.', 'web_url': 'https://london2012.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/06/29/the-voice-of-track-and-field/', 'snippet': 'Paul Swangard, the “Voice of Heyward Field,” discussed some of the finer points of track and field announcing.', 'lead_paragraph': 'EUGENE, Ore. — Since the early 1990s, Paul Swangard has been known as the “Voice of Hayward Field,” announcing a medley of University of Oregon track meets and championships. He is wielding the microphone during the United States Olympic trials here this week. When he is not tucked away in his booth in the back of Hayward’s stands, Swangard works as managing director of Oregon’s Warsaw Sports Marketing Center. He discussed the often-overlooked world of track and field announcing.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Voice of Track and Field', 'kicker': '2012 London Olympics', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Olympic Games', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Track and Field', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Hayward Field (Eugene, Ore)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'University of