1/books/review/white-bread-by-aaron-bobrow-strain.html', 'snippet': 'A history of baking and dietary reform tells how white bread, once a symbol of American progress, became “white trash.”', 'lead_paragraph': '“And the house of Israel called its name Manna: and it was like coriander seed, white; and the taste of it like wafers made with honey.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '19', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/07/01/books/review/01ADLER/01ADLER-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 375, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/07/01/books/review/01ADLER/01ADLER-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 375}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/07/01/books/review/01ADLER/01ADLER-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 898, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/07/01/books/review/01ADLER/01ADLER-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1795, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/07/01/books/review/01ADLER/01ADLER-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/07/01/books/review/01ADLER/01ADLER-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/07/01/books/review/01ADLER/01ADLER-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Against the Grain', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Against the Grain', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Diet and Nutrition', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bobrow-Strain, Aaron', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Food', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Bread', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-29T16:07:35+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Tamar Adler', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Tamar', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Adler', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/3f04903b-ee9f-5acc-a162-b945fff7377d', 'word_count': 1248, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3f04903b-ee9f-5acc-a162-b945fff7377d'}, {'abstract': 'Starting in the 1830s, a group of wealthy Englishmen attempted to establish a replica of home on the American plains.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/07/01/books/review/prairie-fever-by-peter-pagnamenta.html', 'snippet': 'Starting in the 1830s, a group of wealthy Englishmen attempted to establish a replica of home on the American plains.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It is in names like Victoria, Rugby and Runnymede (a dinky little community long since buried beneath a sea of Kansas wheat) that modern travelers across the great plains of the Midwest can still catch a glimpse of the lost — and deeply weird — world that has been lovingly excavated and brought back to life in “Prairie Fever,” by Peter Pagnamenta, a writer and documentary producer for the BBC.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '17', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/07/01/books/review/01seymour-img/01seymour-img-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 349, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/07/01/books/review/01seymour-img/01seymour-img-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 349}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/07/01/books/review/01seymour-img/01seymour-img-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 596, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/07/01/books/review/01seymour-img/01seymour-img-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 853, 'width': 1466, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/07/01/books/review/01seymour-img/01seymour-img-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/07/01/books/review/01seymour-img/01seymour-img-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/07/01/books/review/01seymour-img/01seymour-img-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 87, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Little Estate on the Prairie', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Little Estate on the Prairie', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Pagnamenta, Peter', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-29T16:07:55+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Miranda Seymour', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Miranda', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Seymour', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/01762afc-2bd1-5b10-8c5d-5b620e9b455f', 'word_count': 995, 'uri': 'nyt://article/01762afc-2bd1-5b10-8c5d-5b620e9b455f'}, {'abstract': 'A college love returns in Christopher R. Beha’s first novel.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/07/01/books/review/what-happened-to-sophie-wilder-by-christopher-r-beha.html', 'snippet': 'A college love returns in Christopher R. Beha’s first novel.', 'lead_paragraph': 'One of the pleasures of Christopher R. Beha’s 2009 memoir, “The Whole Five Feet: What the Great Books Taught Me About Life, Death, and Pretty Much Everything Else,” in which he sets out to spend a year reading the Harvard Classics, is being diverted, along with him, into another, much more personal project. “The book I intended to write,” he tells us, “was essentially a comedy, about a feckless, somewhat lost young man who shuts himself away from the modern world.” But when he learned that his adored aunt had terminal cancer, his reaction to her suffering created a very different book: his love for her became both his subject and his inspiration. On the way to a radiation treatment, when he asked permission to write about her, she gave her approval. “Hearing the pleasure in her voice,” Beha confesses, “was one of my happiest moments. . . . I allowed myself to think that, no matter what happened . . . I could save her in some way if I got everything down just right on the page.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/07/01/books/review/Towers/Towers-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 871, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/07/01/books/review/Towers/Towers-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1742, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/07/01/books/review/Towers/Towers-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/07/01/books/review/Towers/Towers-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/07/01/books/review/Towers/Towers-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Ex and the City', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Ex and the City', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Beha, Christopher R', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-29T16:08:36+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sarah Towers', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sarah', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Towers', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/d1391e3b-6fc4-5bed-9e12-c66e23ebd9ef', 'word_count': 963, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d1391e3b-6fc4-5bed-9e12-c66e23ebd9ef'}, {'abstract': 'The young homesteader at the center of Anna Keesey’s first novel is entangled in a range war.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/07/01/books/review/little-century-a-novel-by-anna-keesey.html', 'snippet': 'The young homesteader at the center of Anna Keesey’s first novel is entangled in a range war.', 'lead_paragraph': 'There are staples we’ve come to expect in the American frontier novel: unlikely homesteaders, stoic farmhands and scheming railroad men; unchecked ambitions, mysterious pasts and vigilante justice. Winter always seems to be looming. Or summer. Somebody will probably divulge a secret to a horse or a child. Mishandled, these western standbys can seem like threadbare, dime-\xadnovel clichés. Handled deftly, these conventions are the stuff of American myth. The line is finer than one might think. While Anna Keesey’s first novel, “Little Century,” may walk a well-worn path, the familiar is rendered vividly through fluid and restrained prose, solid plotting and a keen eye for detail.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '16', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/07/01/books/review/01EVISON/01EVISON-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 790, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/07/01/books/review/01EVISON/01EVISON-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1580, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/07/01/books/review/01EVISON/01EVISON-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/07/01/books/review/01EVISON/01EVISON-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/07/01/books/review/01EVISON/01EVISON-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', '