ouac, and new books by Annalena McAfee, Anne Korkeakivi, Delia Ephron and Rosie Sultan.', 'lead_paragraph': ' THE SEA IS MY BROTHER  By Jack Kerouac.  Da Capo, $23.  ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/07/01/books/review/McCulloch/McCulloch-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 683, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/07/01/books/review/McCulloch/McCulloch-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1365, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/07/01/books/review/McCulloch/McCulloch-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/07/01/books/review/McCulloch/McCulloch-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/07/01/books/review/McCulloch/McCulloch-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Fiction Chronicle', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Fiction Chronicle', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kelly, Ellsworth', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kerouac, Jack', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'McAfee, Annalena', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Korkeakivi, Anne', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Sultan, Rosie', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ephron, Delia', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-29T16:09:42+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Alison McCulloch', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Alison', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'McCulloch', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/f9404af5-2284-5296-9bab-66826fa2c27d', 'word_count': 1133, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f9404af5-2284-5296-9bab-66826fa2c27d'}, {'abstract': 'In Jeffery Deaver’s thriller “XO,” a country-pop star is stalked by an obsessive fan.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/07/01/books/review/xo-by-jeffery-deaver-and-more.html', 'snippet': 'In Jeffery Deaver’s thriller “XO,” a country-pop star is stalked by an obsessive fan.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Jeffery Deaver is a man of many skills. And XO (Simon & Schuster, $26.99), a heart-in-mouth thriller about a country-pop star being stalked by an obsessive fan, owes a great deal to one of them — his previous career as a folk musician, a vocation Deaver hasn’t entirely given up. He wrote the lyrics to the country-\xadwestern songs that figure in the story, and he’s given his informed passion for country music to many of his characters.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '23', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/07/01/books/review/0701-CRIME/0701-CRIME-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 935, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/07/01/books/review/0701-CRIME/0701-CRIME-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1869, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/07/01/books/review/0701-CRIME/0701-CRIME-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/07/01/books/review/0701-CRIME/0701-CRIME-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/07/01/books/review/0701-CRIME/0701-CRIME-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Hopelessly Devoted', 'kicker': 'Crime', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Hopelessly Devoted', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Estleman, Loren D', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Zimmerman, Jean', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Deaver, Jeffery', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-29T16:09:44+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Marilyn Stasio', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Marilyn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Stasio', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/17db4dd1-8da2-5379-a5ec-67a8e3be5357', 'word_count': 903, 'uri': 'nyt://article/17db4dd1-8da2-5379-a5ec-67a8e3be5357'}, {'abstract': 'With the rift between North and South widening, the Compromise of 1850 both staved off a civil war and made one inevitable.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/07/01/books/review/americas-great-debate-by-fergus-m-bordewich.html', 'snippet': 'With the rift between North and South widening, the Compromise of 1850 both staved off a civil war and made one inevitable.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Fergus M. Bordewich has written a lively, attractive book about a fearsome and almost intractable crisis: the tangle of issues involving expansion and slavery that confronted the political class of the United States in 1850. Sectional passions ran so high then that there was a real danger of secession, perhaps even civil war. But thanks to two senators — Henry Clay and Stephen Douglas — and numerous bit players, Congress adopted a package of compromises that allowed the country to stumble along in one piece for 11 more years.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '20', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/07/01/books/review/01brookhiser-img/01brookhiser-img-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 352, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/07/01/books/review/01brookhiser-img/01brookhiser-img-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 352}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/07/01/books/review/01brookhiser-img/01brookhiser-img-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 600, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/07/01/books/review/01brookhiser-img/01brookhiser-img-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 600, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/07/01/books/review/01brookhiser-img/01brookhiser-img-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/07/01/books/review/01brookhiser-img/01brookhiser-img-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/07/01/books/review/01brookhiser-img/01brookhiser-img-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Defending the Union', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Defending the Union', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bordewich, Fergus M', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Slavery', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Douglas, Stephen A', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Clay, Henry', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Civil War (US) (1861-65)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-29T16:10:29+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Richard Brookhiser', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Richard', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Brookhiser', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/75ef8988-424e-576e-aa43-3a52f36b3957', 'word_count': 1205, 'uri': 'nyt://article/75ef8988-424e-576e-aa43-3a52f36b3957'}, {'abstract': 'Allen C. Guelzo details the arguments behind the Civil War.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/07/01/books/review/fateful-lightning-by-allen-c-guelzo.html', 'snippet': 'Allen C. Guelzo details the arguments behind the Civil War.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Civil War fever is raging. Last year, the 150th anniversary of the outbreak of the war, brought a number of war-related books, ranging from microstudies of a specific time or theme (including Adam Goodheart’s “1861: The Civil War Awakening,” and Amanda Foreman’s “World on Fire: Britain’s Crucial Role in the American Civil War”) to macro-\xadoverviews of the whole era, most notably David Goldfield’s “America Aflame: How the Civil War Created a Nation.” The micro/\xadmacro phenomenon continues this year, with the appearance of a single-theme book like Fergus M. Bordewich’s “America’s Great Debate” (see the opposite page) and, from the macro perspective, Allen C. Guelzo’s sweeping “Fateful Lightning: A New History of the Civil War and Reconstruction.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '21', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/07/01/books/review/01REYNOLDS/01REYNOLDS-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 645, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/07/01/books/review/01REYNOLDS/01REYNOLDS-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1290, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/07/01/books/review/01REYNOLDS/01REYNOLDS-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/07/01/books/review/01REYNOLDS/01REYNOLDS-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/07/01/books/review/01REYNOLDS/01REYNOLDS-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Destroying the Union', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Destroying the Union', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Slavery', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Guelzo, Allen C', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Reconstruction Era', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Civil War (US) (1861-65)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-29T16:10:38+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By David S. Reynolds', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': 'S.', 'lastname': 'Reynolds', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/69036c87-fa88-595f-a093-55e6e25c22b8', 'word_count': 1154, 'uri': 'nyt://article/69036c87-fa88-595f-a093-55e6e25c22b8'}, {'abstract': 'For Dave Eggers, who addresses the decline of American manufacturing in his best-selling novel “A Hologram for the King,” it felt right to ensure the books were printed in the United States.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/07/08/books/review/inside-the-list.html', 'snippet': 'For Dave Eggers, who addresses the decline of American manufacturing in his best-selling novel “A Hologram for the King,” it felt right to ensure the books were printed in the United States.', 'lead_paragraph': 'MADE IN AMERICA: Dave Eggers’s new novel, “A Hologram for the King,” cracks the hardcover fiction list this week at No. 16. The book (our review is coming soon) is about an American businessman hoping to land a big deal in Saudi Arabia, and one of its recurring themes is the decline of American manufacturing. So it’s noteworthy that Eggers — who publishes his own books, through his McSweeney’s publishing house — devotes a paragraph of his acknowledgments to the staff of Thomson-Shore printers in Dexter, Mich., where the novel was produced.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/07/08/books/review/08list-img/08list-img-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 286, 'width': 275, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/07/08/books/review/08list-img/08list-img-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 286, 'width': 275, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/07/08/books/review/08list-img/08list-img-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/07/08/books/review/08list-img/08list-img-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/07/08/books/review/08list-img/08list-img-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Inside the List', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'TBR: Inside the List', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-29T16:11:09+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Gregory Cowles', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Gregory', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cowles', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/708a11db-2d11-5778-