': 'https://well.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/06/29/bread-pudding-for-dinner-or-dessert/', 'snippet': 'In this week’s Recipes for Health, Martha Rose Shulman offers five new ways to make sweet, savory and satisfying bread puddings.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I still remember the first time I tasted bread pudding — a heavenly concoction conjured up at Commander’s Palace in New Orleans. But it was only recently that I learned how easy it is to make bread pudding at home after trying a chocolate-pumpkin bread pudding recipe from the vegan chef Chloe Coscarelli. (Coconut milk makes it irresistible.)', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Bread Pudding for Dinner or Dessert', 'kicker': 'Well', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Bread', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cooking and Cookbooks', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Recipes', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-29T17:37:25+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Science', 'section_name': 'Health', 'byline': {'original': 'By Tara Parker-Pope', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Tara', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Parker-Pope', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/019177bf-73f3-5cf6-bec0-34e0e91f578d', 'word_count': 374, 'uri': 'nyt://article/019177bf-73f3-5cf6-bec0-34e0e91f578d'}, {'abstract': 'After more than a decade, students at a community college in Lower Manhattan will no longer have to take classes inside a trailer.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/06/29/cuny-building-at-ground-zero-nears-completion-after-11-years/', 'snippet': 'After more than a decade, students at a community college in Lower Manhattan will no longer have to take classes inside a trailer.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It took much less time to build the second Fiterman Hall than it took to tear down the first one.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'CUNY Building at Ground Zero Nears Completion After 11 Years', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Colleges and Universities', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'September 11 (2001)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Borough of Manhattan Community College', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'City University of New York', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-29T17:38:30+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By David W. Dunlap', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': 'W.', 'lastname': 'Dunlap', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/803aadb9-17f6-54b7-9214-b2d15849ecf4', 'word_count': 745, 'uri': 'nyt://article/803aadb9-17f6-54b7-9214-b2d15849ecf4'}, {'abstract': 'Contractors will pay $5.4 million to women who can demonstrate that they were discriminated against in hiring for cleanup jobs.', 'web_url': 'https://green.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/06/29/settlement-in-gulf-spill-cleanup-dispute/', 'snippet': 'Contractors will pay $5.4 million to women who can demonstrate that they were discriminated against in hiring for cleanup jobs.', 'lead_paragraph': 'During the oil spill in the Gulf of Mexico in 2010, I walked along mucky beaches in Louisiana watching cleanup and rescue crews scrape down the sand and hunt for oil-soaked wildlife. The crews’ effectiveness was questioned at the time, but from a logistical standpoint the vast deployment seemed impressive. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Settlement in Gulf Spill Cleanup Dispute', 'kicker': 'Green', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Accidents and Safety', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Discrimination', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Gulf of Mexico Oil Spill (2010)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Oil (Petroleum) and Gasoline', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'BP Plc', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Equal Employment Opportunity Commission', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'ALABAMA', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Florida', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Gulf of Mexico', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Louisiana', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Mississippi', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Texas', 'rank': 12, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-29T17:41:51+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Science', 'subsection_name': 'Environment', 'byline': {'original': 'By Leslie Kaufman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Leslie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kaufman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/63cd6432-5799-5c42-9992-343aff5717eb', 'word_count': 186, 'uri': 'nyt://article/63cd6432-5799-5c42-9992-343aff5717eb'}, {'abstract': 'The police said that in 2010 the singer, Randy Blythe, had tussled with a fan who tried to leap on the stage while the group was pounding through a heavy-metal set at Club Abaton in Prague. The man later died of his injuries.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/06/29/singer-in-u-s-metal-band-arrested-in-fans-death-in-czech-republic/', 'snippet': 'The police said that in 2010 the singer, Randy Blythe, had tussled with a fan who tried to leap on the stage while the group was pounding through a heavy-metal set at Club Abaton in Prague. The man later died of his injuries.', 'lead_paragraph': ' The lead singer with the metal band Lamb of God has been arrested on manslaughter charges in the Czech Republic in connection with the death of a fan who jumped onstage two years ago during a concert, according to The Associated Press.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Singer in U.S. Metal Band Arrested in Fan’s Death in Czech Republic', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Lamb of God', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-29T17:43:31+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By James C. McKinley Jr', 'person': [{'firstname': 'James', 'middlename': 'C.', 'lastname': 'McKinley', 'qualifier': 'Jr', 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/1dcabc29-1525-5fa4-b007-d6ac34b75614', 'word_count': 260, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1dcabc29-1525-5fa4-b007-d6ac34b75614'}, {'abstract': 'The fact that the famed philosopher was born in Switzerland, not France, has not kept the citizens of the Oise, the region north of Paris where Rousseau lived briefly and died, from celebrating the philosopher all year as a national hero.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/06/29/rousseaumania-runs-rampant-in-france/', 'snippet': 'The fact that the famed philosopher was born in Switzerland, not France, has not kept the citizens of the Oise, the region north of Paris where Rousseau lived briefly and died, from celebrating the philosopher all year as a national hero.', 'lead_paragraph': 'ERMENONVILLE, France—– Jean-Jacques Rousseau may have been born in Switzerland (Geneva in fact, in 1712) and not in France. But that tiny geographic detail has not kept the citizens of the Oise, the region north of Paris where Rousseau lived briefly and died, from celebrating the philosopher all year as a national hero.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Rousseaumania Runs Rampant in France', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': "Women's Rights", 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Rousseau, Jean-Jacques', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-29T17:45:14+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Elaine Sciolino', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Elaine', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Sciolino', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/48f9d399-d019-5e16-8827-1809a6e574c2', 'word_count': 530, 'uri': 'nyt://article/48f9d399-d019-5e16-8827-1809a6e574c2'}, {'abstract': 'Roger Federer rallied from two sets down to defeat the 29th-seeded Julien Benneteau in the third round. Novak Djokovic, Maria Sharapova and Kim Clijsters were among the other winners Friday.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/06/30/sports/tennis/day-after-nadals-loss-djokovic-restores-order.html', 'snippet': 'Roger Federer rallied from two sets down to defeat the 29th-seeded Julien Benneteau in the third round. Novak Djokovic, Maria Sharapova and Kim Clijsters were among the other winners Friday.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WIMBLEDON, England — On the eve of his match against Roger Federer, Julien Benneteau tuned into the biggest upset at Wimbledon in 25 years and thought to himself, “Why not me?” Why couldn’t he knock the tennis world off its axis, as Lukas Rosol did on Thursday with a stunning victory against Rafael Nadal? Why couldn’t Benneteau topple Federer, as he did the last time they played, two and a half years ago in a hardcourt tournament in Paris?', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/30/sports/WIMBLEDON/WIMBLEDON-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 263, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/06/30/sports/WIMBLEDON/WIMBLEDON-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 263}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/30/sports/WIMBLEDON/WIMBLEDON-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 449, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/30/sports/WIMBLEDON/WIMBLEDON-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 898, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/30/sports/WIMBLEDON/WIMBLEDON-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/06/30/sports/WIMBLEDON/WIMBLEDON-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/06/30/sports/WIMBLEDON/WIMBLEDON-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Second Shocker Lurks, but Federer Slips By', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Second Shocker Lurks, but Federer Slips By', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Clijsters, Kim', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Wimbledon Tennis Tournament', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Tennis', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Federer, Roger', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Benneteau, Julien', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Sharapova, Maria', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Djokovic, Novak', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-29T17:51:51+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Tennis', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ben Shpigel', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ben', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Shpigel', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5dc5b91c-1ec8-59cb-8ae4-4b1a2b983c9f', 'word_count': 1020, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5dc5b91c-1ec8-59cb-8ae4-4b1a2b983c9f'}, {'abstract': 'And no, yesterday’s decision isn’t a big long-term victory for conservatives.', 'web_url': 'https://douthat.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/06/29/yes-liberals-won/', 'snippet': 'And no, yesterday’s decision isn’t a big long-term victory for conservatives.', 'lead_paragraph': 'There was a widespread and bipartisan impulse, in the wake of yesterday’s health care ruling, to cast John Roberts’ exercise in political finesse as a potentially significant long-term win for conservatism. Variants of this case were made by George Will and\xa0Jay Cost\xa0on the right, Jonathan Chait\xa0and Tom Scocca\xa0on the liberal side of things, and many others besides. By affirming a limited and limiting reading of the Congress’s Commerce Clause powers even as he upheld the individual mandate for other reasons, Will wrote, the Chief Justice “rejected a constitutional rationale for the mandate … that was pregnant with rampant statism,” and laid the groundwork for a future in which the judiciary returns to its pre-New Deal habit of “viewing congressional actions with a skeptical constitutional squint, searching for congruence with the Constitution’s architecture of enumerated powers.” From the left,\xa0Chait made a version of the same point, worrying that a Roberts-led Court would now be able to advance a much more limited understanding of federal power by “a process of slow constriction,” rather than “one suddenly and transparently partisan attack.” The right lost the battle, these and other writers agreed, but thanks to Roberts’ savvy it could well be on its way to winning the larger size-of-government war.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Yes, Liberals Won', 'kicker': 'Ross Douthat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-06-29T17:53:19+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ross Douthat', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ross', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Douthat', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/05748212-dbf1-54a8-bc30-e1840a3e3385', 'word_count': 637, 'uri': 'nyt://article/05748212-dbf1-54a8-bc30-e1840a3e3385'}, {'abstract': 'The U.S. women’s field hockey team played four games against Argentina in preparation for the Olympics. They were practice games but definitely did not lack intensity.', 'web_url': 'https://london2012.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/06/29/the-field-hockey-chronicles-a-warmup-with-some-fire/', 'snippet': 'The U.S. women’s field hockey team played four games against Argentina in preparation for the Olympics. They were practice games but definitely did not lack intensity.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Michelle Kasold, a striker, has been a member of the United States field hockey team since 2006 and will compete in her first Olympics in London this summer. She will be writing regularly for the 2012 London Olympics blog. This is her second post.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Field Hockey Chronicles: A Warm-Up With Some Fire', 'kicker': '2012 London Olympics', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hockey, Field', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Olympic Games (2012)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-29T18:00:13+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Olympics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michelle Kasold', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michelle', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kasold', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/bf68e63d-95cc-54c6-9851-29e7d259e3f1', 'word_count': 600, 'uri': 'nyt://article/bf68e63d-95cc-54c6-9851-29e7d259e3f1'}, {'abstract': 'Women are the biggest social media users, but have less influence in online punditry. What gives?', 'web_url': 'https://kristof.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/06/29/new-medias-old-problem/', 'snippet': 'Women are the biggest social media users, but have less influence in online punditry. What gives?', 'lead_paragraph': 'Women dominate social media. They’re the biggest and most engaged users of social networking sites in America, according to the Pew Research Center. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'New Media’s Old Problem', 'kicker': 'Nicholas Kristof', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-06-29T18:02:28+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'se