, 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'State Legislatures', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-30T01:33:14+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kate Zernike', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kate', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Zernike', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/96dd3c4a-9a0f-5b28-b7e1-9f1caa9728d0', 'word_count': 525, 'uri': 'nyt://article/96dd3c4a-9a0f-5b28-b7e1-9f1caa9728d0'}, {'abstract': 'Every Sunday for the past 27 years, Ina Garten, the “Barefoot Contessa,” has had a massage to decompress.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/07/01/nyregion/for-ina-garten-the-barefoot-contessa-oatmeal-and-a-massage-on-sundays.html', 'snippet': 'Every Sunday for the past 27 years, Ina Garten, the “Barefoot Contessa,” has had a massage to decompress.', 'lead_paragraph': '  When Ina Garten left her job as a nuclear energy budget analyst for the Carter administration to open her tiny specialty food shop, the Barefoot Contessa, in Westhampton, N.Y., in 1978, she began a domestic-arts empire that is still evolving. Ms. Garten, 64, has been the star of the Emmy-winning “Barefoot Contessa” on the Food Network since 2002; in 2006 she formulated a line of designer edibles, known as Barefoot Contessa Pantry, with her business partner, Frank Newbold. Ms. Garten, the author of many cookbooks, lives in East Hampton with her husband, Jeffrey Garten, 65, a professor of business at Yale; the couple also have apartments in New York and Paris.', 'print_section': 'MB', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/07/01/nyregion/01ROUTINE1_SPAN/01ROUTINE1_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 360, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop