.S. futsal team on the road to the FIFA Futsal World Cup in Thailand later this year.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Futsal: U.S. Set for World Cup Qualifying', 'kicker': 'Goal', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Soccer', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'International Federation of Association Football (FIFA)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Guatemala', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-06-30T17:01:42+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Soccer', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e7cdb3a1-4751-545b-95cf-846d78e1f854', 'word_count': 198, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e7cdb3a1-4751-545b-95cf-846d78e1f854'}, {'abstract': 'A return to prosperity for a war-ravaged country is welcome, but economists fear growth of a consumer culture masks problems with an oil-dependent economy.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/07/01/world/middleeast/fueled-by-oil-wealth-malls-rise-in-baghdad.html', 'snippet': 'A return to prosperity for a war-ravaged country is welcome, but economists fear growth of a consumer culture masks problems with an oil-dependent economy.', 'lead_paragraph': 'BAGHDAD — One has a designated prayer room. Another frisks patrons at the entrance, requiring them to check their pistols, like coats in a fancy restaurant. They are good places to escape the desert heat, and in a conservative Islamic culture, they are one of the few places where young couples openly flirt or women smoke cigarettes in public.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/20